-- Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab) textbook MCQs, chapter 2 (Atomic Structure) CHE-0060 to CHE-0107, chapter 3 (Chemical Bonding) CHE-0108 to CHE-0155, chapter 4 (Stoichiometry) CHE-0156 to CHE-0201, chapter 5 (States and Phases of Matter) CHE-0202 to CHE-0250, chapter 6 (Chemical Energetics) CHE-0251 to CHE-0302, chapter 7 (Reaction Kinetics) CHE-0303 to CHE-0348, chapter 8 (Chemical Equilibrium) CHE-0349 to CHE-0390, chapter 9 (Acid-Base Chemistry) CHE-0391 to CHE-0432, chapter 10 (Electrochemistry) CHE-0433 to CHE-0469, chapter 11 (Hydrocarbons) CHE-0470 to CHE-0508, chapter 12 (Nitrogen and Sulfur) CHE-0509 to CHE-0547, chapter 13 (Halogens) CHE-0548 to CHE-0593, chapter 14 (Atmosphere) CHE-0594 to CHE-0636, chapter 15 (Basic Separation Techniques) CHE-0637 to CHE-0672, chapter 16 (Lab Safety and Practical Skills) CHE-0673 to CHE-0712.
-- Original questions in the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is
-- wrong) with the source page in the textbook. Approved for the bank by the site owner, so they go straight to live.
-- Each question's own chapter is its MDCAT chapter (NUMS and AKU reuse it); FSc Part 1 lists them in bank_questions
-- under the textbook chapter. Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site
-- owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0060', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic number, mass number and ions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 22 (§2.1)', 'How many protons, neutrons and electrons are present in the ion ³⁷Cl⁻ (Z = 17)?', '["17 protons, 20 neutrons, 18 electrons", "17 protons, 20 neutrons, 16 electrons", "18 protons, 19 neutrons, 18 electrons", "17 protons, 37 neutrons, 18 electrons"]', 0, 'Protons = Z = 17. Neutrons = A − Z = 37 − 17 = 20. The 1− charge means one electron has been gained, so electrons = 17 + 1 = 18.', '{"1": "16 electrons would be a 1+ ion. A negative ion has gained an electron.", "2": "The number of protons is fixed by the element: chlorine always has 17.", "3": "37 is the mass number (protons + neutrons), not the number of neutrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0061', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic number, mass number and ions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 22 (§2.1)', 'How many electrons are present in the ion ⁵⁶Fe³⁺ (Z = 26)?', '["26", "29", "23", "30"]', 2, 'A neutral iron atom has 26 electrons (equal to Z). The 3+ charge means three electrons have been lost: 26 − 3 = 23.', '{"0": "26 is the number of protons, or the electrons in a neutral Fe atom, not in Fe³⁺.", "1": "29 adds three electrons. A positive ion has lost electrons.", "3": "30 is the number of neutrons (56 − 26)."}'),
  ('CHE-0062', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic number, mass number and ions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 22 (§2.1, Table 2.1)', 'Which of the following species contains 18 electrons?', '["O²⁻", "Na⁺", "Cl", "S²⁻"]', 3, 'Sulfur has Z = 16; the 2− charge adds two electrons, giving 16 + 2 = 18 (as in Table 2.1).', '{"0": "O²⁻ has 8 + 2 = 10 electrons.", "1": "Na⁺ has 11 − 1 = 10 electrons.", "2": "A neutral chlorine atom has 17 electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0063', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic number: Moseley', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 21 (§2.1)', 'Moseley bombarded different elements with cathode rays and measured the frequency (ν) of the X-rays produced. Which relationship did he find with the atomic number Z?', '["√ν ∝ Z", "ν ∝ √Z", "ν ∝ 1/Z", "λ ∝ Z"]', 0, 'Moseley found that the square root of the frequency of the characteristic X-rays is directly proportional to the atomic number: √ν ∝ Z. He concluded that Z is a fundamental property of an element.', '{"1": "This puts the square root on the wrong side: it is the square root of the frequency that is proportional to Z.", "2": "Frequency increases with atomic number, so it is not inversely proportional to Z.", "3": "Wavelength gets shorter as frequency rises, so wavelength does not increase in proportion to Z."}'),
  ('CHE-0064', 'Atomic structure', 'Particles in an electric field', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 22 (§2.2)', 'Beams of protons, neutrons and electrons moving at the same speed pass through an electric field. Which statement is correct?', '["Neutrons are deflected the most because they are heaviest.", "All three are deflected equally because they move at the same speed.", "Protons are deflected towards the positive plate.", "Electrons are deflected the most, towards the positive plate."]', 3, 'Electrons are negatively charged, so they bend towards the positive plate, and because they are about 1836 times lighter than protons they are deflected much more.', '{"0": "Neutrons have no charge, so they pass straight through without deflection.", "1": "Deflection depends on charge and mass, which differ between the particles; the neutron is not deflected at all.", "2": "Protons are positive, so they are deflected towards the negative plate."}'),
  ('CHE-0065', 'Atomic structure', 'Particles in an electric field', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 23 (§2.2)', 'When a charged particle is deflected in a field and follows a circular path, its radius of deflection is proportional to', '["charge × mass", "charge ÷ mass", "1 ÷ (charge × mass)", "mass ÷ charge"]', 3, 'The radius of deflection is proportional to mass/charge: heavier particles curve less and more highly charged ones curve more. This is the reciprocal of the angle of deflection, which is proportional to charge/mass.', '{"0": "The radius does not increase with charge; more charge bends the particle more, giving a smaller radius.", "1": "Charge/mass gives the angle of deflection, not the radius.", "2": "The radius increases with mass, so mass cannot be in the denominator."}'),
  ('CHE-0066', 'Atomic structure', 'Properties of fundamental particles', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 22 to 23 (§2.2, Table 2.2)', 'The mass of an electron is approximately what fraction of the mass of a proton?', '["1/18", "1/1836", "1/2", "1/18360"]', 1, 'Electrons are about 1836 times lighter than protons (9.1095 × 10⁻³¹ kg compared with 1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg).', '{"0": "1/18 is far too large; the electron is about a hundred times lighter than that.", "2": "1/2 would make the electron comparable in mass to the proton, which it is not.", "3": "1/18360 is ten times too small."}'),
  ('CHE-0067', 'Atomic structure', 'Properties of fundamental particles', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 23 (Table 2.2)', 'According to Table 2.2, which statement about the masses of the proton and neutron is correct?', '["The neutron is slightly heavier than the proton.", "They have exactly the same mass.", "The proton is slightly heavier than the neutron.", "The neutron is about 1836 times heavier than the proton."]', 0, 'The neutron''s mass is 1.0087 amu (1.6750 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) and the proton''s is 1.0073 amu (1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg), so the neutron is slightly heavier.', '{"1": "The values in Table 2.2 differ slightly (1.0087 and 1.0073 amu).", "2": "It is the other way round: the neutron is the heavier one.", "3": "The factor 1836 compares the proton with the electron, not with the neutron."}'),
  ('CHE-0068', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 24 (§2.3.1)', 'How do the dark lines in the absorption spectrum of hydrogen compare with the coloured lines in its emission spectrum?', '["They appear at exactly the same wavelengths.", "They appear at longer wavelengths.", "They appear at shorter wavelengths.", "They have no relationship to each other."]', 0, 'The wavelengths of the dark lines in the absorption spectrum are exactly the same as those of the coloured lines in the emission spectrum, because both come from electrons moving between the same fixed energy levels.', '{"1": "The lines are not shifted; the same energy gaps give the same wavelengths.", "2": "The lines are not shifted; the same energy gaps give the same wavelengths.", "3": "Both spectra come from the same energy levels, so the lines match."}'),
  ('CHE-0069', 'Atomic structure', 'Atomic spectra', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 24 (§2.3.1)', 'Atomic spectra are called the "fingerprints" of elements because', '["all elements give the same spectrum.", "spectra show the number of neutrons in the nucleus.", "spectra are produced only by metals.", "each element has a unique set of energy levels, so its spectrum is unique."]', 3, 'Each element has a unique arrangement of electrons and so a unique set of energy levels. The wavelengths emitted or absorbed are therefore unique, and an element can be identified from its spectrum.', '{"0": "If all spectra were the same they could not identify elements.", "1": "Spectral lines come from electrons changing energy levels, not from the neutrons.", "2": "Any element in the gaseous state gives a spectrum when heated or given an electric discharge."}'),
  ('CHE-0070', 'Atomic structure', 'Successive ionization energies', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 25 (§2.3.2)', 'The first four ionization energies of an element are 496, 4562, 6910 and 9543 kJ mol⁻¹. To which group does it most likely belong?', '["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 13", "Group 14"]', 0, 'The huge jump comes between the first and second ionization energies. So only one electron is in the outer shell; the second must come from an inner shell much closer to the nucleus. One valence electron means group 1.', '{"1": "A group 2 element would show its big jump between the second and third ionization energies.", "2": "A group 13 element would show its big jump between the third and fourth.", "3": "A group 14 element would show no big jump until after the fourth."}'),
  ('CHE-0071', 'Atomic structure', 'Successive ionization energies', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 25 to 26 (§2.3.2, Fig. 2.4)', 'The plot of successive ionization energies of magnesium shows two very large jumps. What does this show?', '["Magnesium''s electrons are arranged in three shells.", "Magnesium has two valence electrons and two shells.", "Magnesium has twelve shells.", "Magnesium''s electrons are all in the same shell."]', 0, 'The first jump comes when the third electron is removed (moving from the outer shell to the second shell) and a much bigger one when the eleventh is removed (from the second to the first shell). Two jumps separate three groups of electrons, which is evidence for three shells.', '{"1": "Magnesium does have two valence electrons, but two jumps mean three shells, not two.", "2": "Twelve is the number of electrons; most removals within a shell rise only gradually.", "3": "Electrons in one shell would give a gradual rise with no large jumps."}'),
  ('CHE-0072', 'Atomic structure', 'First ionization energies', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 26 (§2.3.2, Fig. 2.5)', 'Which statement about first ionization energies is correct?', '["They are all endothermic.", "Alkali metals have the highest values in each period.", "They increase down a group.", "Noble gases have the lowest values in each period."]', 0, 'Energy is always needed to remove an electron from an atom, so all ionization energies are strongly endothermic.', '{"1": "Alkali metals have the lowest values in each period.", "2": "They decrease down a group because the atom gets larger and the outer electron is held less tightly.", "3": "Noble gases have the highest values in each period."}'),
  ('CHE-0073', 'Atomic structure', 'Principal quantum number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 27 (§2.4.1)', 'What is the maximum number of electrons that the shell with n = 4 can hold?', '["8", "16", "18", "32"]', 3, 'The maximum number of electrons in a shell is 2n². For n = 4: 2 × 4² = 32.', '{"0": "8 is the capacity of the n = 2 shell.", "1": "16 is n² (the number of orbitals in the shell); each orbital holds 2 electrons.", "2": "18 is the capacity of the n = 3 shell."}'),
  ('CHE-0074', 'Atomic structure', 'Azimuthal quantum number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (§2.4.2)', 'Which value of the azimuthal quantum number (ℓ) is NOT allowed when n = 3?', '["0", "1", "2", "3"]', 3, 'ℓ can take whole-number values from 0 to (n − 1). For n = 3 that is 0, 1 and 2 (3s, 3p and 3d); ℓ = 3 is not possible.', '{"0": "ℓ = 0 (3s) is allowed.", "1": "ℓ = 1 (3p) is allowed.", "2": "ℓ = 2 (3d) is allowed, since n − 1 = 2."}'),
  ('CHE-0075', 'Atomic structure', 'Azimuthal quantum number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (§2.4.2, Table 2.3)', 'The maximum number of electrons in an f subshell (ℓ = 3) is', '["6", "7", "10", "14"]', 3, 'The number of electrons in a subshell is 2(2ℓ + 1). For ℓ = 3: 2(7) = 14.', '{"0": "6 is the capacity of a p subshell.", "1": "7 is the number of f orbitals; each holds two electrons.", "2": "10 is the capacity of a d subshell."}'),
  ('CHE-0076', 'Atomic structure', 'Shapes of orbitals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (Table 2.3)', 'What is the general shape of an orbital with ℓ = 2?', '["Spherical", "Dumb-bell", "Cloverleaf (double dumb-bell)", "Linear"]', 2, 'ℓ = 2 means a d orbital, which has a cloverleaf (double dumb-bell) shape.', '{"0": "Spherical is the shape of an s orbital (ℓ = 0).", "1": "Dumb-bell is the shape of a p orbital (ℓ = 1).", "3": "No orbital is described as linear."}'),
  ('CHE-0077', 'Atomic structure', 'Subshells in a shell', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (§2.4.2, Table 2.4)', 'How many subshells are present in the shell with n = 4?', '["2", "3", "4", "16"]', 2, 'The number of subshells in a shell equals its shell number. For n = 4 these are 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f.', '{"0": "Two subshells belong to n = 2 (2s, 2p).", "1": "Three subshells belong to n = 3 (3s, 3p, 3d).", "3": "16 is the number of orbitals (n²) in the fourth shell, not subshells."}'),
  ('CHE-0078', 'Atomic structure', 'Magnetic quantum number', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (§2.4.3)', 'Which value of the magnetic quantum number (m) is impossible for an electron in a 3p orbital?', '["−1", "0", "+1", "+2"]', 3, 'For a p orbital ℓ = 1, so m can only be −1, 0 or +1 (three orientations). m = +2 needs ℓ of at least 2.', '{"0": "m = −1 is one of the three allowed values for ℓ = 1.", "1": "m = 0 is allowed for every value of ℓ.", "2": "m = +1 is one of the three allowed values for ℓ = 1."}'),
  ('CHE-0079', 'Atomic structure', 'Degenerate orbitals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 29 (§2.4.3)', 'Degenerate orbitals are orbitals that', '["belong to different shells.", "have different shapes in the same shell.", "contain no electrons.", "have the same energy within the same subshell."]', 3, 'Orbitals of the same subshell have the same energy and are called degenerate; for example the three 2p orbitals. They can be told apart in a magnetic field.', '{"0": "Orbitals in different shells have different energies.", "1": "Orbitals of different shapes (for example 3s and 3p) have different energies.", "2": "Degeneracy is about energy, not about whether the orbital is occupied."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0080', 'Atomic structure', 'Spin quantum number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 29 (§2.4)', 'Why is a fourth quantum number (the spin quantum number) needed?', '["To describe the size of an orbital.", "To describe the shape of an orbital.", "To tell apart the two electrons that can occupy one orbital.", "To describe the orientation of an orbital."]', 2, 'Three quantum numbers (n, ℓ, m) describe an orbital; the spin quantum number (+½ or −½) is needed to distinguish the two electrons that occupy the same orbital.', '{"0": "Size is given by the principal quantum number, n.", "1": "Shape is given by the azimuthal quantum number, ℓ.", "3": "Orientation is given by the magnetic quantum number, m."}'),
  ('CHE-0081', 'Atomic structure', 'Orbitals in a shell', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 29 (Did you Know?)', 'How many orbitals are present in the third shell (n = 3)?', '["3", "6", "9", "18"]', 2, 'A shell with principal quantum number n has n² orbitals: 3² = 9 (one 3s, three 3p and five 3d).', '{"0": "3 is the number of subshells in the third shell.", "1": "6 is not n² for any whole number n; the count 1 + 3 + 5 gives 9.", "3": "18 is the maximum number of electrons (2n²), not orbitals."}'),
  ('CHE-0082', 'Atomic structure', 'Shapes of atomic orbitals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 30 (§2.5)', 'An atomic orbital is best described as', '["the three-dimensional region around the nucleus where the probability of finding an electron is maximum.", "a fixed circular path in which an electron moves.", "the region inside the nucleus where protons are found.", "the path of an electron between two energy levels."]', 0, 'The book defines an atomic orbital as the three-dimensional region in space around the nucleus where the probability of finding the electron is maximum.', '{"1": "A fixed circular path is a Bohr orbit, not a wave-mechanical orbital.", "2": "Orbitals describe electrons, which are outside the nucleus.", "3": "An orbital is a region of space, not a path between levels."}'),
  ('CHE-0083', 'Atomic structure', 'Shapes of atomic orbitals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 30 (§2.5.1, Fig. 2.7)', 'How do the 1s and 2s orbitals compare?', '["Both are spherical, and the 2s orbital is larger.", "The 1s is spherical and the 2s is dumb-bell shaped.", "Both are spherical and of the same size.", "Both are dumb-bell shaped."]', 0, 'All s orbitals are spherical, with electron density spread evenly in all directions. As the principal quantum number increases, the s orbital becomes larger.', '{"1": "The dumb-bell shape belongs to p orbitals; every s orbital is spherical.", "2": "The size of an s orbital increases with n, so 2s is larger.", "3": "s orbitals are spherical, not dumb-bell shaped."}'),
  ('CHE-0084', 'Atomic structure', 'Shapes of d orbitals', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 30 (§2.5.3)', 'Which d orbital has its lobes lying along the x and y axes?', '["dxy", "dxz", "dz²", "dx²−y²"]', 3, 'The lobes of the dx²−y² orbital lie along the x and y axes.', '{"0": "dxy lies in the xy plane, but its lobes point between the axes.", "1": "dxz lies in the xz plane.", "2": "dz² has two lobes along the z axis and a doughnut in the xy plane."}'),
  ('CHE-0085', 'Atomic structure', 'Aufbau principle: (n + ℓ) rule', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 32 (§2.6.2)', 'Which of these subshells is filled first?', '["4f", "5d", "6s", "6p"]', 2, 'The subshell with the lower (n + ℓ) value has lower energy and fills first. 6s: 6 + 0 = 6; 4f: 4 + 3 = 7; 5d: 5 + 2 = 7; 6p: 6 + 1 = 7. So 6s fills first.', '{"0": "4f has n + ℓ = 7, higher than 6s.", "1": "5d has n + ℓ = 7, higher than 6s.", "3": "6p has n + ℓ = 7, higher than 6s."}'),
  ('CHE-0086', 'Atomic structure', 'Aufbau principle: (n + ℓ) rule', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 32 (§2.6.2)', 'The 4d and 5p subshells have the same (n + ℓ) value. Which is filled first, and why?', '["5p, because it has the higher n value.", "Both fill at the same time.", "5p, because p subshells always fill before d subshells.", "4d, because it has the lower n value."]', 3, '4d: 4 + 2 = 6 and 5p: 5 + 1 = 6. When (n + ℓ) is equal, the subshell with the lower n fills first, so 4d comes before 5p.', '{"0": "The rule is the reverse: the lower n value fills first.", "1": "The tie is broken by the n value, so one fills first.", "2": "p does not always come before d; 3d fills before 4p, for example."}'),
  ('CHE-0087', 'Atomic structure', 'Aufbau principle: filling order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 32 (§2.6.2, Fig. 2.10)', 'In the building-up order, after the 5p subshell is completely filled, the next electron enters', '["4f", "5d", "6s", "4d"]', 2, 'The energy order is … 5s < 4d < 5p < 6s < 4f < 5d … so after 5p the next electron enters 6s (n + ℓ = 6, lower n than any other subshell left with that value).', '{"0": "4f comes after 6s (n + ℓ = 7).", "1": "5d comes after 6s and 4f.", "3": "4d is already filled before 5p."}'),
  ('CHE-0088', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration of elements', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 33 (Table 2.6)', 'What is the ground-state electronic configuration of copper (Z = 29)?', '["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁹ 4s²", "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹", "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4p¹", "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 4p⁶ 3d¹"]', 1, 'Table 2.6 gives copper as 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. A completely filled 3d subshell is especially stable, so one 4s electron moves into 3d.', '{"0": "3d⁹ 4s² is what the simple filling order predicts, but copper''s actual configuration has a full 3d¹⁰.", "2": "4p fills only after 4s and 3d are complete; 4s is not empty in copper.", "3": "3d fills before 4p, so this order is wrong."}'),
  ('CHE-0089', 'Atomic structure', 'Pauli''s exclusion principle', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 33 to 34 (§2.6.3)', 'Which pair of electrons in the same atom would violate Pauli''s exclusion principle? (Values are n, ℓ, m, s.)', '["(1, 0, 0, +½) and (1, 0, 0, −½)", "(2, 1, 0, +½) and (2, 1, 1, +½)", "(2, 0, 0, +½) and (2, 0, 0, +½)", "(2, 1, −1, −½) and (3, 1, −1, −½)"]', 2, 'No two electrons in an atom can have the same values for all four quantum numbers. In this pair all four values (2, 0, 0, +½) are identical.', '{"0": "These two electrons share an orbital but have opposite spins, which is allowed (as in helium).", "1": "The m values differ, so they are in different 2p orbitals.", "3": "The n values differ, so they are in different shells."}'),
  ('CHE-0090', 'Atomic structure', 'Hund''s rule', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 34 (§2.6.3, Fig. 2.12)', 'How many unpaired electrons are present in a ground-state oxygen atom (Z = 8)?', '["0", "1", "2", "4"]', 2, 'Oxygen is 1s² 2s² 2p⁴. By Hund''s rule the first three 2p electrons go singly into the three 2p orbitals and the fourth pairs with one of them, leaving two unpaired electrons.', '{"0": "Oxygen''s 2p subshell is not full, so it must have unpaired electrons.", "1": "One unpaired electron would be fluorine (2p⁵).", "3": "There are only four 2p electrons, and one pair must form in three orbitals."}'),
  ('CHE-0091', 'Atomic structure', 'Hund''s rule', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 34 (§2.6.3)', 'A carbon atom has two electrons in its 2p subshell. According to Hund''s rule, these electrons are placed', '["in the same 2p orbital with opposite spins.", "in separate 2p orbitals with the same spin.", "in separate 2p orbitals with opposite spins.", "one in 2p and one in 3s."]', 1, 'Hund''s rule says electrons entering degenerate orbitals occupy separate orbitals with the same spin before any pairing.', '{"0": "Pairing in one orbital happens only after each degenerate orbital has one electron.", "2": "Electrons in separate degenerate orbitals have parallel (same) spins.", "3": "3s is higher in energy; the 2p orbitals fill first (Aufbau)."}'),
  ('CHE-0092', 'Atomic structure', 'Hund''s rule', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 33 (Table 2.6); p. 34 (§2.6.3)', 'How many unpaired electrons does a ground-state iron atom (Z = 26) have?', '["2", "4", "5", "6"]', 1, 'Iron is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². The six 3d electrons fill the five 3d orbitals singly first (Hund''s rule) and the sixth pairs, leaving four unpaired electrons. The 4s² pair is paired.', '{"0": "Two would mean eight d electrons in five orbitals (as in nickel).", "2": "Five unpaired electrons is 3d⁵, as in manganese.", "3": "Five orbitals can hold at most five unpaired electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0093', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration and the periodic table', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 35 (§2.7)', 'What is the general outer-shell configuration of the Group 2 elements?', '["ns¹", "ns²", "ns² np¹", "ns² np²"]', 1, 'Groups 1 and 2 are the s-block; Group 2 elements all have the outer configuration ns².', '{"0": "ns¹ is the outer configuration of Group 1.", "2": "ns² np¹ is the outer configuration of Group 13.", "3": "ns² np² is the outer configuration of Group 14."}'),
  ('CHE-0094', 'Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration and the periodic table', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 35 (§2.7)', 'The d-block of the periodic table is ten columns wide because', '["there are ten transition metals in each period.", "a d subshell can hold a maximum of ten electrons.", "d orbitals have ten lobes.", "the d-block starts from Group 10."]', 1, 'The widths of the blocks match the subshell capacities: s holds 2, p 6, d 10 and f 14 electrons, so the d-block is ten columns wide.', '{"0": "This only restates the width; the reason is the capacity of the d subshell.", "2": "A d orbital has four lobes (or two lobes and a ring for dz²).", "3": "The d-block runs from Group 3 to Group 12."}'),
  ('CHE-0095', 'Atomic structure', 'Classification of elements', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 36 (§2.7.2)', 'The main-group (representative) elements have valence-shell configurations of the type', '["(n−1)d ns", "ns^a np^b", "(n−2)f ns", "(n−1)d^a np^b"]', 1, 'Main-group elements all have valence-shell configurations nsᵃ npᵇ: their outer s or p subshell is being filled.', '{"0": "Filling an (n−1)d subshell describes the d-block transition elements.", "2": "Filling an (n−2)f subshell describes the inner-transition (f-block) elements.", "3": "Main-group elements fill ns and np, not an inner d subshell."}'),
  ('CHE-0096', 'Atomic structure', 'Building-up order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 37 (§2.7.2)', 'In the fourth period, in what order are the subshells filled?', '["4s, 4p, 3d", "3d, 4s, 4p", "4s, 3d, 4p", "4s, 4p, 4d"]', 2, 'In the fourth period 4s fills first, then 3d (the transition metals), then 4p.', '{"0": "3d fills before 4p.", "1": "4s fills before 3d because it has the lower (n + ℓ) value.", "3": "4d belongs to the fifth period; 3d fills in the fourth."}'),
  ('CHE-0097', 'Atomic structure', 'Position from configuration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 35 to 37 (§2.7)', 'An element has the configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p². Where is it in the periodic table?', '["Period 4, group 14, p-block", "Period 4, group 2, s-block", "Period 3, group 14, p-block", "Period 4, group 12, d-block"]', 0, 'The highest shell is n = 4, so it is in period 4. The last electrons enter 4p, so it is a p-block element; with four outer electrons (4s² 4p²) it is in group 14. The element is germanium.', '{"1": "The last electrons go into 4p, not 4s, so it is not an s-block element.", "2": "The outermost shell is n = 4, not 3.", "3": "The 3d subshell is already complete; the last electrons are in 4p."}'),
  ('CHE-0098', 'Atomic structure', 'Configuration of ions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 37 (§2.8.1)', 'Which ion has the same electronic configuration as argon?', '["Na⁺", "Mg²⁺", "F⁻", "S²⁻"]', 3, 'S²⁻ has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons, giving 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶, the configuration of argon.', '{"0": "Na⁺ has 10 electrons, the configuration of neon.", "1": "Mg²⁺ has 10 electrons, the configuration of neon.", "2": "F⁻ has 10 electrons, the configuration of neon."}'),
  ('CHE-0099', 'Atomic structure', 'Configuration of d-block ions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 37 to 38 (§2.8.1)', 'What is the electronic configuration of Ti²⁺ (Z = 22)?', '["[Ar] 3d² 4s²", "[Ar] 3d²", "[Ar] 4s²", "[Ar] 3d¹ 4s¹"]', 1, 'Titanium is [Ar] 3d² 4s². When d-block atoms form ions, the 4s electrons are lost first, so Ti²⁺ is [Ar] 3d².', '{"0": "This is the neutral titanium atom, not the ion.", "2": "This removes the 3d electrons instead; the 4s electrons are lost first.", "3": "The two electrons removed are both 4s electrons."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0100', 'Atomic structure', 'Configuration of d-block ions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, pp. 37 to 38 (§2.8.1); Table 2.6, p. 33', 'What is the electronic configuration of Mn²⁺ (Z = 25)?', '["[Ar] 3d⁵", "[Ar] 3d³ 4s²", "[Ar] 3d⁴ 4s¹", "[Ar] 3d⁶"]', 0, 'Manganese is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² (Table 2.6). On forming a positive ion the 4s electrons are lost first, so Mn²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁵.', '{"1": "This removes 3d electrons first; d-block atoms lose their 4s electrons first.", "2": "Both electrons come from 4s, not one from 4s and one from 3d.", "3": "3d⁶ would need an extra electron added to 3d; Mn²⁺ has lost electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0101', 'Atomic structure', 'Free radicals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 38 (§2.8.2)', 'Which of the following is a free radical?', '["CH₄", "H₂O", "•CH₃", "Na⁺"]', 2, 'A free radical is a species with one or more unpaired electrons. The methyl radical, •CH₃, has an unpaired electron (shown by the dot).', '{"0": "In CH₄ all electrons are paired in four C–H bonds.", "1": "In H₂O all electrons are paired (two bonds and two lone pairs).", "3": "Na⁺ has the neon configuration with all electrons paired."}'),
  ('CHE-0102', 'Atomic structure', 'Semiconductors', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 38 (§2.9.1)', 'A P-type semiconductor is made by adding to pure silicon atoms of', '["aluminium, which create holes that act as positive charge carriers.", "phosphorus, which provide extra free electrons.", "aluminium, which provide extra free electrons.", "phosphorus, which create holes."]', 0, 'Trivalent atoms such as aluminium have only three valence electrons, so where they replace silicon one bond lacks an electron. These holes act as positive charge carriers, giving a P-type (positive-type) semiconductor.', '{"1": "Phosphorus (five valence electrons) supplies extra electrons and gives an N-type semiconductor.", "2": "Aluminium has fewer valence electrons than silicon, so it creates holes, not extra electrons.", "3": "Phosphorus has more valence electrons than silicon, so it gives extra electrons, not holes."}'),
  ('CHE-0103', 'Atomic structure', 'Semiconductors', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 39 (§2.9.2)', 'When phosphorus atoms replace some silicon atoms in a silicon crystal, the crystal conducts because', '["each phosphorus atom creates a hole.", "each phosphorus atom contributes a fifth, extra electron that is free to move.", "phosphorus atoms are metals.", "silicon atoms lose all their valence electrons."]', 1, 'Phosphorus has five valence electrons. Four form bonds with neighbouring silicon atoms and the fifth is extra, free to move as a negative charge carrier. This gives an N-type semiconductor.', '{"0": "Holes are created by trivalent atoms such as aluminium.", "2": "Phosphorus is a non-metal; it conducts here because of its extra electron.", "3": "The silicon atoms keep their four bonds; the free electrons come from phosphorus."}'),
  ('CHE-0104', 'Atomic structure', 'Semiconductors', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 38 (§2.9)', 'Pure crystalline silicon (configuration 2, 8, 4) does not conduct electricity well because', '["silicon has no valence electrons.", "each silicon atom uses all four valence electrons in bonds to four other silicon atoms, leaving no free electrons.", "silicon atoms are ions.", "silicon has a full outer shell of eight electrons as an isolated atom."]', 1, 'In pure crystalline silicon each atom is bonded to four other silicon atoms, using all four valence electrons, so there are no free electrons to carry current.', '{"0": "Silicon has four valence electrons (2, 8, 4).", "2": "Pure silicon is held together by covalent bonds between atoms, not ions.", "3": "An isolated silicon atom has four outer electrons, not eight."}'),
  ('CHE-0105', 'Atomic structure', 'Electrons in subshells', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (Table 2.3); p. 33 (Table 2.6)', 'In a ground-state argon atom (Z = 18), how many electrons have the azimuthal quantum number ℓ = 1?', '["6", "8", "12", "18"]', 2, 'ℓ = 1 means p electrons. Argon is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶, so it has 6 (2p) + 6 (3p) = 12 p electrons.', '{"0": "6 counts only one p subshell; argon has both 2p and 3p filled.", "1": "8 is the number of electrons in argon''s outer shell (3s² 3p⁶).", "3": "18 is the total number of electrons, including the s electrons (ℓ = 0)."}'),
  ('CHE-0106', 'Atomic structure', 'Quantum numbers', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 27 (§2.4)', 'Which quantum numbers come from Schrödinger''s wave equation to describe an orbital?', '["n only", "n, ℓ and m", "n, ℓ, m and s", "ℓ and s only"]', 1, 'Schrödinger''s model treats the electron in three-dimensional space, so three quantum numbers come from the wave equation: principal (n), azimuthal (ℓ) and magnetic (m). These describe the size, shape and orientation of an orbital.', '{"0": "n alone was enough for the one-dimensional Bohr model, not for three-dimensional orbitals.", "2": "The spin quantum number is not one of the three that describe an orbital; it describes the electron''s spin.", "3": "n and m are needed; s does not describe an orbital."}'),
  ('CHE-0107', 'Atomic structure', 'Azimuthal quantum number: designations', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Atomic Structure, p. 28 (§2.4.2)', 'The letters s, p, d and f used for subshells come from descriptions of spectral lines. What does "p" stand for?', '["Polar", "Principal", "Primary", "Paired"]', 1, 'The letters stand for sharp, principal, diffused and fundamental, describing spectral lines.', '{"0": "\"Polar\" describes the dumb-bell shape of a p orbital in Table 2.3, but the letter p stands for principal.", "2": "\"Primary\" is not one of the spectral-line names.", "3": "\"Paired\" refers to electron spins, not to the letter p."}'),
  ('CHE-0108', 'Chemical bonding', 'Lewis concept of bonding', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 44 (§3.1)', 'According to the Lewis concept, atoms form chemical bonds in order to', '["increase their atomic mass.", "increase the number of unpaired electrons.", "lose all their valence electrons.", "attain a noble-gas electronic configuration, usually an octet."]', 3, 'Lewis theory says atoms bond to complete their outermost shells and reach a noble-gas configuration, mostly by forming an octet in the valence shell.', '{"0": "Bonding does not change atomic mass.", "1": "Bonding usually pairs electrons, reducing the number of unpaired electrons.", "2": "Atoms need not lose all valence electrons; they may gain or share them to complete a shell."}'),
  ('CHE-0109', 'Chemical bonding', 'Ionic bond', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 44 (§3.1.1)', 'In the formation of sodium chloride, the chloride ion Cl⁻ attains the electronic configuration of', '["neon (2, 8).", "helium (2).", "sodium (2, 8, 1).", "argon (2, 8, 8)."]', 3, 'Chlorine (2, 8, 7) gains one electron to form Cl⁻ (2, 8, 8), the configuration of argon, the next noble gas.', '{"0": "Neon''s configuration (2, 8) is reached by Na⁺, not Cl⁻.", "1": "Helium''s configuration has only two electrons; Cl⁻ has 18.", "2": "2, 8, 1 is a neutral sodium atom."}'),
  ('CHE-0110', 'Chemical bonding', 'Covalent bond', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 45 (§3.1.2)', 'Which molecule contains a purely non-polar covalent bond?', '["HCl", "HBr", "Br₂", "H₂O"]', 2, 'In Br₂ both atoms are identical and have exactly the same electronegativity, so the shared pair is shared equally and neither atom carries a charge.', '{"0": "H and Cl have different electronegativities, so H–Cl is polar.", "1": "H and Br have different electronegativities, so H–Br is polar.", "3": "O is more electronegative than H, so the O–H bonds are polar."}'),
  ('CHE-0111', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dative bond', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 45 (§3.1.3)', 'How is the dative bond formed in the hydronium ion, H₃O⁺?', '["The oxygen atom of water donates a lone pair to a proton (H⁺).", "H⁺ donates an electron pair to the oxygen atom.", "Oxygen and H⁺ each contribute one electron.", "An electron is transferred completely from oxygen to H⁺."]', 0, 'A dative bond forms when both electrons of the shared pair come from one atom. In H₃O⁺ the oxygen of water, which has two lone pairs, donates one to the electron-deficient proton.', '{"1": "A proton has no electrons to donate; it is the acceptor.", "2": "Each atom contributing one electron describes an ordinary covalent bond.", "3": "Complete transfer of an electron describes an ionic bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0112', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dative bond', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 45 (§3.1.3, Fig. 3.3)', 'In carbon monoxide, the dative bond is formed when', '["oxygen donates one of its lone pairs to carbon to complete carbon''s octet.", "carbon donates a lone pair to oxygen.", "each atom shares one more electron.", "carbon transfers two electrons to oxygen."]', 0, 'Carbon and oxygen first form a normal double bond, but carbon still lacks an octet. Oxygen then donates one of its lone pairs to carbon, forming a dative bond.', '{"1": "The donor is oxygen; carbon is the atom whose octet is incomplete.", "2": "Each atom sharing one electron would be an ordinary covalent bond, not a dative one.", "3": "CO is a covalent molecule; no electrons are transferred."}'),
  ('CHE-0113', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dative bond: ozone', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 45 to 46 (§3.1.3)', 'In the ozone molecule described in the textbook, which statement about the formal charges is correct?', '["The central oxygen carries a positive charge and the oxygen that accepts the lone pair carries a negative charge.", "All three oxygen atoms are neutral.", "The central oxygen carries a negative charge.", "Both end oxygen atoms carry positive charges."]', 0, 'Ozone forms when an O₂ molecule reacts with an oxygen atom. The central oxygen keeps a double bond on one side and donates a lone pair to the other oxygen (a coordinate bond). The donor (central) oxygen carries a positive charge and the acceptor oxygen a negative charge.', '{"1": "The donor and acceptor atoms of the coordinate bond carry charges.", "2": "The central oxygen donates a pair, so it becomes positive, not negative.", "3": "Only one end oxygen (the acceptor) is charged, and it is negative."}'),
  ('CHE-0114', 'Chemical bonding', 'Expanded octet', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 46 (§3.1.4)', 'How many electrons are present in the valence shell of sulfur in the sulfate ion, SO₄²⁻, according to its Lewis structure in the textbook?', '["8", "10", "12", "14"]', 2, 'In the Lewis structure S forms two double bonds and two single bonds: 2(4) + 2(2) = 12 electrons, which exceeds the octet by four.', '{"0": "8 would be a normal octet; sulfate is an example of an expanded octet.", "1": "10 is the count for the central iodine in I₃⁻.", "3": "14 electrons is more than this structure gives."}'),
  ('CHE-0115', 'Chemical bonding', 'Expanded octet', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 47 (§3.1.4)', 'In the tri-iodide ion, I₃⁻, by how many electrons does the central iodine atom exceed the octet?', '["2", "4", "6", "0"]', 0, 'The central iodine has two single bonds and three lone pairs: 2(2) + 2(3) = 10 electrons, which is two more than an octet.', '{"1": "Exceeding by 4 is the case for sulfur in SO₄²⁻ (12 electrons).", "2": "That would mean 14 electrons around iodine.", "3": "I₃⁻ is one of the book''s examples of an expanded octet."}'),
  ('CHE-0116', 'Chemical bonding', 'Expanded octet', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 46 (§3.1.4)', 'Sulfur can have an expanded octet in SO₄²⁻ mainly because', '["it is a metal.", "it has no lone pairs.", "it has vacant 3d orbitals that can accommodate extra electrons.", "it is in period 2."]', 2, 'Octet expansion is caused by the involvement of d orbitals in bonding. Sulfur''s empty 3d orbitals let it unpair electrons and accommodate more than eight electrons.', '{"0": "Sulfur is a non-metal.", "1": "Having lone pairs or not is not what allows expansion; the d orbitals are.", "3": "Sulfur is in period 3; period 2 elements have no 2d orbitals and cannot expand their octet."}'),
  ('CHE-0117', 'Chemical bonding', 'Electronegativity and bond type', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 47 (§3.2, Fig. 3.4)', 'Two atoms differ in electronegativity by 1.2. According to the textbook, the bond between them is', '["pure (non-polar) covalent.", "metallic.", "ionic.", "polar covalent."]', 3, 'A difference below 0.4 gives a pure covalent bond, 0.4 to 1.8 a polar covalent bond, and above 1.8 an ionic bond. 1.2 falls in the polar covalent range.', '{"0": "Pure covalent bonds have a difference below 0.4.", "1": "Metallic bonding is not classified by electronegativity difference in this scheme.", "2": "Ionic bonds have a difference above 1.8."}'),
  ('CHE-0118', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dipole moment', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 48 (§3.2.1)', 'In which unit is dipole moment measured?', '["Debye (D)", "Pascal (Pa)", "Joule (J)", "Picometre (pm)"]', 0, 'Dipole moment, a quantitative measure of the polarity of a bond or molecule, is measured in Debye units (D).', '{"1": "The pascal is a unit of pressure.", "2": "The joule is a unit of energy.", "3": "The picometre is used for bond lengths and radii."}'),
  ('CHE-0119', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dipole moment and shape', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 48 (§3.2.1)', 'Water has a dipole moment of 1.85 D. What does this tell us about the shape of the molecule?', '["It is non-linear (V-shaped).", "It is linear.", "It is tetrahedral with four atoms.", "Its O–H bonds are non-polar."]', 0, 'A linear H–O–H molecule would have zero dipole moment because the two bond moments would cancel. A non-zero dipole moment shows water is non-linear; experiments show it is V-shaped.', '{"1": "In a linear molecule the two equal O–H bond moments would cancel, giving zero.", "2": "Water has only three atoms, so it cannot be a tetrahedral molecule.", "3": "The O–H bonds are polar; that is why the molecule can have a dipole moment."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0120', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dipole moment and symmetry', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 48 to 49 (§3.2.1)', 'Which statement about carbon tetrachloride, CCl₄, is correct?', '["Its C–Cl bonds are non-polar, so the molecule is non-polar.", "It is polar because chlorine is more electronegative than carbon.", "Its C–Cl bonds are polar, but the molecule is non-polar because the bond moments cancel in the tetrahedral shape.", "It is polar because it has lone pairs on carbon."]', 2, 'Each C–Cl bond is polar because of the electronegativity difference, but in the perfectly tetrahedral molecule the four bond moments cancel, so the net dipole moment is zero.', '{"0": "The C–Cl bonds are polar; the molecule is non-polar because of its symmetry.", "1": "Bond polarity alone does not make a molecule polar; the symmetric shape cancels the bond moments.", "3": "Carbon in CCl₄ has no lone pairs; all four of its pairs are bonding."}'),
  ('CHE-0121', 'Chemical bonding', 'Dipole moment and symmetry', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 48 to 49 (§3.2.1)', 'Which of the following molecules has a net dipole moment?', '["BeCl₂", "CCl₄", "BF₃", "H₂S"]', 3, 'H₂S is non-linear, so its two polar S–H bond moments do not cancel and the molecule has a net dipole moment.', '{"0": "BeCl₂ is linear (180°), so its equal bond moments cancel.", "1": "CCl₄ is perfectly tetrahedral, so its bond moments cancel.", "2": "BF₃ is trigonal planar and symmetrical, so its bond moments cancel."}'),
  ('CHE-0122', 'Chemical bonding', 'Intermolecular forces', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 49 (§3.3)', 'Which of the following is NOT one of the van der Waals (intermolecular) forces named in the textbook?', '["London dispersion forces", "Permanent dipole–permanent dipole forces", "Hydrogen bonding", "Ionic bonding"]', 3, 'The book lists three intermolecular forces: instantaneous dipole–induced dipole (London) forces, permanent dipole–permanent dipole forces and hydrogen bonding. Ionic bonding is a chemical bond, not an intermolecular force.', '{"0": "London dispersion forces are one of the three listed.", "1": "Dipole–dipole forces are one of the three listed.", "2": "Hydrogen bonding is one of the three listed."}'),
  ('CHE-0123', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond energy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 49 (§3.4)', 'Bond energy is defined as', '["the energy released when one bond forms in a single molecule.", "the energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom.", "the distance between two bonded nuclei.", "the average energy required to break all bonds of a particular type in one mole of a substance."]', 3, 'Bond energy is the average energy needed to break all the bonds of a particular type in one mole of a substance, in kJ mol⁻¹. It measures bond strength.', '{"0": "Bond energy refers to a mole of bonds and to breaking them.", "1": "Removing an electron from a gaseous atom is ionization energy.", "2": "The distance between bonded nuclei is the bond length."}'),
  ('CHE-0124', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 50 (§3.4)', 'The H–F bond energy (565 kJ mol⁻¹) is much higher than the H–I bond energy (295 kJ mol⁻¹). The textbook''s explanation is that', '["the greater electronegativity difference in H–F makes the bond more polar, adding extra attraction.", "iodine is more electronegative than fluorine.", "H–F is a double bond.", "H–I is an ionic bond."]', 0, 'Bond energy rises with the electronegativity difference between the bonded atoms. The larger difference in H–F makes it more polar, which adds extra attraction between the atoms.', '{"1": "Fluorine is the most electronegative element, far more than iodine.", "2": "Both are single bonds.", "3": "H–I is a polar covalent bond with a small electronegativity difference."}'),
  ('CHE-0125', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond energy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 50 (§3.4)', 'Which order of bond energy is correct for carbon–carbon bonds?', '["C–C > C=C > C≡C", "C=C > C≡C > C–C", "C≡C > C=C > C–C", "All three are equal."]', 2, 'Multiple bonds have greater bond energies than single bonds: C≡C > C=C > C–C.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order; more shared electron pairs make a stronger bond.", "1": "The triple bond is the strongest of the three.", "3": "The energies differ with the number of shared pairs."}'),
  ('CHE-0126', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond length', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 50 (§3.4)', 'The C–Cl bond (about 180 pm) is longer than the C–F bond (135 pm) because', '["chlorine is more electronegative than fluorine.", "C–Cl is a double bond.", "the chlorine atom is much larger than the fluorine atom.", "C–F is an ionic bond."]', 2, 'Covalent bond length increases with the size of the bonded atoms. Chlorine is much larger than fluorine, so C–Cl is longer.', '{"0": "Fluorine is more electronegative; a greater electronegativity difference would shorten a bond, not lengthen it.", "1": "Both are single bonds.", "3": "C–F is a polar covalent bond, not ionic."}'),
  ('CHE-0127', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond length', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 50 to 51 (§3.4)', 'The Si–F bond length in SiF₄ is about 155 pm, although adding the covalent radii of Si and F gives 181 pm. Why is the actual bond shorter?', '["Si–F is a triple bond.", "The covalent radii were measured incorrectly.", "Fluorine is larger than silicon.", "The large electronegativity difference gives the bond ionic character, and the extra attraction shortens it."]', 3, 'A large electronegativity difference gives a covalent bond partial ionic character. The additional attraction between the bonded atoms pulls them closer, so the bond is shorter than the sum of covalent radii.', '{"0": "Si–F in SiF₄ is a single bond.", "1": "The book explains the difference by ionic character, not measurement error.", "2": "Fluorine (64 pm) is smaller than silicon (117 pm)."}'),
  ('CHE-0128', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond length', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 50 (§3.4)', 'Which method is NOT used to determine bond lengths experimentally?', '["X-ray diffraction", "Electron diffraction", "Spectral studies", "Acid–base titration"]', 3, 'Bond lengths are found by physical techniques such as electron diffraction, X-ray diffraction and spectral studies. Titration measures concentrations, not bond lengths.', '{"0": "X-ray diffraction is one of the methods named.", "1": "Electron diffraction is one of the methods named.", "2": "Spectral studies are one of the methods named."}'),
  ('CHE-0129', 'Chemical bonding', 'Comparing bonds and intermolecular forces', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 51 (§3.5, Table 3.3)', 'Which order of strength is correct?', '["Ionic bond > covalent bond > hydrogen bond > London forces", "Hydrogen bond > ionic bond > covalent bond > London forces", "London forces > hydrogen bond > covalent bond > ionic bond", "Covalent bond > London forces > ionic bond > hydrogen bond"]', 0, 'The book gives ionic (NaCl) 760, covalent (O–H) 464, hydrogen bond 20 to 25 and London forces 1 to 20 kJ mol⁻¹. Chemical bonds are much stronger than intermolecular forces.', '{"1": "Hydrogen bonds (20 to 25 kJ mol⁻¹) are far weaker than ionic and covalent bonds.", "2": "This is the reverse of the correct order.", "3": "London forces are the weakest here, much weaker than ionic bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0130', 'Chemical bonding', 'Comparing bonds and intermolecular forces', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 51 (§3.5)', 'The metallic bond (100 to 150 kJ mol⁻¹) is weaker than ionic and covalent bonds. The textbook''s explanation for this is', '["extensive delocalization of electrons within the metallic crystal.", "the absence of electrons between metal atoms.", "the presence of hydrogen bonds in metals.", "the high electronegativity of metals."]', 0, 'The book''s explanation for the lower strength of the metallic bond is the extensive delocalization of electrons throughout the metallic crystal.', '{"1": "Metals have a sea of delocalized electrons; electrons are not absent.", "2": "Metals are not held together by hydrogen bonds.", "3": "Metals have low electronegativities."}'),
  ('CHE-0131', 'Chemical bonding', 'Valence bond theory', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 52 (§3.6)', 'According to valence bond theory, a covalent bond is formed when', '["two completely filled orbitals overlap.", "an electron is transferred from one atom to another.", "half-filled valence orbitals of two atoms with similar energy overlap.", "two empty orbitals overlap."]', 2, 'VBT says a covalent bond forms by overlap of half-filled valence orbitals of similar energy on two atoms; greater overlap gives a stronger bond.', '{"0": "Filled orbitals have no unpaired electrons to pair up into a bond.", "1": "Electron transfer gives an ionic bond.", "3": "Empty orbitals contain no electrons to share."}'),
  ('CHE-0132', 'Chemical bonding', 'Sigma bond', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 52 (§3.6.1)', 'A sigma (σ) bond is formed by', '["sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals.", "overlap of orbitals above and below the nuclear axis only.", "transfer of electrons between atoms.", "head-on (linear) overlap of orbitals along the nuclear axis."]', 3, 'A σ bond forms by linear (head-on) overlap of two half-filled orbitals that approach each other along the nuclear axis. Both s and p orbitals can form σ bonds.', '{"0": "Sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals forms a π bond.", "1": "Electron density above and below the axis describes a π bond.", "2": "Electron transfer forms an ionic bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0133', 'Chemical bonding', 'Sigma bond', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 52 (§3.6.1, Fig. 3.9)', 'The bond in the HCl molecule is formed by which type of overlap?', '["s–p", "s–s", "p–p", "sp³–s"]', 0, 'The half-filled 1s orbital of hydrogen overlaps with a half-filled 3p orbital of chlorine (s–p overlap). Electron density is greater near Cl, making HCl polar.', '{"1": "s–s overlap forms the H₂ molecule.", "2": "p–p overlap forms the Cl₂ molecule.", "3": "sp³–s overlap forms the C–H bonds of methane."}'),
  ('CHE-0134', 'Chemical bonding', 'Valence bond theory: H₂S', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 52 to 53 (§3.6.1, Fig. 3.11)', 'According to valence bond theory as described in the textbook, H₂S has a bond angle of about 92° because', '["sulfur is sp hybridized.", "sulfur has no lone pairs.", "the two half-filled 3p orbitals of sulfur, which are nearly at right angles, overlap with the 1s orbitals of hydrogen.", "the H atoms repel each other strongly."]', 2, 'Two half-filled 3p orbitals of sulfur (for example 3py and 3pz) overlap with the 1s orbitals of two H atoms. Since p orbitals are perpendicular, the H–S–H angle is close to 90° (92°).', '{"0": "sp hybridization would give a linear 180° molecule.", "1": "Sulfur in H₂S has lone pairs; the angle is explained by the use of p orbitals.", "3": "Strong H–H repulsion would widen the angle, not keep it near 90°."}'),
  ('CHE-0135', 'Chemical bonding', 'Pi bond', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 53 (§3.6.1, Fig. 3.12)', 'The double bond in an O₂ molecule consists of', '["two σ bonds.", "one σ bond and one dative bond.", "two π bonds.", "one σ bond formed by head-on overlap and one π bond formed by sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals."]', 3, 'The px orbitals of the two oxygen atoms overlap end-to-end to form a σ bond; the py orbitals lie parallel and overlap sideways, above and below the plane of the nuclei, to form a π bond.', '{"0": "Only one σ bond can form between two atoms; the second bond is a π bond.", "1": "No lone pair is donated in O=O; both bonds are ordinary covalent bonds.", "2": "One of the two bonds is formed by head-on overlap, which is a σ bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0136', 'Chemical bonding', 'Hybridization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 53 to 54 (§3.7)', 'How many hybrid orbitals are formed when one s orbital mixes with two p orbitals?', '["2", "3", "4", "6"]', 1, 'The number of hybrid orbitals always equals the number of atomic orbitals mixed: 1 + 2 = 3 sp² hybrid orbitals.', '{"0": "Two hybrid orbitals come from one s and one p orbital (sp).", "2": "Four hybrid orbitals come from one s and three p orbitals (sp³).", "3": "Three atomic orbitals cannot give six hybrids."}'),
  ('CHE-0137', 'Chemical bonding', 'sp hybridization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 54 (§3.7.1)', 'In BeCl₂, beryllium uses sp hybrid orbitals. What is the angle between them?', '["90°", "109.5°", "120°", "180°"]', 3, 'The two sp hybrid orbitals lie in a straight line at 180°, giving linear BeCl₂.', '{"0": "90° is the angle in octahedral and some trigonal bipyramidal arrangements.", "1": "109.5° is the angle between sp³ hybrid orbitals.", "2": "120° is the angle between sp² hybrid orbitals."}'),
  ('CHE-0138', 'Chemical bonding', 'sp² hybridization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 55 (§3.7.1, Fig. 3.15)', 'BF₃ is trigonal planar with bond angles of 120°. The boron atom in BF₃ is', '["sp² hybridized.", "sp hybridized.", "sp³ hybridized.", "not hybridized."]', 0, 'Boron''s 2s and two 2p orbitals mix to form three sp² hybrid orbitals in a trigonal planar arrangement at 120°; each overlaps with a half-filled 2p orbital of fluorine.', '{"1": "sp hybridization gives a linear (180°) arrangement.", "2": "sp³ hybridization gives a tetrahedral (109.5°) arrangement.", "3": "Boron''s ground state has only one unpaired electron; it must be excited and hybridized to form three equal bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0139', 'Chemical bonding', 'sp³ hybridization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 55 to 56 (§3.7.1)', 'In methane, each C–H bond is formed by the overlap of', '["an sp² orbital of carbon with a 1s orbital of hydrogen.", "a 2p orbital of carbon with a 1s orbital of hydrogen.", "an sp³ orbital of carbon with a 1s orbital of hydrogen.", "an sp orbital of carbon with a 1s orbital of hydrogen."]', 2, 'Carbon''s 2s and three 2p orbitals mix to give four sp³ hybrid orbitals pointing to the corners of a regular tetrahedron. Each overlaps with a hydrogen 1s orbital, forming four σ (sp³–s) bonds.', '{"0": "sp² orbitals would give a planar, not tetrahedral, molecule.", "1": "Unhybridized p orbitals are at 90° and could not give four equal bonds at 109.5°.", "3": "sp orbitals give a linear arrangement."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0140', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR model', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 56 (§3.8)', 'The VSEPR model was presented in 1940 by', '["Lewis and Langmuir.", "Mulliken and Hund.", "Heitler and London.", "Sidgwick and Powell."]', 3, 'The book states that the VSEPR model was presented by Sidgwick and Powell in 1940.', '{"0": "Lewis and Langmuir are associated with the electron-pair (octet) idea of bonding, not VSEPR.", "1": "Mulliken and Hund are associated with molecular orbital theory.", "2": "Heitler and London are associated with valence bond theory."}'),
  ('CHE-0141', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR model', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 56 (§3.8)', 'According to the VSEPR model, which order of repulsion between electron pairs is correct?', '["bp–bp > lp–bp > lp–lp", "lp–lp > lp–bp > bp–bp", "lp–bp > lp–lp > bp–bp", "All repulsions are equal."]', 1, 'Lone pairs occupy more space than bond pairs, so repulsion decreases in the order lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order; bond pairs repel each other least.", "2": "Lone pair–lone pair repulsion is the greatest.", "3": "Lone pairs repel more strongly than bond pairs."}'),
  ('CHE-0142', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: multiple bonds', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 56 and 59 (§3.8)', 'SO₃ is perfectly trigonal planar with 120° angles, even though each S=O is a double bond. This is because, in the VSEPR model,', '["double bonds are ignored completely.", "the electron pairs of a double bond are treated as a single region (one pair).", "sulfur has a lone pair.", "double bonds repel less than single bonds."]', 1, 'The two electron pairs of a double bond (and three of a triple bond) are regarded as a single pair. SO₃ therefore has three regions and no lone pair around sulfur, giving a trigonal planar shape.', '{"0": "Double bonds are counted, but as one region each.", "2": "If sulfur had a lone pair, SO₃ would not be planar with 120° angles (compare SO₂).", "3": "Multiple bonds have higher electron density; they are counted as one region, not as weaker repellers."}'),
  ('CHE-0143', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: AB₂ with a lone pair', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 59 (§3.8.1, Fig. 3.19)', 'What is the shape of a SnCl₂ molecule in the vapour phase?', '["Linear, 180°", "V-shaped, angle less than 120°", "Trigonal planar, 120°", "Tetrahedral, 109.5°"]', 1, 'Tin has four valence electrons: two form bonds with Cl and two remain as a lone pair. The three electron regions are trigonal, but excluding the lone pair the molecule is V-shaped, with the angle squeezed below 120° by the lone pair.', '{"0": "A linear shape needs two regions and no lone pair, as in BeCl₂.", "2": "Trigonal planar needs three bond pairs; SnCl₂ has two bonds and a lone pair.", "3": "Tetrahedral needs four electron pairs around the central atom."}'),
  ('CHE-0144', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: AB₃ with a lone pair', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 58 to 59 (§3.8.1)', 'Ammonia has one lone pair on nitrogen. What are its shape and H–N–H bond angle?', '["Trigonal planar, 120°", "Tetrahedral, 109.5°", "Triangular pyramidal, 107°", "V-shaped, 104.5°"]', 2, 'N has four electron pairs (three bond pairs and one lone pair), so the electron pairs are tetrahedral. The lone pair repels the bond pairs more strongly, reducing the angle from 109.5° to 107°, and the molecule is pyramidal.', '{"0": "Trigonal planar would need three electron pairs with no lone pair.", "1": "109.5° is the angle with no lone pair, as in CH₄.", "3": "V-shaped and 104.5° describe water, which has two lone pairs."}'),
  ('CHE-0145', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: effect of electronegative atoms', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 59 to 60 (§3.8.1)', 'The bond angle in NF₃ (102.5°) is smaller than in NH₃ (107°). Which reason does the textbook give?', '["NF₃ has two lone pairs on nitrogen.", "The N–F bond pairs are drawn towards the F atoms, so they repel each other less, while the lone pair repels more strongly.", "Fluorine atoms are smaller than hydrogen atoms.", "NF₃ is sp² hybridized."]', 1, 'Highly electronegative F atoms pull the bonding pairs away from nitrogen. The bond pairs are then further apart from each other near N, repel less and can come closer, while the lone pair exerts a stronger repulsion. The angle closes to 102.5°.', '{"0": "NF₃, like NH₃, has one lone pair on nitrogen.", "2": "Fluorine atoms are larger than hydrogen atoms; size is not the reason given.", "3": "NF₃ has four electron pairs around N, as NH₃ does."}'),
  ('CHE-0146', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: bond angles', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 59 to 60 (§3.8.1)', 'Which order of bond angles is correct?', '["CH₄ > NH₃ > H₂O", "H₂O > NH₃ > CH₄", "NH₃ > CH₄ > H₂O", "All three are 109.5°."]', 0, 'All three have four electron pairs around the central atom. CH₄ has no lone pair (109.5°), NH₃ has one (107°) and H₂O has two (104.5°). More lone pairs compress the bond angle more.', '{"1": "This is the reverse; extra lone pairs reduce the angle.", "2": "NH₃ (107°) has a smaller angle than CH₄ (109.5°).", "3": "Only CH₄ has the ideal 109.5°; lone pairs reduce the angles in NH₃ and H₂O."}'),
  ('CHE-0147', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: ions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 60 (§3.8.1, Fig. 3.24)', 'What is the shape of the amide ion, NH₂⁻?', '["Linear", "V-shaped", "Trigonal pyramidal", "Tetrahedral"]', 1, 'In NH₂⁻ nitrogen has two bond pairs and two lone pairs (four electron pairs). Excluding the lone pairs, the ion is V-shaped, like water.', '{"0": "Two bond pairs with two lone pairs do not give a straight line.", "2": "Trigonal pyramidal needs three bond pairs and one lone pair, as in H₃O⁺.", "3": "Tetrahedral needs four bond pairs and no lone pair."}'),
  ('CHE-0148', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: AB₅ type', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 61 (§3.8.1, Fig. 3.25)', 'Which bond angles are found in the trigonal bipyramidal PCl₅ molecule?', '["Only 109.5°", "120° in the plane and 90° between axial and planar bonds", "Only 90°", "180° only"]', 1, 'In PCl₅ the three planar (equatorial) bonds are at 120° to each other and each axial bond is at 90° to the planar ones.', '{"0": "109.5° is the tetrahedral angle.", "2": "All angles of 90° describe an octahedral molecule such as SF₆.", "3": "Only the two axial bonds are at 180° to each other; other angles are 120° and 90°."}'),
  ('CHE-0149', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: AB₅ type', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 61 (§3.8.1, Fig. 3.26)', 'The tri-iodide ion, I₃⁻, has five electron pairs around the central iodine atom. What is its actual shape?', '["Trigonal bipyramidal", "T-shaped", "Linear", "V-shaped"]', 2, 'The central I has two bond pairs and three lone pairs. The three lone pairs take the planar (trigonal) positions and the two iodine atoms sit above and below the plane, so the ion is linear (180°).', '{"0": "Trigonal bipyramidal is the electron-pair geometry; the shape counts atoms only.", "1": "A T-shape needs three bond pairs and two lone pairs.", "3": "The lone pairs occupy the equatorial positions, leaving the two bonded atoms opposite each other."}'),
  ('CHE-0150', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR: AB₆ type', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 61 to 62 (§3.8.1, Fig. 3.28)', 'How many lone pairs are present on xenon in XeF₄?', '["0", "1", "2", "3"]', 2, 'XeF₄ is an AB₆ system: six electron pairs around Xe, four bond pairs and two lone pairs. The lone pairs sit 180° apart, leaving a square planar molecule.', '{"0": "With no lone pairs XeF₄ would need only four electron pairs and would be tetrahedral.", "1": "Xenon has eight valence electrons; four are used in bonds, leaving two pairs.", "3": "Three lone pairs plus four bonds would be seven pairs."}'),
  ('CHE-0151', 'Chemical bonding', 'VSEPR in drug design', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 62 to 63 (§3.8.2)', 'Why is the VSEPR model useful in drug design?', '["It predicts the colour of drugs.", "It predicts molecular shapes, and only a drug of suitable shape fits the active site of its target, like a key in a lock.", "It measures the mass of drug molecules.", "It shows which drugs are ionic."]', 1, 'Molecular shape determines how a drug interacts with its biological target; only ligands of suitable shape fit the active site. For example, aspirin blocks the enzyme COX by binding to its active site through its acetyl group.', '{"0": "VSEPR predicts shapes, not colours.", "2": "VSEPR does not measure mass.", "3": "VSEPR deals with the shapes of covalent molecules, not ionic character."}'),
  ('CHE-0152', 'Chemical bonding', 'Molecular orbital theory', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 63 (§3.9)', 'According to molecular orbital theory, when two atomic orbitals combine,', '["one molecular orbital forms.", "a bonding MO of lower energy and an antibonding MO of higher energy than the atomic orbitals form.", "two bonding MOs of lower energy form.", "two antibonding MOs form."]', 1, 'Two atomic orbitals give two molecular orbitals. Overlap of lobes with the same sign gives a bonding MO lower in energy than the parent orbitals; opposite signs give an antibonding MO higher in energy.', '{"0": "The number of molecular orbitals equals the number of atomic orbitals combined: two.", "2": "One of the pair is antibonding, of higher energy.", "3": "One of the pair is bonding, of lower energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0153', 'Chemical bonding', 'Bond order', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 63 to 65 (§3.9, Fig. 3.33)', 'Why does a stable He₂ molecule not form?', '["Helium atoms have no electrons.", "Two electrons enter the bonding MO and two enter the antibonding MO, giving a bond order of zero.", "The bond order of He₂ is 2.", "Helium has unpaired electrons."]', 1, 'He₂ would have four electrons: two in σ1s and two in σ*1s. Bond order = (2 − 2)/2 = 0, so no bond forms.', '{"0": "Each helium atom has two electrons (1s²).", "2": "The bond order works out to zero, not 2.", "3": "A helium atom (1s²) has no unpaired electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0154', 'Chemical bonding', 'MOT: oxygen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, pp. 65 to 66 (§3.9.1, Fig. 3.35)', 'Molecular orbital theory explains that O₂ is paramagnetic because', '["all its electrons are paired.", "it has two unpaired electrons, one in each of the π* orbitals (π*2py and π*2pz).", "its bond order is zero.", "it has a triple bond."]', 1, 'The MO diagram of O₂ places one unpaired electron in each of π*2py and π*2pz. These unpaired electrons make oxygen paramagnetic, attracted by a magnetic field.', '{"0": "A molecule with all electrons paired would not be paramagnetic.", "2": "The bond order of O₂ is 2 (a double bond).", "3": "O₂ has a double bond (bond order 2); N₂ has the triple bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0155', 'Chemical bonding', 'Paramagnetism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Chemical Bonding, p. 65 (§3.9.1)', 'A paramagnetic substance is one that', '["is repelled by a magnetic field and has no unpaired electrons.", "is attracted by a magnetic field because it has one or more unpaired electrons.", "conducts electricity because of free ions.", "has a zero dipole moment."]', 1, 'Paramagnetic substances have one or more unpaired electrons and are attracted by a magnetic field; more unpaired electrons make them more strongly paramagnetic. Liquid oxygen, for example, is held between the poles of a strong magnet.', '{"0": "Being repelled with all electrons paired describes diamagnetism.", "2": "Electrical conduction is not what paramagnetism means.", "3": "Dipole moment is about charge separation, not magnetism."}'),
  ('CHE-0156', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Concept of mole', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 71 (§4.1)', 'One mole of a substance is the amount that contains as many elementary entities as there are atoms in', '["12 g of carbon-12.", "1 g of hydrogen-1.", "16 g of oxygen-16.", "22.4 dm³ of any liquid."]', 0, 'The mole is defined as the amount of substance containing as many elementary entities as there are atoms in 0.012 kg (12 g) of carbon-12.', '{"1": "The standard used in the definition is carbon-12, not hydrogen.", "2": "The standard used in the definition is carbon-12, not oxygen.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the molar volume of a gas at STP, not a liquid, and it is not the basis of the definition."}'),
  ('CHE-0157', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole and Avogadro''s number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 72 to 73 (§4.1 to 4.2)', 'How many molecules are present in 9.0 g of water? (H = 1, O = 16; Nᴀ = 6.02 × 10²³)', '["6.02 × 10²³", "1.204 × 10²⁴", "3.01 × 10²³", "9.0 × 10²³"]', 2, 'Moles = 9.0 ÷ 18 = 0.5 mol. Molecules = 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³.', '{"0": "6.02 × 10²³ is the number in one mole (18 g), not 9 g.", "1": "1.204 × 10²⁴ is the number in two moles.", "3": "The mass in grams cannot be used directly as a number of molecules."}'),
  ('CHE-0158', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Moles from mass', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 72 (§4.1)', 'How many moles are present in 11 g of carbon dioxide? (C = 12, O = 16)', '["0.25 mol", "0.5 mol", "4 mol", "11 mol"]', 0, 'Molar mass of CO₂ = 12 + 2(16) = 44 g mol⁻¹. n = m/M = 11 ÷ 44 = 0.25 mol.', '{"1": "0.5 mol would be 22 g of CO₂.", "2": "4 mol comes from dividing 44 by 11, the wrong way round.", "3": "11 is the mass in grams, not the number of moles."}'),
  ('CHE-0159', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mass from moles', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 72 (§4.1)', 'What is the mass of 0.25 mol of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃? (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', '["25 g", "100 g", "400 g", "10 g"]', 0, 'Molar mass of CaCO₃ = 40 + 12 + 3(16) = 100 g mol⁻¹. Mass = n × M = 0.25 × 100 = 25 g.', '{"1": "100 g is the mass of one whole mole.", "2": "400 g comes from dividing 100 by 0.25 instead of multiplying.", "3": "10 g would be 0.1 mol."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0160', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar mass', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 71 (§4.1)', 'What is the molar mass of sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄? (H = 1, S = 32, O = 16)', '["49 g mol⁻¹", "82 g mol⁻¹", "98 g mol⁻¹", "112 g mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'Molar mass is the sum of the masses of the component atoms: 2(1) + 32 + 4(16) = 98 g mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "49 is half the molar mass.", "1": "82 counts only three oxygen atoms.", "3": "112 counts five oxygen atoms."}'),
  ('CHE-0161', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Number of ions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 71 to 73 (§4.1 to 4.2)', 'How many sodium ions are present in one mole of sodium sulfate, Na₂SO₄? (Nᴀ = 6.02 × 10²³)', '["6.02 × 10²³", "1.806 × 10²⁴", "3.01 × 10²³", "1.204 × 10²⁴"]', 3, 'One mole of Na₂SO₄ contains 6.02 × 10²³ formula units, and each contains two Na⁺ ions: 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴ Na⁺ ions.', '{"0": "6.02 × 10²³ is the number of formula units (or sulfate ions), not sodium ions.", "1": "1.806 × 10²⁴ is the total number of ions (2 Na⁺ + 1 SO₄²⁻) in one mole.", "2": "3.01 × 10²³ would be half a mole of ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0162', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Number of atoms', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 73 (§4.2)', 'How many atoms in total are present in 0.5 mol of ammonia, NH₃? (Nᴀ = 6.02 × 10²³)', '["3.01 × 10²³", "6.02 × 10²³", "1.204 × 10²⁴", "2.408 × 10²⁴"]', 2, '0.5 mol of NH₃ contains 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³ molecules. Each molecule has 4 atoms, so total atoms = 4 × 3.01 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴.', '{"0": "3.01 × 10²³ is the number of molecules, not atoms.", "1": "6.02 × 10²³ would be the atoms in 0.5 mol of a diatomic molecule.", "3": "2.408 × 10²⁴ would be the atoms in one whole mole of NH₃."}'),
  ('CHE-0163', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Moles from particles', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 73 (§4.2)', 'A sample contains 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules of glucose. How many moles is this? (Nᴀ = 6.02 × 10²³)', '["0.5 mol", "1 mol", "2 mol", "12 mol"]', 2, 'Number of moles = number of particles ÷ Avogadro''s number = 1.204 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = 2 mol.', '{"0": "0.5 mol would be 3.01 × 10²³ molecules.", "1": "1 mol would be 6.02 × 10²³ molecules.", "3": "12 comes from reading the digits of the number, not dividing by Nᴀ."}'),
  ('CHE-0164', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Equal numbers of atoms', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 72 to 73 (§4.2)', 'Which sample contains the same number of atoms as 6 g of carbon? (C = 12, He = 4, Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)', '["2 g of helium", "23 g of sodium", "16 g of oxygen gas (O₂)", "1 g of hydrogen gas (H₂)"]', 0, '6 g of carbon is 0.5 mol of atoms. Helium is monatomic, so 2 g ÷ 4 g mol⁻¹ = 0.5 mol of atoms, the same number.', '{"1": "23 g of sodium is 1 mol of atoms, twice as many.", "2": "16 g of O₂ is 0.5 mol of molecules but 1 mol of atoms, since each molecule has two atoms.", "3": "1 g of H₂ is 0.5 mol of molecules but 1 mol of atoms."}'),
  ('CHE-0165', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar volume', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 75 (§4.3)', 'What volume does 8.0 g of oxygen gas (O₂) occupy at STP? (O = 16)', '["5.6 dm³", "2.8 dm³", "11.2 dm³", "22.4 dm³"]', 0, 'Moles of O₂ = 8.0 ÷ 32 = 0.25 mol. Volume = n × Vm = 0.25 × 22.4 = 5.6 dm³.', '{"1": "2.8 dm³ is 0.125 mol, which uses 64 instead of 32 as the molar mass.", "2": "11.2 dm³ uses 16 g mol⁻¹, the molar mass of O atoms instead of O₂.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the volume of a whole mole (32 g)."}'),
  ('CHE-0166', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar volume', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 75 (§4.3)', 'What is the mass of 44.8 dm³ of methane (CH₄) at STP? (C = 12, H = 1)', '["8 g", "16 g", "32 g", "44.8 g"]', 2, 'Moles = 44.8 ÷ 22.4 = 2 mol. Mass = 2 × 16 = 32 g.', '{"0": "8 g is half a mole of methane.", "1": "16 g is one mole, which occupies only 22.4 dm³.", "3": "A volume in dm³ is not a mass in grams."}'),
  ('CHE-0167', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Avogadro''s law', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 75 (§4.3)', 'Avogadro''s law states that', '["equal masses of all gases occupy equal volumes.", "all gases have the same density at STP.", "the volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure.", "equal volumes of all ideal gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules."]', 3, 'Avogadro''s law: equal volumes of all ideal gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. So 22.4 dm³ of any ideal gas at STP contains 6.02 × 10²³ molecules.', '{"0": "Equal masses of different gases contain different numbers of moles, so they occupy different volumes.", "1": "Gases have different densities at STP because their molar masses differ.", "2": "This is Boyle''s law, not Avogadro''s law."}'),
  ('CHE-0168', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Avogadro''s law', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 75 (§4.3)', 'At STP, 11.2 dm³ of hydrogen and 11.2 dm³ of carbon dioxide are compared. Which statement is correct?', '["Carbon dioxide contains more molecules because its molecules are bigger.", "They have the same mass but different numbers of molecules.", "They contain the same number of molecules but have different masses.", "They contain different numbers of molecules and have the same density."]', 2, 'Equal volumes at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules (0.5 mol each here). Their masses differ: 1 g of H₂ and 22 g of CO₂.', '{"0": "Molecular size does not change the number of molecules in a given gas volume (Avogadro''s law).", "1": "Equal volumes give equal numbers of molecules; the masses differ because the molar masses differ.", "3": "They contain the same number of molecules, and CO₂ is much denser."}'),
  ('CHE-0169', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Density and molar mass of gases', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 76 (§4.4)', 'A gas has a density of 2.86 g dm⁻³ at STP. What is its molar mass?', '["64 g mol⁻¹", "44 g mol⁻¹", "32 g mol⁻¹", "128 g mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'One mole of gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at STP, so M = d × Vm = 2.86 × 22.4 ≈ 64 g mol⁻¹ (for example SO₂).', '{"1": "44 g mol⁻¹ would correspond to a density of about 1.96 g dm⁻³.", "2": "32 g mol⁻¹ would correspond to a density of about 1.43 g dm⁻³.", "3": "128 g mol⁻¹ doubles the correct value."}'),
  ('CHE-0170', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Density and molar mass of gases', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 76 (§4.4)', 'Which of the following gases has the highest density at STP? (C = 12, H = 1, N = 14, O = 16)', '["CH₄", "N₂", "O₂", "CO₂"]', 3, 'One mole of every gas occupies the same volume at STP, so the gas with the highest molar mass has the highest density. CO₂ (44) is heaviest here.', '{"0": "CH₄ (16 g mol⁻¹) is the lightest of these, so it is least dense.", "1": "N₂ (28 g mol⁻¹) is lighter than CO₂.", "2": "O₂ (32 g mol⁻¹) is lighter than CO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0171', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar concentration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 76 (§4.5)', '5.85 g of sodium chloride is dissolved to make 500 cm³ of solution. What is the molar concentration? (Na = 23, Cl = 35.5)', '["0.1 mol dm⁻³", "0.05 mol dm⁻³", "0.2 mol dm⁻³", "11.7 mol dm⁻³"]', 2, 'Moles of NaCl = 5.85 ÷ 58.5 = 0.1 mol. Volume = 500 cm³ = 0.5 dm³. C = n/V = 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = 0.2 mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "0.1 is the number of moles; it must be divided by the volume in dm³ (0.5).", "1": "0.05 multiplies by the volume instead of dividing.", "3": "11.7 divides the mass, not the moles, by the volume."}'),
  ('CHE-0172', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 76 (§4.5)', 'How many moles of solute are present in 250 cm³ of a 0.4 mol dm⁻³ solution?', '["1.6 mol", "0.1 mol", "100 mol", "0.4 mol"]', 1, 'n = C × V = 0.4 mol dm⁻³ × 0.250 dm³ = 0.1 mol.', '{"0": "1.6 comes from dividing 0.4 by 0.25 instead of multiplying.", "2": "100 comes from using 250 cm³ without converting to dm³.", "3": "0.4 mol is the amount in a full 1 dm³."}'),
  ('CHE-0173', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar concentration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 76 (§4.5)', 'What mass of sodium hydroxide is needed to make 2.0 dm³ of a 0.5 mol dm⁻³ solution? (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)', '["40 g", "20 g", "10 g", "80 g"]', 0, 'n = C × V = 0.5 × 2.0 = 1.0 mol. Mass = 1.0 × 40 = 40 g.', '{"1": "20 g is 0.5 mol, enough for only 1 dm³.", "2": "10 g is 0.25 mol.", "3": "80 g is 2 mol; it multiplies by the volume twice."}'),
  ('CHE-0174', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Volume–volume relationship', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 78 to 80 (§4.6)', 'N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g). What volume of ammonia forms from 10 dm³ of nitrogen with excess hydrogen, all at the same temperature and pressure?', '["10 dm³", "30 dm³", "20 dm³", "40 dm³"]', 2, 'Gas volumes react in the mole ratio (22.4 dm³ N₂ gives 44.8 dm³ NH₃). 1 volume of N₂ gives 2 volumes of NH₃, so 10 dm³ gives 20 dm³.', '{"0": "The ratio N₂ : NH₃ is 1 : 2, not 1 : 1.", "1": "30 dm³ is the volume of hydrogen used, not ammonia formed.", "3": "40 dm³ adds the volumes of the reactants."}'),
  ('CHE-0175', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Interpreting a balanced equation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 78 (§4.6)', 'Which statement about N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) is correct?', '["1 g of N₂ reacts with 3 g of H₂ to form 2 g of NH₃.", "28 g of N₂ reacts with 6 g of H₂ to form 34 g of NH₃.", "28 g of N₂ reacts with 3 g of H₂ to form 31 g of NH₃.", "1 mole of N₂ reacts with 1 mole of H₂ to form 2 moles of NH₃."]', 1, '1 mol N₂ (28 g) reacts with 3 mol H₂ (6 g) to form 2 mol NH₃ (34 g). Total mass is conserved: 28 + 6 = 34 g.', '{"0": "The coefficients give mole ratios, not mass ratios.", "2": "3 mol of H₂ is 6 g, not 3 g.", "3": "The equation shows 3 mol of H₂ reacting with each mole of N₂, not 1 mol."}'),
  ('CHE-0176', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Assumptions in stoichiometry', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 78 (§4.6, Keep in Mind)', 'Which of the following is NOT one of the assumptions made in stoichiometric calculations?', '["All reactants are completely converted into products.", "No side reaction occurs.", "The law of conservation of mass is obeyed.", "The reaction is reversible and reaches equilibrium."]', 3, 'The calculations assume complete conversion to products, that the laws of conservation of mass and definite proportions hold, and that no side reaction occurs. A reversible reaction would not convert reactants completely.', '{"0": "Complete conversion is one of the assumptions.", "1": "No side reaction is one of the assumptions.", "2": "Obeying conservation of mass is one of the assumptions."}'),
  ('CHE-0177', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Laws of chemical combination', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 70', 'The law of definite proportions states that', '["matter can neither be created nor destroyed.", "gases react in simple whole-number ratios of volume.", "equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules.", "a pure compound always contains the same elements combined in the same ratio by mass."]', 3, 'The law of definite proportions: a pure compound always contains the same elements combined in the same ratio by mass.', '{"0": "This is the law of conservation of mass.", "1": "This describes Gay-Lussac''s law of combining volumes, not definite proportions.", "2": "This is Avogadro''s law."}'),
  ('CHE-0178', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole–mole relationship', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 78 to 79 (§4.6)', '2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l). How many moles of oxygen are needed to react completely with 5 moles of hydrogen?', '["2.5 mol", "1.25 mol", "5 mol", "10 mol"]', 0, 'The mole ratio H₂ : O₂ is 2 : 1, so 5 mol H₂ needs 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5 mol O₂.', '{"1": "1.25 mol divides by 4 instead of 2.", "2": "5 mol uses a 1 : 1 ratio.", "3": "10 mol inverts the ratio (multiplies by 2)."}'),
  ('CHE-0179', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole–mole relationship', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 78 to 79 (§4.6)', 'Propane burns as C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. How many moles of CO₂ are produced from 2 mol of propane in excess oxygen?', '["2 mol", "6 mol", "3 mol", "10 mol"]', 1, 'The ratio C₃H₈ : CO₂ is 1 : 3, so 2 mol propane gives 2 × 3 = 6 mol CO₂.', '{"0": "2 mol uses a 1 : 1 ratio.", "2": "3 mol is the amount from one mole of propane.", "3": "10 mol is the oxygen needed for 2 mol propane, not the CO₂ formed."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0180', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mass–mass relationship', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 79 (§4.6)', '2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. What mass of magnesium oxide forms from 12 g of magnesium? (Mg = 24, O = 16)', '["10 g", "24 g", "20 g", "40 g"]', 2, 'Moles of Mg = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 mol. The ratio Mg : MgO is 2 : 2 (1 : 1), so 0.5 mol MgO forms: 0.5 × 40 = 20 g.', '{"0": "10 g would be 0.25 mol of MgO.", "1": "24 g is the molar mass of magnesium, not the mass of oxide.", "3": "40 g is one whole mole of MgO, which needs 24 g of Mg."}'),
  ('CHE-0181', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mass–volume relationship', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 81 (§4.6)', 'Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. What volume of hydrogen at STP is produced from 6.5 g of zinc with excess acid? (Zn = 65)', '["1.12 dm³", "2.24 dm³", "4.48 dm³", "22.4 dm³"]', 1, 'Moles of Zn = 6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.1 mol. Zn : H₂ is 1 : 1, so 0.1 mol H₂ forms: 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³.', '{"0": "1.12 dm³ would be 0.05 mol, halving the ratio.", "2": "4.48 dm³ would be 0.2 mol; the 2 in front of HCl does not apply to H₂.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the volume of a whole mole of H₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0182', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mole–mass relationship', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 80 (§4.6)', 'CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. What mass of calcium oxide forms when 0.2 mol of calcium carbonate decomposes completely? (Ca = 40, O = 16)', '["5.6 g", "11.2 g", "20 g", "56 g"]', 1, 'CaCO₃ : CaO is 1 : 1, so 0.2 mol CaO forms. Mass = 0.2 × 56 = 11.2 g.', '{"0": "5.6 g is 0.1 mol of CaO.", "2": "20 g is the mass of 0.2 mol CaCO₃, not CaO.", "3": "56 g is one whole mole of CaO."}'),
  ('CHE-0183', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Volume–volume relationship', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 80 (§4.6)', 'CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). What volume of oxygen is needed to burn 5 dm³ of methane completely, at the same temperature and pressure?', '["2.5 dm³", "10 dm³", "5 dm³", "15 dm³"]', 1, 'Gas volumes react in the mole ratio, CH₄ : O₂ = 1 : 2, so 5 dm³ of methane needs 10 dm³ of oxygen.', '{"0": "2.5 dm³ inverts the ratio.", "2": "5 dm³ uses a 1 : 1 ratio.", "3": "15 dm³ adds the methane volume to the oxygen needed."}'),
  ('CHE-0184', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 82 (§4.7)', 'The limiting reactant in a chemical reaction is the reactant that', '["is present in the largest mass.", "has the highest molar mass.", "is left over at the end of the reaction.", "is consumed first and so controls the amount of product formed."]', 3, 'The limiting reactant is used up first. Once it is consumed the reaction stops, so the maximum amount of product depends on it.', '{"0": "The largest mass does not decide which reactant runs out; mole ratios do.", "1": "Molar mass alone does not decide which reactant is limiting.", "2": "The reactant left over is the excess reactant."}'),
  ('CHE-0185', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting and excess reactant', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 81 (§4.7)', 'Why is a cheaper reactant often supplied in excess in industrial processes?', '["To make the reaction reversible.", "To reduce the amount of product formed.", "To make sure the expensive reactant is completely converted into the desired product.", "To lower the rate of reaction."]', 2, 'A large amount of inexpensive reactant is used to ensure that all of the expensive reactant is converted to product, to obtain the maximum product and to increase the rate of reaction.', '{"0": "An excess reactant does not make a reaction reversible.", "1": "Using excess reactant aims to maximise product, not reduce it.", "3": "The book says excess reactant increases the rate of reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0186', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting and excess reactant', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 83 to 84 (§4.7)', '2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l). If 4 mol of H₂ and 3 mol of O₂ are mixed and react completely, which statement is correct?', '["O₂ is limiting; 6 mol of water forms.", "Neither is limiting; 7 mol of water forms.", "H₂ is limiting; 3 mol of water forms.", "H₂ is limiting; 4 mol of water forms and 1 mol of O₂ is left over."]', 3, '4 mol H₂ needs only 2 mol O₂. H₂ runs out first (limiting) and gives 4 mol H₂O; 3 − 2 = 1 mol O₂ remains in excess.', '{"0": "3 mol O₂ would need 6 mol H₂, which is not available; H₂ is limiting.", "1": "The reactants are not in the 2 : 1 ratio, so one must be in excess.", "2": "Water formed equals the moles of H₂ (2 : 2 ratio), so 4 mol, not 3."}'),
  ('CHE-0187', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 82 to 84 (§4.7)', 'N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g). 28 g of N₂ and 9 g of H₂ are reacted. What is the maximum mass of ammonia that can form? (N = 14, H = 1)', '["34 g", "17 g", "51 g", "37 g"]', 0, 'Moles: N₂ = 28 ÷ 28 = 1 mol; H₂ = 9 ÷ 2 = 4.5 mol. 1 mol N₂ needs only 3 mol H₂, so N₂ is limiting and gives 2 mol NH₃ = 2 × 17 = 34 g.', '{"1": "17 g is one mole of NH₃; 1 mol N₂ gives 2 mol NH₃.", "2": "51 g assumes all 4.5 mol H₂ reacts (giving 3 mol NH₃), but there is not enough N₂.", "3": "37 g simply adds the reactant masses; some H₂ is left unreacted."}'),
  ('CHE-0188', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Excess reactant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 83 to 85 (§4.7.2)', '2CO(g) + O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g). 56 g of CO is mixed with 48 g of O₂. What mass of oxygen is left unreacted? (C = 12, O = 16)', '["0 g", "16 g", "8 g", "32 g"]', 1, 'Moles: CO = 56 ÷ 28 = 2 mol; O₂ = 48 ÷ 32 = 1.5 mol. 2 mol CO needs 1 mol O₂, so 0.5 mol O₂ is left: 0.5 × 32 = 16 g.', '{"0": "Oxygen is in excess, so some remains.", "2": "8 g is 0.25 mol of O₂.", "3": "32 g is the oxygen that reacts, not the amount left."}'),
  ('CHE-0189', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 82 (§4.7, Did You Know?)', 'Smothering a fire with foam or CO₂ works mainly because it', '["adds more fuel.", "acts as a catalyst.", "increases the temperature.", "reduces the amount of available oxygen, the excess reactant in burning."]', 3, 'Smothering lowers the oxygen supply below the level needed to support combustion, decreasing the amount of the excess reactant.', '{"0": "Adding fuel would feed the fire.", "1": "A catalyst speeds up reactions; it does not stop combustion.", "2": "Raising the temperature would not put out a fire."}'),
  ('CHE-0190', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Limiting reactant in solution', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 83 (§4.7)', '100 cm³ of 0.2 mol dm⁻³ AgNO₃ is mixed with 50 cm³ of 0.2 mol dm⁻³ NaCl. Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl. How many moles of AgCl precipitate?', '["0.03 mol", "0.02 mol", "0.01 mol", "0.2 mol"]', 2, 'n = CV: Ag⁺ = 0.2 × 0.100 = 0.02 mol; Cl⁻ = 0.2 × 0.050 = 0.01 mol. They react 1 : 1, so Cl⁻ is limiting and 0.01 mol AgCl forms.', '{"0": "0.03 mol adds the two amounts.", "1": "0.02 mol is the silver present, but there is only 0.01 mol chloride.", "3": "0.2 is a concentration, not the amount formed."}'),
  ('CHE-0191', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Theoretical and actual yield', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 85 (§4.8)', 'The theoretical yield of a reaction is', '["the mass of product actually obtained.", "the amount of excess reactant left over.", "always less than the actual yield.", "the maximum amount of product calculated from the balanced equation for a given amount of reactant."]', 3, 'The theoretical yield is the maximum amount of product that can be made from a given amount of reactant, calculated from the balanced chemical equation.', '{"0": "The amount actually obtained is the actual yield.", "1": "Leftover reactant is the excess reactant.", "2": "The actual yield is usually less than the theoretical yield, not the other way round."}'),
  ('CHE-0192', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Theoretical and actual yield', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 85 to 86 (§4.8)', 'Which of the following is NOT a reason for the actual yield being less than the theoretical yield?', '["Losses during filtration, washing or crystallization", "A competing side reaction", "The reaction being reversible", "The reaction obeying the law of conservation of mass"]', 3, 'The book gives three reasons for low yields: losses in separation steps, side reactions and reversibility. Obeying conservation of mass does not reduce yield; it is assumed in calculating the theoretical yield.', '{"0": "Handling losses are one of the reasons given.", "1": "Side reactions are one of the reasons given.", "2": "Reversibility is one of the reasons given."}'),
  ('CHE-0193', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 86 (§4.8)', 'The theoretical yield of a product is 20 g, but only 15 g is obtained. What is the percentage yield?', '["75%", "25%", "133%", "5%"]', 0, '% yield = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100 = (15 ÷ 20) × 100 = 75%.', '{"1": "25% is the percentage lost, not the yield.", "2": "133% divides theoretical by actual, the wrong way round.", "3": "5 g is the mass lost, not a percentage."}'),
  ('CHE-0194', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 86 (§4.8)', '2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. 4.8 g of magnesium is burned and 6.0 g of MgO is collected. What is the percentage yield? (Mg = 24, O = 16)', '["60%", "75%", "80%", "125%"]', 1, 'Moles Mg = 4.8 ÷ 24 = 0.2 mol, so theoretical MgO = 0.2 mol × 40 = 8.0 g. % yield = 6.0 ÷ 8.0 × 100 = 75%.', '{"0": "60% would need a theoretical yield of 10 g.", "2": "80% compares 4.8 with 6.0, using the mass of Mg instead of the theoretical MgO.", "3": "125% divides the actual yield by the mass of Mg."}'),
  ('CHE-0195', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Percentage yield', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 86 (§4.8)', 'A higher percentage yield indicates that a reaction', '["is less efficient.", "is more efficient.", "is faster.", "has no limiting reactant."]', 1, 'The efficiency of a reaction is expressed by its percentage yield; the greater the percentage yield, the higher the efficiency.', '{"0": "A higher yield means higher efficiency.", "2": "Percentage yield measures how much product is obtained, not how fast.", "3": "Every reaction with non-stoichiometric amounts still has a limiting reactant; yield does not show this."}'),
  ('CHE-0196', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Stoichiometry in medicine', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 87 (§4.9)', 'Why is stoichiometry important in deciding the dose of a medicine such as paracetamol?', '["Paracetamol overdose has no harmful effects.", "Medicines work equally well at any dose.", "Too small an amount has no effect, while too large an amount can be toxic, for example causing liver damage.", "Stoichiometry decides the colour of the tablet."]', 2, 'Stoichiometry is used to work out the amount of drug in a dose. Too little has no effect, while too much can be toxic; a paracetamol overdose can cause severe liver damage.', '{"0": "The book lists organ and severe liver damage from paracetamol overdose.", "1": "The book stresses that the amount of active ingredient must be exact.", "3": "Stoichiometry concerns amounts of substances, not colour."}'),
  ('CHE-0197', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar mass', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 71 (§4.1)', 'The molar mass of a substance is', '["the mass of one atom in grams.", "the number of particles in one gram.", "the volume of one mole of a gas.", "the mass of one mole of the substance in grams, with unit g mol⁻¹."]', 3, 'Molar mass is the mass of one mole of a substance expressed in grams; its unit is g mol⁻¹. It equals the atomic, molecular or formula mass in grams.', '{"0": "The mass of one atom is far smaller, the molar mass divided by Avogadro''s number.", "1": "This is not a defined quantity in the chapter.", "2": "The volume of one mole of gas is the molar volume."}'),
  ('CHE-0198', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Molar volume', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 75 (§4.3)', 'What volume do 3.01 × 10²³ molecules of any ideal gas occupy at STP?', '["11.2 dm³", "5.6 dm³", "22.4 dm³", "44.8 dm³"]', 0, '3.01 × 10²³ molecules is 0.5 mol. At STP, 0.5 × 22.4 = 11.2 dm³, whatever the gas.', '{"1": "5.6 dm³ is 0.25 mol.", "2": "22.4 dm³ is one mole (6.02 × 10²³ molecules).", "3": "44.8 dm³ is two moles."}'),
  ('CHE-0199', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mass of one molecule', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, pp. 71 to 73 (§4.1 to 4.2)', 'What is the approximate mass of one molecule of water? (H₂O = 18 g mol⁻¹; Nᴀ = 6.02 × 10²³)', '["18 g", "2.99 × 10⁻²³ g", "1.08 × 10²⁵ g", "3.34 × 10⁻²² g"]', 1, 'One mole (6.02 × 10²³ molecules) has a mass of 18 g, so one molecule has a mass of 18 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ ≈ 2.99 × 10⁻²³ g.', '{"0": "18 g is the mass of a whole mole.", "2": "This multiplies 18 by Avogadro''s number instead of dividing.", "3": "This is about ten times too large."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0200', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Stoichiometry', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 70', 'The word stoichiometry comes from Greek words meaning', '["mole and mass.", "element and measure.", "gas and volume.", "reaction and rate."]', 1, 'Stoichiometry comes from the Greek stoicheion (element) and metron (measure). It deals with the quantitative relationships between reactants and products in a balanced equation.', '{"0": "These are quantities used in stoichiometry, not the meaning of the word.", "2": "These are quantities used in stoichiometry, not the meaning of the word.", "3": "Reaction rate is studied in reaction kinetics."}'),
  ('CHE-0201', 'Fundamental concepts', 'Mass–mole relationship', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Stoichiometry, p. 81 (§4.6, Sample Problem 4.15)', 'Baking soda neutralizes stomach acid: NaHCO₃ + HCl → NaCl + H₂O + CO₂. How many moles of HCl are neutralized by 4.2 g of NaHCO₃? (NaHCO₃ = 84 g mol⁻¹)', '["0.05 mol", "0.025 mol", "0.1 mol", "0.5 mol"]', 0, 'Moles of NaHCO₃ = 4.2 ÷ 84 = 0.05 mol. The ratio NaHCO₃ : HCl is 1 : 1, so 0.05 mol HCl is neutralized.', '{"1": "0.025 mol corresponds to 2.1 g of NaHCO₃.", "2": "0.1 mol would need 8.4 g of NaHCO₃.", "3": "0.5 mol is ten times too large."}'),
  ('CHE-0202', 'States of matter', 'States of matter', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92', 'Liquids are less common than solids and gases mainly because', '["liquids have no intermolecular forces.", "liquids cannot be compressed.", "the liquid state of a substance exists only within a relatively narrow range of temperature and pressure.", "all liquids are unstable."]', 2, 'The book explains that liquids are less common because the liquid state of any substance exists only within a relatively narrow range of temperature and pressure.', '{"0": "Liquids have reasonably strong intermolecular forces.", "1": "Low compressibility does not make a state rare.", "3": "Liquids are not inherently unstable; they exist over a limited range of conditions."}'),
  ('CHE-0203', 'States of matter', 'Properties of gases', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92 (§5.1)', 'Gases can be compressed easily because', '["their molecules are very large.", "they have a definite volume.", "their molecules attract each other strongly.", "most of the volume of a gas (about 99.9%) is empty space."]', 3, 'Gas molecules are widely separated and nearly 99.9% of the volume of a gas is empty space, so the molecules can be pushed closer together easily.', '{"0": "Gas molecules occupy a tiny fraction of the volume; size is not why gases compress.", "1": "Gases take the volume of their container; they have no definite volume.", "2": "Intermolecular forces in gases are negligible."}'),
  ('CHE-0204', 'States of matter', 'Properties of gases', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92 (§5.1)', 'The cooling of a gas when it expands suddenly is called', '["the Joule–Thomson effect.", "the Tyndall effect.", "Brownian motion.", "the greenhouse effect."]', 0, 'When sudden expansion of a gas occurs, cooling takes place; this is the Joule–Thomson effect.', '{"1": "The Tyndall effect is the scattering of light by colloidal particles.", "2": "Brownian motion is the random movement of suspended particles.", "3": "The greenhouse effect is the trapping of heat by atmospheric gases."}'),
  ('CHE-0205', 'States of matter', 'Properties of gases', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92 (§5.1)', 'The pressure exerted by a gas is due to', '["the weight of the gas molecules.", "attractive forces between gas molecules.", "collisions of the gas molecules with the walls of the container.", "the volume occupied by the molecules."]', 2, 'Gas pressure arises from the collisions of gas molecules with the walls of their container.', '{"0": "The weight of gas molecules is negligible compared with the effect of collisions.", "1": "Intermolecular forces in gases are negligible; attraction would actually reduce pressure.", "3": "Ideal gas molecules are treated as having zero volume."}'),
  ('CHE-0206', 'States of matter', 'Properties of gases', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92 (§5.1)', 'Which statement describes an ideal gas?', '["Its particles have zero volume and negligible intermolecular forces.", "Its particles have large volume and strong attractive forces.", "It has a definite shape.", "It cannot be compressed."]', 0, 'Ideal gases are treated as having zero particle volume and negligible intermolecular forces.', '{"1": "This is the opposite of an ideal gas; real gases deviate because of molecular volume and attractions.", "2": "Gases have no definite shape.", "3": "Gases are easily compressed."}'),
  ('CHE-0207', 'States of matter', 'Gas laws', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 92 (§5.2)', 'Charles'' law, V ∝ T, holds when which quantities are kept constant?', '["n and T", "V and n", "P and T", "n and P"]', 3, 'Charles'' law relates volume to absolute temperature when the amount of gas (n) and the pressure (P) are held constant.', '{"0": "Constant n and T is the condition for Boyle''s law.", "1": "V is one of the two variables in Charles'' law, so it cannot be held constant.", "2": "Constant P and T is the condition for Avogadro''s law (V ∝ n)."}'),
  ('CHE-0208', 'States of matter', 'Ideal gas equation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 93 (§5.2)', 'The value of the ideal gas constant R used in the textbook is', '["0.0821 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "8.21 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "22.4 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'R = 0.0821 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ in PV = nRT.', '{"1": "8.21 has the decimal point in the wrong place.", "2": "22.4 dm³ is the molar volume of a gas at STP.", "3": "6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ is Avogadro''s number."}'),
  ('CHE-0209', 'States of matter', 'Ideal gas equation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 93 (§5.2)', '2.0 mol of an ideal gas is kept in a 24.63 dm³ container at 300 K. What is its pressure? (R = 0.0821 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)', '["0.5 atm", "1.0 atm", "2.0 atm", "4.0 atm"]', 2, 'P = nRT/V = (2.0 × 0.0821 × 300) ÷ 24.63 = 49.26 ÷ 24.63 = 2.0 atm.', '{"0": "0.5 atm divides by n instead of multiplying.", "1": "1.0 atm is the pressure of 1 mol under these conditions.", "3": "4.0 atm doubles the correct value."}'),
  ('CHE-0210', 'States of matter', 'Relative molecular mass from PV = nRT', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 93 (§5.2)', '3.2 g of a gas occupies 1.231 dm³ at 300 K and 2.0 atm. What is its relative molecular mass? (R = 0.0821 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)', '["32", "16", "64", "44"]', 0, 'Mr = mRT/PV = (3.2 × 0.0821 × 300) ÷ (2.0 × 1.231) = 78.8 ÷ 2.462 ≈ 32.', '{"1": "16 comes from dividing by an extra factor of 2.", "2": "64 forgets to multiply V by the pressure of 2.0 atm.", "3": "44 is the molar mass of CO₂, not what these data give."}'),
  ('CHE-0211', 'States of matter', 'Properties of liquids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 94 (§5.3)', 'Which statement about liquids is correct?', '["Liquids do not diffuse at all.", "Liquids are more compressible than gases.", "Liquids diffuse, but much more slowly than gases.", "Liquid molecules do not move."]', 2, 'Liquids diffuse like gases, but at a much lower rate. Their molecules are in constant random motion, though more slowly than gas molecules.', '{"0": "Diffusion of miscible liquids is evidence that liquid molecules move freely.", "1": "Liquids are about 10⁵ times less compressible than gases.", "3": "Liquid molecules are in constant random motion, which is why liquids flow."}'),
  ('CHE-0212', 'States of matter', 'London dispersion forces', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, pp. 94 to 95 (§5.4.1)', 'How does a London (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole) force arise?', '["From the attraction between permanent positive and negative ends of polar molecules.", "From the complete transfer of electrons.", "From hydrogen bonded to F, O or N.", "From a temporary dipole in one molecule inducing a dipole in a neighbouring molecule."]', 3, 'Movement of electrons makes one molecule a dipole for an instant. This instantaneous dipole disturbs the electrons of a neighbour, inducing a dipole, and the two attract each other momentarily.', '{"0": "Attraction between permanent dipoles is a dipole–dipole force.", "1": "Complete electron transfer forms ionic bonds.", "2": "This is the condition for hydrogen bonding."}'),
  ('CHE-0213', 'States of matter', 'London dispersion forces', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 95 (§5.4.1, Table 5.1)', 'At room temperature fluorine and chlorine are gases, bromine is a liquid and iodine is a solid. The main reason is that, down the group,', '["hydrogen bonding increases.", "the molecules become larger and more polarizable, so London forces become stronger.", "the molecules become more polar.", "ionic bonding develops between the molecules."]', 1, 'Halogen molecules are non-polar, so only London forces act between them. These get stronger with molecular mass and size because larger electron clouds are more easily polarized, raising the boiling point and changing the state.', '{"0": "Halogen molecules cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "2": "Diatomic halogen molecules are non-polar.", "3": "Halogen molecules are held by intermolecular forces, not ionic bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0214', 'States of matter', 'London dispersion forces', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 95 (Table 5.1)', 'Which noble gas has the highest boiling point?', '["Helium", "Neon", "Argon", "Xenon"]', 3, 'Boiling points of the noble gases increase from He (4.22 K) to Xe (165 K) because London forces strengthen as atomic size and mass increase.', '{"0": "Helium has the lowest boiling point (4.22 K).", "1": "Neon (27.1 K) boils far below xenon.", "2": "Argon (87.3 K) boils below krypton and xenon."}'),
  ('CHE-0215', 'States of matter', 'London dispersion forces: shape', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 96 (§5.4.1, Fig. 5.3)', 'The three isomers of pentane have the same molecular mass. Which has the highest boiling point, and why?', '["2,2-Dimethylpropane, because it is the most compact.", "2-Methylbutane, because it is branched.", "n-Pentane, because its straight chain gives the largest surface area for London forces.", "All have the same boiling point."]', 2, 'A larger surface area gives more points of contact between neighbouring molecules and stronger London forces. Straight-chain n-pentane has the largest surface area and the highest boiling point; 2,2-dimethylpropane has the smallest.', '{"0": "The compact 2,2-dimethylpropane has the smallest surface area and the lowest boiling point.", "1": "2-Methylbutane is intermediate in surface area and boiling point.", "3": "The isomers differ in shape and surface area, so their boiling points differ."}'),
  ('CHE-0216', 'States of matter', 'Dipole–dipole forces', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 96 (§5.4.2)', 'Besides London forces, which intermolecular force acts between chloroform (CHCl₃) molecules?', '["Hydrogen bonding", "Metallic bonding", "Ionic bonding", "Permanent dipole–permanent dipole forces"]', 3, 'CHCl₃ is polar, with a positive centre at H and a negative end at the Cl atoms. The positive end of one molecule attracts the negative end of another: a permanent dipole–permanent dipole force.', '{"0": "Its H is bonded to carbon, not to F, O or N, so it cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "1": "Metallic bonding occurs in metals.", "2": "Chloroform is molecular; it has no ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0217', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 97 (§5.4.3)', 'Which of these substances cannot form hydrogen bonds between its own molecules?', '["H₂O", "NH₃", "HF", "CH₄"]', 3, 'A hydrogen bond needs H bonded to a highly electronegative atom (F, O or N) that carries a lone pair. In CH₄, H is bonded to carbon, which has no lone pair.', '{"0": "In water H is bonded to O, which has lone pairs.", "1": "In ammonia H is bonded to N, which has a lone pair.", "2": "In HF H is bonded to F, which has lone pairs."}'),
  ('CHE-0218', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 97 (§5.4.3)', 'According to the textbook, the strength of a hydrogen bond is roughly', '["one tenth of that of an ordinary covalent bond.", "equal to that of a covalent bond.", "greater than that of an ionic bond.", "zero."]', 0, 'A hydrogen bond is the strongest intermolecular force but weaker than ionic, metallic and covalent bonds; its strength is about one tenth of an ordinary covalent bond.', '{"1": "Hydrogen bonds are much weaker than covalent bonds.", "2": "Ionic bonds are much stronger than hydrogen bonds.", "3": "Hydrogen bonds have real strength; they give water its high boiling point."}'),
  ('CHE-0219', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 97 (§5.4.3)', 'On average, how many hydrogen bonds can each water molecule form, according to the textbook?', '["2", "1", "3", "4"]', 0, 'A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and two lone pairs on oxygen, so on average it can form two hydrogen bonds per molecule.', '{"1": "One hydrogen bond per molecule is the case for NH₃ and HF.", "2": "Three would need more hydrogen atoms or lone pairs to match.", "3": "The book counts two per molecule on average (each bond is shared between two molecules)."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0220', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 97 (§5.4.3)', 'Ammonia has three hydrogen atoms but, according to the textbook, forms only one hydrogen bond per molecule on average. Why?', '["Nitrogen is not electronegative.", "Ammonia is non-polar.", "Nitrogen has only one lone pair.", "Ammonia molecules are too large."]', 2, 'The number of hydrogen bonds depends on both the H atoms and the lone pairs available. N in NH₃ has only one lone pair, which limits it to one hydrogen bond per molecule; so its hydrogen bonding is weaker and its boiling point lower than water''s.', '{"0": "Nitrogen is highly electronegative, which is why NH₃ can hydrogen bond at all.", "1": "Ammonia is a polar molecule.", "3": "Molecular size is not the limiting factor."}'),
  ('CHE-0221', 'States of matter', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 97 (§5.4.3)', 'HF and NH₃ each form only one hydrogen bond per molecule on average, yet HF has the higher boiling point. Why?', '["HF has more hydrogen atoms.", "HF is non-polar.", "NH₃ has no lone pair.", "F is more electronegative than N, so the hydrogen bond in HF is stronger."]', 3, 'Although HF has three lone pairs, it has only one H atom, so it forms one hydrogen bond per molecule. That bond is stronger than in NH₃ because fluorine is more electronegative, so HF boils higher.', '{"0": "HF has only one hydrogen atom; NH₃ has three.", "1": "HF is strongly polar.", "2": "NH₃ has one lone pair on nitrogen."}'),
  ('CHE-0222', 'States of matter', 'Properties of water: ice', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 98 (§5.4.4)', 'Fish can survive under frozen lakes in winter mainly because', '["ice is denser than water and sinks.", "ice is less dense than water, floats and insulates the water beneath.", "water has a low heat capacity.", "ice conducts heat well."]', 1, 'When water freezes, hydrogen bonding forms an open structure with empty spaces, so ice occupies about 9% more space and is less dense. It floats, forming a blanket that insulates the water below.', '{"0": "Ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why it floats.", "2": "Water has a high heat capacity.", "3": "The floating ice layer acts as an insulator, not a good conductor."}'),
  ('CHE-0223', 'States of matter', 'Properties of water: heat capacity', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 98 (§5.4.4)', 'Water can absorb or release a large amount of heat with only a small change in temperature. This is because', '["it has a high specific heat capacity, due to strong hydrogen bonding.", "it has a low boiling point.", "it is non-polar.", "it has weak London forces only."]', 0, 'Water has a high specific heat capacity (4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) because of its strong hydrogen bonds.', '{"1": "Water''s boiling point is high, not low.", "2": "Water is a polar molecule.", "3": "Water molecules are held by hydrogen bonds, which are much stronger than London forces alone."}'),
  ('CHE-0224', 'States of matter', 'Properties of water: boiling point', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, pp. 98 to 99 (§5.4.4, Fig. 5.8)', 'Among the group 16 hydrides H₂O, H₂S, H₂Se and H₂Te, which has the highest boiling point?', '["H₂S", "H₂Se", "H₂Te", "H₂O"]', 3, 'From H₂S to H₂Te (and H₂Po), boiling points rise as London forces grow. Water breaks the trend and has the highest boiling point and enthalpy of vaporization because of its extensive hydrogen bonding.', '{"0": "H₂S has the lowest boiling point here; its molecules are held by weaker forces.", "1": "H₂Se boils lower than H₂Te and much lower than water.", "2": "H₂Te is highest among the heavier hydrides, but water''s hydrogen bonding makes it higher still."}'),
  ('CHE-0225', 'States of matter', 'Surface tension', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, pp. 99 to 100 (§5.5)', 'Liquid droplets tend to be spherical because', '["of viscosity.", "of vapour pressure.", "surface tension makes liquids minimize their surface area.", "gravity pulls them into spheres."]', 2, 'Surface molecules feel a net inward pull, so a liquid tends to minimize its surface area. A sphere has the smallest surface area for a given volume.', '{"0": "Viscosity is resistance to flow, not the reason for spherical drops.", "1": "Vapour pressure concerns evaporation, not drop shape.", "3": "Gravity tends to flatten drops; surface tension makes them round."}'),
  ('CHE-0226', 'States of matter', 'Surface tension', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 100 (§5.5)', 'What is the effect of increasing temperature on the surface tension of a liquid?', '["It increases.", "It first decreases and then increases.", "It stays the same.", "It decreases."]', 3, 'Surface tension typically decreases as temperature increases.', '{"0": "Higher temperature weakens the effect of intermolecular forces, so surface tension falls.", "1": "The book describes a steady decrease.", "2": "Surface tension depends on temperature."}'),
  ('CHE-0227', 'States of matter', 'Surface tension', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 100 (Table 5.2)', 'Which of these liquids has the highest surface tension at 20 °C?', '["Water", "Ethanol", "Acetone", "Benzene"]', 0, 'Table 5.2 gives water 72.75 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹, much higher than ethanol (22.75), acetone (23.70) and benzene (28.85), because of its strong hydrogen bonding.', '{"1": "Ethanol''s surface tension (22.75 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹) is about a third of water''s.", "2": "Acetone''s surface tension (23.70 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹) is much lower than water''s.", "3": "Benzene''s surface tension (28.85 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹) is much lower than water''s."}'),
  ('CHE-0228', 'States of matter', 'Viscosity', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 100 (§5.6)', 'What happens to the viscosity of a liquid when its temperature is raised?', '["It decreases because molecules gain enough kinetic energy to overcome attractive forces.", "It increases because molecules move faster.", "It stays constant.", "It becomes zero."]', 0, 'Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy of molecules, letting them overcome attractive forces more easily, so viscosity decreases.', '{"1": "Faster molecules flow more easily, lowering viscosity.", "2": "Viscosity depends on temperature.", "3": "Liquids still resist flow when warm; viscosity falls but does not vanish."}'),
  ('CHE-0229', 'States of matter', 'Viscosity', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 99 (§5.4.4)', 'Water is more viscous than hexane and ethanol. The main reason is that', '["water molecules are larger.", "hexane forms stronger hydrogen bonds.", "water has strong and extensive hydrogen bonding.", "ethanol cannot form hydrogen bonds."]', 2, 'Water''s high viscosity is due to strong hydrogen bonds among its molecules. Ethanol forms hydrogen bonds to a far smaller extent, and hydrocarbons like hexane cannot form them.', '{"0": "Water molecules are smaller than hexane and ethanol molecules.", "1": "Hexane is a hydrocarbon and cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "3": "Ethanol can form hydrogen bonds, but fewer and weaker than water."}'),
  ('CHE-0230', 'States of matter', 'Evaporation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 101 (§5.7)', 'Water stored in an earthenware (clay) pot stays cool because', '["clay absorbs heat from the water chemically.", "evaporation raises the temperature of the remaining water.", "clay is a good conductor of heat.", "water seeps through the pores and evaporates, taking energy from the remaining water."]', 3, 'Water molecules with enough kinetic energy escape through the pores and evaporate. The energy comes from the remaining liquid, whose average kinetic energy and temperature fall.', '{"0": "No chemical reaction is involved; the cooling is due to evaporation.", "1": "Evaporation lowers the temperature of the remaining liquid.", "2": "Conduction is not the cooling mechanism described."}'),
  ('CHE-0231', 'States of matter', 'Vapour pressure', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 101 (§5.8)', 'Vapour pressure is the pressure exerted by', '["the vapour in equilibrium with its liquid at a given temperature.", "air above a liquid in an open container.", "a liquid on the walls of its container.", "a gas at STP."]', 0, 'Vapour pressure is the pressure exerted by the vapour of a liquid in equilibrium with the liquid at a given temperature, when the rates of evaporation and condensation are equal.', '{"1": "In an open container equilibrium is not reached; vapour pressure is defined for a closed system.", "2": "Vapour pressure is exerted by the vapour, not by the liquid on the walls.", "3": "This does not define vapour pressure."}'),
  ('CHE-0232', 'States of matter', 'Vapour pressure', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, pp. 101 to 102 (§5.8)', 'The vapour pressure of a given liquid depends on', '["the amount of liquid in the container.", "the volume of the container.", "the surface area of the liquid.", "the temperature."]', 3, 'Vapour pressure increases with temperature. It does not depend on the amount of liquid, the volume of the container or the surface area (a larger surface increases both evaporation and condensation).', '{"0": "The book states vapour pressure does not depend on the amount of liquid.", "1": "The book states vapour pressure does not depend on the volume of the container.", "2": "A larger surface speeds both evaporation and condensation, so vapour pressure is unchanged."}'),
  ('CHE-0233', 'States of matter', 'Vapour pressure', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 102 (§5.8, Fig. 5.10)', 'At the same temperature, which liquid has the highest vapour pressure?', '["Water", "Ethylene glycol", "Diethyl ether", "Glycerine"]', 2, 'Liquids with weaker intermolecular forces have higher vapour pressures. Diethyl ether cannot form hydrogen bonds between its molecules, so its vapour pressure is far higher than that of water, ethylene glycol or glycerine.', '{"0": "Water''s hydrogen bonding gives it a lower vapour pressure than ether.", "1": "Ethylene glycol is strongly hydrogen bonded and has a low vapour pressure.", "3": "Glycerine is strongly hydrogen bonded and has a very low vapour pressure."}'),
  ('CHE-0234', 'States of matter', 'Boiling point and pressure', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 103 (§5.9.1)', 'Water boils at about 98 °C at Murree because', '["the external pressure there (about 700 torr) is lower than at sea level.", "the external pressure there is higher than at sea level.", "the water there contains more hydrogen bonds.", "the water there is purer."]', 0, 'A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure. At Murree the pressure is about 700 torr, below 760 torr, so water''s vapour pressure reaches it at a lower temperature (98 °C).', '{"1": "Higher external pressure would raise the boiling point.", "2": "The hydrogen bonding in water is the same everywhere.", "3": "Purity is not the reason; the lower atmospheric pressure is."}'),
  ('CHE-0235', 'States of matter', 'Boiling point and pressure', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 103 (§5.9.1)', 'Food cooks faster in a pressure cooker because', '["the pressure lowers the boiling point of water.", "the increased pressure raises the boiling point, so the water is hotter.", "steam escapes quickly.", "the food absorbs less heat."]', 1, 'In a closed pressure cooker the vapour cannot escape, raising the pressure on the water surface. The boiling point rises, more heat is absorbed and food cooks faster.', '{"0": "Higher pressure raises, not lowers, the boiling point.", "2": "The vapour is not allowed to escape; that is how the pressure builds up.", "3": "The food is cooked by hotter water, so it absorbs heat faster."}'),
  ('CHE-0236', 'States of matter', 'Boiling point', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 103 (Table 5.3)', 'Using Table 5.3, which of these liquids has the highest boiling point at 1 atm?', '["Acetone", "Ethanol", "Water", "Acetic acid"]', 3, 'Table 5.3 lists acetone 56.00 °C, ethanol 78.30 °C, water 100.00 °C and acetic acid 118.50 °C.', '{"0": "Acetone boils at only 56.00 °C.", "1": "Ethanol boils at 78.30 °C.", "2": "Water boils at 100.00 °C, below acetic acid."}'),
  ('CHE-0237', 'States of matter', 'Boiling point', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, pp. 102 to 103 (§5.9)', 'While a pure liquid is boiling, further heating does not raise its temperature because', '["the extra heat is used to overcome intermolecular forces and convert liquid to vapour.", "the liquid stops absorbing heat.", "the vapour pressure falls.", "the external pressure rises."]', 0, 'At the boiling point, further heat does not increase the temperature; it is used to break intermolecular forces and convert the liquid into vapour.', '{"1": "The liquid keeps absorbing heat, which is used for vaporization.", "2": "At the boiling point the vapour pressure equals the external pressure; it does not fall.", "3": "The external pressure is unchanged by heating an open liquid."}'),
  ('CHE-0238', 'States of matter', 'Energetics of phase changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 104 (§5.10)', 'The molar heat of vaporization is the heat absorbed', '["by one gram of a liquid at 25 °C.", "by one mole of a liquid to convert it into vapour at its boiling point at 1 atm.", "by one mole of a solid to melt it.", "when one mole of vapour condenses."]', 1, 'Molar heat of vaporization is the heat absorbed by one mole of a liquid to convert it into vapour at its boiling point at 1 atmosphere.', '{"0": "The definition is per mole and at the boiling point.", "2": "Melting a solid involves the molar heat of fusion.", "3": "Condensation releases heat; vaporization absorbs it."}'),
  ('CHE-0239', 'States of matter', 'Energetics of phase changes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 104 (§5.10)', 'The molar heat of vaporization of water at 100 °C is 40.6 kJ mol⁻¹. How much heat is needed to vaporize 0.5 mol of water at its boiling point?', '["10.15 kJ", "20.3 kJ", "40.6 kJ", "81.2 kJ"]', 1, 'Heat = n × ΔHv = 0.5 × 40.6 = 20.3 kJ.', '{"0": "10.15 kJ is the heat for 0.25 mol.", "2": "40.6 kJ is the heat for one whole mole.", "3": "81.2 kJ is the heat for 2 mol."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0240', 'States of matter', 'Heat of fusion', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 104 (§5.10)', 'Why does the high heat of fusion of water matter for glaciers and polar ice caps?', '["It makes ice melt very easily.", "A lot of energy is needed to melt ice, which keeps ice caps and glaciers stable.", "It makes ice denser than water.", "It lowers the temperature of the oceans."]', 1, 'Because water has a high heat of fusion, ice needs a lot of energy to melt. This keeps ice caps and icebergs stable, helping regulate Earth''s temperature and sea levels.', '{"0": "A high heat of fusion means ice is hard to melt.", "2": "Ice is less dense than water.", "3": "The book links heat of fusion to the stability of ice, not to ocean cooling."}'),
  ('CHE-0241', 'States of matter', 'Properties of solids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 105 (§5.11.1)', 'What kind of motion do the particles of a solid have?', '["Translational, rotational and vibrational", "Only vibrational motion about their mean positions", "Only translational motion", "No motion at all"]', 1, 'The particles of a solid are held in fixed positions; they only vibrate about their mean positions, so their only kinetic energy is vibrational.', '{"0": "Particles in a solid cannot move from place to place or rotate freely.", "2": "Translational motion is typical of liquids and gases.", "3": "Solid particles vibrate, more intensely at higher temperature."}'),
  ('CHE-0242', 'States of matter', 'Properties of solids', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 105 (§5.11.1)', 'When spherical metal atoms are packed as closely as possible, what is the maximum fraction of space they can occupy?', '["50%", "68%", "74%", "100%"]', 2, 'Spherical particles in hexagonal or cubic close packing touch each other but cannot fill more than 74% of the space.', '{"0": "50% is lower than the maximum for close packing.", "1": "68% is less than the close-packed maximum.", "3": "Spheres always leave gaps, so they cannot fill all the space."}'),
  ('CHE-0243', 'States of matter', 'Crystalline and amorphous solids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 107 (Table 5.4)', 'Crystalline solids are described as anisotropic. This means that', '["they melt over a range of temperature.", "their properties depend on the direction in which they are measured.", "they have no regular arrangement.", "their properties are the same in all directions."]', 1, 'Anisotropic means that properties depend on the direction of measurement. Crystalline solids are anisotropic, while amorphous solids are isotropic.', '{"0": "Melting over a range describes amorphous solids.", "2": "Crystalline solids have a regular, long-range arrangement.", "3": "Properties being the same in all directions is isotropy, shown by amorphous solids."}'),
  ('CHE-0244', 'States of matter', 'Crystalline and amorphous solids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 107 (§5.12.2)', 'Which of the following is an amorphous solid?', '["Sodium chloride", "Diamond", "Glass", "Ice"]', 2, 'Glass is amorphous: it has no regular three-dimensional shape and melts over a wide temperature range. Other examples are wood, plastic sulfur, charcoal, coal and coke.', '{"0": "Sodium chloride is a crystalline solid.", "1": "Diamond is a crystalline solid.", "3": "Ice is a crystalline solid."}'),
  ('CHE-0245', 'States of matter', 'Properties of crystalline solids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 106 (§5.12.1)', 'When a crystalline solid is broken, it splits along definite planes. These are called', '["habit planes.", "cleavage planes.", "lattice points.", "crystallites."]', 1, 'Crystalline solids break along definite planes called cleavage planes, inclined to one another at an angle characteristic of the solid.', '{"0": "Habit is the shape in which a crystal usually grows.", "2": "Lattice points mark particle positions in a crystal lattice.", "3": "Crystallites are small ordered regions within amorphous solids."}'),
  ('CHE-0246', 'States of matter', 'Properties of crystalline solids', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 107 (§5.12.1)', 'Sodium chloride normally crystallizes as cubes but forms needle-like crystals when 10% urea is present in its solution. This shows that', '["sodium chloride becomes amorphous.", "the habit of a crystal can change when the growth conditions change.", "urea reacts with sodium chloride.", "cleavage planes disappear."]', 1, 'The habit of a crystal is the shape in which it usually grows. It stays the same under the same conditions, but changes if the conditions change, as when urea is present.', '{"0": "The needles are still crystalline.", "2": "Urea acts as an impurity affecting growth; no reaction is described.", "3": "Cleavage planes are a property of crystals and still exist."}'),
  ('CHE-0247', 'States of matter', 'Properties of crystalline solids', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 106 (§5.12.1)', 'A supersaturated solution of sodium thiosulfate does not crystallize on slow cooling, but crystallizes rapidly when a small crystal is added. This shows that', '["the solution was saturated and at equilibrium.", "a seed crystal can start crystal growth in a supersaturated solution, which is not at equilibrium with the solid.", "sodium thiosulfate is amorphous.", "the solubility increases on cooling."]', 1, 'A supersaturated solution is not in equilibrium with the solid. Adding a seed crystal provides a starting point, and crystallization happens rapidly.', '{"0": "A supersaturated solution holds more solute than at equilibrium; it is not at equilibrium.", "2": "Sodium thiosulfate forms crystals, so it is crystalline.", "3": "Its solubility falls on cooling (231 g at 100 °C to 50 g at room temperature)."}'),
  ('CHE-0248', 'States of matter', 'Amorphous solids', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 107 (§5.12.2)', 'Which statement about amorphous solids is correct?', '["They have sharp melting points.", "They have long-range order.", "They melt over a range of temperature and may contain small ordered regions called crystallites.", "They are anisotropic."]', 2, 'Amorphous solids lack long-range order, though they may have small ordered regions called crystallites. They melt over a range of temperature and have no definite heat of fusion.', '{"0": "Sharp melting points are typical of crystalline solids.", "1": "Long-range order is absent in amorphous solids.", "3": "Amorphous solids are isotropic."}'),
  ('CHE-0249', 'States of matter', 'Liquid crystals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 108 (§5.13)', 'Which description fits most liquid crystal molecules?', '["Rigid, rod-like molecules, four to eight times longer than wide, roughly parallel to each other", "Spherical molecules with no orientation", "Ions held in a rigid lattice", "Gas molecules widely separated"]', 0, 'In most liquid crystals the molecules are rigid and rod-like, four to eight times longer than their diameter, and pack with their long axes roughly parallel. They can migrate and spin about their long axis but cannot rotate end over end.', '{"1": "Liquid crystal molecules are elongated and partly ordered.", "2": "A rigid ionic lattice describes an ionic crystal, not a liquid crystal.", "3": "Liquid crystals are a condensed phase, not a gas."}'),
  ('CHE-0250', 'States of matter', 'Liquid crystals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 States and Phases of Matter, p. 108 (§5.13.2)', 'Which use of liquid crystals depends on their sensitivity to temperature?', '["Making glass", "Detecting tumours, which are warmer than surrounding tissue", "Purifying water", "Producing fertilizers"]', 1, 'Cholesteric liquid crystals change with temperature, so they can locate a tumour that is warmer than surrounding tissue. The same property is used in thermometers and to find faulty circuit-board connections.', '{"0": "Glass is an amorphous solid; making it does not use liquid crystals.", "2": "Water purification is not a listed use of liquid crystals.", "3": "Fertilizer production is not a listed use of liquid crystals."}'),
  ('CHE-0251', 'Thermochemistry', 'Enthalpy change', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 113 (§6.1)', 'For a reaction in which the total enthalpy of the products (Hₚ) is less than that of the reactants (Hᵣ), which statement is correct?', '["ΔH is positive and heat is evolved to the surroundings.", "ΔH is positive and heat is absorbed from the surroundings.", "ΔH is zero because energy is conserved.", "ΔH is negative and heat is evolved to the surroundings."]', 3, 'ΔH = Hₚ − Hᵣ. When Hₚ < Hᵣ, ΔH is negative and the difference is released as heat, so the process is exothermic.', '{"0": "A positive ΔH means heat is absorbed, not evolved; and here ΔH is negative.", "1": "A positive ΔH requires Hₚ > Hᵣ; that is an endothermic process.", "2": "Energy conservation does not make ΔH zero; the enthalpy difference is exchanged with the surroundings as heat."}'),
  ('CHE-0252', 'Thermochemistry', 'Enthalpy change', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 114 (§6.1)', 'Instant cold packs used for injuries contain ammonium chloride and water in separate compartments. They feel cold when kneaded because', '["water freezes when it touches NH₄Cl crystals.", "the dissolution of NH₄Cl in water is exothermic.", "NH₄Cl reacts with water to release ammonia gas, which is cold.", "the dissolution of NH₄Cl in water is endothermic and absorbs heat from the surroundings."]', 3, 'NH₄Cl(s) dissolving in water has ΔH = +16.2 kJ mol⁻¹. The energy is absorbed from the surroundings, producing the cooling sensation.', '{"0": "No freezing occurs; the temperature drop is due to heat absorbed during dissolution.", "1": "An exothermic process would warm the pack.", "2": "The cooling comes from the endothermic dissolution, not from a gas being released."}'),
  ('CHE-0253', 'Thermochemistry', 'Energy profile diagram', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 114 (§6.2)', 'The minimum energy that colliding reactant molecules must have to cross the energy barrier and form products is called the', '["enthalpy of reaction.", "bond dissociation energy.", "lattice energy.", "activation energy."]', 3, 'The book defines this minimum energy needed just to cross the energy barrier as the energy of activation, Ea.', '{"0": "The enthalpy of reaction is the difference between the enthalpies of products and reactants, not the barrier height.", "1": "Bond dissociation energy is the energy to break one mole of a particular bond.", "2": "Lattice energy concerns forming an ionic solid from gaseous ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0254', 'Thermochemistry', 'Energy profile diagram', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 114 (§6.2, Figure 6.2)', 'On the energy profile diagram of an endothermic reaction, which relation must hold between the activation energy (Ea) of the forward reaction and its enthalpy change (ΔH)?', '["Ea and ΔH have opposite signs.", "Ea is less than ΔH.", "Ea is always zero.", "Ea is greater than ΔH."]', 3, 'In an endothermic reaction the products lie above the reactants, and the peak of the barrier lies above the products. So the climb from reactants to the peak (Ea) is larger than the climb from reactants to products (ΔH).', '{"0": "For an endothermic reaction both Ea and ΔH are positive.", "1": "The transition state lies above the products, so Ea cannot be smaller than ΔH.", "2": "Every reaction shown in Figure 6.2 has a barrier, so Ea is not zero."}'),
  ('CHE-0255', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 115 (§6.3)', 'Standard enthalpy changes are measured at', '["0 °C and 1 atm.", "298 K and 1 atm (101 kPa).", "273 K and 101 kPa.", "25 K and 1 atm."]', 1, 'The book''s standard conditions are a temperature of 25 °C (298 K) and a pressure of 1 atm (101 kPa).', '{"0": "0 °C is 273 K, not the standard temperature for enthalpy changes.", "2": "273 K is 0 °C; standard enthalpy data use 298 K.", "3": "The standard temperature is 25 °C, which is 298 K, not 25 K."}'),
  ('CHE-0256', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 115 (§6.3, Did you know?)', 'Which of the following has a standard enthalpy of formation equal to zero?', '["C(diamond)", "H₂O(l)", "CO₂(g)", "C(graphite)"]', 3, 'The standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is zero. The standard state of carbon is graphite, its most stable form at 298 K and 1 atm.', '{"0": "Diamond is not the standard state of carbon, so its ΔHf is not zero.", "1": "Water is a compound; its ΔHf is about −286 kJ mol⁻¹.", "2": "CO₂ is a compound; its ΔHf is about −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹."}'),
  ('CHE-0257', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 115 (§6.3.2)', 'Which statement about the standard enthalpy change of combustion is correct?', '["It refers to one mole of the substance burnt in limited oxygen, and it can be either sign.", "It refers to one mole of oxygen consumed, and it can be endothermic.", "It refers to one mole of product formed, and it is always endothermic.", "It refers to one mole of the substance burnt completely in excess oxygen, and it is always exothermic."]', 3, 'The book defines ΔHc as the enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is completely burnt in excess oxygen under standard conditions, and states that it is always exothermic.', '{"0": "Complete combustion needs excess oxygen, and ΔHc is always negative.", "1": "The definition is per mole of the substance burnt, and combustion is always exothermic.", "2": "Combustion releases heat; it is never endothermic."}'),
  ('CHE-0258', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 116 (§6.3.3); p. 122', 'Which statement about the standard enthalpy of formation (ΔHf) of compounds is correct?', '["It is always negative.", "It is always positive.", "It can be negative (for example CH₄, −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹) or positive (for example C₂H₄, +52.2 kJ mol⁻¹).", "It is zero for all gases."]', 2, 'The book says ΔHf can be exothermic or endothermic. Methane has ΔHf = −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹, while ethene has ΔHf = +52.2 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "Ethene''s ΔHf is positive, so it is not always negative.", "1": "Methane''s ΔHf is negative, so it is not always positive.", "3": "Only elements in their standard states have zero ΔHf; gaseous compounds such as CH₄ do not."}'),
  ('CHE-0259', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 116 (§6.3.4)', 'The bond energy of H–H is +436 kJ mol⁻¹. The standard enthalpy change of atomization of hydrogen is', '["+436 kJ mol⁻¹", "+218 kJ mol⁻¹", "+872 kJ mol⁻¹", "−218 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'Atomization is defined per mole of gaseous atoms formed: ½H₂(g) → H(g). Breaking one mole of H–H bonds gives two moles of atoms, so ΔHat = 436 ÷ 2 = +218 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "+436 kJ forms two moles of H atoms, not one.", "2": "+872 kJ would form four moles of H atoms.", "3": "Breaking bonds absorbs energy, so atomization is positive."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0260', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 116 and 128', 'Which equation represents the standard enthalpy change of atomization of chlorine?', '["Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g)", "½Cl₂(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)", "Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)", "½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g)"]', 3, 'Atomization is the enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms is formed from the element. For chlorine this is ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g), +121 kJ mol⁻¹ in the Born–Haber cycle of NaCl.', '{"0": "This forms two moles of Cl atoms; its enthalpy change is the Cl–Cl bond energy, twice ΔHat.", "1": "This combines atomization and electron gain; it is not atomization alone.", "2": "This is the first electron affinity of chlorine."}'),
  ('CHE-0261', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 116 (§6.3.5)', 'The enthalpy of neutralization of any strong acid by any strong base is close to −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹ because', '["all strong acids have the same molar mass.", "the salt formed always precipitates.", "the only change in each case is H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l); the other ions are spectators.", "neutralization is endothermic for all strong acids."]', 2, 'Strong acids and strong bases are fully ionized. On mixing, the only change is the formation of water from H⁺ and OH⁻, with ions such as Na⁺ and Cl⁻ left as spectators, so ΔHn is nearly the same, about −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "Molar mass has nothing to do with ΔHn, which is per mole of water formed.", "1": "Salts such as NaCl stay in solution; no precipitation occurs.", "3": "Neutralization is always exothermic."}'),
  ('CHE-0262', 'Thermochemistry', 'Standard enthalpy changes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 116 (§6.3.5)', 'In a calorimeter, 0.10 mol of H₂SO₄ in dilute solution is completely neutralized by 0.20 mol of NaOH, and 11.42 kJ of heat is evolved. The enthalpy of neutralization is', '["−114.2 kJ mol⁻¹", "−57.1 kJ mol⁻¹", "−11.42 kJ mol⁻¹", "−28.6 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'Enthalpy of neutralization is per mole of water formed. H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH gives 2H₂O, so 0.20 mol water forms. ΔHn = −11.42 ÷ 0.20 = −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides by 0.10 mol of acid, giving the heat per mole of H₂SO₄, not per mole of water.", "2": "This is the heat for the whole sample, not per mole of water.", "3": "This divides by 0.40 mol, which double counts the water formed."}'),
  ('CHE-0263', 'Thermochemistry', 'Electron affinity', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 116 (§6.3.6)', 'The first electron affinity of chlorine is −348.8 kJ mol⁻¹. The negative sign means that', '["energy is absorbed when a gaseous chlorine atom gains an electron.", "the electron is removed from chlorine.", "chlorine cannot form negative ions.", "energy is released when one mole of gaseous chlorine atoms gains one mole of electrons."]', 3, 'First electron affinity is the enthalpy change when 1 mole of electrons is added to 1 mole of gaseous atoms to form uninegative ions. Since energy is released, it carries a negative sign.', '{"0": "Energy absorbed would give a positive sign.", "1": "Electron affinity concerns adding an electron; removing one is ionization.", "2": "Cl⁻ forms readily; the negative value shows the process releases energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0264', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 117 (§6.4)', 'The C–C bond energy is 376 kJ mol⁻¹ in ethane, 356 kJ mol⁻¹ in propane and 352 kJ mol⁻¹ in butane. The best explanation is that', '["these values are average bond energies.", "C–C bonds in larger molecules are double bonds.", "the bond energy of a particular bond is affected by the other atoms in the molecule.", "bond energies decrease as molar mass decreases."]', 2, 'The book notes that bonds between the same pair of atoms have different bond energies in different compounds because B.E. is affected by the other atoms in the molecule. Each of these values is an exact bond energy for that molecule.', '{"0": "These are exact bond energies for each molecule; averaging them gives the average C–C bond energy.", "1": "All three molecules contain only C–C single bonds.", "3": "The bond energy falls as molar mass increases here, and molar mass is not the cause."}'),
  ('CHE-0265', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 117–118 (§6.5)', 'A reaction is exothermic when', '["the products have more bonds than the reactants, whatever their strengths.", "the energy absorbed in breaking bonds is greater than the energy released in forming bonds.", "no bonds are broken.", "the energy released in forming new bonds is greater than the energy absorbed in breaking the old bonds."]', 3, 'Bond breaking absorbs energy and bond formation releases it. If the energy released in making new bonds exceeds that absorbed in breaking bonds, the overall reaction is exothermic.', '{"0": "It is the total bond energy, not the number of bonds, that decides the sign of ΔH.", "1": "That describes an endothermic reaction.", "2": "All chemical reactions involve breaking bonds before new ones form."}'),
  ('CHE-0266', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energy calculations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 123 (§6.8 iv)', 'Using average bond energies (kJ mol⁻¹) C=C 615, H–H 436, C–C 348 and C–H 413, the enthalpy change for C₂H₄(g) + H₂(g) → C₂H₆(g) is', '["−123 kJ mol⁻¹", "+123 kJ mol⁻¹", "−1051 kJ mol⁻¹", "−413 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔH = ΣE(bonds broken) − ΣE(bonds formed). Broken: C=C + H–H = 615 + 436 = 1051. Formed: C–C + 2 C–H = 348 + 826 = 1174. The four C–H bonds of ethene appear on both sides and cancel. ΔH = 1051 − 1174 = −123 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This reverses the formula (formed minus broken).", "2": "This counts only the bonds broken, given a negative sign.", "3": "This is just one C–H bond energy with a negative sign."}'),
  ('CHE-0267', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energy calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 123 (§6.8 iv)', 'Using bond energies (kJ mol⁻¹) C–H 413, O=O 498, C=O 805 and O–H 463, the enthalpy change for CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g) is', '["−814 kJ mol⁻¹", "+814 kJ mol⁻¹", "−386 kJ mol⁻¹", "−2648 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'Broken: 4 C–H + 2 O=O = 4(413) + 2(498) = 1652 + 996 = 2648 kJ. Formed: 2 C=O + 4 O–H = 2(805) + 4(463) = 1610 + 1852 = 3462 kJ. ΔH = 2648 − 3462 = −814 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This takes bonds formed minus bonds broken, which gives the wrong sign.", "2": "This ignores the coefficient 2 on O₂ and on H₂O: (1652 + 498) − (1610 + 926) = −386.", "3": "This is the energy of the bonds broken only, with a negative sign."}'),
  ('CHE-0268', 'Thermochemistry', 'Bond energy calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 123 (§6.8 iv, Sample Problem 6.6 method)', 'Using bond energies H–H 436, Br–Br 192 and H–Br 366 kJ mol⁻¹, the standard enthalpy of formation of HBr(g) estimated from H₂(g) + Br₂(g) → 2HBr(g) is', '["−104 kJ mol⁻¹", "−52 kJ mol⁻¹", "+52 kJ mol⁻¹", "+104 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'ΔHr = (436 + 192) − 2(366) = 628 − 732 = −104 kJ for 2 mol HBr. Enthalpy of formation is per mole of compound, so ΔHf = −104 ÷ 2 = −52 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "−104 kJ is for 2 mol HBr; ΔHf is per mole.", "2": "The bonds formed release more energy than the bonds broken absorb, so the value is negative.", "3": "This reverses the formula (formed minus broken) and does not divide by 2."}'),
  ('CHE-0269', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 118 (§6.6)', 'The specific heat capacity of water used in calorimetry calculations is', '["4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ (rounded to 4.2 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹)", "4.18 kJ g⁻¹ K⁻¹", "1.00 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹", "41.8 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹"]', 0, 'The book gives the specific heat capacity of water as 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ (or J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹), rounded off to 4.2 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.', '{"1": "This is 1000 times too large; the value is in joules per gram, not kilojoules.", "2": "1.00 is the value in calories per gram per kelvin, not joules.", "3": "This is ten times too large."}'),
  ('CHE-0270', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 118 (§6.6.1)', 'A simple glass (coffee-cup type) calorimeter is NOT suitable for a reaction that', '["takes place in dilute aqueous solution.", "produces a gas that escapes from the vessel.", "is exothermic.", "is a neutralization."]', 1, 'A glass calorimeter is suitable for reactions in solution. It cannot be used for reactions involving gases, which escape from the vessel, or for reactions whose products reach high temperatures.', '{"0": "Reactions in solution are exactly what it is designed for.", "2": "It measures heat evolved as well as heat absorbed.", "3": "The book''s example of its use is the heat of neutralization."}'),
  ('CHE-0271', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 119 (§6.6.1)', 'In a glass calorimeter, the beaker is surrounded by cotton wool in order to', '["stir the solution.", "keep the pressure above 1 atm.", "minimize the exchange of heat with the surrounding air.", "absorb any gas evolved."]', 2, 'The outside walls of the calorimeter beaker are insulated with cotton wool to minimize heat exchange with the surrounding air.', '{"0": "A separate stirrer is used for mixing.", "1": "The loose-fitting lid keeps the contents at atmospheric pressure.", "3": "Cotton wool is insulation; the calorimeter is not meant for reactions that give off gases."}'),
  ('CHE-0272', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 119 (§6.6.1, Sample Problem 6.2 method)', '50 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ HCl is mixed with 50 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ NaOH in a glass calorimeter, and the temperature rises by 6.8 K. Taking the solution''s mass as 100 g and c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, the enthalpy of neutralization is', '["−28.6 kJ mol⁻¹", "−2.86 kJ mol⁻¹", "−57.1 kJ mol⁻¹", "−114.2 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'q = mcΔT = 100 × 4.2 × 6.8 = 2856 J = 2.856 kJ. Moles of water formed = 1.0 × 50/1000 = 0.050 mol. ΔHn = −2.856 ÷ 0.050 = −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides by 0.10 mol, wrongly adding the moles of acid and base.", "1": "This is the heat evolved by the sample, not per mole of water.", "3": "This divides by 0.025 mol, half the moles of water actually formed (0.050 mol)."}'),
  ('CHE-0273', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorimetry', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 119 (§6.6.1)', 'When calculating heat changes for reactions in dilute aqueous solutions in a glass calorimeter, we can use the specific heat capacity of water for the whole mixture because', '["the calorimeter is made of glass.", "the solutes have no heat capacity.", "the solutions are so dilute that almost all of their mass is water.", "the reaction occurs at constant volume."]', 2, 'The book explains that the solutions are so dilute that nearly all their mass consists of water, so the specific heat capacity of water can be used.', '{"0": "The material of the beaker is not the reason.", "1": "Solutes do have heat capacities; they are just a negligible part of the mass.", "3": "The glass calorimeter works at constant (atmospheric) pressure, not constant volume."}'),
  ('CHE-0274', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorie content of food', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 120 (§6.7.1)', 'A food releases 16.7 kJ of heat per gram when completely oxidized. Its calorie content is about', '["4.0 kcal g⁻¹", "69.9 kcal g⁻¹", "16.7 kcal g⁻¹", "0.25 kcal g⁻¹"]', 0, 'Calorie content = −ΔH (kJ g⁻¹) ÷ 4.184 kJ kcal⁻¹ = 16.7 ÷ 4.184 ≈ 4.0 kcal g⁻¹.', '{"1": "This multiplies by 4.184 instead of dividing.", "2": "This leaves the value in kJ g⁻¹ without converting to kcal.", "3": "This inverts the conversion (4.184 ÷ 16.7)."}'),
  ('CHE-0275', 'Thermochemistry', 'Calorie content of food', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 115 and 120', 'The standard enthalpy of combustion of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46 g mol⁻¹) is −1368 kJ mol⁻¹. Its energy content is closest to', '["7.1 kcal g⁻¹", "29.7 kcal g⁻¹", "327 kcal g⁻¹", "1.7 kcal g⁻¹"]', 0, 'Per gram: 1368 ÷ 46 = 29.7 kJ g⁻¹. In kilocalories: 29.7 ÷ 4.184 ≈ 7.1 kcal g⁻¹.', '{"1": "29.7 is the value in kJ g⁻¹, not kcal g⁻¹.", "2": "This converts kJ mol⁻¹ to kcal mol⁻¹ (1368 ÷ 4.184) without dividing by the molar mass.", "3": "This divides by 4.184 twice."}'),
  ('CHE-0276', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 121 (§6.8)', 'Hess''s law states that the total enthalpy change of a reaction', '["depends on the number of steps in the route.", "is independent of the route, provided the initial and final conditions are the same.", "is always equal to the activation energy.", "is zero for a reaction carried out in several steps."]', 1, 'Hess''s law: the total enthalpy change in a chemical reaction is independent of the route by which it takes place, as long as the initial and final conditions are the same.', '{"0": "The route, including the number of steps, does not affect the total ΔH.", "2": "Activation energy depends on the pathway; ΔH does not.", "3": "The sum of the step enthalpies equals the direct ΔH; it is not zero."}'),
  ('CHE-0277', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 121 (§6.8)', 'Hess''s law is especially useful for finding the enthalpy of formation of tetrachloromethane (CCl₄) because', '["CCl₄ is an element.", "CCl₄ cannot be prepared directly by combining carbon and chlorine, so its ΔHf cannot be measured directly in a calorimeter.", "the formation of CCl₄ has no enthalpy change.", "CCl₄ burns completely in oxygen."]', 1, 'The book gives CCl₄ as an example of a compound that cannot be made directly from carbon and chlorine, so ΔH cannot be measured calorimetrically; Hess''s law lets it be calculated from other reactions.', '{"0": "CCl₄ is a compound of carbon and chlorine.", "2": "Its formation does have an enthalpy change; it just cannot be measured directly.", "3": "That is not the reason the book gives; CCl₄ does not burn readily."}'),
  ('CHE-0278', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 121 (§6.8, Sample Problem 6.3 method)', 'Given: S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g), ΔH = −297 kJ mol⁻¹; SO₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → SO₃(g), ΔH = −99 kJ mol⁻¹. The enthalpy change for S(s) + 1½O₂(g) → SO₃(g) is', '["−198 kJ mol⁻¹", "−396 kJ mol⁻¹", "+198 kJ mol⁻¹", "−594 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 1, 'Adding the two steps gives the overall reaction, so by Hess''s law ΔH = (−297) + (−99) = −396 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This subtracts the second step instead of adding it.", "2": "This subtracts the first step from the second; both steps should be added.", "3": "This doubles the first step; it should be used once."}'),
  ('CHE-0279', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 122 (§6.8 ii, Sample Problem 6.4 method)', 'Given ΔHf of C₂H₄(g) = +52.2 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔHf of C₂H₆(g) = −84.7 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔH for C₂H₄(g) + H₂(g) → C₂H₆(g) is', '["−32.5 kJ mol⁻¹", "+136.9 kJ mol⁻¹", "−136.9 kJ mol⁻¹", "+32.5 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'ΔHr = ΣΔHf(products) − ΣΔHf(reactants) = (−84.7) − (52.2 + 0) = −136.9 kJ mol⁻¹. H₂ is an element in its standard state, so its ΔHf is zero.', '{"0": "This adds the two values: −84.7 + 52.2.", "1": "This is reactants minus products, which gives the wrong sign.", "3": "This adds them and changes the sign."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0280', 'Thermochemistry', 'Hess''s law', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 121–122 (§6.8)', 'Standard enthalpies of combustion (kJ mol⁻¹): C(graphite) −394, H₂(g) −286, CH₄(g) −890. The standard enthalpy of formation of methane, C(graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g), is', '["−76 kJ mol⁻¹", "+76 kJ mol⁻¹", "+210 kJ mol⁻¹", "−1856 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'Using combustion data, ΔHf = ΣΔHc(reactants) − ΣΔHc(products) = [−394 + 2(−286)] − (−890) = −966 + 890 = −76 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This is products minus reactants for combustion data, which reverses the sign.", "2": "This uses only one mole of H₂: (−394 − 286) + 890 = +210; the coefficient 2 must be used.", "3": "This adds all three combustion enthalpies instead of subtracting methane''s."}'),
  ('CHE-0281', 'Thermochemistry', 'Energetics of solution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 125 (§6.9.1)', 'Hydration enthalpy is always negative because', '["the lattice of the solid is broken.", "the ions lose their charges in water.", "water molecules break apart into H⁺ and OH⁻.", "energy is released when ion–dipole attractions form between the ions and water molecules."]', 3, 'When water molecules surround and interact with ions, ion–dipole forces form. Their formation releases energy, so hydration is always exothermic.', '{"0": "Breaking a lattice absorbs energy; that is the reverse of lattice energy, not hydration.", "1": "Ions keep their charges in solution; they are just surrounded by water.", "2": "Water molecules stay intact around the ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0282', 'Thermochemistry', 'Energetics of solution', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 125 (§6.9.1)', 'Which ion has the most exothermic enthalpy of hydration?', '["Na⁺", "K⁺", "Mg²⁺", "Al³⁺"]', 3, 'Hydration energy increases with charge density (more charge on a smaller ion). Al³⁺ has the highest charge and the smallest radius of these ions, so its hydration is the most exothermic.', '{"0": "Na⁺ has only a +1 charge.", "1": "K⁺ has a +1 charge and a large radius, so its charge density is lowest.", "2": "Mg²⁺ has a high charge density, but Al³⁺ is smaller and more highly charged."}'),
  ('CHE-0283', 'Thermochemistry', 'Energetics of solution', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 127 (§6.9.3)', 'For NaCl, the lattice energy (formation of the solid from gaseous ions) is −787 kJ mol⁻¹, and the hydration enthalpies of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are −406 and −377 kJ mol⁻¹. Using the cycle ΔHlatt + ΔHsol = ΔHhyd, the enthalpy of solution of NaCl is', '["+4 kJ mol⁻¹", "−4 kJ mol⁻¹", "−1570 kJ mol⁻¹", "+1570 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔHsol = ΔHhyd − ΔHlatt = (−406 − 377) − (−787) = −783 + 787 = +4 kJ mol⁻¹. The small positive value shows dissolving NaCl is slightly endothermic.', '{"1": "This reverses the subtraction, ΔHlatt − ΔHhyd.", "2": "This adds the lattice energy to the hydration enthalpies.", "3": "This adds the magnitudes with the wrong sign."}'),
  ('CHE-0284', 'Thermochemistry', 'Lattice energy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 126 (§6.9.2, Figure 6.7)', 'Among the alkali metal halides, which has the most negative (largest) lattice energy?', '["KBr", "NaCl", "LiF", "CsI"]', 2, 'Lattice energies of alkali metal halides decrease as the cation or anion gets larger, because the ions are less tightly packed. LiF has the smallest cation and the smallest anion, so its lattice energy is the largest.', '{"0": "K⁺ and Br⁻ are larger still, so the lattice energy is smaller.", "1": "Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are both larger than Li⁺ and F⁻.", "3": "CsI has the largest ions and the smallest lattice energy of the four."}'),
  ('CHE-0285', 'Thermochemistry', 'Lattice energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 126 (§6.9.2)', 'MgO and LiF have the same arrangement of ions and ions of similar size, yet the lattice energy of MgO (−3923 kJ mol⁻¹) is far larger than that of LiF (−1049 kJ mol⁻¹). This is because', '["LiF has a different crystal structure.", "MgO has larger ions than LiF.", "MgO is a covalent compound.", "Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ carry double charges and attract each other more strongly than singly charged Li⁺ and F⁻."]', 3, 'Lattice energy is directly related to the charges on the ions. The doubly charged Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions attract each other much more strongly than the singly charged ions of the same size in LiF.', '{"0": "The book says the two have the same arrangement of ions.", "1": "The book states the ion sizes are comparable; larger ions would lower lattice energy anyway.", "2": "MgO is ionic; the comparison is about ionic charge."}'),
  ('CHE-0286', 'Thermochemistry', 'Lattice energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 126 (§6.9.2)', 'The solubility of group 2 hydroxides increases from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂. The book''s explanation is that, down the group,', '["hydration energy increases while lattice energy stays constant.", "lattice energy decreases more rapidly than hydration energy, so ΔHsol becomes more exothermic.", "hydration energy decreases more rapidly than lattice energy, so ΔHsol becomes more endothermic.", "both lattice energy and hydration energy increase."]', 1, 'Both lattice and hydration energies decrease down group 2 as cation charge density falls, but for the hydroxides lattice energy falls faster. The lattice factor dominates, ΔHsol becomes more exothermic and solubility increases.', '{"0": "Hydration energy decreases down the group, and lattice energy also decreases.", "2": "This is the explanation for the sulphates, whose solubility decreases down the group.", "3": "Both decrease down the group because the cations get larger."}'),
  ('CHE-0287', 'Thermochemistry', 'Lattice energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 126 (§6.9.2)', 'The solubility of group 2 sulphates decreases from MgSO₄ to BaSO₄ mainly because', '["the large SO₄²⁻ ion makes the fall in lattice energy small, while the hydration energy of the cation falls by a larger amount.", "lattice energy increases down the group.", "hydration energy of the cation increases down the group.", "SO₄²⁻ is smaller than OH⁻."]', 0, 'Because SO₄²⁻ is much larger than OH⁻, lattice energy changes little from MgSO₄ to BaSO₄, but cation hydration energy decreases by more. Hydration dominates, ΔHsol becomes more endothermic and solubility decreases.', '{"1": "Lattice energy decreases (slightly) down the group, not increases.", "2": "Hydration energy decreases down the group as the cation gets bigger.", "3": "The book says SO₄²⁻ is much larger than OH⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-0288', 'Thermochemistry', 'Born–Haber cycle', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 128 (§6.10)', 'The lattice energy of an ionic compound such as NaCl is usually found using', '["a single direct calorimetric experiment.", "the Born–Haber cycle, an application of Hess''s law using other experimental data.", "the boiling point of the compound.", "the Gibbs free energy equation."]', 1, 'The book states that lattice energy cannot be determined by a single direct experiment; it is calculated from other experimental data with the Born–Haber cycle, an application of Hess''s law.', '{"0": "Forming a crystal from gaseous ions cannot be carried out directly in a calorimeter.", "2": "Boiling point does not give lattice energy.", "3": "ΔG = ΔH − TΔS predicts spontaneity; it is not used to find lattice energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0289', 'Thermochemistry', 'Born–Haber cycle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 128–129 (§6.10)', 'For KCl: ΔHat(K) = +89, first ionization energy of K = +419, ΔHat(Cl) = +121, first electron affinity of Cl = −349 and ΔHf(KCl) = −437 (all in kJ mol⁻¹). The lattice energy of KCl is', '["−717 kJ mol⁻¹", "−157 kJ mol⁻¹", "−1415 kJ mol⁻¹", "+717 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔHx = 89 + 419 + 121 − 349 = +280 kJ mol⁻¹. ΔHf = ΔHx + ΔHlatt, so ΔHlatt = −437 − 280 = −717 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This adds ΔHx to ΔHf instead of subtracting it.", "2": "This takes the electron affinity as +349 (ΔHx = 978).", "3": "Lattice energy, as defined here, is the formation of the solid from gaseous ions, which releases energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0290', 'Thermochemistry', 'Born–Haber cycle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 129 (§6.10, Sample Problem 6.7 method)', 'Calculate ΔHf of LiCl from: ΔHat(Li) = +161, IE₁(Li) = +520, ΔHat(Cl) = +121, EA₁(Cl) = −349 and lattice energy = −834 kJ mol⁻¹.', '["−381 kJ mol⁻¹", "+317 kJ mol⁻¹", "−834 kJ mol⁻¹", "−542 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔHf = ΔHat(Li) + IE₁ + ΔHat(Cl) + EA₁ + ΔHlatt = 161 + 520 + 121 − 349 − 834 = −381 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This uses +349 for the electron affinity.", "2": "This is the lattice energy alone; the other steps of the cycle must be added.", "3": "This leaves out the atomization of lithium."}'),
  ('CHE-0291', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 129 (§6.11)', 'Entropy is best described as', '["the heat content of a system.", "a measure of the randomness or disorder of a system, related to the number of ways energy can be distributed.", "the energy needed to start a reaction.", "the energy released when a bond forms."]', 1, 'The book defines entropy as a measure of the number of ways energy can be distributed within a system, which can also be thought of as its randomness or disorder.', '{"0": "Heat content is enthalpy, H.", "2": "That is activation energy.", "3": "That is (minus) bond energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0292', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 130 (§6.11.1)', 'Using the book''s formula xʸ, how many ways can 4 gas molecules be arranged among 3 connected jars?', '["12", "64", "81", "7"]', 2, 'The number of arrangements is xʸ, where x is the number of places and y is the number of particles. Here x = 3 and y = 4, so 3⁴ = 81.', '{"0": "This multiplies 3 × 4 instead of raising 3 to the power 4.", "1": "This is 4³, with the places and particles swapped.", "3": "This adds 3 + 4."}'),
  ('CHE-0293', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 130 (§6.11.2)', 'Diamond has a lower standard entropy than graphite because', '["diamond is much harder, and its stronger forces limit vibrations.", "diamond contains more atoms per mole.", "diamond is a gas at room temperature.", "graphite is a compound."]', 0, 'For substances of similar chemical nature, harder substances have lower entropy. Diamond is much harder than graphite; its stronger forces limit vibrations and decrease the probability of disorder.', '{"1": "One mole of either form contains the same number of carbon atoms.", "2": "Diamond is a solid.", "3": "Graphite and diamond are both forms of the element carbon."}'),
  ('CHE-0294', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 130 (§6.11.2)', 'Which order of standard entropy for water is correct?', '["ice > liquid water > water vapour", "liquid water > water vapour > ice", "ice < liquid water < water vapour", "water vapour < ice < liquid water"]', 2, 'A substance has lower entropy as a solid than as a liquid or gas. The book gives ice 48.0, liquid water 69.9 and water vapour 188.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order; solids have the lowest entropy.", "1": "Water vapour has the highest entropy of the three states.", "3": "Water vapour has the highest entropy, not the lowest."}'),
  ('CHE-0295', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy changes in reactions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 131 (§6.11.3)', 'Which reaction has a positive entropy change?', '["2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g)", "H₂O(g) → H₂O(l)", "NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s)", "2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g)"]', 3, 'Entropy increases when a gas is produced from solids. Decomposing solid KClO₃ gives 3 moles of O₂ gas, so ΔS is positive.', '{"0": "Three moles of gas become two, so entropy decreases.", "1": "Condensing a gas to a liquid decreases entropy.", "2": "Two gases combine to form a solid, so entropy decreases."}'),
  ('CHE-0296', 'Thermochemistry', 'Entropy changes in reactions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 130–131 (§6.11.4)', 'Using S°(H₂, g) = 130.6, S°(O₂, g) = 205.0 and S°(H₂O, l) = 69.9 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, ΔS°system for 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l) is', '["−326.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "+326.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "−265.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "−121.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔS = ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants) = 2(69.9) − [2(130.6) + 205.0] = 139.8 − 466.2 = −326.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Three moles of gas turning into liquid gives a large decrease.', '{"1": "This is reactants minus products, which gives the wrong sign.", "2": "This ignores the coefficients: 69.9 − (130.6 + 205.0).", "3": "This leaves out O₂: 139.8 − 261.2."}'),
  ('CHE-0297', 'Thermochemistry', 'Total entropy change', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 132 (§6.11.5)', 'A reaction has ΔHr = −92.0 kJ mol⁻¹ at 300 K. The entropy change of the surroundings is', '["+306.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "−306.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "+0.307 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹", "−27 600 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔSsurr = −ΔHr/T, with ΔH in J: −(−92 000) ÷ 300 = +306.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Heat given to the surroundings increases their entropy.', '{"1": "This drops the minus sign in the equation.", "2": "This leaves ΔH in kJ instead of multiplying by 1000.", "3": "This multiplies by T instead of dividing."}'),
  ('CHE-0298', 'Thermochemistry', 'Total entropy change', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 131–132 (§6.11.4–6.11.5)', 'For 2Ca(s) + O₂(g) → 2CaO(s), ΔSsystem is −208.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, yet calcium burns spontaneously in oxygen. This is because', '["the reaction is endothermic.", "the large amount of heat released makes ΔSsurroundings strongly positive, so ΔStotal is positive.", "ΔSsystem is always ignored for solids.", "a negative ΔSsystem always means a reaction is spontaneous."]', 1, 'Spontaneity depends on ΔStotal = ΔSsystem + ΔSsurroundings. The highly exothermic reaction gives ΔSsurr = +4262.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ at 298 K, so ΔStotal = +4054.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, which is positive.', '{"0": "The reaction is strongly exothermic (ΔH = −1270.2 kJ mol⁻¹).", "2": "ΔSsystem is part of ΔStotal and is not ignored.", "3": "Spontaneity requires a positive total entropy change, not a negative system entropy change."}'),
  ('CHE-0299', 'Thermochemistry', 'Gibbs free energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 133–134 (§6.12)', 'A reaction has ΔH = +40 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +120 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. At 400 K, ΔG and the conclusion are', '["+88 kJ mol⁻¹; non-spontaneous", "−8 kJ mol⁻¹; spontaneous", "+8 kJ mol⁻¹; non-spontaneous", "−47 960 kJ mol⁻¹; spontaneous"]', 1, 'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 40 − 400(0.120) = 40 − 48 = −8 kJ mol⁻¹. ΔG is negative, so the reaction can occur spontaneously at 400 K.', '{"0": "This adds TΔS instead of subtracting it.", "2": "This reverses the subtraction, TΔS − ΔH.", "3": "This uses ΔS in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ with ΔH in kJ; ΔS must be converted to 0.120 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0300', 'Thermochemistry', 'Gibbs free energy', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, pp. 133–134 (§6.12)', 'If ΔG for a process is zero,', '["the process is spontaneous.", "the reverse process is spontaneous.", "neither the process nor its reverse occurs spontaneously; the system is at equilibrium.", "the process is explosive."]', 2, 'The book states that when ΔG = 0, neither the indicated process nor its reverse can occur spontaneously; the system is in a state of equilibrium.', '{"0": "A spontaneous process needs ΔG < 0.", "1": "The reverse is spontaneous when ΔG > 0 for the forward process.", "3": "ΔG = 0 describes equilibrium, not a fast reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0301', 'Thermochemistry', 'Gibbs free energy', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 135 (Table 6.3)', 'A reaction has negative ΔH and negative ΔS. According to ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, it is', '["spontaneous at all temperatures.", "non-spontaneous at all temperatures.", "spontaneous at low temperature but non-spontaneous at high temperature.", "non-spontaneous at low temperature but spontaneous at high temperature."]', 2, 'With ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0, the −TΔS term is positive and grows with temperature. At low T, ΔH dominates and ΔG is negative; at high T, −TΔS dominates and ΔG becomes positive.', '{"0": "That applies when ΔH is negative and ΔS is positive.", "1": "That applies when ΔH is positive and ΔS is negative.", "3": "That applies when both ΔH and ΔS are positive."}'),
  ('CHE-0302', 'Thermochemistry', 'Gibbs free energy', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Chemical Energetics, p. 135 (Sample Problem 6.13)', 'For CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g), ΔH = +178.0 kJ and ΔS = +160.4 J K⁻¹. Assuming both are constant with temperature, above about what temperature does the decomposition become spontaneous?', '["298 K", "1.11 K", "1110 K", "1273 K"]', 2, 'The reaction becomes spontaneous when ΔG = ΔH − TΔS turns negative, that is when T > ΔH/ΔS = 178.0 ÷ 0.1604 ≈ 1110 K (about 837 °C).', '{"0": "At 298 K, ΔG = 178.0 − 47.8 = +130 kJ, so it is not spontaneous.", "1": "This divides kJ by J K⁻¹ without converting units (178.0 ÷ 160.4).", "3": "1273 K (1000 °C) is the temperature used in the book''s example, where ΔG is already negative; it is not the threshold."}'),
  ('CHE-0303', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Collision theory', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 141 (§7.1)', 'According to collision theory, a collision between reactant particles leads to products only if the particles', '["are of equal mass.", "are in the solid state.", "are moving in the same direction.", "collide with at least a certain minimum energy and with the proper orientation."]', 3, 'The book says effective collisions occur only when the colliding particles possess a certain minimum energy (the activation energy) and approach each other with the proper orientation. Otherwise they just bounce back.', '{"0": "Mass is not a condition for an effective collision.", "1": "Collision theory applies to particles in any phase; the book stresses a homogeneous mixture of reactants.", "2": "Particles moving in the same direction collide gently if at all; energy and orientation are what matter."}'),
  ('CHE-0304', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Collision theory', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 141 (§7.1)', 'Most chemical reactions are much slower than the rate at which their particles collide. This shows that', '["particles rarely collide.", "products are formed before the reactants collide.", "activation energy is zero for most reactions.", "not all collisions are effective."]', 3, 'The book notes that most reactions are slow, showing that not all collisions are equally effective; only collisions with enough energy and the right orientation give products.', '{"0": "Particles collide very frequently; the issue is how many collisions succeed.", "1": "Products form only as a result of effective collisions.", "2": "If activation energy were zero, nearly every collision would succeed and reactions would be fast."}'),
  ('CHE-0305', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate of reaction', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 141–142 (§7.2)', 'When concentration is in mol dm⁻³ and time is in seconds, the units of rate of reaction are', '["dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³", "s⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"]', 3, 'Rate is the change in concentration divided by the time taken, so its units are mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.', '{"0": "These are units of a second-order rate constant.", "1": "These are units of concentration, not of rate.", "2": "s⁻¹ is the unit of a first-order rate constant."}'),
  ('CHE-0306', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate of reaction', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 142 (§7.2)', 'In the expression rate = −Δ[A]/Δt = +Δ[B]/Δt for A → B, the negative sign shows that', '["the rate is negative.", "B is consumed during the reaction.", "the reaction is endothermic.", "the concentration of reactant A decreases with time."]', 3, 'The concentration of the reactant falls with time, so Δ[A] is negative; the minus sign makes the rate a positive quantity and shows that A is decreasing.', '{"0": "Rate is always reported as a positive quantity; the minus sign ensures this.", "1": "B is the product; its concentration increases, hence the positive sign.", "2": "The sign has nothing to do with the heat change."}'),
  ('CHE-0307', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate of reaction', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 142 (§7.2)', 'For a reaction between gases, the rate may be expressed using units of', '["temperature per unit time.", "mass per mole.", "volume only.", "pressure per unit time."]', 3, 'The book states that for a gas-phase reaction, units of pressure are used in place of molar concentrations.', '{"0": "Temperature change is not a measure of reaction rate here.", "1": "Mass per mole is a molar mass, not a rate.", "2": "Volume alone does not include time."}'),
  ('CHE-0308', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Instantaneous and average rate', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 142 (§7.2.1)', 'Over a chosen time interval of a reaction, how does the instantaneous rate compare with the average rate?', '["The instantaneous rate is always equal to the average rate.", "At the start of the interval the instantaneous rate is lower, and at the end it is higher.", "The instantaneous rate is always lower than the average rate.", "At the start of the interval the instantaneous rate is higher than the average rate, and at the end it is lower."]', 3, 'The book says the average and instantaneous rates are equal at only one instant in an interval. At first the instantaneous rate is higher than the average rate; by the end of the interval it is lower, because the reaction slows down.', '{"0": "They are equal at only one instant in the interval.", "1": "This is the reverse; the reaction slows as reactants are used up.", "2": "At the start of the interval the instantaneous rate is higher than the average."}'),
  ('CHE-0309', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate of reaction', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 143 (§7.2.1, Sample Problem 7.1 method)', 'The concentration of a product is 0.80 mol dm⁻³ after 2.0 min and 3.20 mol dm⁻³ after 8.0 min. The average rate of formation of the product over this interval is', '["0.40 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹", "0.53 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹", "0.30 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹", "1.6 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹"]', 0, 'Average rate = Δc/Δt = (3.20 − 0.80) ÷ (8.0 − 2.0) = 2.40 ÷ 6.0 = 0.40 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹.', '{"1": "This divides the final concentration by the time interval (3.20 ÷ 6.0) instead of using the change.", "2": "This divides the change by the final time (2.40 ÷ 8.0) instead of the interval.", "3": "This divides the final concentration by the initial time."}'),
  ('CHE-0310', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring rate', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 143 (Table 7.1)', 'In the decomposition of HI, [HI] falls from 0.100 mol dm⁻³ at 0 s to 0.0716 mol dm⁻³ at 50 s. The average rate of disappearance of HI in this interval is', '["5.68 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "1.43 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "2.84 × 10⁻² mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "2.00 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Rate = −Δ[HI]/Δt = (0.100 − 0.0716) ÷ 50 = 0.0284 ÷ 50 = 5.68 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.', '{"1": "This divides the concentration at 50 s by 50 (0.0716 ÷ 50) instead of using the change.", "2": "This is the change in concentration, not divided by the time.", "3": "This divides the initial concentration by 50."}'),
  ('CHE-0311', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring rate', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 143 (Table 7.1, Figure 7.2)', 'Using the data for HI decomposition in Table 7.1, in which 50-second interval is the average rate of reaction greatest?', '["0 to 50 s", "100 to 150 s", "200 to 250 s", "250 to 300 s"]', 0, '[HI] falls by 0.0284 mol dm⁻³ between 0 and 50 s, more than in any later interval (for example by only 0.0040 mol dm⁻³ between 250 and 300 s). The curve is steepest at the start because the reactant concentration is highest then.', '{"1": "[HI] falls by only 0.0101 mol dm⁻³ between 100 and 150 s.", "2": "[HI] falls by only 0.0051 mol dm⁻³ between 200 and 250 s.", "3": "This is the slowest interval listed; [HI] falls by only 0.0040 mol dm⁻³."}'),
  ('CHE-0312', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring rate', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 143 (§7.2.2)', 'On a graph of reactant concentration against time, the rate of reaction at a particular instant is found from the', '["slope of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time.", "highest point of the curve.", "area under the curve.", "x-intercept of the curve."]', 0, 'To find the rate at a given time (for example at 100 s), a tangent is drawn to the curve at that time; its slope gives the rate at that instant.', '{"1": "The highest point is the initial concentration.", "2": "The area under a concentration–time graph does not give the rate.", "3": "The intercept does not give the rate at a chosen time."}'),
  ('CHE-0313', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring concentration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 144–145 (§7.2.3)', 'In following the acid hydrolysis of ethyl acetate by the chemical method, a sample of the reaction mixture is run into about four times its volume of ice-cold water. The purpose of this step is to', '["speed up the hydrolysis.", "remove the catalyst by precipitation.", "neutralize the acetic acid.", "stop the reaction by dilution and chilling before the acid formed is titrated."]', 3, 'The book explains that dilution and chilling stop the reaction. The acid formed is then titrated against a standard alkali such as NaOH using phenolphthalein.', '{"0": "Cooling and diluting slow the reaction down; they do not speed it up.", "1": "No precipitation occurs; the acid catalyst remains in solution.", "2": "Neutralization happens in the titration with NaOH, not in the ice-cold water."}'),
  ('CHE-0314', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 145 (§7.2.3)', 'Spectrophotometry (colorimetry) can be used to follow the rate of a reaction when', '["all the species are colourless and do not absorb radiation.", "the reaction gives off a gas.", "a reactant or a product absorbs ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation.", "the reaction occurs in the solid state only."]', 2, 'The book says spectrophotometry applies when a reactant or product absorbs UV, visible or IR radiation; the rate is measured from the amount of radiation absorbed.', '{"0": "If nothing absorbs radiation, spectrophotometry cannot follow the reaction.", "1": "A reaction giving off a gas is best followed by the volume change method.", "3": "The method is based on absorption of radiation by a species, not on the solid state."}'),
  ('CHE-0315', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 145 (§7.2.3)', 'The electrical conductivity method is suitable for following the rate of a reaction that', '["has no change in concentration.", "involves only non-polar molecules.", "occurs entirely in the gas phase.", "involves ions whose concentration changes."]', 3, 'The rate of a reaction involving ions can be studied by electrical conductivity, since conductivity depends on the concentration of ions being used up or formed.', '{"0": "Any method needs a measurable change in concentration.", "1": "Non-polar molecules do not conduct, so conductivity would not change.", "2": "Gas-phase reactions involving volume changes are followed by the volume change method."}'),
  ('CHE-0316', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 145 (§7.2.3)', 'The rate of the reaction CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g) can most conveniently be followed by', '["titrating the CaCO₃.", "spectrophotometry of a coloured product.", "measuring the volume of gas given off at regular intervals.", "measuring the boiling point of the mixture."]', 2, 'The volume change method is used for reactions in which a gas is given off; the volume of CO₂ collected is proportional to the extent of reaction.', '{"0": "CaCO₃ is a solid reactant and cannot simply be titrated during the reaction.", "1": "None of the species is coloured, so spectrophotometry is not convenient.", "3": "Boiling point is not used to follow reaction rate."}'),
  ('CHE-0317', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Measuring concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 145 (Interesting information)', 'The rates of very fast reactions can be monitored using', '["distillation.", "simple titration.", "weighing the reaction vessel once a day.", "stopped-flow spectrophotometry."]', 3, 'In stopped-flow spectrophotometry, small volumes of reactants are driven at high speed into a mixing chamber and then into an observation cell, where progress is monitored, usually by UV transmission.', '{"0": "Distillation is a separation technique, not a rate measurement.", "1": "Titration is too slow for very fast reactions.", "2": "Daily weighing could follow only very slow reactions."}'),
  ('CHE-0318', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Factors affecting rate', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 146 (§7.3.1)', 'Increasing the concentration of a reactant increases the rate of reaction because', '["the activation energy decreases.", "the reaction becomes exothermic.", "there are more collisions between reacting particles per unit time.", "the particles become heavier."]', 2, 'The book explains that a higher concentration results in more collisions between the reacting particles, which speeds up the reaction.', '{"0": "Concentration does not change the activation energy; a catalyst does that.", "1": "Concentration does not change ΔH.", "3": "Particle mass is unchanged by concentration."}'),
  ('CHE-0319', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Factors affecting rate', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 146 (Did you know?)', 'Increasing the pressure increases the rate of reaction only when', '["the reactants are solids.", "the reactants are gases, because higher pressure raises their concentration.", "the reactants are liquids.", "a catalyst is absent."]', 1, 'For gaseous reactants, increasing pressure increases their concentration and therefore the rate. Pressure has no effect when the reactants are solids or liquids.', '{"0": "Pressure has no effect on the rate when the reactants are solids.", "2": "Pressure has no effect on the rate when the reactants are liquids.", "3": "The pressure effect does not depend on whether a catalyst is present."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0320', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Temperature and rate', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 146 (§7.3.2)', 'The rate of a reaction doubles for every 10 °C rise in temperature. If the temperature is raised from 25 °C to 55 °C, the rate becomes about', '["3 times the original rate.", "6 times the original rate.", "8 times the original rate.", "30 times the original rate."]', 2, 'A 30 °C rise is three 10 °C steps. Doubling three times gives 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 times the original rate.', '{"0": "This counts the number of 10 °C steps instead of doubling at each step.", "1": "This multiplies 2 by 3 instead of raising 2 to the power 3.", "3": "The rate does not increase in proportion to the number of degrees."}'),
  ('CHE-0321', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Temperature and rate', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 147 (§7.3.2, Figure 7.7)', 'When the temperature of a reacting gas is raised, its Boltzmann distribution curve', '["becomes taller and narrower, with the peak shifted to the left.", "does not change.", "flattens, and the peak shifts to the right.", "moves entirely to energies above Ea."]', 2, 'At a higher temperature the average kinetic energy increases. The Boltzmann curve flattens and its peak shifts to the right, so more molecules have high energies.', '{"0": "That describes cooling, not heating.", "1": "The distribution of molecular energies changes with temperature.", "3": "Some molecules still have low energies; only the fraction above Ea increases."}'),
  ('CHE-0322', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Temperature and rate', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 147 (§7.3.2)', 'On a Boltzmann distribution curve, the shaded area to the right of the activation energy Ea represents', '["the total number of molecules in the sample.", "the number of molecules with energy equal to or greater than Ea, which can react on collision.", "the average kinetic energy of the molecules.", "the enthalpy change of the reaction."]', 1, 'The shaded area under the curve beyond Ea shows the number of particles with energy greater than the activation energy, the ones able to react when they collide. For a 10 °C rise this area approximately doubles.', '{"0": "The total area under the whole curve gives the total number of molecules.", "2": "Average kinetic energy is not represented by an area beyond Ea.", "3": "ΔH is not shown on a Boltzmann distribution curve."}'),
  ('CHE-0323', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 147 (§7.3.3)', 'A catalyst is a substance that', '["is used up completely during the reaction.", "alters the rate of a reaction but remains chemically unchanged at the end.", "always slows down a reaction.", "changes the products formed."]', 1, 'The book defines a catalyst as a substance that alters the rate of a chemical reaction but remains chemically unchanged at the end; it is often present in very small amounts.', '{"0": "A catalyst is not consumed; it is chemically unchanged at the end.", "2": "Catalysts are used to speed reactions up; the definition says they alter the rate.", "3": "A catalyst changes how fast products form, not what they are."}'),
  ('CHE-0324', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 148 (§7.3.3)', 'The decomposition of potassium chlorate, 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂, is speeded up by a small amount of', '["Pt", "MnO₂", "CuCl₂", "Ni"]', 1, 'The book states that KClO₃ decomposes much more rapidly in the presence of a small amount of MnO₂.', '{"0": "Platinum catalyses the reaction of H₂ with O₂ and the oxidation of NH₃.", "2": "CuCl₂ catalyses the oxidation of HCl to Cl₂.", "3": "Nickel catalyses the hydrogenation of unsaturated compounds."}'),
  ('CHE-0325', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 148 (§7.3.3)', 'In the oxidation of HCl to chlorine, 4HCl + O₂ → 2H₂O + 2Cl₂, the catalyst named in the book is', '["Fe", "MnO₂", "V₂O₅", "CuCl₂"]', 3, 'The book gives CuCl₂ as the catalyst for oxidizing HCl to Cl₂.', '{"0": "Iron is not the catalyst given for this reaction.", "1": "MnO₂ is the catalyst for decomposing KClO₃.", "2": "V₂O₅ is the solid catalyst for oxidizing SO₂ to SO₃."}'),
  ('CHE-0326', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 148 (§7.3.3, Figure 7.8)', 'A catalyst increases the rate of a reaction because it', '["increases the average kinetic energy of the molecules.", "makes ΔH more negative.", "provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy, so more molecules can cross the barrier.", "increases the concentration of the reactants."]', 2, 'A catalyst provides a new reaction path with a lower activation energy. A greater number of molecules then have enough energy to get over the barrier, and the rate increases.', '{"0": "Raising the temperature increases kinetic energy; a catalyst does not.", "1": "A catalyst does not change the enthalpies of reactants and products, so ΔH is unchanged.", "3": "A catalyst does not change reactant concentrations."}'),
  ('CHE-0327', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 148–149 (§7.3.3)', 'Which is an example of homogeneous catalysis?', '["Hydrogenation of ethene over finely divided nickel", "Hydrolysis of ethyl ethanoate in the presence of dilute H₂SO₄", "Oxidation of NH₃ to NO over platinum gauze", "Oxidation of SO₂ to SO₃ over solid V₂O₅"]', 1, 'In homogeneous catalysis the catalyst and reactants are in the same phase. In ester hydrolysis both the reactants and the acid catalyst are in solution.', '{"0": "Solid nickel with gaseous reactants is heterogeneous catalysis.", "2": "Solid platinum with gaseous reactants is heterogeneous catalysis.", "3": "Solid V₂O₅ with gaseous reactants is heterogeneous catalysis."}'),
  ('CHE-0328', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 149 (§7.3.3)', 'In the industrial oxidation of ammonia to NO for making nitric acid, 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g), the catalyst and conditions given in the book are', '["dilute H₂SO₄ at 25 °C", "nickel at 150 °C", "MnO₂ at room temperature", "platinum gauze at about 800 °C"]', 3, 'The book gives this as heterogeneous catalysis: platinum gauze at 800 °C converts NH₃ to NO, which is used to make HNO₃.', '{"0": "Dilute acid catalyses ester hydrolysis.", "1": "Nickel at 150 °C is used for hydrogenating ethene.", "2": "MnO₂ catalyses the decomposition of KClO₃."}'),
  ('CHE-0329', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 149 (Did you know?)', 'Which statement about enzymes is correct?', '["They increase the activation energy of reactions.", "Their activity does not depend on pH or temperature.", "They are typically proteins that lower the activation energy of biochemical reactions.", "They are consumed in the reactions they catalyse."]', 2, 'Enzymes are biochemical catalysts, typically proteins, that lower the activation energy required for a reaction. Their activity is influenced by pH, temperature and substrate concentration.', '{"0": "Catalysts, including enzymes, lower the activation energy.", "1": "The book states pH and temperature influence enzyme activity.", "3": "Like all catalysts, enzymes are not used up."}'),
  ('CHE-0330', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Catalysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 149 (Interesting information)', 'Some vitamins act in biochemical reactions as coenzymes. The vitamin mentioned in the book as necessary for blood clotting is', '["vitamin D", "vitamin K", "vitamin C", "vitamin A"]', 1, 'The book gives vitamin K as necessary for blood clotting; low levels can cause bleeding diathesis.', '{"0": "Vitamin D is not the example given for clotting.", "2": "Vitamin C is not the example given for clotting.", "3": "Vitamin A is not the example given for clotting."}'),
  ('CHE-0331', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate law and order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 150 (§7.4.1)', 'In the rate equation rate = k[A]ˣ[B]ʸ for aA + bB → cC + dD, the exponents x and y', '["are always 1.", "are always equal to a and b.", "are determined experimentally and may or may not equal a and b.", "can be found only from the balanced equation."]', 2, 'The book stresses that x and y are found from experimental data and may or may not equal the coefficients in the balanced equation.', '{"0": "Exponents can be 0, 1, 2, 3 or fractions.", "1": "The exponents need not match the coefficients; for example 2FeCl₃ + 6KI is only third order.", "3": "The order cannot be inferred simply from the balanced equation."}'),
  ('CHE-0332', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate law and order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 150 (§7.4.1)', 'The rate constant k of a reaction is', '["the rate of reaction when the concentrations of all reactants are unity, and it changes with temperature.", "independent of temperature.", "always equal to the initial rate.", "the time taken for half the reactant to be used up."]', 0, 'Setting [A] = [B] = 1 mol dm⁻³ makes rate = k, so the specific rate constant is the rate when reactant concentrations are unity. It is constant under given conditions but changes with temperature.', '{"1": "The book states k changes with temperature.", "2": "The initial rate equals k only if all concentrations happen to be unity.", "3": "That is the half-life."}'),
  ('CHE-0333', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate law and order', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 150–151 (§7.4.2)', 'For a reaction with rate = k[X][Y]², the concentration of X is halved and the concentration of Y is tripled. The rate becomes', '["1.5 times the original", "4.5 times the original", "9 times the original", "3 times the original"]', 1, 'New rate ∝ (½)(3)² = ½ × 9 = 4.5, so the rate becomes 4.5 times the original.', '{"0": "This treats the reaction as first order in Y: ½ × 3.", "2": "This ignores the halving of [X].", "3": "This ignores the halving of X and treats Y as first order."}'),
  ('CHE-0334', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Types of reaction order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 151 (§7.4.3)', 'The photochemical reaction H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g) in sunlight has rate = k. The reaction is', '["first order", "zero order", "second order", "third order"]', 1, 'Its rate is independent of the concentrations of H₂ and Cl₂, rate = k[H₂]⁰[Cl₂]⁰, so it is zero order. The book notes that photochemical reactions are usually zero order.', '{"0": "A first-order reaction would have rate proportional to one concentration.", "2": "A second-order reaction would have rate = k[H₂][Cl₂] or similar.", "3": "The rate does not depend on any concentration here."}'),
  ('CHE-0335', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Types of reaction order', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 152 (§7.4.3)', 'The decomposition 2N₂O₅(g) → 2N₂O₄(g) + O₂(g) has the experimental rate equation rate = k[N₂O₅]. Its order is', '["first", "zero", "second", "third"]', 0, 'The exponent of [N₂O₅] in the rate equation is 1, so the reaction is first order even though the balanced equation has a coefficient of 2.', '{"1": "The rate depends on [N₂O₅].", "2": "The coefficient 2 in the equation does not set the order; the experimental exponent is 1.", "3": "The rate equation has a single first-power term."}'),
  ('CHE-0336', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Types of reaction order', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 152 (§7.4.3)', 'The reaction 2FeCl₃(aq) + 6KI(aq) → 2FeI₂(aq) + 6KCl(aq) + I₂ involves eight reactant formula units but is found to be third order, with rate = k[FeCl₃][KI]². This shows that', '["the order of a reaction must be found experimentally and cannot be read from the balanced equation.", "all reactions with eight reactant units are third order.", "FeCl₃ is a catalyst.", "the reaction is zero order in KI."]', 0, 'The sum of the coefficients is 8, but the experimental order is 1 + 2 = 3. Order is an experimentally determined quantity, not inferred from the equation.', '{"1": "There is no rule linking the number of reactant units to the order.", "2": "FeCl₃ is a reactant, and it appears in the rate equation.", "3": "The rate equation shows second order in KI."}'),
  ('CHE-0337', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Types of reaction order', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 152–153 (§7.4.3)', 'For CHCl₃ + Cl₂ → CCl₄ + HCl, rate = k[CHCl₃][Cl₂]^½. The overall order and the type of reaction that often shows such orders are', '["2; ionic reactions", "1.5; reactions involving free radicals", "0.5; photochemical reactions", "3; termolecular reactions"]', 1, 'Overall order = 1 + ½ = 1.5, a fractional order. The book notes that reactions involving free radicals frequently show fractional orders.', '{"0": "The order is 1 + ½, not 2, and the book links fractional orders to free radicals.", "2": "This counts only the Cl₂ exponent; photochemical reactions are described as usually zero order.", "3": "The exponents sum to 1.5, not 3."}'),
  ('CHE-0338', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Units of rate constant', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 153 (§7.4.4)', 'The units of the rate constant for a third-order reaction are', '["dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹", "dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹", "s⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"]', 0, 'kₙ = (mol dm⁻³)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹. For n = 3, k = (mol dm⁻³)⁻² s⁻¹ = dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹.', '{"1": "dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ is the unit for a second-order rate constant.", "2": "s⁻¹ is the unit for a first-order rate constant.", "3": "mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ is the unit for a zero-order rate constant."}'),
  ('CHE-0339', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Units of rate constant', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 153 (§7.4.4)', 'A rate constant has the units mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. The order of the reaction is', '["zero", "first", "second", "third"]', 0, 'Using kₙ = (mol dm⁻³)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹, the units mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ require 1 − n = 1, so n = 0. A zero-order rate constant has the same units as the rate.', '{"1": "First-order k has units s⁻¹.", "2": "Second-order k has units dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹.", "3": "Third-order k has units dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0340', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Determining rate constant', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 154 (§7.5.1)', 'For a reaction with rate = k[A][B], the initial rate is 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ when [A] = 0.20 mol dm⁻³ and [B] = 0.10 mol dm⁻³. The rate constant is', '["2.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹", "8.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹", "2.0 × 10⁻³ dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹", "5.0 × 10² dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹"]', 2, 'k = rate ÷ ([A][B]) = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ (0.20 × 0.10) = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.020 = 2.0 × 10⁻³. For a second-order reaction the units are dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The number is right but the units are those of a first-order constant; this reaction is second order overall.", "1": "This multiplies the rate by the concentrations instead of dividing.", "3": "This inverts the division: 0.020 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻⁵)."}'),
  ('CHE-0341', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Determining rate constant', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 155 (Table 7.2)', 'For H₂O₂ + 2I⁻ + 2H⁺ → 2H₂O + I₂, the rate equation is rate = k[H₂O₂][I⁻]. When calculating k, the concentration of H⁺ is ignored because', '["the reaction is zero order with respect to H⁺, so [H⁺] does not appear in the rate equation.", "H⁺ is not a reactant.", "[H⁺] is always 1 mol dm⁻³.", "H⁺ is a product."]', 0, 'The book explains that [H⁺] is ignored because it does not appear in the rate equation; the reaction is zero order with respect to H⁺.', '{"1": "H⁺ is a reactant in the balanced equation.", "2": "The experiments in Table 7.2 use [H⁺] of 0.0100 and 0.0200 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "H⁺ is consumed, not produced."}'),
  ('CHE-0342', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 155 (§7.5.2)', 'A first-order reaction has a rate constant of 1.155 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. Its half-life is', '["10 minutes", "600 minutes", "14.4 minutes", "0.17 minutes"]', 0, 'For a first-order reaction, t½ = 0.693/k = 0.693 ÷ (1.155 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹) = 600 s = 10 minutes.', '{"1": "600 is the half-life in seconds, not minutes.", "2": "This uses t½ = 1/k (866 s), leaving out the factor 0.693.", "3": "This divides 600 s by 60 twice."}'),
  ('CHE-0343', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 155 (§7.5.2)', 'A first-order reaction has a half-life of 15 minutes. How long does it take for the reactant concentration to fall from 0.80 mol dm⁻³ to 0.10 mol dm⁻³?', '["45 minutes", "30 minutes", "15 minutes", "105 minutes"]', 0, '0.80 → 0.40 → 0.20 → 0.10 is three halvings. Each takes one half-life, so the time is 3 × 15 = 45 minutes.', '{"1": "Two half-lives take it to 0.20 mol dm⁻³.", "2": "One half-life only takes the concentration to 0.40 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "This assumes the concentration falls by 0.10 mol dm⁻³ every 15 minutes (7 steps), which is not first-order behaviour."}'),
  ('CHE-0344', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Reaction mechanism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 156 (§7.6)', 'The molecularity of an elementary step is', '["the sum of the exponents in the overall rate equation.", "the number of products formed.", "the number of reactant molecules (atoms or ions) taking part in that step.", "always equal to the overall order."]', 2, 'Each elementary step is caused by a collision of particles, and the number of reactant particles involved in that step is its molecularity.', '{"0": "That is the order of reaction, an experimental quantity.", "1": "Molecularity counts reactant particles, not products.", "3": "Molecularity refers to a single step; overall order need not match it."}'),
  ('CHE-0345', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Reaction mechanism', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 156 (§7.6)', 'Which elementary step is termolecular?', '["N₂O₅(g) → NO₂(g) + NO₃(g)", "2O₂(g) + O(g) → O₃(g) + O₂(g)", "NO(g) + O₃(g) → NO₂(g) + O₂(g)", "CO(g) + NO₃(g) → NO₂(g) + CO₂(g)"]', 1, 'A termolecular step involves three molecules reacting simultaneously, which is rare. The book''s example is two O₂ molecules and one O atom forming ozone.', '{"0": "One reactant molecule: unimolecular.", "2": "Two reactant molecules: bimolecular.", "3": "Two reactant molecules: bimolecular."}'),
  ('CHE-0346', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Reaction mechanism', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, pp. 157–158 (§7.6)', 'For 2NO₂(g) + F₂(g) → 2NO₂F(g), the accepted mechanism is: Step 1 (slow) NO₂ + F₂ → NO₂F + F; Step 2 (fast) NO₂ + F → NO₂F. Which species is an intermediate?', '["F₂", "F", "NO₂F", "NO₂"]', 1, 'Intermediates are produced in one step and consumed in a later step, so they do not appear in the overall equation. The F atom is formed in step 1 and used up in step 2.', '{"0": "F₂ is a reactant in the overall equation.", "2": "NO₂F is the final product.", "3": "NO₂ is a reactant in the overall equation."}'),
  ('CHE-0347', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate determining step', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 157 (§7.6)', 'For 2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → 2H₂O(g) + N₂(g), the proposed mechanism is: Step 1 (slow) 2NO + H₂ → N₂ + H₂O₂; Step 2 (fast) H₂O₂ + H₂ → 2H₂O. The rate equation consistent with this mechanism is', '["rate = k[NO]²[H₂]²", "rate = k[H₂O₂][H₂]", "rate = k[NO]²[H₂]", "rate = k[NO][H₂]"]', 2, 'The slow step determines the rate, and all reactants in it appear in the rate equation: rate = k[NO]²[H₂]. Step 2 occurs after the rate-determining step and does not affect the rate. This matches the experiments, where doubling [NO] quadruples the rate and doubling [H₂] doubles it.', '{"0": "This takes the exponents from the overall balanced equation.", "1": "This is the rate of the fast second step, which does not control the rate.", "3": "Step 1 involves two NO molecules, so the rate depends on [NO]²."}'),
  ('CHE-0348', 'Reaction kinetics', 'Rate determining step', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Reaction Kinetics, p. 158 (Quick Check 7.9 mechanism)', 'For H₂O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2H₂O + I₂, the mechanism has a slow first step H₂O₂ + I⁻ → H₂O + IO⁻, followed by fast steps involving H⁺. Why does H⁺ not appear in the rate equation rate = k[H₂O₂][I⁻]?', '["H⁺ is a catalyst in the slow step.", "H⁺ is an intermediate.", "H⁺ takes part only in steps after the rate-determining step.", "H⁺ is present in very small amounts."]', 2, 'Species that react only after the rate-determining step do not appear in the rate expression. H⁺ is involved only in the fast steps that follow the slow step, so the rate depends only on H₂O₂ and I⁻.', '{"0": "H⁺ does not appear in the slow step at all, and it is consumed overall.", "1": "H⁺ is a reactant in the overall equation, not an intermediate.", "3": "Its amount is not the reason; it is absent from the slow step."}'),
  ('CHE-0349', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Reversible reactions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 164', 'An irreversible reaction stops when', '["the forward and reverse rates become equal.", "the temperature is raised.", "a catalyst is added.", "the limiting reactant is used up."]', 3, 'Irreversible reactions go to completion, with reactants converted into products, and they stop when the limiting reactant is used up.', '{"0": "Equal forward and reverse rates describe the equilibrium of a reversible reaction.", "1": "Raising the temperature usually speeds a reaction up.", "2": "A catalyst changes the rate; it does not stop a reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0350', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Macroscopic and microscopic events', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 164 (§8.1)', 'Which of the following is a microscopic event in a chemical reaction?', '["A change in colour", "The formation of a precipitate", "The breaking and forming of bonds between atoms", "The evolution of a gas"]', 2, 'Microscopic events cannot be seen with the naked eye; examples are molecular collisions, bond breaking and forming, rearrangement of atoms, and electron transfer.', '{"0": "A colour change is visible, so it is a macroscopic event.", "1": "A precipitate can be seen, so its formation is macroscopic.", "3": "Gas evolution is observable, so it is macroscopic."}'),
  ('CHE-0351', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Dynamic equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 164–166 (§8.2)', 'At chemical equilibrium,', '["the forward and reverse reactions have stopped.", "only the forward reaction continues.", "the concentrations of reactants and products are always equal.", "the rates of the forward and reverse reactions are equal, and the concentrations of reactants and products stay constant."]', 3, 'Equilibrium is dynamic: individual molecules keep reacting in both directions, but the forward and reverse rates are equal, so the composition of the mixture no longer changes.', '{"0": "At the microscopic level the reactions never stop; the book stresses this is a dynamic, not static, state.", "1": "Both reactions continue at equal rates.", "2": "Figure 8.2 shows that at equilibrium [reactant] may be equal to, less than or greater than [product]; only the concentrations being constant is required."}'),
  ('CHE-0352', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Dynamic equilibrium', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 165 (§8.2)', 'Equal amounts of steam and carbon monoxide are mixed in a closed vessel: H₂O(g) + CO(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + CO₂(g). Which describes the rates as the system moves towards equilibrium?', '["Both rates stay constant from the beginning.", "Both rates start at zero and increase together.", "The forward rate increases while the reverse rate decreases.", "The forward rate starts at its maximum and decreases, while the reverse rate starts low and increases until the two are equal."]', 3, 'Collisions between H₂O and CO are most frequent at the start, so the forward rate is maximum then and falls as they are used up. As H₂ and CO₂ build up, the reverse rate rises from a low value. Equilibrium is reached when the two rates are equal.', '{"0": "The rates change until equilibrium is established.", "1": "The forward reaction starts at its maximum rate, since the reactant concentrations are highest at the start.", "2": "This is backwards: the forward rate falls as reactants are consumed."}'),
  ('CHE-0353', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium between physical states', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 166 (§8.4)', 'Which is an example of dynamic equilibrium between two physical states?', '["Dissolving sodium metal in water", "Iron rusting in moist air", "Burning of methane in air", "Ice and water coexisting at 0 °C"]', 3, 'At 0 °C ice and water coexist in equilibrium: water changes into ice while ice changes back into water at the same rate.', '{"0": "Sodium reacting with water is an irreversible chemical reaction.", "1": "Rusting is an irreversible chemical change.", "2": "Combustion of methane is an irreversible chemical reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0354', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium between physical states', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 167 (§8.4)', 'A liquid is kept in a closed container at constant temperature. When its vapour pressure stops changing,', '["evaporation has stopped completely.", "condensation has stopped completely.", "the rate of evaporation equals the rate of condensation.", "all the liquid has evaporated."]', 2, 'Evaporation and condensation both continue. Their rates eventually become equal, a dynamic equilibrium is reached and the vapour pressure stays constant at that temperature.', '{"0": "Molecules continue to escape from the surface; the vapour pressure is constant only because they return at the same rate.", "1": "Vapour molecules keep returning to the liquid.", "3": "If all the liquid evaporated there would be no liquid–vapour equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-0355', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium between physical states', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 167 (§8.4)', 'When the temperature of a liquid in equilibrium with its vapour in a closed container is raised, a new equilibrium is reached in which', '["the vapour pressure is lower.", "evaporation no longer occurs.", "the vapour pressure is unchanged.", "the vapour pressure is higher."]', 3, 'Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy, so the rate of evaporation increases. A new dynamic equilibrium is reached with a higher equilibrium vapour pressure.', '{"0": "More molecules escape at higher temperature, so the vapour pressure rises.", "1": "Evaporation and condensation both continue, at higher rates.", "2": "Vapour pressure is constant only at a given temperature; it changes when the temperature changes."}'),
  ('CHE-0356', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Conditions for equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 167 (§8.5, Figure 8.3)', 'When CaCO₃ is heated in an open container, equilibrium CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g) is not established because', '["CaCO₃ does not decompose.", "CaO is a gas.", "CO₂ escapes, so the reverse reaction cannot occur to the required extent.", "the reaction is irreversible at all temperatures."]', 2, 'Equilibrium can be established only in a closed vessel from which no reactant or product can escape. In an open container the CO₂ escapes, so equilibrium cannot be reached.', '{"0": "CaCO₃ does decompose on heating.", "1": "CaO is a solid.", "3": "In a closed vessel the same reaction does reach equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-0357', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Characteristics of equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 168 (§8.6)', 'Which statement about chemical equilibrium is correct?', '["It can be reached only by starting with the reactants.", "A catalyst increases the equilibrium constant.", "The equilibrium constant depends on the initial concentrations.", "It can be approached from either side, starting with reactants or with products."]', 3, 'The book lists as a feature of equilibrium that it can be approached from either side, whether we start with reactants or products.', '{"0": "Equilibrium can equally be reached by starting with the products.", "1": "A catalyst changes neither the equilibrium position nor the equilibrium constant.", "2": "The equilibrium constant does not depend on initial concentrations; it depends only on temperature."}'),
  ('CHE-0358', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Characteristics of equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 168 and 178 (§8.6, §8.15)', 'Adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction', '["increases the equilibrium yield of products.", "changes the value of Kc.", "lowers the activation energy for both forward and reverse reactions, so equilibrium is reached sooner without changing its position.", "shifts the equilibrium towards the reactants."]', 2, 'A catalyst provides a path of lower activation energy, lowering the maximum of the energy curve for both directions (Figure 8.4). Equilibrium is reached faster, but the equilibrium position, the yield and Kc are unchanged.', '{"0": "The yield at equilibrium stays the same.", "1": "Kc changes only with temperature.", "3": "A catalyst does not shift the position of equilibrium in either direction."}'),
  ('CHE-0359', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Characteristics of equilibrium', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 168 (Keep in mind)', 'The correct Kc expression for C(s) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO(g) + H₂(g) is', '["Kc = [CO][H₂] / ([C][H₂O])", "Kc = [H₂O] / ([CO][H₂])", "Kc = [CO][H₂] / [H₂O]", "Kc = [CO][H₂]"]', 2, 'Concentrations of pure solids and pure liquids are not included in the equilibrium constant expression. Leaving out C(s), Kc = [CO][H₂]/[H₂O].', '{"0": "Solid carbon should not appear in the expression.", "1": "This is the expression for the reverse reaction.", "3": "H₂O is a gas here, so its concentration must appear in the denominator."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0360', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Types of equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 168 (§8.7)', 'Which is an example of a heterogeneous equilibrium?', '["N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)", "2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)", "3Fe(s) + 4H₂O(g) ⇌ Fe₃O₄(s) + 4H₂(g)", "CH₃COOH(l) + C₂H₅OH(l) ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅(l) + H₂O(l)"]', 2, 'In a heterogeneous equilibrium the reactants and products are in more than one phase. Here iron and Fe₃O₄ are solids while steam and hydrogen are gases.', '{"0": "All species are gases, so this is homogeneous.", "1": "All species are gases, so this is homogeneous.", "3": "All species are in the liquid phase, so the book lists this as homogeneous."}'),
  ('CHE-0361', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Law of mass action', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 169 (§8.8)', 'The law of mass action, proposed by Guldberg and Waage, states that the rate of a reaction is directly proportional to the', '["masses of the products.", "molar masses of the reactants.", "volume of the container.", "active masses (molar concentrations) of the reactants, each raised to its coefficient in the balanced equation."]', 3, 'The rate of a chemical reaction is directly proportional to the active masses of the reactants raised to their coefficients; active mass means molar concentration in mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "The law concerns the reactants'' concentrations, not product masses.", "1": "Active mass means molar concentration, not molar mass.", "2": "Volume does not appear in the law directly."}'),
  ('CHE-0362', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Law of mass action', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 170 (§8.8)', 'For a reversible reaction A + B ⇌ C + D, the forward rate constant is 4.0 × 10⁻² and the reverse rate constant is 2.0 × 10⁻³ at the same temperature. The equilibrium constant Kc is', '["8.0 × 10⁻⁵", "0.05", "20", "4.2 × 10⁻²"]', 2, 'At equilibrium kf[A][B] = kr[C][D], so Kc = kf/kr = (4.0 × 10⁻²) ÷ (2.0 × 10⁻³) = 20.', '{"0": "This multiplies the rate constants instead of dividing.", "1": "This is kr/kf, the inverse ratio.", "3": "This adds them; Kc is a ratio."}'),
  ('CHE-0363', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 170 (§8.8.1)', 'The equilibrium constant expression for 2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is', '["Kc = [NO₂]² / ([NO]²[O₂])", "Kc = [NO₂] / ([NO][O₂])", "Kc = [NO]²[O₂] / [NO₂]²", "Kc = 2[NO₂] / (2[NO] + [O₂])"]', 0, 'Kc is the product of the product concentrations raised to their coefficients divided by the product of the reactant concentrations raised to their coefficients: [NO₂]²/([NO]²[O₂]).', '{"1": "The coefficients must appear as powers.", "2": "This is the expression for the reverse reaction.", "3": "Coefficients are powers, not multipliers, and concentrations are multiplied, not added."}'),
  ('CHE-0364', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 170–171 (Sample Problem 8.1 method)', 'A 2.0 dm³ equilibrium mixture for CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O contains 0.20 mol acid, 0.10 mol ethanol, 0.30 mol ester and 0.30 mol water. Kc is', '["1.1", "0.22", "4.5", "15"]', 2, 'Concentrations: acid 0.10, ethanol 0.050, ester 0.15, water 0.15 mol dm⁻³. Kc = (0.15 × 0.15) ÷ (0.10 × 0.050) = 0.0225 ÷ 0.0050 = 4.5. The volume cancels because the moles on each side are equal.', '{"0": "This uses concentrations for the products but moles for the reactants.", "1": "This is the inverse, reactants over products.", "3": "This leaves water out of the expression: 0.30 ÷ (0.20 × 0.10)."}'),
  ('CHE-0365', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium constant calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 171 (Sample Problem 8.2 method)', 'For A(aq) + B(aq) ⇌ C(aq), a solution starts with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ each of A and B. At equilibrium [C] = 0.060 mol dm⁻³. Kc is', '["37.5 dm³ mol⁻¹", "6.0 dm³ mol⁻¹", "1.5", "0.027 mol dm⁻³"]', 0, 'Each mole of C formed uses one mole each of A and B, so [A] = [B] = 0.100 − 0.060 = 0.040 mol dm⁻³. Kc = 0.060 ÷ (0.040 × 0.040) = 37.5 dm³ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "This uses the initial concentrations of A and B instead of the equilibrium ones.", "2": "This divides by only one reactant concentration.", "3": "This is the inverse, with units to match."}'),
  ('CHE-0366', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Units of equilibrium constant', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 172 (§8.8.2)', 'The units of Kc for PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g) are', '["no units", "dm⁶ mol⁻²", "dm³ mol⁻¹", "mol dm⁻³"]', 3, 'Kc = [PCl₃][Cl₂]/[PCl₅] has units (mol dm⁻³)(mol dm⁻³)/(mol dm⁻³) = mol dm⁻³. Units cancel only when the moles on each side are equal.', '{"0": "Units cancel only when the total moles of products and reactants are equal; here there are 2 on the right and 1 on the left.", "1": "dm⁶ mol⁻² is the unit for the Haber process Kc, with two more moles of reactants.", "2": "dm³ mol⁻¹ would apply if there were one more mole on the reactant side."}'),
  ('CHE-0367', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 172–173 (§8.9)', 'For which reaction are Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn all equal?', '["H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)", "N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)", "N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)", "PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)"]', 0, 'When Δn (moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants) is zero, Kp = Kc = Kx = Kn. For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, Δn = 2 − 2 = 0.', '{"1": "Δn = 2 − 4 = −2, so the constants differ.", "2": "Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so the constants differ.", "3": "Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so the constants differ."}'),
  ('CHE-0368', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 173–174 (§8.9)', 'For N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) at a temperature where RT is greater than 1 (in the units used), how does Kp compare with Kc?', '["Kp = Kc", "Kp = Kc/(RT), so Kp is less than Kc", "Kp = Kc(RT), so Kp is greater than Kc", "Kp = Kc(RT)², so Kp is greater than Kc"]', 2, 'Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn. Here Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so Kp = Kc × RT, which is greater than Kc when RT > 1.', '{"0": "Kp = Kc only when Δn = 0.", "1": "Kp = Kc/(RT) applies when Δn = −1, as for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.", "3": "The power is Δn = +1, not 2."}'),
  ('CHE-0369', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp calculations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 174 (Sample Problem 8.4 method)', 'At equilibrium in N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), the partial pressures are p(N₂O₄) = 0.50 atm and p(NO₂) = 0.20 atm. Kp is', '["0.40", "0.080 atm", "12.5 atm⁻¹", "0.020 atm"]', 1, 'Kp = p(NO₂)²/p(N₂O₄) = (0.20)² ÷ 0.50 = 0.040 ÷ 0.50 = 0.080 atm.', '{"0": "This forgets to square p(NO₂).", "2": "This is the inverse, reactant over product.", "3": "This multiplies by p(N₂O₄) instead of dividing."}'),
  ('CHE-0370', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 174 (Sample Problem 8.4 method)', 'For H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), the equilibrium partial pressures are p(H₂) = p(I₂) = 2.0 × 10⁴ Pa and p(HI) = 1.4 × 10⁵ Pa. Kp is', '["49, with no units", "7.0, with no units", "49 Pa⁻¹", "3.5 Pa"]', 0, 'Kp = p(HI)²/(p(H₂)p(I₂)) = (1.4 × 10⁵)² ÷ (2.0 × 10⁴)² = 1.96 × 10¹⁰ ÷ 4.0 × 10⁸ = 49. The pressure units cancel because Δn = 0.', '{"1": "This forgets to square p(HI).", "2": "The units cancel when the moles of gas on each side are equal.", "3": "This divides p(HI) by the sum of the reactant pressures."}'),
  ('CHE-0371', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Equilibrium calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 170–171 (§8.8)', '1.0 mol of H₂ and 1.0 mol of I₂ are heated in a 1.0 dm³ vessel: H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), Kc = 49. The equilibrium concentration of HI is about', '["1.56 mol dm⁻³", "0.78 mol dm⁻³", "0.22 mol dm⁻³", "1.75 mol dm⁻³"]', 0, 'If x mol dm⁻³ of H₂ reacts, [H₂] = [I₂] = 1.0 − x and [HI] = 2x. Kc = (2x)²/(1.0 − x)² = 49, so 2x/(1.0 − x) = 7, giving x = 7/9 = 0.778. [HI] = 2x ≈ 1.56 mol dm⁻³.', '{"1": "0.78 is x, the amount of H₂ that reacts; [HI] is 2x.", "2": "0.22 mol dm⁻³ is the equilibrium concentration of H₂ (or I₂).", "3": "This solves x/(1 − x) = 7 (x = 0.875), leaving out the 2 in [HI] = 2x inside the expression, and then doubles x."}'),
  ('CHE-0372', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Le Chatelier''s principle', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 175 (§8.11)', 'Le Chatelier''s principle states that if a system at equilibrium is disturbed, it', '["behaves in such a way as to counteract the effect of the disturbance.", "stops reacting.", "always shifts towards the products.", "changes its equilibrium constant in every case."]', 0, 'The principle: if a system in equilibrium is disturbed, it behaves in a way that nullifies or counteracts the effect of the disturbance.', '{"1": "The reactions continue, and the system moves to a new equilibrium.", "2": "The direction of the shift depends on the disturbance.", "3": "Only a temperature change alters the equilibrium constant."}'),
  ('CHE-0373', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of concentration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 176 (§8.12.1)', 'For BiCl₃ + H₂O ⇌ BiOCl + 2HCl, a solution has become cloudy with white BiOCl. Adding concentrated HCl to it', '["makes it cloudier, because the equilibrium shifts forward.", "makes the precipitate dissolve, because the equilibrium shifts in the reverse direction.", "has no effect, because HCl is a product.", "increases the value of Kc."]', 1, 'Adding a product (HCl) disturbs the equilibrium; to counteract this, the reaction moves in the reverse direction, forming more BiCl₃ and using up BiOCl. Kc stays the same.', '{"0": "Adding a product shifts the equilibrium to the left, not forward.", "2": "Changing the concentration of a product does disturb the equilibrium position.", "3": "Kc is unchanged by concentration changes; only the position changes."}'),
  ('CHE-0374', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of concentration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 176 (§8.12.1)', 'When more BiCl₃ is added to the equilibrium BiCl₃ + H₂O ⇌ BiOCl + 2HCl at constant temperature,', '["Kc increases and the position shifts forward.", "the equilibrium position shifts backward and Kc increases.", "the equilibrium position does not change.", "the equilibrium position shifts forward and Kc stays the same."]', 3, 'Adding a reactant pushes the reaction forward, producing more BiOCl and HCl until equilibrium is re-established. The position changes but Kc remains constant.', '{"0": "Kc changes only with temperature.", "1": "Adding a reactant shifts the equilibrium forward, and Kc does not change.", "2": "The added reactant disturbs the equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-0375', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of pressure', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 176–177 (§8.13)', 'For C(s) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO(g) + H₂(g), increasing the pressure by decreasing the volume will', '["shift the equilibrium to the right.", "shift the equilibrium to the left.", "have no effect, because carbon is a solid.", "increase Kc."]', 1, 'Only gases count. There is 1 mole of gas on the left and 2 on the right, so increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium towards the side with fewer gaseous moles, the left.', '{"0": "The right side has more moles of gas, so higher pressure disfavours it.", "2": "The solid does not count, but the gaseous moles are still unequal (1 vs 2).", "3": "Pressure changes alter the position, not Kc."}'),
  ('CHE-0376', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of temperature', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 177 (§8.14)', 'Which factor changes the value of the equilibrium constant of a reaction?', '["Concentration of a reactant", "Temperature", "Pressure", "A catalyst"]', 1, 'The book states that temperature is the only factor that also affects the value of the equilibrium constant.', '{"0": "Changing a concentration shifts the position, but Kc stays constant.", "2": "Pressure changes shift the position for some gaseous reactions, but Kc is unchanged.", "3": "A catalyst changes neither the position nor Kc."}'),
  ('CHE-0377', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of temperature', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 178 (§8.14)', 'Kc for N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is 7.7 × 10⁻⁵ at 273 K and 0.4 at 373 K. This shows that', '["the forward reaction is exothermic.", "Kc does not depend on temperature.", "the forward reaction is endothermic, so heating shifts the equilibrium to the right.", "heating favours the formation of N₂O₄."]', 2, 'Kc increases with temperature, meaning heating favours the products. By Le Chatelier''s principle, heating favours the endothermic direction, so the forward reaction is endothermic (ΔH = +57.2 kJ).', '{"0": "For an exothermic forward reaction, Kc would fall as temperature rises.", "1": "Kc changes greatly between 273 K and 373 K.", "3": "Heating increases [NO₂] relative to [N₂O₄]."}'),
  ('CHE-0378', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of temperature', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 177–178 (§8.14)', 'For an exothermic reaction at equilibrium, lowering the temperature will', '["shift the equilibrium to the left and decrease Kc.", "shift the equilibrium to the right and increase Kc.", "shift the equilibrium to the right without changing Kc.", "have no effect on the position or on Kc."]', 1, 'Removing heat favours the forward (exothermic) reaction, so more product forms and Kc increases. For example, Kc for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ is 1 × 10²⁶ at 298 K but only 2.8 × 10² at 1000 K.', '{"0": "That is the effect of raising the temperature.", "2": "Temperature changes do alter the value of Kc.", "3": "Temperature is the one factor that changes both the position and Kc."}'),
  ('CHE-0379', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Haber process', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 179 (§8.16.1)', 'In the Haber process, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (exothermic), a temperature of about 400 °C is used instead of 200 °C, although the yield is higher at 200 °C. The reason is that', '["Kc is larger at 400 °C.", "ammonia decomposes completely at 200 °C.", "at 200 °C the rate of reaction is too slow for the process to be economical.", "the catalyst works only at 200 °C."]', 2, 'At 200 °C the yield is favoured but the reaction is very slow and uneconomical, so a moderate temperature of about 400 °C is used together with a catalyst to increase the rate.', '{"0": "For an exothermic reaction Kc is smaller at the higher temperature.", "1": "Ammonia is more stable at lower temperature.", "3": "The catalyst is used at the moderate temperature of about 400 °C."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0380', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Haber process', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 180 (§8.16.1)', 'The optimum conditions for the industrial synthesis of ammonia given in the book are', '["1 atm, about 273 K, V₂O₅ catalyst", "200–300 atm, about 673 K, iron catalyst in a fused mixture of MgO, Al₂O₃ and SiO₂", "1000 atm, 2000 K, platinum catalyst", "1 atm, 400 °C, nickel catalyst"]', 1, 'The book lists pressure of 200–300 atmospheres, temperature around 673 K (400 °C), and pieces of iron crystals in a fused mixture of MgO, Al₂O₃ and SiO₂ as catalyst.', '{"0": "V₂O₅ at about 1 atm is used in the contact process for SO₃.", "2": "Very high temperature lowers the ammonia yield, and platinum is not the Haber catalyst.", "3": "High pressure, not 1 atm, is needed, and the catalyst is iron."}'),
  ('CHE-0381', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Haber process', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 179 (§8.16.1)', 'Which change does NOT increase the equilibrium yield of ammonia in N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH negative?', '["Removing ammonia from the reaction vessel from time to time", "Increasing the pressure", "Lowering the temperature", "Adding an iron catalyst"]', 3, 'A catalyst only speeds up the attainment of equilibrium; it does not shift the position or change the yield. Removing NH₃, raising the pressure (4 moles of gas give 2) and cooling (the reaction is exothermic) all shift the equilibrium to the right.', '{"0": "Removing a product shifts the equilibrium forward.", "1": "Higher pressure favours the side with fewer moles of gas, the product side.", "2": "Cooling favours the exothermic forward reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0382', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Contact process', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 180 (§8.16.2)', 'In the contact process, the mixture of SO₂ and air is passed over a catalyst of', '["V₂O₅ at about 1 atm", "iron at 200–300 atm", "nickel at 150 °C", "platinum gauze at 800 °C"]', 0, 'To get a good yield of SO₃ within a reasonable time, SO₂ and air at about one atmosphere are passed over a solid catalyst such as V₂O₅.', '{"1": "Iron at high pressure is used in the Haber process.", "2": "Nickel is used for hydrogenation.", "3": "Platinum gauze is used for oxidizing ammonia to NO."}'),
  ('CHE-0383', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Contact process', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 180 (Table 8.2)', 'Table 8.2 shows that as the temperature for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ is raised from 200 °C to 700 °C at 1 atm,', '["Kc and the mole % of SO₃ both increase.", "Kc and the mole % of SO₃ both decrease.", "Kc increases while the mole % of SO₃ decreases.", "Kc stays constant."]', 1, 'Kc falls from 5500 to 13 and the mole % of SO₃ falls from 98% to 31%, as expected for an exothermic forward reaction. Higher temperature increases the rate but lowers the yield.', '{"0": "Both values fall in the table.", "2": "Kc falls with temperature for this exothermic reaction.", "3": "Kc changes with temperature, as the table shows."}'),
  ('CHE-0384', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Macroscopic and microscopic events', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 166 (§8.3)', 'The relation between macroscopic and microscopic events in an equilibrium system is that', '["macroscopic changes are the result of many simultaneous microscopic events.", "microscopic events can be seen with the naked eye.", "macroscopic events cause molecular collisions.", "the two are unrelated."]', 0, 'The book states that macroscopic events are the result of multiple simultaneous microscopic events such as collisions and bond formation; understanding the microscopic events explains the observed macroscopic changes.', '{"1": "Microscopic events, by definition, cannot be seen with the naked eye.", "2": "The cause runs the other way: collisions and bond changes produce the observable changes.", "3": "The book links them directly."}'),
  ('CHE-0385', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Characteristics of equilibrium', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 168 (Keep in mind; §8.7.2)', 'The equilibrium constant expression for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g) is', '["Kc = [CO₂]", "Kc = [CaO][CO₂] / [CaCO₃]", "Kc = [CaCO₃] / [CaO]", "Kc = 1 / [CO₂]"]', 0, 'Pure solids are left out of the equilibrium constant expression, so only the gas remains: Kc = [CO₂].', '{"1": "CaO and CaCO₃ are pure solids and are not included.", "2": "This includes only the solids, which must be left out.", "3": "This is the expression for the reverse reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0386', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Position of equilibrium', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 169 (§8.8)', 'The position of equilibrium refers to', '["the relative amounts of products and reactants present in the equilibrium mixture.", "the time needed to reach equilibrium.", "the rate of the forward reaction.", "the temperature at which equilibrium is reached."]', 0, 'The book defines the position of equilibrium as the relative amounts of products and reactants present in an equilibrium mixture.', '{"1": "The time to reach equilibrium depends on rate, not on the equilibrium position.", "2": "At equilibrium the forward rate equals the reverse rate; position is about composition.", "3": "Temperature affects the position but is not its definition."}'),
  ('CHE-0387', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, pp. 173–174 (§8.9, Sample Problem 8.3)', 'For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), the relation between Kp and Kc is', '["Kp = Kc(RT)", "Kp = Kc(RT)⁻¹", "Kp = Kc(RT)³", "Kp = Kc"]', 1, 'Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn, where Δn = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants = 2 − 3 = −1. So Kp = Kc(RT)⁻¹ = Kc/(RT).', '{"0": "This uses Δn = +1; there are fewer moles of gas on the product side.", "2": "This adds the moles instead of taking the difference.", "3": "Kp = Kc only when Δn = 0."}'),
  ('CHE-0388', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Effect of pressure', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 177 (§8.13)', 'For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) at equilibrium, increasing the volume of the container (lowering the pressure) will', '["shift the equilibrium to the right, forming more NH₃.", "shift the equilibrium to the left, decomposing some NH₃.", "have no effect, since all species are gases.", "change Kc."]', 1, 'Lowering the pressure moves the reaction towards the side that occupies the greater volume, the side with more moles of gas (4 moles of reactants against 2 of product). The synthesis shifts in the reverse direction.', '{"0": "More NH₃ forms when pressure is increased, not decreased.", "2": "The gaseous moles on the two sides are unequal, so pressure matters.", "3": "A volume change alters the position, not the equilibrium constant."}'),
  ('CHE-0389', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Haber process', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 179 (§8.16.1)', 'According to the book, the most complete conversion of N₂ and H₂ to ammonia, 98.3%, occurs at', '["673 K and 250 atm", "473 K and 1000 atm", "773 K and 1 atm", "298 K and 1 atm"]', 1, 'The book states the most complete conversion is 98.3% at 473 K (200 °C) and 1000 atmospheres, showing how strongly low temperature and high pressure favour the yield.', '{"0": "These are close to the industrial optimum conditions, chosen for a reasonable rate, not maximum yield.", "2": "High temperature and low pressure both lower the yield.", "3": "At 298 K the reaction would be extremely slow, and 1 atm does not favour the yield."}'),
  ('CHE-0390', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Chemical Equilibrium, p. 173 (§8.9)', 'When the amounts of reactants and products in an equilibrium expression are given as mole fractions, the constant is written as', '["Kx", "Kc", "Kp", "Kn"]', 0, 'The book uses Kc for molar concentrations, Kp for partial pressures, Kn for numbers of moles and Kx for mole fractions.', '{"1": "Kc uses molar concentrations in mol dm⁻³.", "2": "Kp uses partial pressures.", "3": "Kn uses numbers of moles."}'),
  ('CHE-0391', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Brønsted–Lowry concept', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 186 (§9.1)', 'Which limitation of the Arrhenius theory did the Brønsted–Lowry concept overcome?', '["It could not explain neutralization.", "It could not explain the sour taste of acids.", "It did not involve protons at all.", "It was limited to acids and bases in aqueous solution."]', 3, 'The book notes that the Arrhenius theory was limited to acid–base reactions in water; Brønsted and Lowry defined acids and bases as proton donors and acceptors, which applies in other solvents and with no solvent.', '{"0": "The Arrhenius theory did explain neutralization in water.", "1": "Taste was not the limitation the book describes.", "2": "Arrhenius acids give H⁺ ions in water, so protons are involved."}'),
  ('CHE-0392', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Brønsted–Lowry concept', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 187 (Table 9.1)', 'In the reaction H₂SO₄ + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + HSO₄⁻, the conjugate base of H₂SO₄ is', '["H₃O⁺", "SO₄²⁻", "H₂O", "HSO₄⁻"]', 3, 'A conjugate base is the species left when an acid donates a proton. H₂SO₄ loses one H⁺ to give HSO₄⁻.', '{"0": "H₃O⁺ is the conjugate acid of water.", "1": "SO₄²⁻ would require the loss of two protons.", "2": "H₂O is the base in this reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0393', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Brønsted–Lowry concept', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 187 (Table 9.1)', 'In the reaction H₂O + CO₃²⁻ ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻, the Brønsted–Lowry acid is', '["CO₃²⁻", "OH⁻ in the forward direction", "HCO₃⁻ in the forward direction", "H₂O"]', 3, 'Water donates a proton to CO₃²⁻, forming HCO₃⁻ and OH⁻, so H₂O is the acid and CO₃²⁻ the base in the forward reaction.', '{"0": "CO₃²⁻ accepts the proton, so it is the base.", "1": "OH⁻ is the conjugate base of water.", "2": "HCO₃⁻ is formed in the forward direction; it is the conjugate acid of CO₃²⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-0394', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Brønsted–Lowry concept', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 186 (§9.1)', 'Water is described as amphoteric because it', '["is neutral.", "has a high boiling point.", "conducts electricity.", "acts as a base towards HCl and as an acid towards NH₃."]', 3, 'With HCl, water accepts a proton (base) to form H₃O⁺; with NH₃, water donates a proton (acid) to form OH⁻. A species that can act as either acid or base is amphoteric.', '{"0": "Being neutral is not the same as being able to act as both acid and base.", "1": "Boiling point has nothing to do with acid–base behaviour.", "2": "Pure water is a poor conductor, and conductivity is not what amphoteric means."}'),
  ('CHE-0395', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Brønsted–Lowry concept', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 186 (§9.1)', 'In the reaction HCl(g) + NH₃(g) → NH₄Cl(s), NH₃ is classed as a Brønsted–Lowry base because it', '["releases OH⁻ ions.", "accepts a proton from HCl, even though no OH⁻ ions are present.", "donates a proton to HCl.", "dissolves in water."]', 1, 'The Brønsted–Lowry concept does not need water or OH⁻ ions. NH₃ accepts a proton from HCl, so it is a base in this solvent-free reaction.', '{"0": "No OH⁻ ions are present in this gas-phase reaction.", "2": "HCl is the proton donor here.", "3": "The reaction occurs between gases, without water."}'),
  ('CHE-0396', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Lewis concept', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 187 (§9.2)', 'Boron trifluoride, BF₃, is a good Lewis acid because', '["it donates a proton.", "it releases H⁺ in water.", "it contains fluorine, the most electronegative element.", "the boron atom has only six valence electrons and can accept an electron pair."]', 3, 'Boron in BF₃ has an incomplete octet (six valence electrons), so it can accept a pair of electrons from a Lewis base such as F⁻ or NH₃.', '{"0": "BF₃ has no hydrogen to donate.", "1": "BF₃ contains no hydrogen.", "2": "It is the electron-deficient boron that accepts electrons, not the fluorine."}'),
  ('CHE-0397', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Lewis concept', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 187 (§9.2)', 'When two NH₃ molecules donate electron pairs to Ag⁺, the acid–base adduct formed carries a charge of', '["0", "+2", "+1", "−1"]', 2, 'The sum of the charges on the left (2NH₃ + Ag⁺) is +1, so the adduct [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ must also carry a +1 charge.', '{"0": "NH₃ is neutral, so the adduct keeps the +1 charge of Ag⁺.", "1": "Only one positive charge is present on the left side.", "3": "Neither reactant is negatively charged."}'),
  ('CHE-0398', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionic product of water', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 188 (§9.3)', 'In deriving Kw, the concentration of pure water is taken as', '["1.0 mol dm⁻³", "55.5 mol dm⁻³", "18 mol dm⁻³", "1000 mol dm⁻³"]', 1, '[H₂O] = 1000 g dm⁻³ ÷ 18 g mol⁻¹ ≈ 55.5 mol dm⁻³. It stays effectively constant and is combined with Kc to give Kw.', '{"0": "1.0 mol dm⁻³ is not the concentration of pure water.", "2": "18 g mol⁻¹ is the molar mass of water.", "3": "1000 g dm⁻³ is the density of water expressed as mass per volume, not moles."}'),
  ('CHE-0399', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionic product of water', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 188–189 (§9.3, Table 9.2)', 'Kw is 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 40 °C. The pH of pure water at 40 °C, and its nature, are', '["pH 6.76; acidic", "pH 7.00; neutral", "pH 6.76; neutral", "pH 7.24; basic"]', 2, 'In pure water [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = √(3.0 × 10⁻¹⁴) = 1.73 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³, so pH = 6.76. Since [H⁺] still equals [OH⁻], the water is neutral.', '{"0": "The pH is below 7, but [H⁺] = [OH⁻], so the water is neutral, not acidic.", "1": "pH 7 is neutral only at 25 °C, where Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴.", "3": "Kw is larger at 40 °C, so [H⁺] is larger and pH is below 7."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0400', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionic product of water', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 189 (§9.3)', 'When a small amount of acid is added to pure water at 25 °C,', '["Kw increases.", "[OH⁻] becomes greater than [H⁺].", "[H⁺] and [OH⁻] both increase.", "[H⁺] becomes greater than [OH⁻], but Kw stays 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴."]', 3, 'Adding acid makes [H⁺] > [OH⁻]; [OH⁻] falls so that the product [H⁺][OH⁻] remains equal to Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C.', '{"0": "Kw depends only on temperature, not on added acid or base.", "1": "That happens when a base is added.", "2": "If [H⁺] rises, [OH⁻] must fall to keep Kw constant."}'),
  ('CHE-0401', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'pH and pOH', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 189 (§9.4)', 'The pH of 0.020 mol dm⁻³ nitric acid (a strong acid) at 25 °C is', '["12.30", "2.00", "1.70", "0.020"]', 2, 'HNO₃ is fully ionized, so [H⁺] = 0.020 mol dm⁻³. pH = −log(0.020) = 1.70.', '{"0": "12.30 is the pOH, not the pH.", "1": "pH 2.00 corresponds to [H⁺] = 0.010 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "0.020 is [H⁺], not its negative logarithm."}'),
  ('CHE-0402', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'pH and pOH', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 189 (§9.4)', 'The pH of 0.0050 mol dm⁻³ Ba(OH)₂ (a strong base) at 25 °C is', '["12.0", "11.7", "2.0", "2.3"]', 0, 'Each Ba(OH)₂ gives two OH⁻, so [OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0050 = 0.010 mol dm⁻³. pOH = 2.0, and pH = 14 − 2.0 = 12.0.', '{"1": "This uses [OH⁻] = 0.0050, forgetting that each formula unit releases two OH⁻ ions.", "2": "2.0 is the pOH.", "3": "2.3 is the pOH you get if you forget the factor of 2."}'),
  ('CHE-0403', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionization constant of acids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 192 (Table 9.3)', 'Using Ka values from Table 9.3, which acid is the strongest?', '["HF (Ka = 6.7 × 10⁻⁵)", "CH₃COOH (Ka = 1.85 × 10⁻⁵)", "H₂S (Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁷)", "HCO₃⁻ (Ka = 4.7 × 10⁻¹¹)"]', 0, 'Ka is a quantitative measure of acid strength: the larger Ka, the more the acid ionizes. HF has the largest Ka of the four.', '{"1": "Acetic acid''s Ka is smaller than that of HF.", "2": "H₂S has a much smaller Ka.", "3": "HCO₃⁻ has the smallest Ka of the four, so it is the weakest."}'),
  ('CHE-0404', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ionization constant of acids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 191 (§9.5)', 'According to the book''s classification, an acid with Ka = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ is', '["strong", "moderately strong", "weak", "neutral"]', 2, 'The book classifies acids as weak when Ka < 10⁻³, moderately strong when Ka is between 1 and 10⁻³, and strong when Ka > 1. 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ is less than 10⁻³.', '{"0": "A strong acid has Ka greater than 1.", "1": "Moderately strong acids have Ka from 10⁻³ to 1.", "3": "Any substance with a Ka is acting as an acid, however weak."}'),
  ('CHE-0405', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Calculating H₃O⁺ from Ka', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 192–193 (§9.5.1)', 'A weak acid HA has Ka = 4.0 × 10⁻⁶. The concentration of H₃O⁺ in a 0.040 mol dm⁻³ solution is about', '["0.040 mol dm⁻³", "1.6 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³", "4.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³", "2.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³"]', 2, 'For a weak acid, [H₃O⁺] ≈ √(Ka × C) = √(4.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.040) = √(1.6 × 10⁻⁷) = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "A weak acid does not ionize completely, so [H₃O⁺] is far less than C.", "1": "This is Ka × C without taking the square root.", "3": "This is √Ka, ignoring the concentration."}'),
  ('CHE-0406', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Calculating Ka', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 191–193 (§9.5)', 'A 0.010 mol dm⁻³ solution of a weak monoprotic acid has pH 4.0. Its Ka is about', '["1.0 × 10⁻⁶", "1.0 × 10⁻⁴", "1.0 × 10⁻²", "1.0 × 10⁻⁸"]', 0, '[H₃O⁺] = [A⁻] = 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³. Ka = [H₃O⁺][A⁻]/[HA] ≈ (10⁻⁴)² ÷ 0.010 = 10⁻⁸ ÷ 10⁻² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶.', '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁻⁴ is [H₃O⁺], not Ka.", "2": "1.0 × 10⁻² is the concentration of the acid.", "3": "This is [H₃O⁺]² without dividing by [HA]."}'),
  ('CHE-0407', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 193–194 (§9.6)', 'Sodium chloride is purified by passing HCl gas through brine. NaCl crystallizes out because', '["HCl reacts with Na⁺ to form a precipitate.", "water evaporates when HCl is added.", "HCl lowers the temperature of the solution.", "the added Cl⁻ (a common ion) shifts the equilibrium towards solid NaCl."]', 3, 'HCl ionizes to give Cl⁻, a common ion. The higher [Cl⁻] shifts Na⁺ + Cl⁻ ⇌ NaCl(s) to the right, so pure NaCl crystallizes out. This is the common ion effect.', '{"0": "HCl provides Cl⁻; it does not react with Na⁺ to give a new compound.", "1": "No evaporation is involved.", "2": "The crystallization is caused by the common ion, not by cooling."}'),
  ('CHE-0408', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 194 (§9.6)', 'In salt analysis, H₂S is passed through an acidic solution (containing HCl) to precipitate group II radicals. HCl is added because', '["the common ion H⁺ suppresses the ionization of H₂S, keeping [S²⁻] low.", "it oxidizes H₂S to sulphur.", "it increases the concentration of S²⁻ ions.", "it dissolves the group II sulphides."]', 0, 'H⁺ from the strong acid HCl is a common ion that suppresses the dissociation of H₂S. The resulting low [S²⁻] is enough to precipitate the group II basic radicals.', '{"1": "HCl is not acting as an oxidizing agent here.", "2": "The common ion effect lowers, not raises, [S²⁻].", "3": "The aim is to precipitate the group II sulphides, not dissolve them."}'),
  ('CHE-0409', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 194 (§9.6)', 'NH₄Cl is added to aqueous ammonia when it is used as the group III reagent in salt analysis. The effect of NH₄Cl is to', '["increase [OH⁻].", "precipitate ammonia.", "make the solution strongly acidic.", "suppress the concentration of OH⁻ through the common ion NH₄⁺."]', 3, 'NH₄Cl is a strong electrolyte that supplies a large excess of NH₄⁺, the common ion. This suppresses NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ and lowers [OH⁻].', '{"0": "The common ion shifts the equilibrium left, lowering [OH⁻].", "1": "Ammonia stays in solution.", "2": "The mixture remains basic; it is a basic buffer."}'),
  ('CHE-0410', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 195 (§9.7)', 'Which mixture gives a basic buffer?', '["CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa", "HCl and NaCl", "NH₄OH and NH₄Cl", "NaOH and NaCl"]', 2, 'A basic buffer is made by mixing a weak base with its salt of a strong acid; NH₄OH with NH₄Cl is the book''s example, with pH above 7.', '{"0": "A weak acid with its salt of a strong base gives an acidic buffer.", "1": "A strong acid and its salt do not form a buffer.", "3": "A strong base and its salt do not form a buffer."}'),
  ('CHE-0411', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 195 (§9.7)', 'When a small amount of strong acid is added to an acetic acid–sodium acetate buffer, the pH hardly changes because', '["acetic acid is a strong acid.", "the added acid evaporates.", "Na⁺ ions neutralize the acid.", "the added H₃O⁺ ions combine with CH₃COO⁻ ions to form undissociated acetic acid."]', 3, 'The buffer is a reservoir of CH₃COOH and CH₃COO⁻. Added H₃O⁺ reacts with CH₃COO⁻ to give back acetic acid, which, being weak, stays mostly undissociated, so the pH remains almost unchanged.', '{"0": "Acetic acid is a weak acid; that is why it stays undissociated.", "1": "The added acid reacts; it does not evaporate.", "2": "Na⁺ is a spectator ion and does not react with H₃O⁺."}'),
  ('CHE-0412', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 195 (Table 9.4)', 'Table 9.4 shows that adding sodium acetate to 0.10 mol dm⁻³ acetic acid', '["increases the percentage dissociation and lowers the pH.", "has no effect on the pH.", "decreases the percentage dissociation and raises the pH.", "decreases the pH to below 2."]', 2, 'As [CH₃COO⁻] rises from 0 to 0.15 mol dm⁻³, the percentage dissociation falls from 1.3% to 0.012% and the pH rises from 2.89 to 4.92. The acetate ion is a common ion that suppresses the acid''s dissociation.', '{"0": "The common ion suppresses dissociation, so fewer H⁺ ions form and the pH rises.", "1": "The pH rises from 2.89 to 4.92 in the table.", "3": "The pH rises above 2.89."}'),
  ('CHE-0413', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Henderson equation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 196 (§9.7.1)', 'A buffer is made from acetic acid (pKa = 4.74) and sodium acetate, with [salt]/[acid] = 10. Its pH is', '["4.74", "3.74", "5.74", "14.74"]', 2, 'Henderson equation: pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.74 + log 10 = 4.74 + 1 = 5.74.', '{"0": "pH = pKa only when [salt] = [acid].", "1": "This uses the inverse ratio, [acid]/[salt].", "3": "This adds 10 instead of log 10."}'),
  ('CHE-0414', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Henderson equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 196 (§9.7.1)', 'A buffer contains 0.10 mol dm⁻³ of a weak acid HA (pKa = 4.20) and 0.30 mol dm⁻³ of its sodium salt NaA. The pH is about', '["4.68", "3.72", "4.20", "4.50"]', 0, 'pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.20 + log(0.30/0.10) = 4.20 + log 3 = 4.20 + 0.48 = 4.68.', '{"1": "This uses log([acid]/[salt]) = −0.48.", "2": "pH = pKa only when the concentrations are equal.", "3": "This adds 0.30 directly instead of the log of the ratio."}'),
  ('CHE-0415', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Buffer solutions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 196 (Interesting information)', 'The buffering system in blood that helps maintain a stable pH involves', '["nitrates", "sulphates", "chlorides", "bicarbonates"]', 3, 'The book notes that the body''s blood buffering system, involving bicarbonates, helps keep blood pH stable.', '{"0": "Nitrates are not the blood buffer named.", "1": "Sulphates are not the blood buffer named.", "2": "Chloride ions do not act as the buffer."}'),
  ('CHE-0416', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility product', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 197 (§9.8, Table 9.5)', 'The solubility product expression for Al(OH)₃ is', '["Ksp = [Al³⁺][OH⁻]³", "Ksp = [Al³⁺]³[OH⁻]", "Ksp = [Al³⁺][OH⁻]", "Ksp = [Al³⁺][3OH⁻]"]', 0, 'Al(OH)₃(s) ⇌ Al³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq). Ksp is the product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient: [Al³⁺][OH⁻]³.', '{"1": "The power 3 belongs to OH⁻, not Al³⁺.", "2": "The coefficient 3 of OH⁻ must appear as a power.", "3": "Coefficients are written as powers, not as multipliers inside the brackets."}'),
  ('CHE-0417', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility product', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 197 (§9.8)', 'Which statement about the solubility product (Ksp) is correct?', '["It is used for very soluble salts such as NaCl.", "It is independent of temperature.", "It applies to sparingly soluble salts and depends on temperature.", "It includes the concentration of the undissolved solid."]', 2, 'Ksp describes the equilibrium between a sparingly soluble salt and its ions in a saturated solution. The book notes Ksp is usually very small and is temperature dependent.', '{"0": "Soluble salts such as NaCl dissociate completely; Ksp is used for slightly soluble salts.", "1": "The book states the value of Ksp depends on temperature.", "3": "The concentration of the solid is constant and is absorbed into Ksp."}'),
  ('CHE-0418', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility from Ksp', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 198 (§9.8.1)', 'The Ksp of AgBr is 5.0 × 10⁻¹³ at 25 °C. Its molar solubility in pure water is about', '["2.5 × 10⁻²⁵ mol dm⁻³", "7.1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³", "5.0 × 10⁻¹³ mol dm⁻³", "3.5 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³"]', 1, 'AgBr(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Br⁻, so Ksp = S². S = √(5.0 × 10⁻¹³) = 7.1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³.', '{"0": "This squares Ksp instead of taking its square root.", "2": "Ksp itself is not the solubility.", "3": "This uses Ksp = 4S², which applies to neither AgBr nor any 1:1 salt."}'),
  ('CHE-0419', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Solubility from Ksp', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 198 (Sample Problem 9.4 method)', 'The Ksp of CaF₂ is 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹. Its molar solubility in water is', '["2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³", "5.7 × 10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³", "3.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³", "8.0 × 10⁻¹² mol dm⁻³"]', 0, 'CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻. If the solubility is S, [Ca²⁺] = S and [F⁻] = 2S, so Ksp = S(2S)² = 4S³. S³ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ ÷ 4 = 8.0 × 10⁻¹², so S = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.', '{"1": "This treats CaF₂ like a 1:1 salt (S = √Ksp).", "2": "This takes the cube root of Ksp without dividing by 4.", "3": "This is S³, before taking the cube root."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0420', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Ksp from solubility', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 197–198 (§9.8)', 'The molar solubility of BaCO₃ in water is 1.05 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³. Its Ksp is about', '["1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰", "1.05 × 10⁻⁵", "2.1 × 10⁻⁵", "4.6 × 10⁻¹⁵"]', 0, 'BaCO₃ ⇌ Ba²⁺ + CO₃²⁻, so Ksp = S × S = (1.05 × 10⁻⁵)² = 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰, which matches Table 9.5.', '{"1": "This is the solubility, not the product of the ion concentrations.", "2": "This adds the two ion concentrations instead of multiplying them.", "3": "This uses 4S³, which applies to a 1:2 salt, not BaCO₃."}'),
  ('CHE-0421', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Predicting precipitation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 199 (§9.8.1 c)', 'Equal volumes of 4.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ Ca²⁺ and 4.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ SO₄²⁻ are mixed at 25 °C. The Ksp of CaSO₄ is 2 × 10⁻⁵. Will CaSO₄ precipitate?', '["No, because the ionic product, 4.0 × 10⁻⁶, is less than Ksp.", "Yes, because the ionic product, 1.6 × 10⁻⁵, is greater than Ksp.", "Yes, because any mixing of Ca²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ gives a precipitate.", "No, because the ionic product, 1.6 × 10⁻⁵, equals Ksp."]', 0, 'Mixing equal volumes halves each concentration to 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. The ionic product is (2.0 × 10⁻³)² = 4.0 × 10⁻⁶, which is less than Ksp, so the solution is unsaturated and no precipitate forms.', '{"1": "This forgets that mixing equal volumes halves each concentration; and 1.6 × 10⁻⁵ is still less than Ksp.", "2": "A precipitate forms only when the ionic product exceeds Ksp.", "3": "The concentrations must be halved, and 1.6 × 10⁻⁵ does not equal 2 × 10⁻⁵ anyway."}'),
  ('CHE-0422', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect on solubility', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 198–199 (§9.8.1 b)', 'The Ksp of AgBr is 5.0 × 10⁻¹³. Its solubility in 0.010 mol dm⁻³ NaBr solution is about', '["7.1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³", "5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ mol dm⁻³", "5.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ mol dm⁻³", "7.1 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³"]', 1, 'Br⁻ is a common ion. [Br⁻] ≈ 0.010 mol dm⁻³, so S = [Ag⁺] = Ksp/[Br⁻] = 5.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 0.010 = 5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ mol dm⁻³, far lower than in pure water.', '{"0": "This is the solubility in pure water; the common ion lowers it.", "2": "This multiplies Ksp by 0.010 instead of dividing.", "3": "A common ion decreases solubility, so it cannot be higher than in pure water."}'),
  ('CHE-0423', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Common ion effect on solubility', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 199 (§9.8.1 b)', 'When Na₂CrO₄ is added to a saturated solution of PbCrO₄,', '["PbCrO₄ precipitates, because the common ion CrO₄²⁻ shifts the equilibrium to the left.", "more PbCrO₄ dissolves.", "Ksp of PbCrO₄ increases.", "nothing happens, because Na⁺ does not react."]', 0, 'CrO₄²⁻ is a common ion. It combines with Pb²⁺ to form more insoluble PbCrO₄, shifting the equilibrium to the left while Ksp stays constant.', '{"1": "A common ion decreases solubility.", "2": "Ksp depends only on temperature.", "3": "Na⁺ is a spectator, but the CrO₄²⁻ ions do shift the equilibrium."}'),
  ('CHE-0424', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Salt hydrolysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 200 (§9.9.2)', 'An aqueous solution of which salt is acidic?', '["NH₄Cl", "NaCl", "CH₃COONa", "K₂CO₃"]', 0, 'NH₄Cl is a salt of a strong acid and a weak base. NH₄⁺ reacts with water, NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺, making the solution acidic.', '{"1": "NaCl is from a strong acid and strong base, so its solution is neutral.", "2": "Sodium acetate is from a weak acid and strong base, so its solution is basic.", "3": "K₂CO₃ is from a weak acid and strong base, so its solution is basic."}'),
  ('CHE-0425', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Salt hydrolysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, pp. 200–201 (§9.9.3)', 'An aqueous solution of sodium acetate is basic because', '["Na⁺ reacts with water to form NaOH.", "CH₃COO⁻, the conjugate base of a weak acid, reacts with water to form OH⁻.", "sodium acetate is a strong base.", "acetic acid is a strong acid."]', 1, 'CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. The acetate ion is the conjugate base of a weak acid and hydrolyses to give OH⁻; Na⁺ does not affect the pH.', '{"0": "Na⁺, the conjugate acid of a strong base, does not react significantly with water.", "2": "Sodium acetate is a salt; the basicity comes from hydrolysis of its anion.", "3": "Acetic acid is weak, which is why its conjugate base hydrolyses."}'),
  ('CHE-0426', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Salt hydrolysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 201 (§9.9.4)', 'The pH of an ammonium acetate solution depends on', '["only the hydrolysis of NH₄⁺.", "only the hydrolysis of CH₃COO⁻.", "the relative strengths of NH₄⁺ as an acid and CH₃COO⁻ as a base.", "nothing; it is always exactly 7."]', 2, 'Both ions hydrolyse: NH₄⁺ produces H₃O⁺ and CH₃COO⁻ produces OH⁻. The resulting pH depends on which reaction is more dominant, so the solution may be acidic, basic or nearly neutral.', '{"0": "Acetate also hydrolyses and affects the pH.", "1": "NH₄⁺ also hydrolyses and affects the pH.", "3": "The book says the solution may be acidic, basic or nearly neutral."}'),
  ('CHE-0427', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Acid–base indicators', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 201 (§9.10)', 'A weak-acid indicator HIn ionizes as HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻. In a strongly acidic solution, the indicator is present mainly as', '["In⁻, because H⁺ removes HIn.", "HIn, because In⁻ accepts protons from the acid.", "equal amounts of HIn and In⁻.", "H₂In⁺ only."]', 1, 'In acid, In⁻ ions act as Brønsted bases and accept protons, so the indicator exists largely in its unionized form, HIn, and shows the acid colour.', '{"0": "Added H⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left, towards HIn.", "2": "The two forms are present in comparable amounts only within the indicator''s colour-change range.", "3": "The book''s model involves only HIn and In⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-0428', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Acid–base indicators', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 202 (§9.10)', 'Methyl red changes from red to yellow between pH', '["1.2 and 2.8", "8.2 and 10.0", "4.4 and 6.2", "10.0 and 12.0"]', 2, 'The book states methyl red changes from red to yellow between pH 4.4 and 6.2; at pH 4.4 it exists mostly as red HIn molecules.', '{"0": "This is not the range given for methyl red.", "1": "8.2–10.0 is the range given for phenolphthalein.", "3": "This is not the range of methyl red."}'),
  ('CHE-0429', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Acid–base indicators', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 202 (§9.10.1)', 'An acid–base indicator changes colour over a range of about', '["0.1 pH unit", "2 pH units", "7 pH units", "14 pH units"]', 1, 'Each indicator changes colour over a defined pH range, the indicator range, which is about 2 pH units.', '{"0": "The change is not that sharp for a single indicator.", "2": "A 7-unit range would make the indicator useless for locating an end point.", "3": "That is the whole normal pH scale."}'),
  ('CHE-0430', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Titration curves', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 202 (§9.10.2)', 'In a titration, the end point is', '["the point at which the amounts of acid and base are exactly stoichiometric.", "the point at which the indicator changes colour, chosen to be close to the equivalence point.", "always at pH 7.", "the point where the burette is empty."]', 1, 'The equivalence point is where the titrant exactly neutralizes the analyte; the end point is where the indicator changes colour. An indicator is chosen so that its end point is close to the equivalence point.', '{"0": "That is the equivalence point.", "2": "The pH at equivalence is 7 only for strong acid–strong base titrations.", "3": "The amount left in the burette has nothing to do with the end point."}'),
  ('CHE-0431', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Titration curves', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 203 (§9.10.2)', 'In the titration of aqueous ammonia with HCl, the pH at the equivalence point is', '["exactly 7, so phenolphthalein is the only suitable indicator.", "less than 7 (about 5.3), so methyl orange is suitable.", "greater than 7, so phenolphthalein is suitable.", "about 12, so no indicator works."]', 1, 'At equivalence the solution contains NH₄⁺, which releases H⁺, so the pH is below 7 (the book gives 5.27). Methyl orange changes colour in this region and is used for strong acid–weak base titrations.', '{"0": "The conjugate acid NH₄⁺ makes the solution acidic at equivalence.", "2": "A pH above 7 at equivalence is typical of weak acid–strong base titrations.", "3": "The pH at equivalence is acidic, not 12."}'),
  ('CHE-0432', 'Chemical equilibrium', 'Titration curves', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Acid-Base Chemistry, p. 203 (§9.10.2)', 'In the titration of HCl with NaOH, the pH changes steeply near the equivalence point from about', '["1 to 2", "4 to 10", "7 to 8", "11 to 13"]', 1, 'For a strong acid–strong base titration the pH at equivalence is 7.0, and near the end point it changes sharply from about 4.0 to 10.0, so indicators such as phenolphthalein that change within this range can be used.', '{"0": "This is the low-pH region at the start, not the steep jump.", "2": "The steep part covers far more than one pH unit.", "3": "This is the high-pH region after excess NaOH has been added."}'),
  ('CHE-0433', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation and reduction', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 208–209 (§10.1–10.2)', 'In terms of oxidation number, oxidation is', '["a decrease in oxidation number.", "the gain of electrons.", "no change in oxidation number.", "an increase in oxidation number."]', 3, 'Oxidation is loss of electrons, which raises the oxidation number; a higher positive oxidation number means an atom is more oxidized.', '{"0": "A decrease in oxidation number is reduction.", "1": "Gain of electrons is reduction.", "2": "If the oxidation number does not change, the atom is neither oxidized nor reduced."}'),
  ('CHE-0434', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 209–210 (§10.2.1)', 'The oxidation number of sulphur in sodium thiosulphate, Na₂S₂O₃, is', '["+2", "+4", "+6", "−2"]', 0, '2(+1) + 2S + 3(−2) = 0, so 2S = +4 and S = +2 (average per sulphur atom).', '{"1": "+4 is sulphur''s oxidation number in SO₂.", "2": "+6 is sulphur''s oxidation number in SO₄²⁻.", "3": "−2 is sulphur''s oxidation number in Na₂S."}'),
  ('CHE-0435', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 209 (§10.2.1)', 'The oxidation number of nitrogen in the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺, is', '["−3", "+3", "+4", "+1"]', 0, 'In a polyatomic ion the oxidation numbers add up to the charge: N + 4(+1) = +1, so N = −3.', '{"1": "The sign is wrong; hydrogen is +1 and nitrogen is the more electronegative element.", "2": "This ignores the +1 charge on the ion and the hydrogens.", "3": "+1 is the overall charge on the ion, not nitrogen''s oxidation number."}'),
  ('CHE-0436', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 210 (Keep in mind)', 'The oxidation number of oxygen in potassium superoxide, KO₂, is', '["−2", "−1", "−½", "+2"]', 2, 'K is +1, so 2(O) = −1 and O = −½. The book lists oxygen as −2 in H₂O, −1 in Na₂O₂ and −½ in KO₂.', '{"0": "−2 is oxygen''s usual value, as in H₂O.", "1": "−1 is oxygen''s value in peroxides such as Na₂O₂.", "3": "+2 is oxygen''s value in F₂O."}'),
  ('CHE-0437', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 209 (§10.2.1)', 'In which compound does hydrogen have an oxidation number of −1?', '["HCl", "H₂O", "NaH", "NH₃"]', 2, 'Hydrogen is +1 in compounds with non-metals but −1 in metal hydrides such as NaH, where sodium is +1.', '{"0": "Hydrogen is +1 in HCl.", "1": "Hydrogen is +1 in water.", "3": "Hydrogen is +1 in ammonia."}'),
  ('CHE-0438', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidation number', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 209 (§10.2.1)', 'Oxygen has a positive oxidation number of +2 in', '["H₂O₂", "KO₂", "Na₂O", "F₂O"]', 3, 'Oxygen is negative in its compounds except with fluorine, which is more electronegative. In F₂O, each F is −1, so O is +2.', '{"0": "Oxygen is −1 in the peroxide H₂O₂.", "1": "Oxygen is −½ in KO₂.", "2": "Oxygen is −2 in Na₂O."}'),
  ('CHE-0439', 'Electrochemistry', 'Disproportionation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 211 (§10.3)', 'Which reaction is a disproportionation reaction?', '["2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl", "Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu", "2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂", "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"]', 2, 'In a disproportionation the same element is both oxidized and reduced. Oxygen in H₂O₂ (−1) is reduced to −2 in H₂O and oxidized to 0 in O₂.', '{"0": "Sodium is oxidized and chlorine is reduced.", "1": "Zinc is oxidized and copper is reduced; two different elements change.", "3": "No oxidation numbers change; this is not a redox reaction."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0440', 'Electrochemistry', 'Oxidizing and reducing agents', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 211 (§10.4)', 'In the reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), the oxidizing agent is', '["Zn", "Cu", "Zn²⁺", "Cu²⁺"]', 3, 'An oxidizing agent oxidizes another substance and is itself reduced by gaining electrons. Cu²⁺ gains two electrons to become Cu, while oxidizing Zn to Zn²⁺.', '{"0": "Zn loses electrons; it is the reducing agent.", "1": "Cu is the product of reduction.", "2": "Zn²⁺ is the product of oxidation."}'),
  ('CHE-0441', 'Electrochemistry', 'Balancing redox equations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 212–214 (§10.5)', 'When Cu + HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + NO₂ + H₂O (concentrated acid) is balanced by the oxidation number method, the coefficient of HNO₃ is', '["2", "4", "6", "8"]', 1, 'Cu goes from 0 to +2 (loses 2e⁻); N goes from +5 to +4 in NO₂ (gains 1e⁻), so 2NO₂ are needed. Adding 2 NO₃⁻ that stay unchanged in Cu(NO₃)₂ gives Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O.', '{"0": "Two HNO₃ only supply the nitrogen reduced to NO₂; two more are needed as nitrate in Cu(NO₃)₂.", "2": "Six HNO₃ would leave nitrogen and hydrogen unbalanced.", "3": "Eight is the coefficient for the reaction with dilute acid giving NO, not NO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0442', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolytic cell', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 214–215 (§10.6)', 'In an electrolytic cell,', '["chemical energy is converted into electrical energy.", "the anode is negative and oxidation takes place there.", "the cathode is negative and reduction takes place there.", "an alternating current is used."]', 2, 'An electrolytic cell converts electrical energy into chemical energy. The cathode is the negative electrode, where cations gain electrons (reduction); the anode is positive, where anions lose electrons (oxidation).', '{"0": "That describes a galvanic (voltaic) cell.", "1": "In an electrolytic cell the anode is the positive electrode.", "3": "The book states the power supply must be direct current."}'),
  ('CHE-0443', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrolysis', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 215 (§10.7)', 'During the electrolysis of molten zinc chloride, the reaction at the anode is', '["Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn", "Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻", "Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻", "2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻"]', 3, 'Anions move to the anode and lose electrons (oxidation): 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. Zinc is deposited at the cathode.', '{"0": "This reduction happens at the cathode.", "1": "This is a reduction; oxidation occurs at the anode.", "2": "In molten ZnCl₂ there is no zinc metal at the anode to be oxidized."}'),
  ('CHE-0444', 'Electrochemistry', 'Quantity of electricity', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 216 (§10.8)', 'A current of 2.0 A flows for 10 minutes. The quantity of charge passed is', '["20 C", "1200 C", "120 C", "300 C"]', 1, 'Q = I × t = 2.0 A × (10 × 60 s) = 1200 C. Time must be in seconds.', '{"0": "This uses time in minutes instead of seconds.", "2": "This uses 60 s instead of 600 s.", "3": "This divides 600 by 2 instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('CHE-0445', 'Electrochemistry', 'Faraday''s law calculations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 216 (§10.8–10.9)', 'What mass of copper is deposited from CuSO₄ solution by 1930 C of charge? (Cu = 63.5, F = 96 500 C mol⁻¹)', '["1.27 g", "0.635 g", "0.318 g", "63.5 g"]', 1, 'Moles of electrons = 1930 ÷ 96 500 = 0.0200 mol. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles of Cu = 0.0100 mol, and mass = 0.0100 × 63.5 = 0.635 g.', '{"0": "This ignores the 2 electrons needed per Cu²⁺ ion.", "2": "This divides by 4 instead of 2.", "3": "63.5 g needs 2 × 96 500 C, far more charge."}'),
  ('CHE-0446', 'Electrochemistry', 'Faraday''s law calculations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 216 (§10.8)', 'How much charge is needed to deposit 9.0 g of aluminium from Al³⁺ ions? (Al = 27, F = 96 500 C mol⁻¹)', '["96 500 C", "32 167 C", "289 500 C", "48 250 C"]', 0, '9.0 g Al = 9.0 ÷ 27 = ⅓ mol. Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al, so ⅓ mol Al needs 3 × ⅓ = 1 mol of electrons = 1 F = 96 500 C.', '{"1": "This forgets that each Al³⁺ needs 3 electrons.", "2": "This is the charge for a full mole (27 g) of aluminium.", "3": "This treats aluminium as needing 1.5 electrons per ion."}'),
  ('CHE-0447', 'Electrochemistry', 'Faraday''s law calculations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 216–217 (§10.9)', 'A charge of 9650 C is passed through dilute sulphuric acid. The volume of hydrogen liberated at the cathode, measured at STP, is (F = 96 500 C mol⁻¹, molar volume 22.4 dm³)', '["2.24 dm³", "1.12 dm³", "0.56 dm³", "11.2 dm³"]', 1, 'Moles of electrons = 9650 ÷ 96 500 = 0.100 mol. 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, so moles of H₂ = 0.0500 mol and volume = 0.0500 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³.', '{"0": "This takes one mole of H₂ per mole of electrons.", "2": "This takes four electrons per H₂ molecule, as for O₂.", "3": "This uses 0.5 mol of H₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0448', 'Electrochemistry', 'Avogadro constant by electrolysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 217–218 (§10.10)', 'In an electrolysis experiment the charge on 1 mole of electrons is found to be 95 250 C. Taking the charge on one electron as 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, the value obtained for the Avogadro constant is', '["5.95 × 10²³ mol⁻¹", "1.52 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol⁻¹", "6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹", "1.68 × 10⁻²⁴ mol⁻¹"]', 0, 'NA = charge on 1 mole of electrons ÷ charge on 1 electron = 95 250 ÷ (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 5.95 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, close to the accepted value.', '{"1": "This multiplies the two charges.", "2": "6.02 × 10²³ is the accepted value, not the value from these data.", "3": "This divides the electron charge by 95 250, the inverse."}'),
  ('CHE-0449', 'Electrochemistry', 'Avogadro constant by electrolysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 218 (§10.10.1)', 'In the electrolytic determination of the Avogadro constant using copper electrodes in CuSO₄ solution, the loss in mass of the anode is used rather than the gain in mass of the cathode because', '["the anode does not change in mass.", "copper is deposited on the anode.", "copper does not always stick well to the cathode.", "the cathode dissolves."]', 2, 'The cathode gains copper and the anode loses copper. The anode''s mass loss is preferred because deposited copper does not always stick to the cathode very well.', '{"0": "The anode loses mass as copper goes into solution.", "1": "Copper is deposited on the cathode, not the anode.", "3": "The anode dissolves; the cathode gains copper."}'),
  ('CHE-0450', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode potential', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 220 (§10.11.1)', 'The absolute potential of a single electrode cannot be measured directly because', '["the metal does not react with its ions.", "metal ions do not conduct electricity.", "electrodes have no charge.", "an electrical double layer forms at the metal–solution interface, so only potential differences between two systems can be measured."]', 3, 'When a metal is placed in a solution of its ions, an electrical double layer forms. The potential cannot be measured directly, but its difference from a reference system (the standard hydrogen electrode) can.', '{"0": "There is an equilibrium between the metal and its ions; that is what sets up the potential.", "1": "Ions in solution do carry current.", "2": "The metal surface does become charged, for example zinc gains excess electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0451', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard hydrogen electrode', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 220 (§10.12)', 'In the standard hydrogen electrode, the platinum is coated with platinum black because', '["it reacts with hydrogen to form a compound.", "it makes the electrode potential +1.00 V.", "the finely divided platinum gives close contact between H₂ gas and H⁺ ions, so equilibrium is reached quickly.", "it acts as an oxidizing agent."]', 2, 'Platinum black is finely divided platinum, allowing close contact of hydrogen gas and H⁺ ions so the H₂/H⁺ equilibrium is set up quickly. The platinum itself is inert.', '{"0": "Platinum is inert and does not form a compound here.", "1": "The SHE is assigned E° = 0.00 V.", "3": "Platinum is inert; it only provides a surface and electrical contact."}'),
  ('CHE-0452', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard electrode potential', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 221 (§10.13)', 'Standard electrode potentials are measured with ion concentrations of', '["0.10 mol dm⁻³ at 273 K", "any concentration at 298 K", "1.00 mol dm⁻³ at 373 K", "1.00 mol dm⁻³ at 298 K, with gases at 1 atm"]', 3, 'Standard conditions are ion concentrations of 1.00 mol dm⁻³, 25 °C (298 K) and gases at 1 atm (101 kPa), measured against the SHE.', '{"0": "Neither 0.10 mol dm⁻³ nor 273 K is standard.", "1": "Electrode potential depends on concentration, so it must be fixed at 1.00 mol dm⁻³.", "2": "The standard temperature is 298 K, not 373 K."}'),
  ('CHE-0453', 'Electrochemistry', 'Measuring electrode potentials', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 221 (§10.14)', 'E° for Cu²⁺/Cu is +0.34 V. This means that, compared with H⁺ ions,', '["Cu²⁺ ions are harder to reduce.", "copper metal is a stronger reducing agent than hydrogen.", "Cu²⁺ ions are easier to reduce and accept electrons from the H₂/H⁺ half-cell.", "copper reacts with dilute acids to release hydrogen."]', 2, 'Cu²⁺ has a more positive E° than H⁺, so Cu²⁺ ions are easier to reduce; they accept electrons from the H⁺/H₂ half-cell.', '{"0": "A more positive E° means easier to reduce.", "1": "Hydrogen gives electrons to Cu²⁺, so H₂ is the stronger reducing agent.", "3": "Table 10.2 shows copper is unreactive with acids."}'),
  ('CHE-0454', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrochemical cell', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 222 (§10.14)', 'In an electrochemical cell made from a Zn²⁺/Zn half-cell and the SHE, reduction takes place', '["at the negative terminal, the zinc electrode.", "at both electrodes equally.", "in the salt bridge.", "at the positive terminal, the hydrogen electrode."]', 3, 'Zn (E° = −0.76 V) loses electrons at the negative terminal (oxidation), and 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ occurs at the positive terminal. Reduction always takes place at the positive terminal of such a cell.', '{"0": "Oxidation takes place at the negative terminal (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻).", "1": "One electrode is the site of reduction, the other of oxidation.", "2": "The salt bridge only lets ions move; no electron transfer occurs there."}'),
  ('CHE-0455', 'Electrochemistry', 'Galvanic cell', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 223 (§10.15)', 'The salt bridge in a galvanic cell can be made from', '["a copper wire.", "a glass rod.", "a strip of filter paper soaked in saturated potassium nitrate solution.", "a strip of zinc metal."]', 2, 'The book describes a salt bridge made from filter paper (or other inert porous material) soaked in saturated KNO₃. It lets ions move to keep the half-cells balanced, but not electrons.', '{"0": "A wire would carry electrons, which a salt bridge must not do.", "1": "Glass does not conduct ions.", "3": "A metal strip would carry electrons and react."}'),
  ('CHE-0456', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell potential', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 223–224 (§10.15)', 'Given E°(Mg²⁺/Mg) = −2.37 V and E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V, the standard potential of a Mg–Cu galvanic cell is', '["2.71 V", "2.03 V", "−2.71 V", "1.10 V"]', 0, 'E°cell = E°(cathode, reduced) − E°(anode, oxidized) = +0.34 − (−2.37) = 2.71 V. Magnesium is the anode.', '{"1": "This adds the two values as written: −2.37 + 0.34.", "2": "The cell potential of a working galvanic cell is positive.", "3": "1.10 V is the Zn–Cu cell."}'),
  ('CHE-0457', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell potential', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 224–225 (§10.16)', 'A cell is made from Zn²⁺/Zn (E° = −0.76 V) and Ag⁺/Ag (E° = +0.80 V) half-cells. Its standard cell potential is', '["1.56 V", "0.04 V", "0.84 V", "2.36 V"]', 0, 'E°cell = E°(Ag⁺/Ag) − E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = 0.80 − (−0.76) = 1.56 V. E° values are not multiplied by the coefficients when the silver half-equation is doubled.', '{"1": "This adds −0.76 and +0.80 instead of subtracting.", "2": "This doubles E°(Ag⁺/Ag) and then adds −0.76.", "3": "This doubles E°(Ag⁺/Ag) because the equation needs 2Ag⁺; electrode potentials do not depend on the coefficients."}'),
  ('CHE-0458', 'Electrochemistry', 'Direction of electron flow', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 224 (§10.16.1)', 'In a cell made from Zn²⁺/Zn and Ag⁺/Ag half-cells, electrons flow', '["from the Ag electrode to the Zn electrode through the solution.", "through the salt bridge.", "from the Ag electrode to the Zn electrode through the external wire.", "from the Zn electrode to the Ag electrode through the external wire."]', 3, 'Zinc has the more negative E°, so Zn loses electrons. They travel through the external wires from the Zn²⁺/Zn half-cell (negative pole) to the Ag⁺/Ag half-cell (positive pole). Electrons do not travel through the electrolyte.', '{"0": "Electrons never travel through the electrolyte solution.", "1": "The salt bridge carries ions, not electrons.", "2": "This is the reverse direction; zinc is the electron source."}'),
  ('CHE-0459', 'Electrochemistry', 'Feasibility using E°', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 226 (§10.16.3)', 'Copper cannot reduce Zn²⁺ ions to zinc. Which metal is a suitable reducing agent to reduce Zn²⁺ to Zn?', '["Magnesium (E° = −2.37 V)", "Silver (E° = +0.80 V)", "Copper (E° = +0.34 V)", "Lead (E° = −0.13 V)"]', 0, 'A reducing agent for Zn²⁺ must have an E° more negative than that of Zn²⁺/Zn (−0.76 V). Magnesium qualifies, so Mg is the better reducing agent and can reduce Zn²⁺.', '{"1": "Silver has a far more positive E°; it is a very weak reducing agent.", "2": "The question states copper cannot reduce Zn²⁺; its E° is more positive than −0.76 V.", "3": "Lead''s E° (−0.13 V) is less negative than −0.76 V, so it cannot reduce Zn²⁺."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0460', 'Electrochemistry', 'Feasibility using E°', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 225–227 (§10.16.2)', 'Given E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = +0.77 V and E°(I₂/I⁻) = +0.54 V, which statement is correct under standard conditions?', '["Fe²⁺ reduces I₂ to I⁻.", "I₂ oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺.", "Fe³⁺ ions oxidize I⁻ ions to iodine.", "No reaction is possible in either direction."]', 2, 'The more positive E° belongs to Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺, so Fe³⁺ is the stronger oxidizing agent and is reduced, while I⁻ is oxidized: 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂, E°cell = 0.77 − 0.54 = +0.23 V.', '{"0": "This is the same non-feasible reaction as option B, described from the other side.", "1": "This would give E°cell = 0.54 − 0.77 = −0.23 V, which is not feasible.", "3": "The reaction of Fe³⁺ with I⁻ has a positive E°cell, so it is feasible."}'),
  ('CHE-0461', 'Electrochemistry', 'Nernst equation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 227–228 (§10.18)', 'Using E = E° + (0.059/z) log[oxidized form] at 25 °C, the electrode potential of a silver electrode in 0.010 mol dm⁻³ Ag⁺ solution (E° = +0.80 V) is', '["+0.74 V", "+0.68 V", "+0.92 V", "+0.80 V"]', 1, 'For Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, z = 1 and the reduced form is solid silver. E = 0.80 + 0.059 × log(0.010) = 0.80 + 0.059 × (−2) = 0.80 − 0.118 = +0.68 V.', '{"0": "This uses z = 2; silver ions gain only one electron.", "2": "log(0.010) is −2, so the potential falls, not rises.", "3": "E equals E° only when [Ag⁺] = 1.00 mol dm⁻³."}'),
  ('CHE-0462', 'Electrochemistry', 'Variation of E with concentration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 226–228 (§10.17)', 'For a metal/metal-ion half-cell Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ ⇌ M, decreasing the concentration of Mⁿ⁺ ions makes the electrode potential', '["less positive (more negative)", "more positive", "unchanged", "exactly zero"]', 0, 'By Le Chatelier''s principle, lowering [Mⁿ⁺] shifts the equilibrium to the left, so the electrode potential becomes less positive. For example, Zn in 0.1 M Zn²⁺ has E below −0.76 V.', '{"1": "Increasing the concentration of the oxidized form makes E more positive.", "2": "Electrode potential depends on ion concentration, as the Nernst equation shows.", "3": "There is no reason for it to become zero."}'),
  ('CHE-0463', 'Electrochemistry', 'Activity series', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 229 (Table 10.2)', 'According to the activity series, which metal reacts with cold water to release hydrogen?', '["Copper", "Zinc", "Iron", "Sodium"]', 3, 'Metals from Li to Na in Table 10.2 react with cold water, replacing hydrogen. Zinc and iron react with steam or acids, and copper does not react with water or dilute acids.', '{"0": "Copper is unreactive with water and dilute acids.", "1": "Zinc reacts with steam but not cold water.", "2": "Iron reacts with steam (or acids), not cold water."}'),
  ('CHE-0464', 'Electrochemistry', 'Activity series', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 228–230 (§10.19–10.20)', 'Which reaction will occur?', '["Cu(s) + Zn²⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + Zn(s)", "Fe(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Fe²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s)", "2Ag(s) + Fe²⁺(aq) → 2Ag⁺(aq) + Fe(s)", "Cu(s) + Mg²⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + Mg(s)"]', 1, 'A metal higher in the activity series (more negative E°) displaces a metal lower in the series from its ions. Iron (−0.44 V) displaces silver (+0.80 V); E°cell = 0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V.', '{"0": "Copper is below zinc in the series, so it cannot displace zinc.", "2": "Silver is below iron, so it cannot displace iron.", "3": "Copper is far below magnesium, so it cannot displace magnesium."}'),
  ('CHE-0465', 'Electrochemistry', 'Photovoltaic cells', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 231 (§10.21)', 'In a photovoltaic cell, the PN junction', '["stores light energy as chemical energy.", "separates the electrons and holes produced by light, creating a voltage.", "converts electricity into light.", "produces heat from sunlight."]', 1, 'Photons excite electrons from the valence band to the conduction band of a semiconductor. The PN junction separates electrons and holes, creating the voltage that drives a current.', '{"0": "A PV cell converts light directly into electrical energy, not chemical energy.", "2": "That is what a light-emitting device does.", "3": "The photovoltaic effect produces voltage and current, not heat."}'),
  ('CHE-0466', 'Electrochemistry', 'Photovoltaic cells', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 231 (§10.21.2)', 'Which is a merit of photovoltaic cells as a source of energy given in the book?', '["They are renewable and use the sun''s energy, independent of fossil fuels.", "They depend on fossil fuels.", "They work without any light.", "They convert heat directly into nuclear energy."]', 0, 'The book lists PV systems as renewable and sustainable: they convert sunlight into electricity as long as light is present and are independent of fossil fuels.', '{"1": "PV systems are independent of fossil fuels.", "2": "The photovoltaic effect needs light.", "3": "PV cells convert light into electricity."}'),
  ('CHE-0467', 'Electrochemistry', 'BOD and DO', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 231 (§10.22)', 'Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) is a measure of', '["the oxygen dissolved in water at any moment.", "the oxygen consumed by microorganisms in oxidizing the organic matter in a water sample over five days.", "the oxygen released by plants in water.", "the oxygen needed to burn the water sample."]', 1, 'BOD is the amount of oxygen consumed by biological oxidation of dissolved organic matter, catalysed by microorganisms, over a five-day period in a sealed sample kept in the dark.', '{"0": "That is dissolved oxygen (DO).", "2": "Plants add oxygen to water; BOD measures oxygen used up.", "3": "BOD measures biological oxidation, not combustion."}'),
  ('CHE-0468', 'Electrochemistry', 'BOD and DO', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, pp. 231–232 (§10.22)', 'Water from a river is found to have a dissolved oxygen (DO) level of 3 mg dm⁻³. According to the book, this water', '["is unpolluted, since DO is above 2 mg dm⁻³.", "is polluted, since DO is below the minimum of 5 mg dm⁻³, which usually goes with a high BOD.", "has a low BOD.", "has more oxygen than normal water."]', 1, 'Normal DO is 5–8 mg dm⁻³, and the minimum acceptable level is 5 mg dm⁻³; below that the water is regarded as polluted. DO and BOD are inversely related, so low DO indicates high BOD.', '{"0": "The book''s minimum is 5 mg dm⁻³, not 2.", "2": "BOD and DO are inversely related, so low DO goes with high BOD.", "3": "3 mg dm⁻³ is below the normal range of 5–8 mg dm⁻³."}'),
  ('CHE-0469', 'Electrochemistry', 'Winkler method', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electrochemistry, p. 232 (§10.22)', 'In the Winkler method for dissolved oxygen, the iodine liberated is titrated with', '["sodium thiosulphate, using starch as indicator", "potassium permanganate, with no indicator", "sodium hydroxide, using phenolphthalein", "silver nitrate, using potassium chromate"]', 0, 'Dissolved oxygen oxidizes KI to iodine, and the iodine formed is titrated with sodium thiosulphate using starch, which gives a dark blue colour, to find the end point.', '{"1": "Permanganate titrations are not used in this method.", "2": "This is an acid–base titration, not the Winkler method.", "3": "This is a titration for chloride ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0470', 'Organic chemistry', 'Classification of hydrocarbons', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 237 (§11.1)', 'Which of the following is an aromatic hydrocarbon?', '["Methylbenzene", "Hexane", "Hex-1-ene", "Cyclohexane"]', 0, 'Aromatic hydrocarbons are based on benzene (C₆H₆) and similar compounds, with a high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio. Methylbenzene (toluene) is a benzene derivative.', '{"1": "Hexane is an acyclic saturated aliphatic hydrocarbon.", "2": "Hex-1-ene is an acyclic unsaturated aliphatic hydrocarbon.", "3": "Cyclohexane is a cyclic aliphatic hydrocarbon (a cycloalkane)."}'),
  ('CHE-0471', 'Organic chemistry', 'Alkanes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 238 (§11.2.1)', 'The molecular formula of the straight-chain alkane with 12 carbon atoms is', '["C₁₂H₂₄", "C₁₂H₂₈", "C₁₂H₂₂", "C₁₂H₂₆"]', 3, 'Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. For n = 12, the formula is C₁₂H₂₆.', '{"0": "C₁₂H₂₄ fits CₙH₂ₙ, the formula of alkenes or cycloalkanes.", "1": "No alkane has more than 2n + 2 hydrogens.", "2": "C₁₂H₂₂ fits CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, as for alkynes."}'),
  ('CHE-0472', 'Organic chemistry', 'Cycloalkanes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 237 and 241 (§11.2.3)', 'The molecular formula of cyclopentane is', '["C₅H₁₂", "C₆H₁₂", "C₅H₈", "C₅H₁₀"]', 3, 'Forming a ring removes two hydrogens from the corresponding alkane, so cycloalkanes have the formula CₙH₂ₙ. Cyclopentane is C₅H₁₀.', '{"0": "C₅H₁₂ is pentane, the open-chain alkane.", "1": "C₆H₁₂ is cyclohexane.", "2": "C₅H₈ has two fewer hydrogens than cyclopentane."}'),
  ('CHE-0473', 'Organic chemistry', 'Alkyl groups', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 239 (Table 11.3)', 'The IUPAC name of the isobutyl group, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–, is', '["1-methylpropyl", "butyl", "1,1-dimethylethyl", "2-methylpropyl"]', 3, 'The isobutyl group attaches through a CH₂; numbering from that carbon, the methyl branch is on carbon 2 of a propyl chain: 2-methylpropyl.', '{"0": "1-methylpropyl is the sec-butyl group, CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)–.", "1": "Butyl is the straight-chain n-butyl group.", "2": "1,1-dimethylethyl is the tert-butyl group, (CH₃)₃C–."}'),
  ('CHE-0474', 'Organic chemistry', 'Nomenclature of alkanes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 239–241 (§11.2.2)', 'The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is', '["4,5-Dimethylhexane", "2-Ethyl-3-methylpentane", "2,3-Diethylbutane", "3,4-Dimethylhexane"]', 3, 'The longest chain has six carbons (hexane). Numbering from either end puts the two methyl groups on carbons 3 and 4, and the prefix di shows two identical substituents.', '{"0": "The chain must be numbered to give the substituents the lowest numbers (3,4).", "1": "This chooses a five-carbon chain; the longest chain has six carbons.", "2": "This chooses a four-carbon chain; the ethyl groups are part of the longest chain."}'),
  ('CHE-0475', 'Organic chemistry', 'Nomenclature of alkanes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 239–241 (§11.2.2)', 'The IUPAC name of (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–CH₃ is', '["2,2-Dimethylbutane", "Hexane", "2-Ethyl-2-methylpropane", "3,3-Dimethylbutane"]', 0, 'The longest chain has four carbons (butane). Numbering from the end nearer the substituents puts both methyl groups on carbon 2, and the number is used twice: 2,2-dimethylbutane.', '{"1": "Hexane is the unbranched isomer; this compound is branched.", "2": "The longest chain is four carbons long, not three.", "3": "Numbering must begin from the end nearer to the substituents."}'),
  ('CHE-0476', 'Organic chemistry', 'Shapes of alkanes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 241–242 (§11.2.4)', 'In alkanes, each carbon atom is', '["sp hybridized with bond angles of 180°.", "sp² hybridized with bond angles of 120°.", "unhybridized.", "sp³ hybridized with bond angles of about 109.5° and C–C bond length 1.54 Å."]', 3, 'Carbon in an alkane has four sp³ hybrid orbitals forming four σ bonds arranged tetrahedrally, with bond angles of 109.5° and a C–C bond length of 1.54 Å.', '{"0": "sp hybridization with 180° angles occurs in alkynes.", "1": "sp² hybridization with 120° angles occurs in alkenes such as ethene.", "2": "Alkane carbons use hybrid orbitals to form four equivalent bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0477', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structure of alkenes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 248–249 (§11.7.1)', 'The π bond in ethene is formed by', '["head-on overlap of two sp² orbitals.", "head-on overlap of two sp³ orbitals.", "lateral (sideways) overlap of two parallel unhybridized p orbitals.", "overlap of an sp² orbital with a hydrogen 1s orbital."]', 2, 'Each carbon in ethene keeps one unhybridized p orbital perpendicular to the plane of its sp² orbitals. These parallel p orbitals overlap sideways to form the π bond.', '{"0": "Head-on overlap of sp² orbitals forms the C–C σ bond.", "1": "Ethene''s carbons are sp² hybridized, not sp³.", "3": "sp²–1s overlap forms the C–H σ bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0478', 'Organic chemistry', 'Physical properties of alkanes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 243 (§11.2.5)', 'At room temperature, the alkane C₂₀H₄₂ is', '["a gas", "a liquid", "a plasma", "a waxy solid"]', 3, 'Alkanes with up to four carbons are gases, C₅ to C₁₇ are liquids, and those from C₁₈ onwards are waxy solids. C₂₀H₄₂ has 20 carbons.', '{"0": "Only alkanes up to C₄ are gases.", "1": "Liquids run from C₅ to C₁₇.", "2": "Alkanes are not plasmas at room temperature."}'),
  ('CHE-0479', 'Organic chemistry', 'Physical properties of alkanes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 243 (§11.2.5)', 'Which isomer of C₅H₁₂ is expected to have the lowest boiling point?', '["Pentane", "2-Methylbutane", "2,2-Dimethylpropane", "All have the same boiling point."]', 2, 'Branched alkanes have lower boiling points than their straight-chain isomers (for example butane −0.5 °C, isobutane −11.7 °C). 2,2-Dimethylpropane is the most branched isomer.', '{"0": "The straight-chain isomer has the highest boiling point.", "1": "2-Methylbutane is less branched than 2,2-dimethylpropane.", "3": "Branching changes the boiling point of isomers."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0480', 'Organic chemistry', 'Physical properties of alkanes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 243 (§11.2.5)', 'Alkanes are', '["soluble in water because they are polar.", "insoluble in all solvents.", "insoluble in water but soluble in non-polar solvents such as benzene and ether.", "soluble in both water and benzene."]', 2, 'Alkanes are non-polar or very weakly polar, so they do not dissolve in polar water but dissolve in non-polar solvents such as hexane, benzene and ether.', '{"0": "Alkanes are non-polar, which is why they are insoluble in water.", "1": "They dissolve readily in non-polar solvents.", "3": "They do not dissolve in water."}'),
  ('CHE-0481', 'Organic chemistry', 'Bond fission', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 244 (§11.3)', 'When a covalent bond breaks so that each atom keeps one of the shared electrons, the process is called', '["hydrolysis", "heterolytic fission, giving ions", "ionization", "homolytic fission, giving free radicals"]', 3, 'In homolytic fission the bond breaks evenly, each atom taking one electron, producing free radicals, which are very reactive because of their unpaired electrons.', '{"0": "Hydrolysis is reaction with water.", "1": "In heterolytic fission one atom takes both electrons, producing oppositely charged ions.", "2": "Ionization forms ions, not radicals."}'),
  ('CHE-0482', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactivity of alkanes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 244 (§11.4)', 'Alkanes (paraffins) are unreactive towards polar reagents mainly because', '["their C–H and C–C bonds are almost non-polar and their σ bonds are strong.", "they have lone pairs on carbon.", "they are ionic compounds.", "they contain π bonds."]', 0, 'The electronegativities of carbon (2.5) and hydrogen (2.1) are close, so C–H and C–C bonds are almost non-polar. Their σ bonds hold electrons tightly between the nuclei and need a lot of energy to break.', '{"1": "Carbon in alkanes has no lone pairs.", "2": "Alkanes are covalent, not ionic.", "3": "Alkanes have only σ bonds; π bonds would make them more reactive."}'),
  ('CHE-0483', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 245 (§11.5.1)', 'The initiation step in the chlorination of methane in UV light is', '["CH₄ + Cl• → CH₃• + HCl", "CH₄ → CH₃⁺ + H⁻", "CH₃• + Cl• → CH₃Cl", "Cl₂ → 2Cl• (homolytic fission by UV light)"]', 3, 'Initiation is the homolytic breaking of the Cl–Cl bond by energy from UV light, producing chlorine free radicals.', '{"0": "A radical attacking methane and forming another radical is a propagation step.", "1": "Free radical substitution does not involve forming ions.", "2": "Two radicals combining is a termination step."}'),
  ('CHE-0484', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 245–246 (§11.5.1)', 'Traces of ethane are found in the products of the chlorination of methane. This is evidence that', '["the reaction goes through free radicals, since two methyl radicals combine in a termination step.", "chlorine is a catalyst.", "methane undergoes addition reactions.", "the reaction is ionic."]', 0, 'Two CH₃• radicals can combine in a termination step to give ethane (C₂H₆). This happens only rarely, but the presence of ethane is evidence of the free radical mechanism.', '{"1": "Chlorine is a reactant and is consumed.", "2": "Alkanes undergo substitution, not addition.", "3": "Combination of methyl radicals shows a radical, not an ionic, mechanism."}'),
  ('CHE-0485', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 245 (§11.5.1)', 'Iodine does not substitute directly into alkanes because', '["the reaction is too slow and is reversible.", "iodine is a gas at room temperature.", "iodine is the most reactive halogen.", "alkanes react only with fluorine."]', 0, 'The book states that iodine does not substitute directly because the reaction is too slow and reversible. Halogen reactivity is F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂.', '{"1": "Iodine is a solid at room temperature, and this is not the reason given.", "2": "Iodine is the least reactive halogen in this series.", "3": "Alkanes also react with chlorine and bromine in light."}'),
  ('CHE-0486', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 246 (§11.5.1)', 'Trichloromethane, formed when methane is chlorinated with excess chlorine, is commonly known as', '["carbon tetrachloride", "vinyl chloride", "chloroform", "methylene chloride"]', 2, 'Successive substitution gives CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄. Trichloromethane (CHCl₃) is chloroform; tetrachloromethane is carbon tetrachloride.', '{"0": "Carbon tetrachloride is CCl₄, tetrachloromethane.", "1": "Vinyl chloride is chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl.", "3": "Methylene chloride is CH₂Cl₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0487', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkenes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 248 (§11.6.2)', 'Ethene is prepared in the laboratory by heating ethanol with excess concentrated sulphuric acid at about', '["25 °C", "80 °C", "500 °C", "180 °C"]', 3, 'Ethanol heated with excess concentrated H₂SO₄ at 180 °C loses a water molecule to form ethene, through ethyl hydrogen sulphate as an intermediate.', '{"0": "At room temperature no dehydration occurs.", "1": "This temperature is too low for the book''s method.", "2": "This is far higher than the temperature used."}'),
  ('CHE-0488', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkenes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 248 (§11.6.2)', 'Heating bromoethane with alcoholic KOH gives ethene. This reaction is', '["a free radical substitution", "an addition reaction", "a dehydrohalogenation (elimination) reaction", "a hydrogenation"]', 2, 'H and Br are removed from adjacent carbon atoms to create a C=C double bond. Removing a small molecule (here HBr) in this way is an elimination; with a hydrogen halide it is called dehydrohalogenation.', '{"0": "No radicals are involved, and a double bond is formed rather than a substitution.", "1": "In an addition reaction atoms are added across a double bond, not removed.", "3": "Hydrogenation adds H₂ to a double bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0489', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactivity of alkenes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 249 (§11.7.2)', 'Alkenes are more reactive than alkanes because', '["the π electrons lie away from the line joining the nuclei, are less firmly held and are exposed to electrophiles.", "alkenes contain more hydrogen atoms.", "σ bonds are weaker than π bonds.", "alkenes are ionic."]', 0, 'In a π bond the electron density lies above and below the line joining the nuclei, so the electrons are held less firmly. The π bond breaks more easily and its exposed electrons attract electrophiles, so alkenes readily undergo electrophilic addition.', '{"1": "Alkenes have fewer hydrogen atoms than the corresponding alkanes.", "2": "A π bond is weaker than a σ bond.", "3": "Alkenes are covalent."}'),
  ('CHE-0490', 'Organic chemistry', 'Inductive effect', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 250–251 (§11.7.3–11.7.4)', 'Which atom or group attached to a carbon chain shows a negative inductive effect (−I)?', '["–CH₃", "–C₂H₅", "–Cl", "–C(CH₃)₃"]', 2, 'Electron-withdrawing species such as halogen atoms have a −I effect: chlorine pulls bonding electrons towards itself, leaving the carbon slightly positive. Alkyl groups are electron donating (+I).', '{"0": "Methyl is an alkyl group with a +I effect.", "1": "Ethyl is an alkyl group with a +I effect.", "3": "tert-Butyl is an alkyl group with a +I effect."}'),
  ('CHE-0491', 'Organic chemistry', 'Carbocations', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 250 (§11.7.3)', 'The carbocation (CH₃)₂CH⁺ is classified as', '["secondary", "primary", "methyl", "tertiary"]', 0, 'The positively charged carbon is bonded directly to two carbon atoms (two methyl groups), so it is a secondary (2°) carbocation.', '{"1": "A primary carbocation has one alkyl group on the positive carbon.", "2": "A methyl carbocation, CH₃⁺, has only hydrogens on the positive carbon.", "3": "A tertiary carbocation has three alkyl groups on the positive carbon."}'),
  ('CHE-0492', 'Organic chemistry', 'Halogenation of alkenes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 252 (§11.8.1)', 'Decolourization of red-brown bromine solution is used as a test for', '["alkanes", "alcohols", "carbon–carbon unsaturation", "carboxylic acids"]', 2, 'When bromine is added to ethene, its red-brown colour is discharged at once as bromine adds across the C=C bond. This reaction identifies unsaturation in an organic compound.', '{"0": "Alkanes do not decolourize bromine in the dark, because they have no double bonds.", "1": "This is not the book''s test for alcohols.", "3": "This is not a test for carboxylic acids."}'),
  ('CHE-0493', 'Organic chemistry', 'Halogenation of alkenes', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 252 (§11.8.1)', 'Bromine is a non-polar molecule, yet it acts as an electrophile towards ethene because', '["bromine is ionic.", "light breaks Br₂ into radicals.", "the electron-rich double bond repels the Br–Br bonding electrons, inducing a dipole so the nearer Br atom becomes δ+.", "ethene is positively charged."]', 2, 'As Br₂ approaches the C=C bond, the high electron density repels the Br–Br electron pair away from the nearer bromine, which becomes slightly positive (an induced dipole) and is attacked by the π electrons.', '{"0": "Br₂ is a covalent, non-polar molecule.", "1": "Addition to alkenes is electrophilic here, not a radical reaction.", "3": "Ethene is neutral; it is the π electrons that matter."}'),
  ('CHE-0494', 'Organic chemistry', 'Hydrohalogenation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 253 (§11.8.1)', 'The rate of addition of hydrogen halides to alkenes increases in the order', '["HI < HBr < HCl < HF", "All hydrogen halides add at the same rate.", "HCl < HF < HI < HBr", "HF < HCl < HBr < HI"]', 3, 'The book states that all hydrogen halides add across C=C in the same way, but the rate of reaction increases from HF to HI.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order.", "1": "The rates differ, increasing from HF to HI.", "2": "This order does not follow the halogen group."}'),
  ('CHE-0495', 'Organic chemistry', 'Markovnikov''s rule', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 253–254 (§11.8.1)', 'The major product of the addition of HCl to 2-methylpropene, (CH₃)₂C=CH₂, is', '["1-chloro-2-methylpropane", "2-chloro-2-methylpropane", "1-chlorobutane", "2-chlorobutane"]', 1, 'By Markovnikov''s rule, H adds to the double-bonded carbon with more hydrogens (=CH₂), giving the tertiary carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺. Cl⁻ then attacks it, giving (CH₃)₃CCl, 2-chloro-2-methylpropane.', '{"0": "This would come from the less stable primary carbocation; it is the minor product.", "2": "The carbon skeleton is branched; it cannot become a straight chain.", "3": "The carbon skeleton of 2-methylpropene is branched, not a straight four-carbon chain."}'),
  ('CHE-0496', 'Organic chemistry', 'Hydrogenation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 254 (§11.8.1)', 'On an industrial scale, the hydrogenation of alkenes over a nickel catalyst is used to', '["make PVC.", "convert vegetable oil into margarine (banaspati ghee).", "make ethanol.", "make chloroform."]', 1, 'Addition of hydrogen to C=C bonds in the presence of Ni converts liquid vegetable oils into solid margarine (banaspati ghee).', '{"0": "PVC is made by polymerizing chloroethene.", "2": "Ethanol is made by hydrating ethene with steam.", "3": "Chloroform is made by chlorinating methane."}'),
  ('CHE-0497', 'Organic chemistry', 'Hydration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 254–255 (§11.8.1)', 'When propene reacts with steam in the presence of an acid catalyst, the major product (following Markovnikov''s rule) is', '["propan-1-ol", "propan-2-ol", "propanal", "propane"]', 1, 'Hydration goes through a carbocation, like hydrohalogenation. H⁺ adds to the CH₂ end, giving the more stable secondary carbocation, which is attacked to give CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, propan-2-ol.', '{"0": "Propan-1-ol would come from the less stable primary carbocation.", "2": "Hydration gives an alcohol, not an aldehyde.", "3": "Propane is the product of hydrogenation."}'),
  ('CHE-0498', 'Organic chemistry', 'Epoxidation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 255 (§11.8.1)', 'Ethene reacts with oxygen over a silver catalyst to form a product that gives ethane-1,2-diol on acid hydrolysis. The product is', '["ethanol", "ethylene oxide (an epoxide)", "ethanal", "ethanoic acid"]', 1, 'Epoxidation of ethene with O₂ and a metal catalyst (Ag) gives ethylene oxide, a three-membered cyclic ether. Acid hydrolysis of the epoxide gives ethane-1,2-diol.', '{"0": "Ethanol is made by hydration, and it is not hydrolysed to a diol.", "2": "Ethanal is a product of ozonolysis, not epoxidation.", "3": "Ethanoic acid is formed by oxidizing ethanol with acidified dichromate."}'),
  ('CHE-0499', 'Organic chemistry', 'Ozonolysis', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 255–256 (§11.8.1)', 'Ozonolysis of but-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, followed by reductive workup (Zn/H₂O) gives', '["methanal and propanal", "two molecules of ethanal", "propanone and methanal", "one molecule of butanal"]', 1, 'Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C bond and turns each double-bonded carbon into a C=O group. But-2-ene is symmetrical, so it gives two molecules of CH₃CHO (ethanal).', '{"0": "This would come from but-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃.", "2": "This would come from 2-methylpropene, (CH₃)₂C=CH₂.", "3": "Ozonolysis breaks the molecule at the double bond into two carbonyl compounds."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0500', 'Organic chemistry', 'Polymerization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 256 (§11.8.1)', 'The repeating unit of poly(chloroethene), PVC, is', '["–CH₂–CH₂–", "–CH₂–CHCl–", "–CHCl–CHCl–", "–CH=CHCl–"]', 1, 'In an addition polymer the repeating unit is the monomer with its C=C changed to C–C. Chloroethene (CH₂=CHCl) gives the repeating unit –CH₂–CHCl–.', '{"0": "This is the repeating unit of poly(ethene).", "2": "This would need a monomer with two chlorines, CHCl=CHCl.", "3": "The C=C double bond becomes a single bond in the polymer."}'),
  ('CHE-0501', 'Organic chemistry', 'Conjugated dienes', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 257 (§11.9)', 'Which compound is a conjugated diene?', '["CH₂=CH–CH=CH₂", "CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH=CH₂", "CH₂=C=CH₂", "CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃"]', 0, 'In a conjugated diene the two C=C bonds are separated by one single bond, allowing π electrons to delocalize over the system. Buta-1,3-diene is the book''s example and is used to make synthetic rubber.', '{"1": "The double bonds are separated by a CH₂ group (two single bonds), so they are isolated, not conjugated.", "2": "Here the double bonds share a carbon atom; there is no single bond between them.", "3": "This has only one double bond."}'),
  ('CHE-0502', 'Organic chemistry', 'Isomerism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 258 (§11.10)', 'How many structural isomers does pentane (C₅H₁₂) have?', '["2", "3", "4", "5"]', 1, 'The book states that pentane has three isomers: pentane, 2-methylbutane (isopentane) and 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane).', '{"0": "Butane (C₄H₁₀) has two isomers.", "2": "C₅H₁₂ has only three possible carbon skeletons.", "3": "There are only three possible arrangements."}'),
  ('CHE-0503', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 258 (§11.10.1)', 'Butane and 2-methylpropane are', '["position isomers", "chain isomers", "functional group isomers", "metamers"]', 1, 'Both are C₄H₁₀ but differ in the carbon chain: one is straight, the other branched. This is chain isomerism.', '{"0": "Position isomers have the same chain with a group in a different position.", "2": "Neither compound has a functional group.", "3": "Metamers differ in the alkyl groups on either side of a functional group."}'),
  ('CHE-0504', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 259 (§11.10.1)', 'But-1-ene and but-2-ene are an example of', '["position isomerism", "chain isomerism", "functional group isomerism", "metamerism"]', 0, 'They have the same carbon chain and the same functional group (C=C), but the double bond is in a different position. This is position isomerism.', '{"1": "The carbon chain is the same unbranched four-carbon chain in both.", "2": "Both contain the same functional group, C=C.", "3": "Metamerism involves different alkyl groups around a functional group such as –O– or C=O."}'),
  ('CHE-0505', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 259 (§11.10.1)', 'Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) and methoxymethane (CH₃OCH₃) are', '["chain isomers", "functional group isomers", "position isomers", "tautomers"]', 1, 'Both have the formula C₂H₆O, but one is an alcohol (–OH) and the other an ether (–O–). Same formula, different functional groups: functional group isomerism.', '{"0": "The difference is the functional group, not the carbon chain.", "2": "The functional groups themselves differ, not just their positions.", "3": "Tautomerism involves a proton shifting within a molecule."}'),
  ('CHE-0506', 'Organic chemistry', 'Structural isomerism', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 259 (§11.10.1)', 'Pentan-2-one and pentan-3-one (both C₅H₁₀O) are', '["chain isomers", "metamers", "functional group isomers", "geometrical isomers"]', 1, 'Both are ketones of the same homologous series, but the carbon atoms are distributed unequally on either side of the C=O group (CH₃ and C₃H₇ against C₂H₅ and C₂H₅). This is metamerism.', '{"0": "Both have an unbranched five-carbon chain.", "2": "Both contain the same ketone functional group.", "3": "Geometrical isomerism is a kind of stereoisomerism involving positions in space, not different connectivity."}'),
  ('CHE-0507', 'Organic chemistry', 'Organic redox reactions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, pp. 260–261 (§11.11)', 'Which reaction is an organic reduction?', '["C₂H₅OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O", "2C₂H₆ + 7O₂ → 4CO₂ + 6H₂O", "CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃–CH₃", "CH₂=CH₂ + O₃ → ozonide"]', 2, 'In organic reduction hydrogen is added or oxygen is removed. Adding H₂ across the C=C bond of ethene is the book''s example of organic reduction.', '{"0": "Converting ethanol to ethanoic acid with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ is oxidation.", "1": "Combustion adds oxygen; it is oxidation.", "3": "Ozonolysis is oxidative cleavage."}'),
  ('CHE-0508', 'Organic chemistry', 'Organic redox reactions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Hydrocarbons, p. 261 (§11.11.1)', 'A primary alcohol such as ethanol is oxidized to a carboxylic acid by', '["alcoholic KOH", "H₂ over Ni", "acidified K₂Cr₂O₇", "Br₂ in CCl₄"]', 2, 'The book gives the oxidation of primary alcohols with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ to make carboxylic acids, for example ethanol to ethanoic acid.', '{"0": "Alcoholic KOH is used for dehydrohalogenation.", "1": "H₂/Ni is used for reduction (hydrogenation).", "3": "Br₂ in CCl₄ adds to alkenes."}'),
  ('CHE-0509', 'p-block elements', 'Reactivity of nitrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 266 (§12.1)', 'Apart from its very strong triple bond, a second reason the book gives for the low reactivity of N₂ is that', '["N₂ is an ionic molecule.", "the N≡N bond is non-polar because both atoms have the same electronegativity.", "nitrogen has no valence electrons.", "N₂ is a liquid at room temperature."]', 1, 'The two nitrogen atoms are identical, so the electronegativity difference is zero and the bonding electrons are shared equally. The non-polar bond offers no site for attack by polar reagents.', '{"0": "N₂ is a covalent molecule with a triple bond.", "2": "Nitrogen has five valence electrons (2s² 2p³).", "3": "Nitrogen is a gas at room temperature (boiling point −195.8 °C)."}'),
  ('CHE-0510', 'p-block elements', 'Nitrogen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 265', 'In the laboratory, nitrogen gas can be prepared by', '["heating potassium chlorate with MnO₂.", "electrolysing water.", "reacting zinc with dilute HCl.", "heating a solution of ammonium nitrite slowly."]', 3, 'The book gives NH₄NO₂(aq) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) on slow heating. Industrially, nitrogen is obtained by liquefying air.', '{"0": "KClO₃ with MnO₂ gives oxygen.", "1": "Electrolysis of water gives hydrogen and oxygen.", "2": "Zinc with dilute HCl gives hydrogen."}'),
  ('CHE-0511', 'p-block elements', 'Nitrogen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 266 (Interesting information)', 'Ships carrying hydrocarbons or edible oils use a blanket of nitrogen over the cargo because nitrogen', '["is a strong oxidizing agent.", "is lighter than hydrogen.", "is inert and protects the cargo from oxygen and moisture.", "reacts with the oil to preserve it."]', 2, 'Nitrogen''s low reactivity makes it useful as an inert blanket that keeps oxygen and moisture away from flammable or perishable cargo, and as an inert atmosphere in the laboratory.', '{"0": "Nitrogen is very unreactive, not an oxidizing agent.", "1": "Nitrogen (M = 28) is much heavier than hydrogen (M = 2).", "3": "Its value is that it does not react."}'),
  ('CHE-0512', 'p-block elements', 'Basicity of ammonia', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, pp. 266–267 (§12.2.1)', 'Aqueous ammonia is a weak base because', '["it has no lone pair.", "it reacts completely with water.", "it donates protons to water.", "its basicity constant is low (Kb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵), so the equilibrium NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ lies far to the left."]', 3, 'Ammonia accepts a proton from water, but only to a small extent: Kb is 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ and the equilibrium lies far to the left, so few OH⁻ ions are produced.', '{"0": "Ammonia has a lone pair on nitrogen; that is what accepts a proton.", "1": "If it reacted completely it would be a strong base.", "2": "Ammonia accepts protons; it does not donate them in water."}'),
  ('CHE-0513', 'p-block elements', 'Ammonium salts', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 267 (§12.2.3)', 'In the laboratory preparation 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃, which species acts as the Brønsted–Lowry acid?', '["Cl⁻", "OH⁻", "NH₄⁺", "Ca²⁺"]', 2, 'NH₄⁺ donates H⁺ (acid) and OH⁻ accepts it (base), displacing ammonia gas from the ammonium salt.', '{"0": "Cl⁻ is a spectator ion.", "1": "OH⁻ accepts the proton, so it is the base.", "3": "Ca²⁺ is a spectator ion."}'),
  ('CHE-0514', 'p-block elements', 'Ammonium salts', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 267 (Figure 12.3)', 'In Figure 12.3, the ammonia produced from NH₄Cl and Ca(OH)₂ is passed through a tower of', '["CuSO₄, to absorb ammonia", "concentrated H₂SO₄, to dry the gas", "water, to dissolve impurities", "CaO, to dry the gas"]', 3, 'The apparatus passes the ammonia through calcium oxide, a basic drying agent that removes water without reacting with ammonia.', '{"0": "Absorbing ammonia would defeat the purpose of collecting it.", "1": "Concentrated H₂SO₄ would react with ammonia (a base) to form ammonium sulphate.", "2": "Ammonia is very soluble in water and would dissolve."}'),
  ('CHE-0515', 'p-block elements', 'Oxides of nitrogen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 268 (Table 12.2)', 'In which oxide does nitrogen have the oxidation state +1?', '["N₂O", "NO₂", "NO", "N₂O₅"]', 0, 'In N₂O, 2N + (−2) = 0, so N = +1. Nitrogen is +2 in NO, +4 in NO₂ and +5 in N₂O₅.', '{"1": "N is +4 in NO₂.", "2": "N is +2 in NO.", "3": "N is +5 in N₂O₅."}'),
  ('CHE-0516', 'p-block elements', 'Oxides of nitrogen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 268 (Table 12.2)', 'Nitrous oxide (N₂O), "laughing gas", is used as', '["a bleaching agent", "a rocket fuel oxidizer only", "a dental anaesthetic and a propellant for whipped cream", "a fertilizer"]', 2, 'Table 12.2 lists N₂O as a colourless, sweet-smelling gas used as a dental anaesthetic, a propellant for whipped cream, and in making NaN₃.', '{"0": "Bleaching is not a use given for N₂O.", "1": "Table 12.2 lists rocket propellant use for NO₂/N₂O₄.", "3": "N₂O is not used as a fertilizer."}'),
  ('CHE-0517', 'p-block elements', 'Oxides of nitrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 268 (Table 12.2)', 'Nitric oxide (NO) is described in the book as', '["a paramagnetic gas that acts as a biochemical messenger and lowers blood pressure", "a colourless liquid used as rocket fuel", "laughing gas", "a diamagnetic, reddish-brown gas"]', 0, 'NO is a colourless, paramagnetic gas (it has an odd number of electrons) that acts as a biochemical messenger, for example in lowering blood pressure.', '{"1": "N₂O₄ is the colourless liquid used as a rocket propellant.", "2": "Laughing gas is N₂O.", "3": "NO is colourless and paramagnetic; NO₂ is the reddish-brown gas."}'),
  ('CHE-0518', 'p-block elements', 'Oxides of nitrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 268 (Table 12.2)', 'For the equilibrium 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g), cooling the mixture', '["makes it darker brown, because more NO₂ forms.", "has no effect on the colour.", "turns it green.", "makes it paler, because more colourless N₂O₄ forms."]', 3, 'NO₂ is reddish-brown and N₂O₄ is colourless. Table 12.2 shows cooling favours N₂O₄ and heating favours NO₂, so cooling makes the mixture paler.', '{"0": "Heating, not cooling, favours brown NO₂.", "1": "The two species have different colours, so a shift changes the colour.", "2": "Neither species is green."}'),
  ('CHE-0519', 'p-block elements', 'Sources of NOx', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 269 (§12.4)', 'A natural source of nitrogen oxides in the atmosphere is', '["motor vehicles", "power plants", "lightning, in which N₂ and O₂ combine to form NO", "welding"]', 2, 'Natural sources include lightning (N₂ + O₂ → 2NO), volcanic eruptions, forest fires and soil bacteria. Vehicles, power plants and welding are man-made sources.', '{"0": "Vehicle engines are the main anthropogenic source.", "1": "Power plants burning fossil fuels are an anthropogenic source.", "3": "Welding is listed among the anthropogenic sources."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0520', 'p-block elements', 'Photochemical smog', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 269 (§12.5.1)', 'Photochemical (Los Angeles) smog', '["forms from NOx and volatile organic compounds in sunlight, and is oxidizing in nature.", "is caused only by sulphur dioxide.", "contains no ozone.", "is reducing in nature and forms in cold, foggy weather."]', 0, 'Photochemical smog forms from NOx and VOCs in sunlight. It is oxidizing and contains photochemical oxidants such as NO₂, ozone and peroxyacyl nitrates (PANs).', '{"1": "Its key ingredients are NOx and VOCs.", "2": "Ozone is one of its main oxidants.", "3": "Photochemical smog is oxidizing and needs sunlight."}'),
  ('CHE-0521', 'p-block elements', 'Photochemical smog', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 269 (§12.5.1)', 'In the formation of photochemical smog, ground-level ozone is formed by the steps', '["CO + O₂ → CO₂ + O", "N₂ + O₃ → N₂O₃", "SO₂ + O₂ → SO₃", "NO₂ + sunlight → NO + O, then O + O₂ → O₃"]', 3, 'Sunlight splits NO₂ into NO and an oxygen atom; the oxygen atom then combines with O₂ to form ozone.', '{"0": "This reaction is not part of the smog sequence.", "1": "This is not one of the book''s smog reactions.", "2": "This is the oxidation of SO₂, not ozone formation."}'),
  ('CHE-0522', 'p-block elements', 'Peroxyacyl nitrates', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 269 (§12.5.2)', 'In peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN), RC(O)OONO₂, the R group is', '["CH₃", "C₆H₅", "H", "OH"]', 0, 'The book states that the R group in peroxyacetyl nitrate is CH₃; other hydrocarbon chains give other PANs, and C₆H₅ gives peroxybenzoyl nitrate (PBN).', '{"1": "C₆H₅ gives peroxybenzoyl nitrate (PBN).", "2": "R = H would not give the acetyl compound.", "3": "R is a hydrocarbon group."}'),
  ('CHE-0523', 'p-block elements', 'Peroxyacyl nitrates', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 270 (Figure 12.5)', 'In the mechanism of PAN formation, the final step is the reaction of', '["an aldehyde with ozone", "an acyl radical with water", "a peroxyacyl radical with NO₂", "a hydrocarbon with N₂"]', 2, 'Ozone oxidizes a hydrocarbon to an aldehyde; the aldehyde reacts with a hydroxyl radical to give an acyl radical; the acyl radical adds O₂ to give a peroxyacyl radical, which finally reacts with NO₂ to form PAN.', '{"0": "Ozone oxidizing the hydrocarbon to an aldehyde is an early step.", "1": "The acyl radical reacts with oxygen, not water.", "3": "N₂ does not take part; NOx does."}'),
  ('CHE-0524', 'p-block elements', 'Catalytic converter', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, pp. 270–271 (§12.6)', 'In a three-way catalytic converter, the catalytic metals dispersed on alumina are', '["Fe, Cu and Zn", "V, Cr and Ti", "Pt, Pd and Rh", "Ni, Co and Mn"]', 2, 'The honeycomb has a layer of alumina for high surface area, on which the noble metals platinum, palladium and rhodium are dispersed.', '{"0": "These are not the converter metals.", "1": "Vanadium is the contact-process catalyst, not a converter metal.", "3": "These are not the metals used."}'),
  ('CHE-0525', 'p-block elements', 'Catalytic converter', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 271 (§12.6)', 'Which reaction is catalysed by Pt/Rh in a catalytic converter to remove nitrogen oxides?', '["4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O", "N₂ + O₂ → 2NO", "2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂", "2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂"]', 3, 'NO is reduced to N₂ while CO is oxidized to CO₂ over Pt/Rh. CO and hydrocarbons are also oxidized to CO₂ and water over Pt/Pd.', '{"0": "This is the Ostwald oxidation of ammonia, not a converter reaction.", "1": "This forms NO, as in lightning or engines; the converter removes NO.", "2": "This makes NO₂, a pollutant."}'),
  ('CHE-0526', 'p-block elements', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 271 (Table 12.3)', 'Which statement correctly compares nitrification and denitrification?', '["Nitrification occurs under aerobic conditions; denitrification occurs under anaerobic conditions.", "Both occur only under anaerobic conditions.", "Nitrification converts NO₃⁻ to N₂.", "Denitrification converts NH₄⁺ to NO₃⁻."]', 0, 'Nitrifying bacteria oxidize NH₄⁺ to NO₂⁻ and NO₃⁻ under aerobic conditions. Denitrifying bacteria reduce NO₃⁻ and NO₂⁻ back to N₂ under anaerobic conditions.', '{"1": "Nitrification needs aerobic conditions.", "2": "That conversion is denitrification.", "3": "That conversion is nitrification."}'),
  ('CHE-0527', 'p-block elements', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 271 (Table 12.3)', 'The Anammox process, important in wastewater treatment, converts', '["NH₄⁺ and NO₂⁻ into N₂ gas", "N₂ into NH₃", "NO₃⁻ into NH₄⁺", "NH₃ into NO₃⁻ only"]', 0, 'The book notes that Anammox oxidizes NH₄⁺ with NO₂⁻ to form N₂ gas, which is useful in wastewater treatment.', '{"1": "Converting N₂ into NH₃ is nitrogen fixation (or the Haber process).", "2": "This is not the Anammox reaction.", "3": "Oxidizing ammonia to nitrate is nitrification."}'),
  ('CHE-0528', 'p-block elements', 'Reactivity of sulfur', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 272 (§12.8.1)', 'Sulfur forms S₈ rings and chains rather than S=S molecules because', '["poor overlap of its orbitals makes S=S double bonds weak, so it forms single bonds to other sulfur atoms (catenation).", "sulfur is a metal.", "sulfur has no lone pairs.", "sulfur atoms are too small to bond."]', 0, 'Because its orbitals overlap poorly in double bonds, sulfur prefers single bonds to other sulfur atoms, building larger structures by catenation, such as the crown-shaped S₈ molecule.', '{"1": "Sulfur is a non-metal.", "2": "Sulfur atoms have lone pairs.", "3": "Sulfur atoms are larger than oxygen atoms; size is not preventing bonding."}'),
  ('CHE-0529', 'p-block elements', 'Oxidation states of sulfur', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 273 (§12.9)', 'Although the +6 state (as in SO₃) is thermodynamically the most stable, burning sulfur in air gives mainly SO₂ because', '["SO₂ is thermodynamically more stable than SO₃.", "oxygen cannot oxidize sulfur beyond +2.", "kinetic limitations prevent SO₃ from forming readily without high energy or a catalyst.", "sulfur cannot have an oxidation state of +6."]', 2, 'Formation of SO₃ is slow at standard temperatures, so the +4 state in SO₂ is the more frequent product. A catalyst such as vanadium in the contact process speeds up SO₃ formation.', '{"0": "The book says the +6 state is the thermodynamically more stable one.", "1": "Oxygen oxidizes sulfur to +4 directly.", "3": "Sulfur is +6 in SO₃, SO₄²⁻ and H₂SO₄."}'),
  ('CHE-0530', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of sulfur', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 274 (§12.10)', 'When sulfur burns in air,', '["it forms SO₃ with a yellow flame.", "it forms H₂S.", "it does not react.", "it forms SO₂ with a blue flame."]', 3, 'Sulfur burns in air with a blue flame to form SO₂. Forming SO₃ needs a higher temperature and a catalyst.', '{"0": "SO₃ needs a catalyst; burning gives SO₂ with a blue flame.", "1": "H₂S needs hydrogen.", "2": "Sulfur does burn in air."}'),
  ('CHE-0531', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of sulfur', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 274 (§12.10)', 'Silver articles tarnish in air containing sulfur compounds because sulfur', '["reduces silver to Ag⁺.", "forms silver sulphate.", "forms a coating of silver sulphide, Ag₂S, acting as an oxidizing agent.", "dissolves silver."]', 2, 'With less electronegative elements, sulfur acts as an oxidizing agent and forms sulphides: 2Ag + S → Ag₂S, a dark coating.', '{"0": "Forming Ag⁺ from Ag is oxidation, and sulfur is the oxidizing agent.", "1": "The product is silver sulphide, not sulphate.", "3": "A surface coating forms; the silver does not dissolve."}'),
  ('CHE-0532', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of sulfur', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 274 (§12.10)', 'Sulfur reacts with potassium cyanide to form', '["potassium sulphide", "potassium sulphate", "hydrogen cyanide", "potassium thiocyanate (KSCN), containing a pseudohalide ion"]', 3, 'KCN + S → KSCN. Sulfur converts cyanide into thiocyanate, which is known as a pseudohalide.', '{"0": "The product keeps the cyanide group: KSCN.", "1": "No oxygen is involved.", "2": "No hydrogen is present to form HCN."}'),
  ('CHE-0533', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions of sulfur', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 274 (§12.10)', 'Sulfur hexafluoride, SF₆, is used as an insulating gas in electrical devices because it is', '["very reactive", "coloured", "very unreactive", "a good conductor"]', 2, 'Sulfur reacts directly with fluorine to form SF₆, a gas that is very unreactive; it is used as an insulator gas in electrical equipment.', '{"0": "Its usefulness comes from its lack of reactivity.", "1": "Colour is not the reason; SF₆ is colourless.", "3": "An insulating gas must not conduct."}'),
  ('CHE-0534', 'p-block elements', 'Uses of sulfur', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 275 (§12.11.1)', 'In the vulcanization of rubber, sulfur', '["removes carbon from rubber.", "dissolves the rubber.", "makes rubber softer and stickier.", "forms disulphide (S–S) cross-links between rubber chains, making the rubber stronger."]', 3, 'Sulfur acts as a cross-linker, joining rubber molecular chains through disulphide bridges, which improves the strength of rubber.', '{"0": "Sulfur adds bridges; it does not remove carbon.", "1": "Sulfur does not dissolve the rubber.", "2": "Cross-linking makes rubber stronger, not softer."}'),
  ('CHE-0535', 'p-block elements', 'Uses of sulfur', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 275 (§12.11.3)', 'In gunpowder (75% KNO₃, 15% charcoal, 10% sulfur), the role of potassium nitrate is', '["the oxidizer", "a catalyst", "the main fuel", "an inert filler"]', 0, 'Charcoal carbon is the main fuel, potassium nitrate is the oxidizer, and sulfur is an additional fuel that makes the powder burn faster.', '{"1": "KNO₃ is consumed in the reaction, so it is not a catalyst.", "2": "Charcoal is the main fuel.", "3": "KNO₃ reacts; it is the source of oxygen."}'),
  ('CHE-0536', 'p-block elements', 'Sulfur in organic compounds', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 276 (§12.12.3)', 'Mercaptans (thiols) are added to natural gas in order to', '["increase its heat of combustion.", "remove water from it.", "give it an odour so that leaks can be detected.", "make it burn with a blue flame."]', 2, 'The book notes that mercaptans are used to give odour to natural gas.', '{"0": "They are added in tiny amounts for smell, not energy.", "1": "They are not drying agents.", "3": "Flame colour is not the purpose."}'),
  ('CHE-0537', 'p-block elements', 'Contact process', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 277 (§12.13.1)', 'In the contact process, gelatinous Fe(OH)₃ is used in the purification unit to', '["dry the SO₂", "absorb SO₃", "convert SO₂ into SO₃", "absorb arsenic oxide, which would poison the catalyst"]', 3, 'If pyrite is used, the SO₂ contains impurities including arsenic oxide, which reduce the catalyst''s efficiency. Fe(OH)₃ in the arsenic purifier absorbs As₂O₃.', '{"0": "Drying is not the role of ferric hydroxide here.", "1": "SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid.", "2": "SO₂ is converted to SO₃ over V₂O₅ in the contact tower."}'),
  ('CHE-0538', 'p-block elements', 'Contact process', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 277 (§12.13.1)', 'V₂O₅ catalyses the oxidation of SO₂ to SO₃ by', '["being permanently reduced to vanadium metal.", "oxidizing SO₂ to SO₃ while being reduced to V₂O₄, which is then reoxidized to V₂O₅ by oxygen.", "absorbing SO₃ to form vanadium sulphate.", "providing hydrogen ions."]', 1, 'Step 1: SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄ (V⁵⁺ is reduced to V⁴⁺). Step 2: V₂O₄ + ½O₂ → V₂O₅ regenerates the catalyst.', '{"0": "The catalyst is regenerated, not used up.", "2": "SO₃ leaves the converter; it is not trapped by the catalyst.", "3": "The catalytic cycle is a redox cycle of vanadium oxides."}'),
  ('CHE-0539', 'p-block elements', 'Contact process', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 277 (§12.13.1)', 'According to Chapter 12, purified SO₂ and air enter the first converter stage of the contact tower at', '["420–450 °C and 1–2 atm", "25 °C and 200 atm", "1000 °C and 1 atm", "200 °C and 300 atm"]', 0, 'The gases, preheated to 420–450 °C, are fed to the first converter stage at 1–2 atm, where they contact the V₂O₅ catalyst.', '{"1": "Room temperature is far too slow, and such high pressure is not used.", "2": "At 1000 °C the yield of SO₃ would be very low.", "3": "These conditions are not used in the contact process."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0540', 'p-block elements', 'Contact process', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, pp. 277–278 (§12.13.1)', 'Oleum (fuming sulphuric acid) has the formula', '["H₂S₂O₇", "H₂SO₃", "H₂S₂O₃", "SO₃·H₂O"]', 0, 'SO₃ is dissolved in concentrated H₂SO₄ to form oleum: H₂SO₄ + SO₃ → H₂S₂O₇. Oleum is then diluted with water to give concentrated H₂SO₄.', '{"1": "H₂SO₃ is sulphurous acid.", "2": "H₂S₂O₃ is thiosulphuric acid.", "3": "SO₃·H₂O is simply H₂SO₄."}'),
  ('CHE-0541', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 276 (§12.13)', 'The structure of H₂SO₄ is', '["trigonal planar with three S=O bonds", "tetrahedral, with two S–OH bonds and two S=O bonds", "linear", "square planar"]', 1, 'Figure 12.10 shows H₂SO₄ as tetrahedral around sulphur, with two S–O(H) single bonds and two S=O double bonds.', '{"0": "Trigonal planar with three S=O bonds is SO₃.", "2": "Four groups around sulphur cannot be linear.", "3": "The arrangement around sulphur is tetrahedral."}'),
  ('CHE-0542', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 278 (§12.13.3)', 'Sulphuric acid self-ionizes, 2H₂SO₄ ⇌ H₃SO₄⁺ + HSO₄⁻, with K = 2.7 × 10⁻⁴. Because this constant is larger than that of water, sulphuric acid', '["cannot dissolve any substance.", "can be used as a non-aqueous protic solvent.", "is a weak acid.", "is a base."]', 1, 'The book notes that the equilibrium constant for autoprotolysis is greater than that of water, which allows sulphuric acid to be used as a non-aqueous protic solvent.', '{"0": "Its ionizing power makes it a useful solvent.", "2": "Its pKa₁ of −2 shows it is a strong acid.", "3": "It is a strong acid."}'),
  ('CHE-0543', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 278 (§12.13.3)', 'The pKa₁ of H₂SO₄ is −2 and the pKa₂ (for HSO₄⁻) is 1.92. This shows that', '["HSO₄⁻ is a stronger acid than H₂SO₄.", "H₂SO₄ is a strong acid but HSO₄⁻ is a far weaker acid.", "both protons are lost equally easily.", "H₂SO₄ is a weak acid."]', 1, 'A negative pKa₁ means the first ionization is essentially complete (strong acid). The positive pKa₂ shows HSO₄⁻ is a much weaker acid.', '{"0": "A higher pKa means a weaker acid, so HSO₄⁻ is weaker.", "2": "The two pKa values differ by about 4 units.", "3": "pKa₁ = −2 indicates a strong acid."}'),
  ('CHE-0544', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, pp. 278–279 (§12.13.3)', 'When concentrated sulphuric acid is added to sugar, a black porous mass of carbon ("carbon snake") forms. This shows that the acid acts as', '["a reducing agent", "a dehydrating agent", "a catalyst only", "a base"]', 1, 'Concentrated H₂SO₄ removes water from sucrose (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ → 12C + 11H₂O), leaving carbon. It is a powerful dehydrating agent.', '{"0": "The acid removes the elements of water; it is not acting as a reducing agent.", "2": "The acid absorbs the water and does more than catalyse.", "3": "Sulphuric acid is an acid, not a base."}'),
  ('CHE-0545', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 279 (§12.13.3)', 'Concentrated sulphuric acid reacts with sodium chloride to produce', '["chlorine gas", "hydrogen chloride gas", "sulphur dioxide", "sodium metal"]', 1, 'NaCl(s) + H₂SO₄(l) → NaHSO₄(s) + HCl(g). This reaction is a source of hydrogen chloride gas.', '{"0": "Chlorine is not formed with NaCl under these conditions.", "2": "SO₂ forms when the acid acts as an oxidizing agent, as with copper.", "3": "Sodium metal is not produced."}'),
  ('CHE-0546', 'p-block elements', 'Sulphuric acid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 279 (§12.13.3)', 'Copper does not react with dilute H₂SO₄ but reacts with hot concentrated H₂SO₄ to give', '["CuSO₄, H₂ and H₂O", "CuSO₄, SO₂ and H₂O", "CuO and H₂S", "Cu₂S and O₂"]', 1, 'Hot concentrated H₂SO₄ is a moderately strong oxidizing agent: Cu + 2H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + SO₂ + 2H₂O. Copper is below hydrogen and cannot release H₂ from acids.', '{"0": "Copper is below hydrogen in the series and does not liberate hydrogen.", "2": "The acid is reduced to SO₂, not H₂S, in the book''s equation.", "3": "These are not products of this reaction."}'),
  ('CHE-0547', 'p-block elements', 'Uses of sulphuric acid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nitrogen and Sulfur, p. 279 (§12.13.4)', 'The largest single use of sulphuric acid is', '["in lead storage batteries", "in making fertilizers, mainly by digesting phosphate rock", "as a drying agent for gases", "in the food industry"]', 1, 'A major portion, normally about three-fourths, is used in making fertilizers, chiefly to digest phosphate rock; it also makes ammonium sulphate.', '{"0": "Batteries are one use, but a small one.", "2": "Drying gases is a minor use.", "3": "The food industry is a minor use."}'),
  ('CHE-0548', 'p-block elements', 'Group 17 overview', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Did you know)', 'The name "halogen" comes from Greek words meaning', '["salt maker.", "colour maker.", "acid former.", "light bearer."]', 0, 'The book explains that "halos" means salt and "gen" means to make, so halogens are the salt formers.', '{"1": "The name refers to salts, not to the colours of the elements.", "2": "Halogens do form hydrohalic acids, but the name does not come from that.", "3": "\"Light bearer\" is the meaning of phosphorus, not of halogen."}'),
  ('CHE-0549', 'p-block elements', 'Group 17 overview', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Did you know)', 'According to the book, the first halogen to be isolated and recognized as an element was', '["chlorine.", "bromine.", "fluorine.", "iodine."]', 0, 'The book states that chlorine was the first halogen isolated and recognized as an element.', '{"1": "Bromine is not named as the first halogen isolated.", "2": "Fluorine is the most reactive halogen and was not the first to be isolated.", "3": "Iodine is not named as the first halogen isolated."}'),
  ('CHE-0550', 'p-block elements', 'Group 17 overview', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'The outer shell electron configuration of bromine is', '["3s² 3p⁵", "4s² 4p⁶", "4s² 4p⁵", "5s² 5p⁵"]', 2, 'Bromine (proton number 35) has the shell structure 2, 8, 18, 7, with its seven outer electrons in 4s² 4p⁵.', '{"0": "3s² 3p⁵ is the outer configuration of chlorine.", "1": "4s² 4p⁶ is a full octet, the configuration of krypton.", "3": "5s² 5p⁵ is the outer configuration of iodine."}'),
  ('CHE-0551', 'p-block elements', 'Group 17 overview', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'The electron shell structure 2, 8, 18, 18, 7 belongs to', '["chlorine", "iodine", "bromine", "fluorine"]', 1, 'Iodine has proton number 53, and 2 + 8 + 18 + 18 + 7 = 53.', '{"0": "Chlorine (17) is 2, 8, 7.", "2": "Bromine (35) is 2, 8, 18, 7.", "3": "Fluorine (9) is 2, 7."}'),
  ('CHE-0552', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'At 20 °C, which of these halogens is a liquid?', '["Fluorine", "Chlorine", "Bromine", "Iodine"]', 2, 'Bromine melts at −7 °C and boils at 59 °C, so it is a liquid at 20 °C.', '{"0": "Fluorine boils at −188 °C, so it is a gas at 20 °C.", "1": "Chlorine boils at −35 °C, so it is a gas at 20 °C.", "3": "Iodine melts at 113 °C, so it is a solid at 20 °C."}'),
  ('CHE-0553', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'Which order of boiling points of the halogens is correct?', '["F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂", "Br₂ > I₂ > Cl₂ > F₂", "Cl₂ > F₂ > Br₂ > I₂", "I₂ > Br₂ > Cl₂ > F₂"]', 3, 'The boiling points are I₂ 183 °C, Br₂ 59 °C, Cl₂ −35 °C and F₂ −188 °C. They rise down the group as the London dispersion forces get stronger.', '{"0": "This is the reverse of the real order.", "1": "Iodine (183 °C) boils at a higher temperature than bromine (59 °C).", "2": "Fluorine has the lowest boiling point, not one above bromine''s."}'),
  ('CHE-0554', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'The enthalpy change of vaporisation rises from +3.3 kJ mol⁻¹ for fluorine to +30 kJ mol⁻¹ for iodine mainly because', '["the halogen molecules become more polar down the group.", "hydrogen bonding between halogen molecules increases down the group.", "larger, more polarizable molecules have stronger London dispersion forces.", "the X–X covalent bond gets stronger down the group."]', 2, 'Halogen molecules are non-polar, so the forces between them are instantaneous dipole-induced dipole (London) forces. These grow with molecular size and polarizability, so more energy is needed to vaporise the heavier halogens.', '{"0": "X₂ molecules have two identical atoms, so they are non-polar throughout the group.", "1": "Halogen molecules contain no hydrogen and do not hydrogen bond with each other.", "3": "Vaporisation separates molecules and does not break the X–X bond; in any case that bond weakens from chlorine to iodine."}'),
  ('CHE-0555', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 285 (§13.2, Table 13.2)', 'According to Table 13.2, a halogen molecule that is small and has low polarizability will have', '["weak London forces, a high boiling point and high volatility.", "strong London forces, a high boiling point and low volatility.", "weak London forces, a low boiling point and high volatility.", "strong London forces, a low boiling point and high volatility."]', 2, 'Small molecules with low polarizability have weak instantaneous dipole-induced dipole forces. Little energy is needed to separate them, so the boiling point is low and volatility is high.', '{"0": "A high boiling point goes with low volatility, not high; the book says volatility is inversely related to boiling point.", "1": "This describes a large, highly polarizable molecule such as I₂.", "3": "Strong London forces raise the boiling point instead of lowering it."}'),
  ('CHE-0556', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 285 (§13.2)', 'How is the volatility of a substance related to its boiling point?', '["Volatility is inversely related to boiling point.", "Volatility does not depend on boiling point.", "The higher the boiling point, the higher the volatility.", "Only ionic substances show a link between the two."]', 0, 'The book states that volatility is inversely related to boiling point: a more volatile substance has a lower boiling point, because weaker intermolecular forces need less energy to overcome.', '{"1": "Both depend on the strength of the intermolecular forces, so they are linked.", "2": "This is the reverse of the relationship.", "3": "The book applies the relationship to non-polar molecular halogens."}'),
  ('CHE-0557', 'p-block elements', 'Physical properties', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 284 (Table 13.1)', 'Using Table 13.1, which halogen has the lowest solubility in water at 20 °C?', '["Fluorine", "Chlorine", "Bromine", "Iodine"]', 3, 'Iodine dissolves only 0.018 g per 100 g of water, far less than chlorine (0.59 g) or bromine (3.6 g).', '{"0": "Fluorine reacts readily with water instead of simply dissolving.", "1": "Chlorine dissolves 0.59 g per 100 g of water and reacts slightly.", "2": "Bromine dissolves 3.6 g per 100 g of water, the most of the three listed values."}'),
  ('CHE-0558', 'p-block elements', 'Bond strength', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 286 (§13.3)', 'The F–F bond (156 kJ mol⁻¹) is weaker than the Cl–Cl bond (243 kJ mol⁻¹) because', '["fluorine has a lower electronegativity than chlorine.", "fluorine atoms have fewer valence electrons than chlorine atoms.", "the F–F bond length is much greater than the Cl–Cl bond length.", "the small F atoms bring their lone pairs close together, causing strong repulsion."]', 3, 'Fluorine atoms are very small, so the lone pairs on the two atoms of F₂ are close together. Their repulsion weakens the bond despite fluorine''s high electronegativity.', '{"0": "Fluorine is the most electronegative element, more than chlorine.", "1": "Both atoms have seven valence electrons.", "2": "Fluorine atoms are smaller, so the F–F bond is shorter, not longer."}'),
  ('CHE-0559', 'p-block elements', 'Bond strength', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 286 (Table 13.3)', 'Which order of X–X bond energies matches Table 13.3?', '["Cl₂ > F₂ > Br₂ > I₂", "F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂", "Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂", "I₂ > Br₂ > Cl₂ > F₂"]', 2, 'The values are Cl₂ 243, Br₂ 193, F₂ 156 and I₂ 151 kJ mol⁻¹. Fluorine is the exception to the downward trend, falling just above iodine.', '{"0": "F₂ (156) is weaker than Br₂ (193).", "1": "F₂ (156) is weaker than both Cl₂ and Br₂, so it cannot come first.", "3": "This reverses the trend; I₂ is the weakest of the four."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0560', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 286 (§13.4)', 'When sodium burns in chlorine to form NaCl, the chlorine', '["gains electrons and acts as an oxidizing agent.", "shares electrons and forms a covalent compound.", "loses electrons and is oxidized.", "is neither oxidized nor reduced."]', 0, 'On forming ionic compounds with metals, halogens gain electrons and become halide ions, so they are reduced and act as oxidizing agents: 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl.', '{"1": "NaCl is an ionic compound made of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.", "2": "Sodium loses electrons; chlorine gains them.", "3": "Chlorine goes from 0 in Cl₂ to −1 in NaCl, so it is reduced."}'),
  ('CHE-0561', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 286–287 (§13.4)', 'Which of these reactions will NOT take place?', '["Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂", "Br₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Br⁻ + I₂", "I₂ + 2Cl⁻ → 2I⁻ + Cl₂", "Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂"]', 2, 'A halogen displaces only the halides below it. Iodine is the weakest oxidizing agent of the four and cannot oxidize any halide ion, so it cannot release chlorine from chloride.', '{"0": "Chlorine can oxidize and displace Br⁻.", "1": "Bromine can oxidize and displace I⁻.", "3": "Chlorine can oxidize and displace I⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-0562', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (§13.4)', 'Chlorine water is added to potassium bromide solution and bromine is released. In this reaction the oxidizing agent is', '["K⁺", "Br⁻", "Cl₂", "Br₂"]', 2, 'In Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂, chlorine gains electrons (0 to −1) and so oxidizes the bromide ions. The species that is reduced is the oxidizing agent.', '{"0": "K⁺ is a spectator ion and takes no part.", "1": "Br⁻ loses electrons and is oxidized, so it is the reducing agent.", "3": "Br₂ is a product of the oxidation of Br⁻."}'),
  ('CHE-0563', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (Table 13.4)', 'The standard reduction potentials of the halogens become less positive from F₂ (+2.87 V) to I₂ (+0.54 V). This shows that', '["oxidizing power decreases from fluorine to iodine.", "oxidizing power increases from fluorine to iodine.", "iodine is the strongest oxidizing agent.", "all halogens have the same oxidizing power."]', 0, 'A more positive standard reduction potential means a greater tendency to gain electrons. The fall from +2.87 V to +0.54 V reflects the decreasing oxidizing power down the group.', '{"1": "This is the reverse of what the falling values show.", "2": "Iodine has the least positive value, so it is the weakest oxidizing agent.", "3": "The values differ by more than 2 V, so the oxidizing powers are clearly different."}'),
  ('CHE-0564', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (Table 13.4)', 'Which halogen has a standard reduction potential of +1.07 V?', '["Br₂", "Cl₂", "F₂", "I₂"]', 0, 'Table 13.4 lists F₂ +2.87 V, Cl₂ +1.36 V, Br₂ +1.07 V and I₂ +0.54 V.', '{"1": "Cl₂ is +1.36 V.", "2": "F₂ is +2.87 V.", "3": "I₂ is +0.54 V."}'),
  ('CHE-0565', 'p-block elements', 'Halogens as oxidizing agents', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (§13.4)', 'According to the book, a halogen will have a high oxidizing power if it has', '["a high energy of dissociation, low electron affinity and low hydration energy of its ions.", "a low energy of dissociation, low electron affinity and low hydration energy of its ions.", "a high energy of dissociation, high electron affinity and low hydration energy of its ions.", "a low energy of dissociation, high electron affinity and high hydration energy of its ions."]', 3, 'Each step from X₂ to X⁻(aq) is helped when the molecule splits easily (low dissociation energy), the atom readily takes an electron (high electron affinity) and the ion is strongly hydrated (high hydration energy).', '{"0": "All three factors here work against forming X⁻(aq).", "1": "Low electron affinity and low hydration energy both reduce oxidizing power.", "2": "A high dissociation energy and low hydration energy both reduce oxidizing power."}'),
  ('CHE-0566', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with hydrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (§13.5)', 'Chlorine reacts readily with hydrogen to form HCl', '["only on heating with a catalyst, and the reaction is reversible.", "only in the dark at very low temperature.", "in the presence of UV light or a spark.", "never, because chlorine is unreactive towards hydrogen."]', 2, 'The book gives H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl in the presence of UV light (hν) or a spark.', '{"0": "These are the conditions for iodine, whose reaction is slow and reversible.", "1": "Fluorine is the halogen that reacts explosively with hydrogen in the dark at low temperature.", "3": "Chlorine does react readily with hydrogen under UV light."}'),
  ('CHE-0567', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with hydrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 288 (§13.5)', 'Which halogen reacts with hydrogen only at high temperature in the presence of a catalyst, in a slow and reversible reaction?', '["Fluorine", "Chlorine", "Bromine", "Iodine"]', 3, 'Iodine is the least reactive halogen towards hydrogen. H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI needs a high temperature and a catalyst and occurs very slowly.', '{"0": "Fluorine reacts explosively with hydrogen even in the cold and dark.", "1": "Chlorine reacts readily with UV light or a spark.", "2": "Bromine reacts with hydrogen on heating; the book does not describe it as reversible or catalysed."}'),
  ('CHE-0568', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with hydrogen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 287 (§13.5)', 'Hydrogen halides are', '["colourless gases that dissolve in water to form hydrohalic acids.", "white ionic solids at room temperature.", "coloured gases that are insoluble in water.", "colourless liquids that do not dissolve in water."]', 0, 'The book describes the hydrogen halides as colourless gases that dissolve in water to give hydrohalic acids, such as hydrochloric acid from HCl.', '{"1": "They are covalent molecules and gases at room temperature.", "2": "They are colourless and very soluble in water.", "3": "They are gases and dissolve readily in water."}'),
  ('CHE-0569', 'p-block elements', 'Thermal stability of hydrogen halides', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 288 (§13.6)', 'Which order of thermal stability of the hydrogen halides is correct?', '["HI > HBr > HCl > HF", "HBr > HCl > HI > HF", "HCl > HF > HBr > HI", "HF > HCl > HBr > HI"]', 3, 'Thermal stability falls from HF to HI as the H–X bond dissociation energy falls (569, 431, 366, 299 kJ mol⁻¹).', '{"0": "This is the reverse order; HI is the least stable.", "1": "HF is the most stable, not the least.", "2": "HF has the strongest H–X bond, so it is more stable than HCl."}'),
  ('CHE-0570', 'p-block elements', 'Thermal stability of hydrogen halides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 288 (§13.6)', 'Hydrogen iodide is the least thermally stable hydrogen halide because', '["HI molecules are held by hydrogen bonds that break on heating.", "iodine is the most electronegative halogen.", "the large iodine atom overlaps poorly with hydrogen, giving the weakest H–X bond.", "the H–I bond is the shortest of the hydrogen halides."]', 2, 'Iodine''s large atomic radius gives poor orbital overlap with hydrogen, so the H–I bond is the weakest (299 kJ mol⁻¹) and breaks most easily on heating.', '{"0": "Thermal decomposition breaks the H–I covalent bond; HI does not form hydrogen bonds of note.", "1": "Iodine is the least electronegative of the common halogens.", "3": "The large iodine atom makes the H–I bond the longest, not the shortest."}'),
  ('CHE-0571', 'p-block elements', 'Thermal stability of hydrogen halides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 288 (Table 13.5)', 'Using Table 13.5, by how much does the bond dissociation energy fall from H–F to H–I?', '["270 kJ mol⁻¹", "203 kJ mol⁻¹", "138 kJ mol⁻¹", "868 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 0, '569 − 299 = 270 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"1": "203 kJ mol⁻¹ is the fall from H–F to H–Br (569 − 366).", "2": "138 kJ mol⁻¹ is the fall from H–F to H–Cl (569 − 431).", "3": "868 kJ mol⁻¹ is the sum of the two values, not the difference."}'),
  ('CHE-0572', 'p-block elements', 'Thermal stability of hydrogen halides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 286–288 (Tables 13.3 and 13.5)', 'Using bond energies H–H = 436, Cl–Cl = 243 and H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹ (Cl–Cl and H–Cl from the book''s tables), estimate ΔH for H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g).', '["+183 kJ mol⁻¹", "−103 kJ mol⁻¹", "−183 kJ mol⁻¹", "+248 kJ mol⁻¹"]', 2, 'ΔH = bonds broken − bonds formed = (436 + 243) − 2(431) = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ mol⁻¹.', '{"0": "The sign is reversed; more energy is released forming two H–Cl bonds than is used breaking H–H and Cl–Cl.", "1": "−103 kJ mol⁻¹ is the estimate for H₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr: (436 + 193) − 2(366).", "3": "+248 kJ mol⁻¹ comes from counting only one H–Cl bond formed: 679 − 431. The equation forms two H–Cl bonds."}'),
  ('CHE-0573', 'p-block elements', 'Halide ions as reducing agents', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 289 (§13.7)', 'The fluoride ion is the weakest reducing agent among the halide ions because', '["it is small with a high charge density, and fluorine holds its extra electron strongly.", "fluorine is the least electronegative halogen.", "it is the largest halide ion and has a low charge density.", "it has more electrons than the other halide ions."]', 0, 'Fluorine''s high electronegativity and the small size of F⁻ give it a high charge density, so it holds its extra electron tightly and has little tendency to give it up.', '{"1": "Fluorine is the most electronegative halogen.", "2": "F⁻ is the smallest halide ion, and it has the highest charge density.", "3": "F⁻ has the fewest electrons (10) of the halide ions."}'),
  ('CHE-0574', 'p-block elements', 'Halide ions as reducing agents', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 289 (§13.7)', 'Which statement explains why iodide is a strong reducing agent?', '["I⁻ is small and has a high charge density.", "I⁻ cannot lose an electron because its shells are full.", "Iodine is more electronegative than chlorine.", "I⁻ is large with a low charge density, so it donates an electron easily."]', 3, 'Iodine has a low electronegativity and I⁻ is a large ion with a low charge density, so its outer electron is easily removed.', '{"0": "This describes F⁻, the weakest reducing agent.", "1": "I⁻ readily loses an electron to form I₂; that is why it is a reducing agent.", "2": "Iodine is much less electronegative than chlorine."}'),
  ('CHE-0575', 'p-block elements', 'Silver nitrate test', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 290 (§13.8.1)', 'Aqueous silver nitrate is added to sodium fluoride solution. What is seen?', '["A white precipitate", "A cream precipitate", "A yellow precipitate", "No precipitate"]', 3, 'Silver fluoride is soluble in water, so fluoride ions give no visible reaction with aqueous silver ions.', '{"0": "A white precipitate (AgCl) is given by chloride.", "1": "A cream precipitate (AgBr) is given by bromide.", "2": "A yellow precipitate (AgI) is given by iodide."}'),
  ('CHE-0576', 'p-block elements', 'Silver nitrate test', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 290–291 (§13.8, Table 13.6)', 'A solution gives a pale yellow precipitate with aqueous silver nitrate. The precipitate does not dissolve in dilute or concentrated ammonia. The solution contains', '["fluoride ions.", "chloride ions.", "bromide ions.", "iodide ions."]', 3, 'Silver iodide is pale yellow and insoluble in both dilute and concentrated aqueous ammonia.', '{"0": "Fluoride gives no precipitate at all.", "1": "Silver chloride is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia.", "2": "Silver bromide is cream and dissolves in concentrated ammonia."}'),
  ('CHE-0577', 'p-block elements', 'Silver nitrate test', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 290 (§13.8.2)', 'When silver chloride dissolves in dilute aqueous ammonia, the silver ends up as', '["AgNH₂, a white solid", "Ag₂O, a brown solid", "[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺, the diamminesilver(I) ion", "Ag metal"]', 2, 'AgCl(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). The diamminesilver(I) complex is soluble, so a colourless solution forms.', '{"0": "The book shows a soluble complex ion, not a new precipitate.", "1": "No oxide forms; the precipitate dissolves to a colourless solution.", "3": "Silver is not reduced; it stays in the +1 state in the complex."}'),
  ('CHE-0578', 'p-block elements', 'Silver nitrate test', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 290–291 (Table 13.6)', 'Two unlabelled solutions are known to be KCl and KBr. Which test tells them apart?', '["Add aqueous silver nitrate, then dilute aqueous ammonia; only one precipitate dissolves.", "Add aqueous silver nitrate and look for a precipitate; only one will form a precipitate.", "Add aqueous silver nitrate, then concentrated ammonia; only one precipitate dissolves.", "Add dilute ammonia alone; only one solution gives a precipitate."]', 0, 'Both give precipitates with Ag⁺ (white AgCl, cream AgBr). Dilute ammonia dissolves AgCl but not AgBr, so it separates them clearly.', '{"1": "Both chloride and bromide give precipitates with silver nitrate.", "2": "Concentrated ammonia dissolves both AgCl and AgBr, so both precipitates would disappear.", "3": "Ammonia alone gives no precipitate with either potassium halide."}'),
  ('CHE-0579', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with concentrated sulfuric acid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 291 (§13.9)', 'Concentrated sulfuric acid is added to solid sodium fluoride. Only fumes of HF are produced and no further reaction occurs, because', '["HF is a weak reducing agent and cannot reduce H₂SO₄.", "sodium fluoride does not react with sulfuric acid at all.", "HF is a strong oxidizing agent.", "HF reacts with the sulfuric acid to form SO₂."]', 0, 'NaF + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HF. HF is a weak reducing agent, so it does not go on to reduce the sulfuric acid.', '{"1": "It does react, giving NaHSO₄ and fumes of HF.", "2": "The point is HF''s weakness as a reducing agent; it is not acting as an oxidizing agent here.", "3": "SO₂ forms with bromide, whose HBr is a strong enough reducing agent; HF does not reduce H₂SO₄."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0580', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with concentrated sulfuric acid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 291 (§13.9)', 'Which set of products forms when concentrated sulfuric acid reacts with sodium bromide?', '["HBr only", "HBr, Br₂ and H₂S", "HBr, Br₂ and SO₂", "Br₂ and O₂"]', 2, 'Steamy fumes of HBr form first. HBr then reduces some H₂SO₄ to SO₂ and is itself oxidized to brown Br₂: 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.', '{"0": "HBr only is what the reaction would give if HBr were too weak a reducing agent to act, as with HF and HCl.", "1": "H₂S is the reduction product with iodide, which is a stronger reducing agent.", "3": "No oxygen gas is produced; sulfuric acid is reduced to SO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0581', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with concentrated sulfuric acid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 291 (§13.9)', 'In the reaction 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O, hydrogen bromide acts as', '["an oxidizing agent.", "a reducing agent.", "a catalyst.", "a dehydrating agent."]', 1, 'Bromine goes from −1 in HBr to 0 in Br₂, so HBr is oxidized. In doing so it reduces sulfur from +6 in H₂SO₄ to +4 in SO₂, so HBr is the reducing agent.', '{"0": "H₂SO₄ is the oxidizing agent; HBr is oxidized.", "2": "HBr is used up in the reaction, so it is not a catalyst.", "3": "HBr removes no water; it is oxidized to bromine."}'),
  ('CHE-0582', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with concentrated sulfuric acid', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 291 (§13.9)', 'When sodium iodide reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid, H₂S is formed. The oxidation state of sulfur changes from', '["+6 to +4", "+6 to −2", "+4 to −2", "+6 to 0"]', 1, 'HI is a strong reducing agent and reduces H₂SO₄ (S = +6) all the way to H₂S (S = −2): 8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O.', '{"0": "+6 to +4 is the change when H₂SO₄ is reduced to SO₂, as with bromide.", "2": "Sulfur in H₂SO₄ is +6, not +4.", "3": "Sulfur in H₂S is −2, not 0; 0 would be elemental sulfur."}'),
  ('CHE-0583', 'p-block elements', 'Reactions with concentrated sulfuric acid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 291 (§13.9)', 'Purple fumes are seen when concentrated sulfuric acid is added to', '["sodium fluoride.", "sodium chloride.", "sodium bromide.", "sodium iodide."]', 3, 'Iodide is oxidized by the acid to iodine, which gives purple fumes, along with HI fumes and the smell of H₂S.', '{"0": "Sodium fluoride gives only fumes of HF.", "1": "Sodium chloride gives only fumes of HCl.", "2": "Sodium bromide gives brown fumes of bromine, with HBr and SO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0584', 'p-block elements', 'Disproportionation of chlorine', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 292 (§13.10)', 'A disproportionation reaction is one in which', '["two different elements are oxidized together.", "a single element is oxidized and reduced at the same time.", "a compound breaks down on heating.", "an acid and a base form a salt and water."]', 1, 'The book defines disproportionation as a reaction in which a single element undergoes both oxidation and reduction simultaneously.', '{"0": "Disproportionation involves one element, part oxidized and part reduced.", "2": "That is thermal decomposition, which need not involve redox at all.", "3": "That is neutralization."}'),
  ('CHE-0585', 'p-block elements', 'Disproportionation of chlorine', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 292 (§13.10.1)', 'Chlorine reacts with cold aqueous sodium hydroxide to give NaCl, H₂O and', '["NaClO₃", "NaClO", "NaClO₄", "NaOCl₂"]', 1, 'Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O with cold alkali. NaClO is sodium chlorate(I).', '{"0": "NaClO₃, sodium chlorate(V), forms with hot sodium hydroxide.", "2": "NaClO₄ is not a product of either reaction in the book.", "3": "NaOCl₂ is not a real compound."}'),
  ('CHE-0586', 'p-block elements', 'Disproportionation of chlorine', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 292 (§13.10.2)', 'In the reaction of chlorine with hot aqueous sodium hydroxide, chlorine is oxidized from 0 to', '["+1 in NaClO", "+5 in NaClO₃", "+7 in NaClO₄", "−1 in NaCl"]', 1, 'With hot alkali the oxidized product is sodium chlorate(V), NaClO₃, in which chlorine is +5. The reduced product is NaCl, in which chlorine is −1.', '{"0": "NaClO (+1) is the oxidized product with cold alkali.", "2": "NaClO₄ is not formed in this reaction.", "3": "Going to −1 in NaCl is reduction, not oxidation."}'),
  ('CHE-0587', 'p-block elements', 'Disproportionation of chlorine', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 292 (Keep in mind)', 'Sodium chlorate(I) was previously called', '["sodium chlorate", "sodium hypochlorite", "sodium perchlorate", "sodium chlorite"]', 1, 'The book notes that sodium chlorate(I), NaClO, was previously called sodium hypochlorite.', '{"0": "\"Sodium chlorate\" was the older name of NaClO₃, now sodium chlorate(V).", "2": "Sodium perchlorate is NaClO₄, chlorate(VII).", "3": "Sodium chlorite is NaClO₂, chlorate(III)."}'),
  ('CHE-0588', 'p-block elements', 'Disproportionation of chlorine', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293 (§13.11.1) and p. 292 (§13.10)', 'In Cl₂ + H₂O → HCl + HClO, the oxidation states of chlorine in HCl and HClO are', '["+1 and −1, so chlorine is only reduced.", "−1 and +1, so chlorine disproportionates.", "−1 and −1, so chlorine is only reduced.", "0 and +1, so chlorine is only oxidized."]', 1, 'Chlorine goes from 0 in Cl₂ to −1 in HCl (reduced) and +1 in HClO (oxidized). One element being both oxidized and reduced fits the book''s definition of disproportionation.', '{"0": "The values are swapped: chlorine is −1 in HCl and +1 in HClO.", "2": "Chlorine in HClO is +1, since H is +1 and O is −2.", "3": "Chlorine in HCl is −1, not 0."}'),
  ('CHE-0589', 'p-block elements', 'Chlorine in water purification', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293 (§13.11)', 'The primary active species formed when water is chlorinated are', '["HOCl and OCl⁻", "Cl₂ and Cl⁻", "HCl and Cl⁻", "ClO₃⁻ and ClO₄⁻"]', 0, 'Chlorine hydrolyses to HCl and HOCl, and the weak acid HOCl partly dissociates to H⁺ and OCl⁻. HOCl and OCl⁻ are the disinfecting species.', '{"1": "Chloride ions do not disinfect; the book names HOCl and OCl⁻ as the active species.", "2": "HCl is formed, but it is not the disinfecting species.", "3": "Chlorate(V) and chlorate(VII) are not formed in water chlorination."}'),
  ('CHE-0590', 'p-block elements', 'Chlorine in water purification', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293 (§13.11.2)', 'HOCl is a more effective disinfectant than OCl⁻ because', '["HOCl is a stronger acid than HCl.", "HOCl is neutral, so it penetrates the cell walls of micro-organisms more easily.", "OCl⁻ cannot oxidize any cell components.", "HOCl contains more chlorine atoms than OCl⁻."]', 1, 'The book says HOCl is more effective because its neutral charge lets it pass through the cell walls of micro-organisms easily, where it oxidizes proteins, lipids and enzymes.', '{"0": "HOCl is a weak acid; HCl is a strong acid.", "2": "OCl⁻ also oxidizes cell components; it is just less effective.", "3": "Both contain one chlorine atom."}'),
  ('CHE-0591', 'p-block elements', 'Chlorine in water purification', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293–294 (§13.11.3)', 'At a pH above 7.5, chlorinated water', '["contains mainly HOCl, so disinfection is most effective.", "contains mainly OCl⁻, so disinfection is less effective but still works.", "contains no active chlorine species.", "contains mainly Cl₂ gas."]', 1, 'At higher pH (above 7.5) OCl⁻ predominates. It is less effective than HOCl but still disinfects. At pH 6–7.5 HOCl predominates.', '{"0": "HOCl predominates at pH 6–7.5, not above 7.5.", "2": "OCl⁻ is still present and still disinfects.", "3": "Chlorine is hydrolysed in water; Cl₂ is not the main species at any pH discussed."}'),
  ('CHE-0592', 'p-block elements', 'Chlorine in water purification', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293 (§13.11)', 'Water in swimming pools is chlorinated with a slightly higher concentration of chlorine because', '["chlorine is less poisonous in pools.", "it is more likely to contain a higher concentration of bacteria.", "pool water has a lower pH than drinking water.", "higher chlorine makes the water warmer."]', 1, 'The book says pool water is chlorinated more heavily because it is likely to have a higher concentration of bacteria.', '{"0": "Chlorine''s toxicity does not change with where it is used.", "2": "The book gives the bacterial load as the reason, not pH.", "3": "Chlorine does not warm the water."}'),
  ('CHE-0593', 'p-block elements', 'Chlorine in water purification', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Halogens, p. 293–294 (§13.11.3)', 'Which of these is NOT one of the factors the book lists as affecting disinfection by chlorine?', '["pH of the water", "Chlorine dose", "Contact time", "Colour of the water"]', 3, 'The book lists pH, chlorine dose and contact time. Colour is not given as a factor.', '{"0": "pH controls whether HOCl or OCl⁻ predominates.", "1": "A higher chlorine dose gives more HOCl and OCl⁻ and better disinfection.", "2": "Contact time must be long enough for the disinfectants to kill bacteria, viruses and protozoa."}'),
  ('CHE-0594', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Composition of the atmosphere', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 297', 'According to the book, the two major components of the atmosphere are', '["nitrogen and oxygen", "oxygen and argon", "nitrogen and carbon dioxide", "argon and carbon dioxide"]', 0, 'The book lists nitrogen (78.00%) and oxygen (21.01%) as the major components.', '{"1": "Argon (0.93%) is a minor component.", "2": "Carbon dioxide (0.04%) is a minor component.", "3": "Both argon and carbon dioxide are minor components."}'),
  ('CHE-0595', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Composition of the atmosphere', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 297', 'The book classifies argon (0.93%) as a', '["major component of the atmosphere.", "secondary pollutant.", "trace component of the atmosphere.", "minor component of the atmosphere."]', 3, 'Minor components are argon (0.93%) and carbon dioxide (0.04%). Major components are nitrogen and oxygen; trace components include methane, hydrogen, neon, helium, krypton and xenon.', '{"0": "Only nitrogen and oxygen are major components.", "1": "Argon is a natural, unreactive component of air, not a pollutant.", "2": "Trace components are methane, hydrogen, neon, helium, krypton and xenon."}'),
  ('CHE-0596', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Layers of the atmosphere', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'The layers of the atmosphere are distinguished by', '["their colour.", "their distance from the Moon.", "the amount of nitrogen they contain.", "the change in temperature with increasing altitude."]', 3, 'The book says the four layers are determined by the change in temperature observed with increasing altitude.', '{"0": "Layers are not defined by colour.", "1": "The Moon plays no part in defining atmospheric layers.", "2": "Nitrogen is a major gas throughout; it does not define the layers."}'),
  ('CHE-0597', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Layers of the atmosphere', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'Rain, lightning and hurricanes occur in the', '["thermosphere", "stratosphere", "mesosphere", "troposphere"]', 3, 'The troposphere is the lowest and densest layer, extending to about 12 km, and is where major weather events occur.', '{"0": "The thermosphere (80–600 km) is heated by UV and X-ray absorption.", "1": "The stratosphere (12–50 km) holds the ozone layer, not the weather.", "2": "The mesosphere (50–80 km) is the coldest layer."}'),
  ('CHE-0598', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Layers of the atmosphere', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'In which layer does temperature increase with height, from about −58 °C to −2 °C?', '["Troposphere", "Thermosphere", "Mesosphere", "Stratosphere"]', 3, 'In the stratosphere (12–50 km) the temperature rises from −58 °C to −2 °C. Ozone formation there is exothermic and ozone absorbs UV radiation.', '{"0": "In the troposphere temperature falls from 17 °C to −58 °C.", "1": "The thermosphere does warm with height, but to 500–2000 °C or more, not from −58 to −2 °C.", "2": "In the mesosphere temperature falls from −2 °C to −93 °C."}'),
  ('CHE-0599', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Layers of the atmosphere', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'The coldest region of the atmosphere lies in the', '["troposphere", "stratosphere", "thermosphere", "mesosphere"]', 3, 'In the mesosphere (50–80 km) temperature falls to about −93 °C, the coldest part of the atmosphere.', '{"0": "The troposphere only falls to about −58 °C at its top.", "1": "The stratosphere warms from −58 °C to −2 °C.", "2": "The thermosphere reaches 500–2000 °C or more."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0600', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Layers of the atmosphere', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'The temperature in the thermosphere rises with altitude because', '["ozone forms there in an exothermic reaction.", "it is the densest layer.", "the layer absorbs energetic UV radiation and X-rays.", "it contains the most water vapour."]', 2, 'The book says the thermosphere (80–600 km) is heated by the absorption of energetic ultraviolet radiation and X-rays.', '{"0": "Exothermic ozone formation takes place in the middle stratosphere.", "1": "The troposphere is the densest layer.", "3": "Water vapour is concentrated in the troposphere."}'),
  ('CHE-0601', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Ozone layer', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'According to the book, ozone forms in the middle stratosphere by the reaction', '["3O₂ → 2O₃ in the troposphere", "O₃ → O₂ + O, which absorbs UV", "O₂ → 2O, which is exothermic", "O₂ + O → O₃, which is exothermic"]', 3, 'In the middle stratosphere, where less UV passes through, monoatomic and diatomic oxygen recombine: O₂ + O → O₃. The reaction is exothermic.', '{"0": "The book places ozone formation in the stratosphere, by O₂ + O.", "1": "O₃ → O₂ + O is the breakdown of ozone by high-energy UV in the upper stratosphere.", "2": "Splitting O₂ into atoms needs energy; bond breaking is endothermic."}'),
  ('CHE-0602', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Ozone layer', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298 (§14.1)', 'Why is ozone not formed in the lower stratosphere?', '["The temperature is too high.", "CFCs destroy all the ozone there.", "It receives very little UV radiation, so there is no monoatomic oxygen.", "There is no oxygen gas in that region."]', 2, 'The lower stratosphere receives very little UV radiation, so O₂ is not split into O atoms, and without O atoms ozone cannot form.', '{"0": "The book links ozone formation to UV supply, not to high temperature.", "1": "The book''s reason is the lack of UV and O atoms, not CFCs.", "3": "O₂ is present; what is missing is monoatomic oxygen."}'),
  ('CHE-0603', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Air pollutants', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 298–299 (§14.2)', 'Which is an example of a primary pollutant?', '["Peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN)", "Ozone in photochemical smog", "Sulphuric acid in acid rain", "Carbon monoxide from a motor vehicle exhaust"]', 3, 'Primary pollutants are emitted directly, like CO from a vehicle exhaust or ash from a volcano. Secondary pollutants form by reactions of primary ones.', '{"0": "PAN forms in photochemical smog, so it is secondary.", "1": "Ozone in smog forms by photochemical reactions of NOx and hydrocarbons, so it is secondary.", "2": "Sulphuric acid forms from SO₂ via SO₃ and water, so it is secondary."}'),
  ('CHE-0604', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Sources of air pollution', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 299 (§14.3.1)', 'Which natural source is correctly matched with the pollutant the book says it releases?', '["Wind erosion – nitrogen oxides", "Swamps and peat bogs – sulphur dioxide", "Algae on the ocean surface – hydrogen sulphide", "Thunderbolts (lightning) – methane"]', 2, 'The book says algae on ocean surfaces give out H₂S, swamps produce methane, lightning produces NOx and wind erosion adds particulate matter.', '{"0": "Wind erosion introduces particulate matter.", "1": "Swamps and peat bogs produce methane.", "3": "Lightning produces oxides of nitrogen."}'),
  ('CHE-0605', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Sources of air pollution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 299 (§14.3.2)', 'Which is a point source of air pollution?', '["A car", "An aeroplane", "A marine engine", "A power plant"]', 3, 'The book divides human-made sources into mobile sources (cars, trucks, aeroplanes, marine engines) and point sources (factories, power plants).', '{"0": "A car is a mobile source.", "1": "An aeroplane is a mobile source.", "2": "A marine engine is a mobile source."}'),
  ('CHE-0606', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Oxides of carbon', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 300 (§14.4.1)', 'Carbon monoxide is produced mainly by', '["complete combustion of fossil fuels.", "photosynthesis.", "incomplete combustion of fossil fuels.", "the action of algae on the ocean surface."]', 2, 'When there is not enough oxygen, carbon burns to CO: 2C + O₂ → 2CO.', '{"0": "Complete combustion gives CO₂.", "1": "Photosynthesis uses CO₂ and releases O₂.", "3": "Ocean algae release hydrogen sulphide."}'),
  ('CHE-0607', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Oxides of carbon', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 300 (Did you know)', 'Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it', '["destroys the ozone layer.", "is a strong oxidizing agent.", "binds to haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen does.", "forms carbonic acid in the lungs."]', 2, 'CO binds blood haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen, keeping oxygen out of normal respiration. The book says high-pressure oxygen can reverse the poisoning.', '{"0": "Ozone depletion is caused by CFCs.", "1": "CO is a reducing agent, and its toxicity comes from binding haemoglobin.", "3": "CO is a neutral oxide and does not form an acid in the lungs."}'),
  ('CHE-0608', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Oxides of nitrogen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 300 (§14.4.2)', 'Nitric oxide forms in engines and furnaces by the reaction', '["N₂ + 2H₂O → 2NO + 2H₂", "2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂ at room temperature only", "NH₃ + O₂ → NO₂ in the absence of heat", "N₂ + O₂ → 2NO at high temperature"]', 3, 'At high temperature nitrogen and oxygen from the air combine: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. NO is then oxidized further to NO₂.', '{"0": "This is not a reaction given in the book.", "1": "2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂ makes NO₂ from NO; it does not make NO from air.", "2": "This is not a reaction given in the book, and it is not balanced."}'),
  ('CHE-0609', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Oxides of sulphur', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 300 (§14.4.3)', 'Oxides of sulphur (SOx) are emitted mainly by', '["photosynthesis.", "lightning.", "burning coal and oil.", "refrigerators."]', 2, 'SO₂ and SO₃ are emitted mainly when coal and oil, which contain sulphur, are burned.', '{"0": "Photosynthesis releases oxygen.", "1": "Lightning produces oxides of nitrogen.", "3": "Refrigerators were a source of CFCs."}'),
  ('CHE-0610', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301 (§14.4.4)', 'According to the book, the most common hydrocarbon air pollutant is', '["naphthalene", "ethane", "benzene", "methane"]', 3, 'The book names methane as the most common hydrocarbon and air pollutant, with automobiles the major source of hydrocarbon emissions.', '{"0": "Naphthalene is given as a PAH.", "1": "Ethane is listed as a hydrocarbon pollutant but not as the most common.", "2": "Benzene is given as a VOC."}'),
  ('CHE-0611', 'Environmental chemistry', 'CFCs and ozone depletion', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301 (§14.4.6)', 'In the stratosphere, CFCs are broken down by UV radiation to release', '["chlorine free radicals, Cl·", "fluorine gas, F₂", "chloride ions, Cl⁻", "carbon dioxide"]', 0, 'UV radiation splits a C–Cl bond in a CFC such as CFCl₃, releasing a chlorine free radical (Cl·) that attacks ozone.', '{"1": "The C–F bond is not broken in the book''s scheme, and F₂ is not released.", "2": "The species released is a neutral atom with an unpaired electron, not an ion.", "3": "The book''s scheme does not produce CO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0612', 'Environmental chemistry', 'CFCs and ozone depletion', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301 (§14.4.6)', 'In the steps Cl· + O₃ → ClO· + O₂ and ClO· + O → Cl· + O₂, why can one chlorine radical destroy many ozone molecules?', '["O₂ formed in the steps reacts with more ozone.", "Cl· reacts with nitrogen to make more ozone-destroying species.", "The second step regenerates Cl·, which can attack another O₃ molecule.", "Each step produces two Cl· radicals."]', 2, 'Cl· is used in the first step and given back in the second, so the two steps repeat many times. The book says one CFC molecule can destroy up to 100,000 ozone molecules.', '{"0": "O₂ does not attack ozone; it is a product of the cycle.", "1": "Nitrogen plays no part in these steps.", "3": "Each cycle uses one Cl· and returns one Cl·; there is no net gain."}'),
  ('CHE-0613', 'Environmental chemistry', 'PAHs and POPs', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301 (§14.4.7)', 'Naphthalene, anthracene and phenanthrene are examples of', '["chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)", "volatile organic compounds (VOCs)", "persistent organic pollutants (POPs)", "polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs)"]', 3, 'PAHs are made of several fused aromatic rings. The book names naphthalene, anthracene and phenanthrene as examples.', '{"0": "CFCs contain chlorine and fluorine and are not aromatic hydrocarbons.", "1": "The book''s VOC examples are benzene, xylene, toluene, ethanol, formaldehyde and acetone.", "2": "The book''s POP examples are PCBs and DDT."}'),
  ('CHE-0614', 'Environmental chemistry', 'PAHs and POPs', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301 (§14.4.7)', 'PAHs are generated mainly by', '["incomplete combustion of fossil fuels and wood.", "the action of UV light on CFCs.", "bacterial denitrification in soil.", "the evaporation of liquid fuels at room temperature."]', 0, 'The book says PAHs come mainly from incomplete combustion of coal, oil, petrol and wood, from vehicle emissions, industrial processes and even grilled food.', '{"1": "UV acting on CFCs releases chlorine radicals.", "2": "Soil bacteria are a source of nitrogen oxides.", "3": "Evaporation of liquid fuels releases VOCs."}'),
  ('CHE-0615', 'Environmental chemistry', 'PAHs and POPs', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 301–302 (§14.4.8)', 'Persistent organic pollutants (POPs) are a particular concern because they', '["break down quickly in sunlight.", "resist chemical, biological and photolytic degradation and accumulate in the environment.", "are found only near the place where they are released.", "are non-toxic but have a strong smell."]', 1, 'POPs resist breakdown, so they build up in the environment. Carried by wind and water, POPs released in one country can affect people and wildlife far away.', '{"0": "POPs resist photolytic (light-driven) breakdown.", "2": "They travel by wind and water far from where they were used.", "3": "The book describes them as toxic and harmful to health."}'),
  ('CHE-0616', 'Environmental chemistry', 'PAHs and POPs', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 302 (Table 14.1)', 'According to Table 14.1, DDT has been used as', '["an insecticide in agriculture.", "a coolant in refrigerators.", "an insulating fluid in electrical equipment.", "a fuel additive."]', 0, 'Table 14.1 lists DDT (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane) as an insecticide used in agriculture.', '{"1": "CFCs were used as coolants in refrigerators.", "2": "PCBs were used in electrical equipment.", "3": "DDT is not described as a fuel additive."}'),
  ('CHE-0617', 'Environmental chemistry', 'VOCs', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 302 (§14.4.9)', 'Which of these is given in the book as a volatile organic compound?', '["Anthracene", "Formaldehyde", "DDT", "Lead"]', 1, 'VOCs evaporate easily at room temperature. The book''s examples include benzene, xylene, toluene, ethanol, formaldehyde and acetone.', '{"0": "Anthracene is a PAH.", "2": "DDT is a POP.", "3": "Lead is a heavy metal, not an organic compound."}'),
  ('CHE-0618', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Particulate matter', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 302 (§14.4.10) and p. 307 (§14.9.1)', 'Which statement about particulate matter (PM) is correct according to the book?', '["PM includes solid particles and liquid droplets, roughly 2.5 to 10 micrometres across.", "PM consists only of solid particles.", "PM comes only from human activities.", "PM is a gas that dissolves in rain."]', 0, 'PM is solid particles, liquid droplets or both, floating in air. The book gives a diameter range of 2.5 to 10.0 micrometres. Its sources are both natural and man-made.', '{"1": "The book includes liquid droplets in PM.", "2": "Natural sources include volcanoes, fires, dust storms and sea salt.", "3": "PM is particles and droplets, not a gas."}'),
  ('CHE-0619', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Heavy metals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 302 (§14.4.11)', 'The book names which major sources of heavy metals such as lead, mercury and cadmium in air?', '["Photosynthesis and respiration", "Metallurgy, battery waste and incineration", "Lightning and ocean algae", "Swamps and peat bogs"]', 1, 'The book lists metallurgy, battery waste and incineration as the major sources of heavy metal air pollution.', '{"0": "Photosynthesis and respiration exchange O₂ and CO₂.", "2": "Lightning gives NOx and ocean algae give H₂S.", "3": "Swamps and peat bogs give methane."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0620', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Smog', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 304 (§14.6.1)', 'Industrial (classical or London) smog results mainly from high levels of', '["CFCs", "sulphur oxides", "PAN", "ozone"]', 1, 'Industrial smog, also called reducing smog, results mostly from large amounts of SOx released into the air.', '{"0": "CFCs deplete stratospheric ozone; they are not the cause of London smog.", "2": "PAN is a secondary pollutant in photochemical smog.", "3": "Ozone is a secondary pollutant in photochemical smog."}'),
  ('CHE-0621', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Smog', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 304 (§14.6.1)', 'Photochemical (Los Angeles) smog needs', '["NOx, VOCs and unburned hydrocarbons in the presence of sunlight.", "SOx and fog at night.", "CFCs and UV light in the stratosphere.", "heavy metals and rain."]', 0, 'Photochemical smog forms when NOx, VOCs and unburned hydrocarbons react in sunlight, producing secondary pollutants such as ozone and PAN.', '{"1": "SOx is the cause of industrial smog, and photochemical smog needs sunlight.", "2": "CFCs and UV in the stratosphere cause ozone depletion.", "3": "Heavy metals and rain are not part of photochemical smog formation."}'),
  ('CHE-0622', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Smog', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 304 (§14.6.1)', 'Industrial smog is also called', '["reducing smog", "oxidizing smog", "Los Angeles smog", "photochemical smog"]', 0, 'The book says industrial smog is also called reducing smog, classical smog or London smog.', '{"1": "Photochemical smog contains oxidants like ozone and PAN; the book gives \"reducing\" for industrial smog.", "2": "Los Angeles smog is photochemical smog.", "3": "Photochemical smog is the other type."}'),
  ('CHE-0623', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Acid rain', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 305 (§14.6.2)', 'Rain is called acid rain when its pH is less than', '["7.0", "5.6", "6.5", "4.0"]', 1, 'The book defines acid rain as rain water with a pH below 5.6.', '{"0": "pH 7 is neutral; normal rain is already slightly acidic.", "2": "6.5 is not the book''s threshold.", "3": "Rain at pH 4 is acid rain, but the threshold is 5.6."}'),
  ('CHE-0624', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Acid rain', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 305 (§14.6.2)', 'Which pair of steps converts SO₂ from fossil fuels into sulphuric acid?', '["SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₄ directly", "SO₂ + O₃ → SO₃ + O₂, then SO₃ + NO → H₂SO₄", "2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃, then SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄", "2SO₃ → 2SO₂ + O₂, then SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₄"]', 2, 'SO₂ is first oxidized to SO₃ (2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃), and SO₃ then reacts with water to form H₂SO₄.', '{"0": "SO₂ + H₂O gives sulphurous acid (H₂SO₃), not sulphuric acid; it is not balanced as written.", "1": "This is not the book''s scheme, and NO has no hydrogen to make H₂SO₄.", "3": "This reduces SO₃ instead of oxidizing SO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0625', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Acid rain', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 305 (§14.6.2)', 'In 3NO₂ + H₂O → 2HNO₃ + NO, the oxidation state of nitrogen changes from +4 in NO₂ to', '["+3 in HNO₃ and +2 in NO", "+5 in HNO₃ and +2 in NO", "+5 in HNO₃ and +4 in NO", "+6 in HNO₃ and +2 in NO"]', 1, 'In HNO₃: +1 + N + 3(−2) = 0, so N = +5. In NO: N = +2. Nitrogen in NO₂ is both oxidized and reduced.', '{"0": "+3 is nitrogen''s state in nitrous acid (HNO₂), not nitric acid.", "2": "In NO, nitrogen is +2, not +4.", "3": "Nitrogen cannot exceed +5, its group number."}'),
  ('CHE-0626', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 306 (§14.7)', 'According to the book, CO₂ and water vapour cause the greenhouse effect because they', '["let short-wavelength solar radiation through but trap the longer-wavelength heat radiated by the Earth.", "block short-wavelength sunlight from reaching the ground.", "react with ozone to release heat.", "reflect all infrared radiation back into space."]', 0, 'Greenhouse gases transmit the short-wavelength solar radiation, but absorb and send back the longer-wavelength infrared that the warmed Earth radiates, so heat is kept in.', '{"1": "Short-wavelength sunlight passes through to warm the surface.", "2": "The effect is absorption of infrared radiation, not a reaction with ozone.", "3": "If all IR were reflected into space there would be no warming."}'),
  ('CHE-0627', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Greenhouse effect', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 305 (§14.7)', 'Global warming, as defined in the book, is', '["the long-term rise in Earth''s average surface temperature due to human activities, mainly greenhouse gas emissions.", "the seasonal warming of summer.", "the heating of the thermosphere by X-rays.", "the warming caused by ozone formation in the stratosphere."]', 0, 'The book defines global warming as the long-term rise in Earth''s average surface temperature caused mainly by greenhouse gases released by burning fossil fuels.', '{"1": "Seasonal change is not a long-term rise.", "2": "Thermospheric heating is a natural feature of that layer.", "3": "Stratospheric warming from ozone is a natural feature of that layer."}'),
  ('CHE-0628', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Air quality index', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 306–307 (§14.8, Table 14.2)', 'An AQI value above 300 is classed as', '["Unhealthy", "Hazardous", "Very unhealthy", "Moderate"]', 1, 'In Table 14.2, 301 to 500 is Hazardous (maroon).', '{"0": "Unhealthy is 151 to 200 (red).", "2": "Very unhealthy is 201 to 300 (purple).", "3": "Moderate is 51 to 100 (yellow)."}'),
  ('CHE-0629', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Air quality index', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 307 (Table 14.2)', 'Which colour does Table 14.2 give for an AQI of 120, "unhealthy for sensitive groups"?', '["Green", "Orange", "Yellow", "Red"]', 1, '101 to 150 is "unhealthy for sensitive groups" and is shown in orange.', '{"0": "Green is 0 to 50 (good).", "2": "Yellow is 51 to 100 (moderate).", "3": "Red is 151 to 200 (unhealthy)."}'),
  ('CHE-0630', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Air quality index', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 306 (§14.8.1)', 'A layer of warm air lying over cooler air near the ground worsens air quality because it', '["makes rain more acidic.", "adds ozone to the troposphere.", "traps pollutants near the ground.", "produces nitrogen oxides."]', 2, 'Under meteorological conditions the book notes that a layer of warm air can trap pollutants near the ground, leading to poor air quality.', '{"0": "Acid rain depends on SOx and NOx dissolving in water, not on the warm layer.", "1": "The warm layer does not create ozone; it stops pollutants dispersing.", "3": "NOx comes from combustion and lightning, not from a warm air layer."}'),
  ('CHE-0631', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Monitoring air quality', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 308 (§14.11)', 'A nephelometer monitors particulate matter by measuring', '["the electric charge on the particles.", "the mass of particles collected on a filter.", "the total amount of light scattered by the particles.", "the radioactivity of the particles."]', 2, 'The book says a nephelometer (also called a photometer) detects particles such as dust, smoke, mist and fumes by measuring the total light they scatter.', '{"0": "The book does not describe charge measurement.", "1": "Weighing particles on a filter is a different method.", "3": "Particles are detected by light scattering, not radioactivity."}'),
  ('CHE-0632', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Monitoring air quality', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 308 (§14.11.1)', 'Which method measures air pollutants over large regional and global areas?', '["Remote sensing by satellites", "Continuous emission monitoring systems at a factory", "A fixed air quality monitoring station", "A diesel particulate filter"]', 0, 'Satellites with sensors can measure atmospheric pollutants over large areas, giving regional and global data.', '{"1": "CEMS monitor emissions at individual industrial sites.", "2": "Fixed stations measure local air in urban and industrial zones.", "3": "A diesel particulate filter removes soot; it does not measure air quality."}'),
  ('CHE-0633', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Reducing air pollution', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 309 (§14.14)', 'In a car''s catalytic converter,', '["CO is reduced to C and NOx is oxidized to NO₃.", "CO is oxidized to CO₂ and NOx is reduced to N₂.", "hydrocarbons are converted to CO.", "SO₂ is converted to H₂SO₄."]', 1, 'The converter oxidizes CO to CO₂, reduces NOx to N₂ and converts unburnt hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O.', '{"0": "Both directions are reversed here.", "2": "Hydrocarbons are converted to CO₂ and water, not to toxic CO.", "3": "The book does not describe the converter treating SO₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0634', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Reducing air pollution', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 309 (§14.14)', 'The main job of a diesel particulate filter is to', '["convert NOx to N₂.", "remove SO₂ with water.", "capture and store soot particles from the exhaust.", "measure the AQI."]', 2, 'A diesel particulate filter captures and stores soot particles from diesel exhaust to cut PM emissions.', '{"0": "NOx reduction is done by catalytic converters and selective catalytic reduction.", "1": "Scrubbers use liquids such as water to remove SO₂.", "3": "Air quality is measured by instruments such as nephelometers."}'),
  ('CHE-0635', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Reducing air pollution', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 310 (§14.14)', 'Which catalysts does the book give for selective catalytic reduction (SCR) in diesel engines?', '["Iron and molybdenum", "TiO₂ and zeolites", "V₂O₅ and platinum gauze", "Nickel and palladium"]', 1, 'SCR reduces NOx to N₂ and oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons using catalysts such as TiO₂ and zeolites.', '{"0": "Iron with molybdenum is associated with the Haber process.", "2": "These are associated with sulphuric acid and nitric acid manufacture.", "3": "These are associated with hydrogenation."}'),
  ('CHE-0636', 'Environmental chemistry', 'Laws and regulations', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Atmosphere, p. 310 (§14.15.1)', 'The Pakistan Environmental Protection Act (PEPA) was passed in', '["2012", "1997", "1987", "2005"]', 1, 'The book lists the Pakistan Environmental Protection Act 1997, together with the National Environmental Quality Standards and the Punjab Environmental Protection (Amendment) Act 2012.', '{"0": "2012 is the year of the Punjab Environmental Protection (Amendment) Act.", "2": "1987 is not given in the book.", "3": "2005 is not given in the book."}'),
  ('CHE-0637', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Methods of separation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 314', 'In qualitative analysis, a chemist is concerned with', '["the relative amounts of elements in a compound.", "measuring the mass of a sample only.", "detecting or identifying the elements present in a compound.", "calculating the yield of a reaction."]', 2, 'The book says qualitative analysis detects or identifies the elements present, while quantitative analysis finds their relative amounts.', '{"0": "Relative amounts are found by quantitative analysis.", "1": "Weighing is part of measurement, not the definition of qualitative analysis.", "3": "Yield calculation is quantitative work."}'),
  ('CHE-0638', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Methods of separation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 314', 'Which is NOT one of the four major steps of a complete quantitative determination listed in the book?', '["Obtaining a sample for analysis", "Separating the desired constituent", "Measurement and calculation of results", "Naming the compound by IUPAC rules"]', 3, 'The four steps are obtaining a sample, separating the desired constituent, measuring and calculating results, and drawing conclusions.', '{"0": "Obtaining a sample is step 1.", "1": "Separating the desired constituent is step 2.", "2": "Measurement and calculation is step 3."}'),
  ('CHE-0639', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 315 (§15.1.1)', 'A mixture of common salt and sand is stirred with water and filtered. The sand left on the filter paper is called the', '["filtrate", "distillate", "solute", "residue"]', 3, 'The liquid that passes through into the flask is the filtrate; the solid left behind on the filter (here sand) is the residue.', '{"0": "The filtrate is the salt solution that runs through.", "1": "A distillate is collected from a condenser in distillation.", "2": "The solute is the dissolved salt."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0640', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 315 (§15.1.1)', 'In the salt solution obtained from the salt–sand mixture, the solvent is', '["common salt, because it dissolves.", "sand, because it does not dissolve.", "water, because it is present in excess.", "the filter paper."]', 2, 'The book defines the solvent as the component present in excess compared with the solute. Water dissolves the salt and is in excess.', '{"0": "Salt is the solute.", "1": "Sand does not form part of the solution at all.", "3": "Filter paper is the filter medium."}'),
  ('CHE-0641', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 315 (§15.1.1)', 'According to the book, at the end of a filtration the filter paper should be', '["completely full of precipitate.", "dry and folded flat.", "almost empty, with the precipitate in the filtrate.", "one-fourth to one-half full of precipitate."]', 3, 'The paper should be large enough to be one-fourth to one-half full of precipitate at the end, so the filtrate runs down without splashing.', '{"0": "A full paper risks overflow and splashing.", "1": "The paper is folded into a cone and moistened, not left flat and dry.", "2": "The precipitate should stay on the paper, not pass into the filtrate."}'),
  ('CHE-0642', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 316 (§15.1.1)', 'Fluted filter paper is used because it', '["resists concentrated HCl.", "can be ignited at high temperature.", "filters faster than a plain conical paper.", "needs no funnel."]', 2, 'Folding the paper into a fan of alternate ridges and hollows (fluting) considerably increases the rate of filtration through a conical funnel.', '{"0": "Solutions that attack paper are filtered through a Gooch crucible with an asbestos mat.", "1": "Precipitates that must be ignited are filtered through a Gooch crucible.", "3": "Fluted paper is used in a conical funnel."}'),
  ('CHE-0643', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 316–317 (§15.1.1)', 'A Gooch crucible is', '["a glass crucible with a porous glass disc sealed into the bottom.", "a desiccator for drying crystals.", "a funnel with a fluted filter paper.", "a porcelain crucible with a perforated bottom covered with paper pulp or a filter paper."]', 3, 'The Gooch crucible is porcelain with a perforated bottom covered by paper pulp or a cut filter paper, and is used with suction.', '{"0": "This describes a sintered glass crucible.", "1": "Crystals are dried in a vacuum desiccator.", "2": "Fluted paper is used in a conical glass funnel."}'),
  ('CHE-0644', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 317 (§15.1.1)', 'To filter concentrated HCl or KMnO₄ solution, which would react with paper, the book suggests', '["an ordinary filter paper in a glass funnel.", "a fluted filter paper.", "a Gooch crucible with its perforations covered by an asbestos mat.", "a cotton wool plug."]', 2, 'When the perforations of a Gooch crucible are covered with an asbestos mat instead of paper, it can filter solutions that attack paper, such as concentrated HCl and KMnO₄.', '{"0": "These solutions react with paper.", "1": "Fluted paper is still paper and would be attacked.", "3": "Cotton is organic and would also be attacked; the book does not suggest it."}'),
  ('CHE-0645', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Filtration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 317 (§15.1.1)', 'The sintered glass crucible is convenient because it', '["is used only for gravity filtration.", "is made of porcelain.", "needs no preparation before use, unlike the Gooch crucible.", "is lined with asbestos."]', 2, 'A sintered glass crucible has a porous glass disc sealed into it, so no pad has to be prepared as with the Gooch crucible.', '{"0": "Crucibles are generally used with suction.", "1": "It is made of glass; the Gooch crucible is porcelain.", "3": "An asbestos mat is an option for the Gooch crucible."}'),
  ('CHE-0646', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 317 (§15.2)', 'The basic principle of crystallization is that the solute', '["is insoluble in the solvent at all temperatures.", "is adsorbed on the stationary phase.", "boils at a lower temperature than the solvent.", "dissolves in a suitable solvent when hot, and the excess comes out as crystals on cooling."]', 3, 'A suitable solvent dissolves much of the solute at high temperature; on cooling, the solubility falls and the excess solute is thrown out as crystals.', '{"0": "An insoluble solute could not be recrystallized from that solvent.", "1": "Adsorption is the basis of adsorption chromatography.", "2": "Boiling points are the basis of distillation."}'),
  ('CHE-0647', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 317 (§15.2.1)', 'An ideal solvent for crystallization should', '["react with the solute to form crystals.", "dissolve the substance equally well at all temperatures.", "dissolve a large amount of the substance at its boiling point and only a small amount at room temperature.", "dissolve the impurities and crystallize them along with the solute."]', 2, 'A big difference in solubility between hot and cold gives a good recovery of crystals on cooling.', '{"0": "The solvent must not react chemically with the solute.", "1": "If solubility does not change with temperature, nothing crystallizes on cooling.", "3": "Impurities should either not dissolve or not crystallize out with the solute."}'),
  ('CHE-0648', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 318 (§15.2.1)', 'If the solvent chosen for crystallization is inflammable, the solution should be heated', '["directly over a Bunsen flame.", "with a hot water funnel.", "in a sealed tube.", "on a water bath."]', 3, 'The book says that with an inflammable solvent a water bath should be used for heating so that the solvent does not catch fire.', '{"0": "A naked flame could ignite the solvent vapour.", "1": "A hot water funnel keeps a solution hot during filtration; it is not the heating method.", "2": "Heating a sealed tube of solvent is dangerous and is not suggested."}'),
  ('CHE-0649', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 317–318 (Keep in mind, §15.2.2)', 'Why is a hot water funnel sometimes used to filter a hot saturated solution during crystallization?', '["To decolourize the solution", "To speed up the cooling", "To stop crystals forming early and choking the filter paper or funnel stem", "To dry the crystals"]', 2, 'If the solution cools during filtration, crystals appear in the paper or stem and block it. A hot water funnel keeps the solution hot.', '{"0": "Coloured impurities are removed with animal charcoal.", "1": "The purpose is the opposite: to keep the solution hot while filtering.", "3": "Crystals are dried in air, an oven or a vacuum desiccator."}'),
  ('CHE-0650', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 318 (Keep in mind)', 'Coloured impurities in a crude product are removed during crystallization by', '["using a fluted filter paper alone.", "adding concentrated sulphuric acid.", "cooling the solution very rapidly.", "boiling with finely powdered animal charcoal and filtering hot."]', 3, 'The coloured impurities are adsorbed by animal charcoal, which is filtered off from the hot solution; the decolourized substance crystallizes on cooling.', '{"0": "Filtration alone does not remove dissolved coloured matter.", "1": "Concentrated sulphuric acid would likely react with the product.", "2": "Rapid cooling affects crystal size, not colour."}'),
  ('CHE-0651', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 318 (§15.2.2)', 'The hot filtered solution is cooled at a moderate rate during crystallization in order to', '["dissolve more solute.", "make the solvent boil.", "form medium-sized crystals.", "remove insoluble impurities."]', 2, 'The book says cooling at a moderate rate gives medium-sized crystals.', '{"0": "Cooling lowers solubility, so solute comes out of solution.", "1": "Boiling happens during dissolving, not cooling.", "3": "Insoluble impurities were already removed by hot filtration."}'),
  ('CHE-0652', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.2.2)', 'Which of these is NOT given in the book as a drying agent in a vacuum desiccator?', '["Calcium chloride", "Silica gel", "Phosphorus pentoxide", "Sodium chloride"]', 3, 'The drying agents named are CaCl₂, silica gel and phosphorus pentoxide.', '{"0": "Anhydrous CaCl₂ is listed as a drying agent.", "1": "Silica gel is listed as a drying agent.", "2": "Phosphorus pentoxide is listed as a drying agent."}'),
  ('CHE-0653', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Crystallization', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 318 (§15.2.2)', 'Crystals should be dried in an oven only if the substance', '["contains water of crystallization that must be removed.", "is coloured.", "is soluble in water.", "does not melt or decompose on heating at 100 °C."]', 3, 'The book says crystals may be oven-dried provided the substance does not melt or decompose at 100 °C; otherwise air-drying or a vacuum desiccator is used.', '{"0": "The book''s condition is stability at 100 °C.", "1": "Colour has no bearing on oven drying.", "2": "Solubility in water is not the condition given."}'),
  ('CHE-0654', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.3)', 'Sea water is made drinkable by', '["filtration", "simple distillation", "paper chromatography", "crystallization"]', 1, 'Sea water is a solution of many inorganic salts in water. Simple distillation boils off the water, which is condensed and collected, leaving the salts behind.', '{"0": "Filtration cannot remove dissolved salts.", "2": "Chromatography separates small samples for analysis.", "3": "Crystallization would recover the salts, not drinkable water."}'),
  ('CHE-0655', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.3.1)', 'Boiling chips are added to the flask in simple distillation to', '["prevent bumping of the liquid.", "raise the boiling point.", "react with impurities.", "condense the vapour."]', 0, 'Boiling chips give smooth boiling and prevent bumping.', '{"1": "They do not change the boiling point.", "2": "They are inert.", "3": "The condenser condenses the vapour."}'),
  ('CHE-0656', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.3.1)', 'In simple distillation the liquid collected in the receiving flask is called the ___ and what remains in the flask is the ___.', '["distillate; residue", "filtrate; residue", "residue; distillate", "solute; solvent"]', 0, 'The condensed vapour collected is the distillate; the components left behind in the distillation flask are the residue.', '{"1": "\"Filtrate\" is the liquid that passes through in filtration.", "2": "The terms are reversed.", "3": "Solute and solvent describe the parts of a solution, not the products of distillation."}'),
  ('CHE-0657', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.3)', 'Which mixture is best separated by fractional distillation?', '["Sand and salt water", "Ethanol and water", "A coloured dye mixture for analysis", "Copper sulphate from its solution"]', 1, 'Fractional distillation separates miscible liquids with different boiling points, such as ethanol (78 °C) and water (100 °C).', '{"0": "Sand is removed by filtration.", "2": "Dye mixtures are analysed by chromatography.", "3": "A salt is recovered from solution by crystallization."}'),
  ('CHE-0658', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 319 (§15.3.1)', 'The fractionating column used in fractional distillation is packed with glass beads to', '["increase the surface area available for condensing the vapour.", "absorb the water.", "catalyse the separation.", "stop the vapour reaching the condenser."]', 0, 'The beads give a large surface on which vapour repeatedly condenses and re-evaporates, improving the separation of the liquids.', '{"1": "The beads do not absorb liquid.", "2": "Separation is physical; there is no catalysis.", "3": "Vapour must pass up the column to reach the condenser."}'),
  ('CHE-0659', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 320 (§15.3.1)', 'During fractional distillation of an ethanol–water mixture, the thermometer stays at 78 °C for some time and then begins to rise. This shows that', '["water is now distilling over.", "nearly all the ethanol has distilled, so the receiver should be changed.", "the mixture has formed a solid.", "the condenser has stopped working."]', 1, 'The temperature stays at 78 °C while ethanol distils. When it rises above 78 °C the ethanol is mostly gone, so the book says to replace the receiving flask before collecting water at 100 °C.', '{"0": "Water distils at 100 °C; at the moment the temperature starts rising it has not yet reached that point.", "2": "Nothing solidifies in the flask.", "3": "The rise in temperature is a reading of the vapour, not a sign of condenser failure."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0660', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Distillation', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 320 (Interesting information)', 'Petrol, diesel, kerosene and furnace oil are separated from petroleum by', '["filtration", "crystallization", "fractional distillation", "paper chromatography"]', 2, 'These components have boiling points not very different from one another, so they are separated as fractions in a fractionating column.', '{"0": "Filtration separates solids from liquids.", "1": "Crystallization purifies solids.", "3": "Paper chromatography is an analytical technique for small samples."}'),
  ('CHE-0661', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 320–321 (§15.4)', 'Chromatography in which the stationary phase is a solid is called', '["partition chromatography", "adsorption chromatography", "paper chromatography", "gas–liquid chromatography"]', 1, 'When the stationary phase is a solid, substances leave the mobile phase and are adsorbed on the solid surface: adsorption chromatography, for example TLC and column chromatography.', '{"0": "Partition chromatography has a liquid stationary phase.", "2": "Paper chromatography is a partition method.", "3": "Gas–liquid chromatography is a partition method."}'),
  ('CHE-0662', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 321 (§15.4)', 'Which pair are both examples of partition chromatography according to the book?', '["Paper chromatography and gas–liquid chromatography", "Thin layer chromatography and column chromatography", "Column chromatography and paper chromatography", "Thin layer chromatography and gas–liquid chromatography"]', 0, 'In partition chromatography the stationary phase is a liquid. The book''s examples are paper chromatography and gas–liquid chromatography.', '{"1": "TLC and column chromatography are the book''s adsorption examples.", "2": "Column chromatography is an adsorption method.", "3": "TLC is an adsorption method."}'),
  ('CHE-0663', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 321 (§15.4.1)', 'In paper chromatography, the stationary phase is', '["the cellulose fibres themselves acting as an adsorbent.", "water trapped in the cellulose fibres of the paper.", "the organic solvent that moves up the paper.", "the air inside the tank."]', 1, 'Paper chromatography is a partition method: the stationary phase is the water entrapped in the cellulose fibres, and the mobile phase is usually an organic liquid immiscible with it.', '{"0": "The book describes paper chromatography as partition, with trapped water as the stationary phase.", "2": "The moving solvent is the mobile phase.", "3": "The air plays no part in the separation."}'),
  ('CHE-0664', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 321 (§15.4.1)', 'In ascending paper chromatography, the solvent moves up the paper by', '["gravity", "capillary action", "suction from a pump", "electric attraction"]', 1, 'In the ascending method the solvent travels upwards by capillary action.', '{"0": "Gravity would pull the solvent down; that is used in the descending method.", "2": "No pump is used.", "3": "Electrophoresis uses electric fields; paper chromatography does not."}'),
  ('CHE-0665', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 321–322 (§15.4.1)', 'The starting line on a paper chromatogram is drawn in pencil because', '["pencil graphite does not dissolve in the solvent and run up the paper.", "ink is too expensive.", "pencil lines glow under UV light.", "pencil acts as the locating agent."]', 0, 'The book uses a thin pencil line about 2.5 cm from the end. Graphite is insoluble, so the line stays put; an ink line would dissolve and separate like the sample.', '{"1": "Cost is not the reason.", "2": "The book does not say pencil glows under UV light.", "3": "Locating agents such as ninhydrin are sprayed on after the run."}'),
  ('CHE-0666', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 322 (§15.4.2)', 'Ninhydrin is used in paper chromatography as a', '["solvent", "locating agent for colourless substances such as amino acids", "stationary phase", "drying agent"]', 1, 'A locating agent reacts with colourless substances such as amino acids to give coloured, visible spots. Ninhydrin is the book''s example.', '{"0": "Ninhydrin is applied after the run, not used as the mobile phase.", "2": "The stationary phase is water held in the paper.", "3": "Drying agents are substances like CaCl₂ or silica gel."}'),
  ('CHE-0667', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 322 (§15.4.3)', 'On a chromatogram, a spot has moved 3.0 cm from the starting line while the solvent front has moved 7.5 cm. The Rf value of the spot is', '["0.40", "2.5", "0.25", "4.5"]', 0, 'Rf = distance moved by the component ÷ distance moved by the solvent = 3.0 ÷ 7.5 = 0.40.', '{"1": "2.5 is 7.5 ÷ 3.0, the ratio upside down; Rf can never exceed 1.", "2": "0.25 does not follow from these distances.", "3": "4.5 is the difference 7.5 − 3.0, not a ratio."}'),
  ('CHE-0668', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 322 (§15.4.3)', 'Two components have Rf values of 0.30 and 0.70. The solvent front is 10.0 cm from the starting line. How far apart are the two spots?', '["4.0 cm", "1.0 cm", "3.0 cm", "7.0 cm"]', 0, 'The spots are at 0.30 × 10.0 = 3.0 cm and 0.70 × 10.0 = 7.0 cm from the start, so they are 7.0 − 3.0 = 4.0 cm apart.', '{"1": "1.0 cm would be the separation if the solvent front were 2.5 cm from the start.", "2": "3.0 cm is the position of the first spot, not the gap.", "3": "7.0 cm is the position of the second spot, not the gap."}'),
  ('CHE-0669', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Paper chromatography', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 322 (§15.4.3)', 'A product gives a single spot on a paper chromatogram with the same Rf value as a pure reference sample. This suggests that the product is', '["impure, containing two substances", "pure and likely the same substance as the reference", "a colourless amino acid", "insoluble in the solvent"]', 1, 'A pure substance gives one spot, and two substances that are likely identical have the same Rf value.', '{"0": "An impure substance gives more than one spot.", "2": "Nothing in the result shows the product is an amino acid.", "3": "An insoluble sample would stay on the starting line instead of moving to the reference''s Rf."}'),
  ('CHE-0670', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Purity', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 323 (§15.5)', 'A sample of aspirin melts gradually over the range 128–133 °C. According to the book this shows that the sample', '["is pure aspirin.", "is impure.", "is salicylic acid.", "has decomposed into acetic anhydride."]', 1, 'A pure product melts sharply (aspirin at about 136 °C). Melting over a range of temperature indicates an impure product, for example aspirin mixed with unreacted salicylic acid.', '{"0": "Pure aspirin would melt sharply at about 136 °C.", "2": "A melting range alone does not identify the substance as salicylic acid.", "3": "There is no evidence of decomposition into acetic anhydride."}'),
  ('CHE-0671', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Purity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 323 (§15.5)', 'In a mixed melting point test, a product is mixed with a pure sample of aspirin. The mixture still melts sharply at the aspirin melting point. This shows that', '["the product is pure aspirin.", "the product is a different compound.", "the product contains salicylic acid.", "the test is invalid."]', 0, 'If the product is pure aspirin, mixing it with pure aspirin changes nothing, so the melting point stays sharp. An impurity or a different compound would lower and broaden it.', '{"1": "A different compound would act as an impurity and give a lower, broader melting range.", "2": "Salicylic acid as an impurity would broaden the melting range.", "3": "The mixed melting point is a standard purity check described in the book."}'),
  ('CHE-0672', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Purity', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Basic Separation Techniques, p. 323 (§15.5)', 'Aspirin is prepared by reacting salicylic acid with', '["excess acetic anhydride", "ethanol", "sodium hydroxide", "methanol and sulphuric acid"]', 0, 'Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is made by reacting salicylic acid with excess acetic anhydride and a few drops of acid catalyst.', '{"1": "Ethanol does not acetylate salicylic acid to aspirin.", "2": "Sodium hydroxide would form a salt.", "3": "Methanol with acid gives methyl salicylate, not aspirin."}'),
  ('CHE-0673', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'General lab instructions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 327 (§16.1)', 'Which of these is one of the book''s general instructions for students in the laboratory?', '["Work alone so that you are not distracted.", "Report broken glassware only at the end of the term.", "Pour small amounts of waste chemicals down the drain.", "Never taste or smell any compound or gas."]', 3, 'The book says not to bring food into the lab and never to taste or smell any compound or gas.', '{"0": "Students are told not to work alone, and to work in the presence of the lab instructor.", "1": "Accidents and breakages must be reported to the lab in-charge immediately.", "2": "Chemicals must not be poured down drains or put into the sewer."}'),
  ('CHE-0674', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'General lab instructions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 327 (§16.1)', 'If a student does not know how to handle an instrument properly, the student should', '["try it anyway and learn by mistakes.", "skip the experiment without telling anyone.", "watch a classmate and copy them.", "ask the instructor for help."]', 3, 'The book says that if you cannot handle an instrument or equipment properly you must seek help from the instructor.', '{"0": "Trying unfamiliar equipment without guidance risks injury and damage.", "1": "The instructor should be told; skipping silently is not advised.", "2": "A classmate may not know the safe procedure either."}'),
  ('CHE-0675', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Laboratory hazards', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 327 (§16.2)', 'The book groups most laboratory hazards into which three categories?', '["Solid, liquid and gas", "Electrical, thermal and nuclear", "Acidic, basic and neutral", "Physical, chemical and biological"]', 3, 'Most hazards in the laboratory fall into physical, chemical and biological categories.', '{"0": "These are states of matter.", "1": "These are not the book''s three categories, though electrical and heat risks are mentioned under fire.", "2": "These describe solutions, not hazard types."}'),
  ('CHE-0676', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Laboratory hazards', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 327 (§16.2.1)', 'According to the book, the most common physical hazards in a laboratory are', '["corrosive acids.", "allergens and microbes.", "toxic gases.", "slips and falls on wet floors."]', 3, 'The book names slips and falls when working on a wet floor as the most common physical hazards.', '{"0": "Corrosive acids are chemical hazards.", "1": "Allergens and microbes are biological hazards.", "2": "Toxic gases are chemical hazards."}'),
  ('CHE-0677', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Laboratory hazards', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 327 (§16.2.1)', 'When handling broken glassware, a worker should', '["pick it up with bare hands quickly.", "put it in the ordinary waste bin.", "wear cut-resistant gloves and put it in a special container.", "wash it down the sink."]', 2, 'Cut-resistant gloves prevent cuts and abrasions, and broken glass should go into a special container to prevent injury.', '{"0": "Bare hands risk cuts.", "1": "The book requires a special container, not the ordinary bin.", "3": "Glass must not be put down the sink."}'),
  ('CHE-0678', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Laboratory hazards', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 328 (§16.2.3)', 'Which is an example of a biological hazard in the lab?', '["A wet floor", "A faulty electric socket", "Concentrated sulphuric acid", "Bacteria and viruses"]', 3, 'The book says the most common biological hazards are allergens and microbes, including viruses and bacteria.', '{"0": "A wet floor is a physical hazard.", "1": "A faulty socket is an electrical (physical) hazard.", "2": "Concentrated acid is a chemical hazard."}'),
  ('CHE-0679', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Waste disposal', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 328 (§16.3)', 'According to the book, chemical waste containers should be labelled with', '["the colour of the waste.", "only the student''s name.", "the type of waste, the date and the place of origin.", "nothing, since they are sent for treatment anyway."]', 2, 'The rules say waste containers must be labelled with the types of waste, the date and the place of origin.', '{"0": "Colour is not what the label must state.", "1": "A name alone does not tell the handlers what is inside.", "3": "Labelling is required before the waste is moved for treatment."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0680', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Waste disposal', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 328 (§16.3)', 'Which is NOT listed in the book as a method of chemical treatment of wastes?', '["Neutralization", "Precipitation", "Ion exchange", "Pouring into the sewer"]', 3, 'Chemical treatment includes neutralization, precipitation, ion exchange, oxidation or reduction. Chemical waste must never go into bins or the sewer.', '{"0": "Neutralization is a listed treatment.", "1": "Precipitation is a listed treatment.", "2": "Ion exchange is a listed treatment."}'),
  ('CHE-0681', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'Acid splashes into a student''s eye. After washing thoroughly with water, the eye should be washed with', '["1% boric acid solution", "1% sodium bicarbonate solution", "2% ammonia solution", "1% acetic acid solution"]', 1, 'Mild alkali neutralizes any remaining acid, so the book gives water followed by 1% sodium bicarbonate solution.', '{"0": "Boric acid solution is used for alkali in the eye.", "2": "2% ammonia is used for bromine burns on the skin.", "3": "1% acetic acid is used for alkali burns on the skin."}'),
  ('CHE-0682', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'Alkali gets into the eye. After washing with water, the book recommends washing with', '["2% ammonia solution", "saturated sodium bicarbonate solution", "olive oil", "1% boric acid solution"]', 3, 'A weak acid (1% boric acid) neutralizes the remaining alkali.', '{"0": "Ammonia is an alkali and would worsen an alkali injury.", "1": "Sodium bicarbonate is used for acids, not alkalis.", "2": "Olive oil is used for soreness in the eye."}'),
  ('CHE-0683', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'A student gets an alkali burn on the hand. After washing freely with water, the skin should be washed with', '["saturated sodium bicarbonate solution", "2% ammonia solution", "1% acetic acid solution", "lime water"]', 2, 'The book gives water, then 1% acetic acid solution and water again, then drying and applying burnol.', '{"0": "Sodium bicarbonate is for acid burns.", "1": "Ammonia is for bromine burns.", "3": "Lime water is alkaline and is suggested for swallowed acid."}'),
  ('CHE-0684', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'Which first aid treatment for a bromine burn is given in the book?', '["Apply mustard oil directly", "Wash with 1% acetic acid and apply olive oil", "Wash with saturated sodium bicarbonate and then cold water", "Wash with 2% ammonia solution, apply glycerin, wipe off and apply burnol"]', 3, 'For bromine burns the book says wash fully with 2% ammonia solution, apply glycerin, wipe it off after some time and apply burnol.', '{"0": "Burnol or mustard oil is for dry-heat burns.", "1": "Acetic acid is for alkali burns; olive oil is for sore eyes.", "2": "Sodium bicarbonate is for acid burns."}'),
  ('CHE-0685', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'Someone accidentally swallows an acid. The book''s first aid is to drink plenty of water or', '["lemon juice", "vinegar", "lime water or milk of magnesia", "egg white only"]', 2, 'Mild bases such as lime water or milk of magnesia neutralize the swallowed acid.', '{"0": "Lemon or orange juice is suggested for swallowed caustic alkali.", "1": "Vinegar is an acid and would not help.", "3": "Milk or egg white is suggested for swallowed heavy metal salts."}'),
  ('CHE-0686', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'For swallowed salts of heavy metals, the book recommends taking', '["dilute HCl", "lime water", "orange juice", "milk or the white of an egg"]', 3, 'Milk or egg white is the book''s first aid for swallowed heavy metal salts.', '{"0": "Acid would not help and could cause more harm.", "1": "Lime water is for swallowed acid.", "2": "Orange juice is for swallowed caustic alkali."}'),
  ('CHE-0687', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'First aid', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 329 (Table 16.1)', 'For a serious cut, the book says to', '["apply burnol immediately.", "wash it with 2% ammonia.", "apply pressure on the cut for about 10 minutes and consult a doctor.", "leave it open to dry."]', 2, 'Pressure for about 10 minutes stops the bleeding, and a doctor should then be consulted.', '{"0": "Burnol is for burns.", "1": "Ammonia is for bromine burns.", "3": "A serious cut needs bleeding control and a doctor."}'),
  ('CHE-0688', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Fire safety', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (Table 16.1)', 'A beaker of inflammable liquid catches fire. The book advises', '["pouring water on it.", "covering the beaker with a duster or damp cloth to cut off oxygen.", "blowing the flame out.", "carrying the beaker outside quickly."]', 1, 'Covering the beaker cuts off the supply of oxygen and puts the fire out.', '{"0": "Water can spread burning liquid; the book uses a damp cloth instead.", "2": "Blowing feeds oxygen to the fire and spreads it.", "3": "Moving a burning beaker risks spilling it and spreading the fire."}'),
  ('CHE-0689', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Fire safety', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (Table 16.1)', 'Why should water NOT be thrown on burning spirit or oil?', '["Water reacts explosively with oil.", "Water simply spreads the fire.", "Water makes the oil more inflammable.", "Water releases toxic chlorine."]', 1, 'The book says throwing water on burning spirit or oil will simply spread the fire. A mixture of sand and sodium bicarbonate is used instead.', '{"0": "The problem is spreading, not an explosive reaction.", "2": "Water does not change how inflammable the oil is.", "3": "No chlorine is produced."}'),
  ('CHE-0690', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Fire safety', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (Table 16.1)', 'If a person''s clothes catch fire, the book says the person should', '["run to the nearest tap.", "not run, wrap in a blanket or dry cotton cloth and lie down on the floor.", "pour spirit on the flames.", "stand still with arms raised."]', 1, 'Running fans the flames. Wrapping in a blanket and lying down smothers the fire.', '{"0": "Running supplies more air to the fire.", "2": "Spirit is a fuel and would make the fire worse.", "3": "Standing still does nothing to smother the flames."}'),
  ('CHE-0691', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Fire safety', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (Table 16.1)', 'When electrical parts catch fire, the first step is to', '["throw water on them.", "switch off the electric supply immediately, then throw sand.", "cover with a damp cloth while the power is on.", "use a mixture of oil and sand."]', 1, 'The book says switch off the electric supply immediately and throw sand, and do not use water.', '{"0": "Water conducts electricity and must not be used.", "2": "The supply must be switched off first.", "3": "Oil is a fuel and has no place in fire fighting."}'),
  ('CHE-0692', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (§16.5)', 'In a titration, the moment at which the indicator changes colour is called the', '["equivalence volume", "end point", "titre", "standard point"]', 1, 'The book defines the end point as the moment at which the indicator changes colour.', '{"0": "The book does not use this term for the colour change.", "2": "The titre is the volume of solution added from the burette.", "3": "This is not a term in the book."}'),
  ('CHE-0693', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (§16.5)', 'Before use in a titration, the pipette for NaOH should be rinsed', '["with distilled water only.", "first with distilled water and then with the NaOH solution.", "with the HCl solution.", "with ethanol."]', 1, 'Rinsing with the solution it will deliver removes water drops that would dilute the NaOH and change its concentration.', '{"0": "Distilled water left inside would dilute the NaOH.", "2": "HCl left inside would react with the NaOH.", "3": "Ethanol rinsing is not part of the procedure."}'),
  ('CHE-0694', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (§16.5)', 'The conical flask used in a titration is rinsed with distilled water only, not with the solution it will hold, because', '["rinsing with solution would make the flask too heavy.", "the solution would dissolve the glass.", "distilled water acts as an indicator.", "the amount of solute in the flask is fixed by the pipetted volume, and extra water does not change it."]', 3, 'The moles of NaOH in the flask come from the measured 10 cm³ pipetted in. Water in the flask changes the volume but not the moles, so the end point is unaffected. Rinsing with NaOH would add extra base.', '{"0": "The mass of the flask is irrelevant.", "1": "Dilute NaOH does not dissolve the glass during a titration.", "2": "Phenolphthalein is the indicator, not water."}'),
  ('CHE-0695', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 331 (§16.5)', 'Before taking the initial burette reading, the acid is run out through the tap into a beaker to', '["remove any air bubble in the nozzle.", "warm the acid.", "dilute the acid.", "check the indicator."]', 0, 'An air bubble in the nozzle would be filled by acid during the titration and make the volume reading wrong.', '{"1": "Running a little acid out does not warm it.", "2": "It does not change the concentration.", "3": "The indicator is in the conical flask."}'),
  ('CHE-0696', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 331 (§16.5)', 'According to the book, concordant titration readings are readings that', '["agree with one another within 0.1 cm³.", "are all exactly the same to 0.001 cm³.", "differ by at least 1 cm³.", "come from the rough titration."]', 0, 'At least three readings that agree with one another within 0.1 cm³ should be taken.', '{"1": "A burette cannot be read to 0.001 cm³.", "2": "Readings 1 cm³ apart are not concordant.", "3": "The rough titration only gives an approximate value."}'),
  ('CHE-0697', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 331 (§16.5)', 'Four titres are recorded: 12.60, 12.15, 12.20 and 12.10 cm³. Using concordant readings only, the volume of acid to use in the calculation is', '["12.15 cm³", "12.26 cm³", "12.60 cm³", "12.35 cm³"]', 0, '12.60 is not within 0.1 cm³ of the others. The concordant readings are 12.15, 12.20 and 12.10, which average (12.15 + 12.20 + 12.10) ÷ 3 = 12.15 cm³.', '{"1": "12.26 cm³ is the average of all four, including the non-concordant 12.60.", "2": "12.60 cm³ is the outlier.", "3": "12.35 cm³ is the midpoint of the highest and lowest values, not a concordant average."}'),
  ('CHE-0698', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 332 (§16.5)', '10.0 cm³ of NaOH solution needs 12.5 cm³ of 0.10 M HCl to reach the end point. The molarity of the NaOH is', '["0.125 M", "0.080 M", "0.25 M", "1.25 M"]', 0, 'NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O, so n₁ = n₂ = 1. M₂ = M₁V₁ / V₂ = (0.10 × 12.5) ÷ 10.0 = 0.125 M.', '{"1": "0.080 M comes from swapping the volumes: 0.10 × 10.0 ÷ 12.5.", "2": "0.25 M comes from wrongly using a 1 : 2 mole ratio.", "3": "1.25 M is ten times too large, a decimal slip."}'),
  ('CHE-0699', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 332 (§16.5)', '20.0 cm³ of 0.050 M H₂SO₄ exactly neutralizes 10.0 cm³ of NaOH solution. Using M₁V₁/n₁ = M₂V₂/n₂, the molarity of the NaOH is', '["0.050 M", "0.10 M", "0.20 M", "0.025 M"]', 2, 'H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, so n₁ = 1 and n₂ = 2. M₂ = M₁V₁n₂ / (n₁V₂) = (0.050 × 20.0 × 2) ÷ (1 × 10.0) = 0.20 M.', '{"0": "0.050 M is the acid''s molarity, not the base''s.", "1": "0.10 M ignores the 1 : 2 mole ratio.", "3": "0.025 M uses the mole ratio and the volumes upside down."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0700', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 330 (Did you know)', 'The light pink colour of phenolphthalein at the end point tends to fade slowly because', '["atmospheric carbon dioxide dissolves in the solution.", "the indicator evaporates.", "light decomposes the NaCl formed.", "the burette leaks."]', 0, 'The book says the fading is caused by atmospheric CO₂, which slowly dissolves in the solution and makes it slightly acidic.', '{"1": "Phenolphthalein does not evaporate from the solution in this time.", "2": "NaCl is not decomposed by light.", "3": "A leaking burette would affect the reading, not the colour fading."}'),
  ('CHE-0701', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Acid-base titration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 331 (§16.5)', 'The conical flask is placed on a white paper under the burette in order to', '["see the colour change of the indicator clearly.", "absorb any spilled acid.", "keep the flask warm.", "act as an anti-parallax card for the flask."]', 0, 'A white background makes the indicator''s colour change easy to see.', '{"1": "That is not its purpose in the book.", "2": "Paper does not keep the flask warm.", "3": "The anti-parallax card is used when reading the burette."}'),
  ('CHE-0702', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for anions', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 332 (§16.6 a)', 'Dilute HCl is added to a solid, and a gas evolves briskly that turns lime water milky. The anion indicated is', '["chloride", "sulphate", "carbonate", "nitrate"]', 2, 'Carbonates give CO₂ with dilute acid (CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂), and CO₂ turns lime water milky by forming CaCO₃.', '{"0": "Chloride is tested with acidified silver nitrate.", "1": "Sulphate is tested with barium nitrate.", "3": "Nitrate is tested with NaOH and aluminium powder."}'),
  ('CHE-0703', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for anions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 333 (§16.6 b, Did you know)', 'In the silver nitrate test for halide ions, dilute nitric acid is added first in order to', '["prevent carbonate ions from precipitating along with the halide.", "dissolve the silver halide precipitate.", "turn the solution yellow.", "oxidize the halide to halogen."]', 0, 'Carbonate would also give a precipitate with Ag⁺. Dilute nitric acid removes carbonate so only silver halides precipitate.', '{"1": "Nitric acid does not dissolve silver halides; that is the point of the test.", "2": "The acid adds no colour.", "3": "Dilute nitric acid is used to remove carbonate, not to oxidize halides."}'),
  ('CHE-0704', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for anions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 333 (§16.6 b)', 'An acidified salt solution gives a bright yellow precipitate with aqueous silver nitrate. The salt contains', '["Cl⁻", "Br⁻", "I⁻", "SO₄²⁻"]', 2, 'In the book''s table, I⁻ gives a bright yellow precipitate of AgI.', '{"0": "Cl⁻ gives a thick white precipitate that dissolves in aqueous ammonia.", "1": "Br⁻ gives a thick cream-yellow precipitate.", "3": "Sulphate is tested with barium nitrate, not silver nitrate."}'),
  ('CHE-0705', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for anions', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 333–334 (§16.6 c)', 'In the test for nitrate, a solution of the salt is warmed with NaOH and powdered aluminium. The gas that turns red litmus blue is', '["NH₃, because aluminium reduces nitrate", "NO₂, because aluminium oxidizes nitrate", "H₂, from aluminium and water only", "N₂, from the decomposition of nitrate"]', 0, 'Aluminium in alkali reduces nitrate to ammonia: 3NO₃⁻ + 8Al + 5OH⁻ + 18H₂O → 3NH₃ + 8[Al(OH)₄]⁻. Ammonia is alkaline and turns red litmus blue.', '{"1": "Nitrogen goes from +5 to −3, a reduction, and NO₂ is not the gas detected.", "2": "Hydrogen is not alkaline and does not turn litmus blue.", "3": "Nitrogen gas is neutral and does not affect litmus."}'),
  ('CHE-0706', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for anions', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 334 (§16.6 d)', 'Which reagent does the book use, after acidifying with dilute nitric acid, to test for sulphate ions?', '["Silver nitrate", "Barium nitrate", "Lime water", "Aluminium powder"]', 1, 'Barium nitrate gives a heavy white precipitate of BaSO₄ with sulphate ions.', '{"0": "Silver nitrate is used for halides.", "2": "Lime water is used to detect CO₂ from carbonates.", "3": "Aluminium powder with NaOH is used for nitrate."}'),
  ('CHE-0707', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 335 (§16.7 b)', 'A salt solution is warmed with NaOH and gives a gas with a distinct smell that turns moist red litmus blue. The cation present is', '["Ca²⁺", "NH₄⁺", "Zn²⁺", "Al³⁺"]', 1, 'NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O on warming; ammonia turns moist red litmus blue.', '{"0": "Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate with NaOH, not a gas.", "2": "Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate soluble in excess NaOH.", "3": "Al³⁺ gives a white gelatinous precipitate with NaOH."}'),
  ('CHE-0708', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 335–337 (§16.7)', 'A solution gives a white precipitate with NaOH that does not dissolve in excess, and only slight turbidity or no precipitate with aqueous ammonia. The cation is', '["Zn²⁺", "Al³⁺", "Ca²⁺", "Cu²⁺"]', 2, 'Ca(OH)₂ is precipitated by NaOH and stays in excess. Aqueous ammonia gives too few OH⁻ ions to precipitate much calcium hydroxide.', '{"0": "Zn(OH)₂ dissolves in excess NaOH and in excess ammonia.", "1": "Al³⁺ gives a white precipitate with aqueous ammonia too.", "3": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate, not a white one."}'),
  ('CHE-0709', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 336 (§16.7 e)', 'Excess aqueous ammonia added to a solution containing Cu²⁺ gives', '["a deep blue solution", "a white precipitate", "a green precipitate", "an orange-brown precipitate"]', 0, 'The book records a deep blue solution with aqueous ammonia; NaOH gives a light-blue Cu(OH)₂ precipitate.', '{"1": "White precipitates come from ions such as Al³⁺, Zn²⁺ and Ca²⁺.", "2": "A green precipitate comes from Cr³⁺ or Fe²⁺.", "3": "An orange-brown precipitate comes from Fe³⁺."}'),
  ('CHE-0710', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 335–336 (§16.7 d)', 'A green precipitate forms with NaOH and dissolves in excess NaOH to give a green solution. With aqueous ammonia a green precipitate forms that does not dissolve in excess. The cation is', '["Fe²⁺", "Cr³⁺", "Cu²⁺", "Fe³⁺"]', 1, 'Cr(OH)₃ is green and dissolves in excess NaOH, but stays undissolved in excess aqueous ammonia.', '{"0": "Fe(OH)₂ is green but does not dissolve in excess NaOH; it turns orange-brown on standing.", "2": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate and a deep blue solution with ammonia.", "3": "Fe³⁺ gives an orange-brown precipitate."}'),
  ('CHE-0711', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 337 (§16.7 g)', 'Which cation gives an orange-brown precipitate with both NaOH and aqueous ammonia?', '["Fe³⁺", "Cu²⁺", "Zn²⁺", "Ca²⁺"]', 0, 'Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃, an orange-brown precipitate, with either reagent.', '{"1": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate.", "2": "Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate.", "3": "Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate with NaOH."}'),
  ('CHE-0712', 'Experimental techniques and lab safety', 'Tests for cations', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Lab Safety and Practical Skills, p. 337 (§16.7 h)', 'A white precipitate dissolves in excess NaOH and also in excess aqueous ammonia. The cation is', '["Al³⁺", "Ca²⁺", "Zn²⁺", "Fe²⁺"]', 2, 'Zn(OH)₂ is white and dissolves in excess of both NaOH and aqueous ammonia, which distinguishes zinc from aluminium and calcium.', '{"0": "The book records only a white precipitate for Al³⁺ with aqueous ammonia, not one that dissolves in excess.", "1": "Ca(OH)₂ does not dissolve in excess NaOH.", "3": "Fe²⁺ gives a green precipitate."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

